2026-05-03 10:14:05 [nitro@ryzen5] sloppy and riff are both appearing but mention breaks ping pong i think
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] I see the issue you're describing. In `sloppybot.ts`, the code alternates between two listener
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] ports (8081 for "sloppy", 8082 for "riff") by toggling a flag `sloppyListen`. When the first
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] connection arrives on port 8081, it sets `riffPromise` and then switches to listen on 8082. This
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] means only one connection is active at a time – the other person is left waiting for a
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] connection that never arrives because the listener is swapped.
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] The "ping pong" break is likely because the Discord bot:
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] 1. Connects on port 8081 (sloppy)
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] 2. A second process or retry arrives on port 8082 (riff)
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] 3. The listener flips back to 8081, but the original fountain connection is now not being
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] read – the `receivePromise` for the first connection may be overwritten or lost.
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] Root cause:
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] The `listenService()` function in `slopprompt.ts` sets up a single listener (on 8081) and
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] then alternates. But `slopPail` and the relay code in `relay.ts` expect a reliable, permanent TCP
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] connection. The alternation is designed for two‑way separate processes (sloppy and riff),
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] but it doesn't handle concurrent or reconnecting peers.
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] Suggested fix:
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] Implement a single persistent listener or maintain both ports independently. In `slopprompt.ts`,
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] change `listenService` to start both ports in parallel:
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] export async function listenService() {
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4]     if (listenerPromise) return;
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4]     // Listen on both ports simultaneously
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4]     sloppyPromise = listenPort(8081, "sloppy");
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4]     riffPromise = listenPort(8082, "riff");
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4]     listenerPromise = Promise.race([sloppyPromise, riffPromise]);
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4]     sloppyListen = !sloppyListen; // initial toggle doesn't matter
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] }
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] But you also need to handle both connections without toggling. Instead of alternation, keep
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] both listener promises alive and handle each new connection independently.
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] Debugging steps:
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] 1. Check if `riff` is a separate client (e.g., another relay instance) – if so, they should each
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] connect to a dedicated port.
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] 2. Add logging in `sloppybot.ts` when a message is received from the fountain, and when it sends a
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] reply.
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] 3. Confirm the `openChannel` is set correctly after `!ping` – the `message.react("🦜")` and
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] `message.reply` might be competing with the fountain write.
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 10:14:17 [deepseek-v4] If you want, I can provide a revised version of `listenService` and the connection‑handling loop
2026-05-03 10:14:18 [deepseek-v4] that supports multiple concurrent clients.
