2026-05-03 10:15:13 [nitro@ryzen5] deno only allows one accept per process
2026-05-03 10:15:35 [deepseek-v4] You're right – Deno's `Deno.listen` returns a listener object, and each listener can only have one
2026-05-03 10:15:35 [deepseek-v4] pending `accept()` at a time. The current code in `slopprompt.ts` uses a single listener and
2026-05-03 10:15:35 [deepseek-v4] alternates between ports 8081 and 8082, meaning only one port can accept at any moment. This breaks
2026-05-03 10:15:35 [deepseek-v4] the "ping‑pong" because the other port's connection is ignored until the listener toggles back (and
2026-05-03 10:15:35 [deepseek-v4] by then the client may have timed out or retried).
2026-05-03 10:15:35 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 10:15:35 [deepseek-v4] The fix: two separate listeners
2026-05-03 10:15:35 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 10:15:35 [deepseek-v4] You can create two independent listeners, one per port, and race their `accept()` promises
2026-05-03 10:15:35 [deepseek-v4] in the main loop. That way both ports can accept connections concurrently.
2026-05-03 10:15:35 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4] In `slopprompt.ts`, replace the `listenService` function and the connection‑handling logic with
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4] something like this:
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4] let sloppyListener: Deno.Listener | null = null;
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4] let riffListener: Deno.Listener | null = null;
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4] export function listenService() {
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4]     if (sloppyListener) return; // already listening
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4]     try {
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4]         sloppyListener = Deno.listen({ hostname: "localhost", port: 8081 });
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4]         riffListener = Deno.listen({ hostname: "localhost", port: 8082 });
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4]         console.log("[PROMPT] listening on ports 8081 (sloppy) and 8082 (riff)");
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4]     } catch (e) {
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4]         console.error("[PROMPT] listen failed:", e);
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4]     }
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4] }
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4] // In the main loop, race both accept promises:
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4] async function acceptBoth() {
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4]     if (!sloppyListener || !riffListener) return;
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4]     const acceptSloppy = sloppyListener.accept().then(conn => ({ connection: conn, name: "sloppy", port: 8081 }));
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4]     const acceptRiff = riffListener.accept().then(conn => ({ connection: conn, name: "riff", port: 8082 }));
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4]     return Promise.race([acceptSloppy, acceptRiff]);
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4] }
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4] Then, in the main `slopPrompt` loop, use `acceptBoth()` inside the race instead of switching
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4] `listenerPromise` and toggling `sloppyListen`.
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4] Changes required in the connection handling
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4] - Each connection is uniquely identified by its port (or a counter) and stored in
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4] `slopConnections`.
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4] - When a connection arrives, you attach a read promise (`readNamedConnection`) but don't
2026-05-03 10:15:36 [deepseek-v4] toggle the listener; the other port remains active.
