2026-05-03 10:18:06 [nitro@ryzen5] both bots reacted to a ping but are ignoring at messages or reply is broken
2026-05-03 10:18:41 [deepseek-v4] Thanks for sharing the files. I see the issue: after a ping, the bots react but then ignore further
2026-05-03 10:18:41 [deepseek-v4] messages or replies are broken. Let’s trace through `sloppybot.ts` because that’s the Discord
2026-05-03 10:18:41 [deepseek-v4] interface.
2026-05-03 10:18:41 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 10:18:42 [deepseek-v4] Key Observations
2026-05-03 10:18:42 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 10:18:42 [deepseek-v4] 1. `openChannel` is only set after a `!ping` command.
2026-05-03 10:18:42 [deepseek-v4]    If the first mention isn’t `!ping`, the bot will never set `openChannel`. All subsequent
2026-05-03 10:18:42 [deepseek-v4] mentions in the correct channel will fail the `message.channelId==openChannel` check and be
2026-05-03 10:18:42 [deepseek-v4] ignored.
2026-05-03 10:18:42 [deepseek-v4]    Fix: Either default `openChannel` to a known channel ID, or listen to all channels where
2026-05-03 10:18:42 [deepseek-v4] the bot is mentioned.
2026-05-03 10:18:42 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 10:18:42 [deepseek-v4] 2. The main loop blocks on `readFountain` while Discord events must be handled
2026-05-03 10:18:42 [deepseek-v4] concurrently.
2026-05-03 10:18:42 [deepseek-v4]    The `messageCreate` handler calls `writeFountain` (async) but the main loop’s `readFountain` is
2026-05-03 10:18:42 [deepseek-v4] synchronous (await) and may starve the event loop. Discord.js relies on a single thread; if the bot
2026-05-03 10:18:42 [deepseek-v4] is busy waiting for the fountain socket, new Discord messages may queue up and not be processed
2026-05-03 10:18:42 [deepseek-v4] until the socket read returns.
2026-05-03 10:18:42 [deepseek-v4]    Fix: Read fountain messages in a non‑blocking way (e.g., use a separate async loop or
2026-05-03 10:18:42 [deepseek-v4] `Promise.race` with a timeout). The current `readFountain` is called in a tight loop with a 500ms
2026-05-03 10:18:42 [deepseek-v4] sleep, but `Promise.race` may still hold the thread.
2026-05-03 10:18:42 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 10:18:42 [deepseek-v4] 3. NDJSON parsing edge case
2026-05-03 10:18:42 [deepseek-v4]    `onFountain` looks for `"}\n{"` to split messages. If the fountain sends multiple messages
2026-05-03 10:18:42 [deepseek-v4] separated by `\r\n` or without a newline at all (e.g., `{"a":1}\n{"b":2}` but the buffer ends
2026-05-03 10:18:42 [deepseek-v4] mid‑object), the parsing may fail silently.
2026-05-03 10:18:42 [deepseek-v4]    Fix: Use a streaming JSON parser or read line‑by‑line.
2026-05-03 10:18:42 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 10:18:42 [deepseek-v4] 4. The `initSystem` promise never resolves (unless SIGINT)
2026-05-03 10:18:42 [deepseek-v4]    `systemPromise` is a promise that waits for `SIGINT`. It never fires `result.system`, so the
2026-05-03 10:18:42 [deepseek-v4] race always goes to `readFountain`. That's fine, but `onSystem` is called (if the branch were
2026-05-03 10:18:42 [deepseek-v4] reached) and is undefined – that would crash. Not your immediate issue, but clean it up.
2026-05-03 10:18:42 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 10:18:42 [deepseek-v4] 5. `readingSlop` flag is set but never checked
2026-05-03 10:18:42 [deepseek-v4]    If a read is already in progress, another `readFountain` call could conflict. Use a mutex or a
2026-05-03 10:18:42 [deepseek-v4] queue.
2026-05-03 10:18:42 [deepseek-v4] 
