2026-05-03 13:44:27 [nitro@ryzen5] can i wait for acceptPromises in my main loop, or should it be only 1?
2026-05-03 13:44:43 [deepseek-v4] You should loop on accepting connections, not rely on a single `acceptPromises`. Here’s
2026-05-03 13:44:43 [deepseek-v4] why:
2026-05-03 13:44:43 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 13:44:43 [deepseek-v4] Current state
2026-05-03 13:44:43 [deepseek-v4] In `relay.ts`, `acceptPromises` is assigned once:
2026-05-03 13:44:43 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 13:44:43 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 13:44:43 [deepseek-v4] acceptPromises = acceptAll();
2026-05-03 13:44:43 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 13:44:43 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 13:44:43 [deepseek-v4] …but it’s never `await`ed or used again. The promise resolves when one of the three
2026-05-03 13:44:43 [deepseek-v4] listeners (8081, 8082, 8083) receives a connection, and then nothing further happens – no new
2026-05-03 13:44:43 [deepseek-v4] accept call is made. This means only a single client can ever connect for the lifetime of the
2026-05-03 13:44:43 [deepseek-v4] program.
2026-05-03 13:44:43 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 13:44:43 [deepseek-v4] What you probably want
2026-05-03 13:44:43 [deepseek-v4] A persistent loop that repeatedly accepts new connections, like this:
2026-05-03 13:44:43 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 13:44:43 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 13:44:43 [deepseek-v4] while (roha.config.listen) {
2026-05-03 13:44:43 [deepseek-v4]   const { connection, name } = await acceptAll();
2026-05-03 13:44:43 [deepseek-v4]   // handle the connection (add to map, start reading, etc.)
2026-05-03 13:44:43 [deepseek-v4] }
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] This mirrors the pattern used in `slopprompt.ts` inside `slopPrompt`, where it re-arms the receive
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] promise after each message.
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] The design choice: one vs. many
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] - One‑shot accept – useful only if you expect exactly one client and want to block until it
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] arrives.
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] - Looping accept – standard for servers that need to handle multiple connections
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] sequentially.
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] If you want to accept multiple clients concurrently (e.g., several Discord bots or
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] scripts), you’d spawn separate handler tasks/workers and let the accept loop continue in the
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] background.
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] Recommended integration into the main chat loop
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] Since `chat()` is your main event loop and already runs indefinitely, you can run the accept loop
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] in a separate async function that you start before calling `chat()`:
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] async function runAcceptor() {
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4]   while (roha.config.listen) {
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4]     const { connection, name } = await acceptAll();
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4]     slopConnections.set(name, connection);
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4]     // start reading from the connection as done in slopprompt.ts
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4]     receivePromises[name] = readNamedConnection(name, connection);
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4]   }
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] }
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] Then in main:
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] if (roha.config.listen) {
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4]   listenService();
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4]   runAcceptor(); // fire-and-forget – runs in background
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] }
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] await chat();
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] This gives you persistent connection handling without blocking the user prompt loop.
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] One small note
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] `acceptAll()` in `slopprompt.ts` already exists and is used inside `slopPrompt`. If you want to
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] avoid duplication, you could consolidate the listening logic into a single service and share it
2026-05-03 13:44:44 [deepseek-v4] between `relay.ts` and `slopprompt.ts`. But for a quick fix, just add a loop as shown above.
