2026-05-03 16:17:39 [nitro@ryzen5] i am reworking listen and accept flow for bots\
2026-05-03 16:18:04 [deepseek-v4] I’ve reviewed the current listen/accept flow in `relay.ts` and `slopprompt.ts`. Here’s a breakdown
2026-05-03 16:18:04 [deepseek-v4] of how it works and the pain points I see:
2026-05-03 16:18:04 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 16:18:04 [deepseek-v4] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-05-03 16:18:04 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 16:18:04 [deepseek-v4] Current architecture
2026-05-03 16:18:04 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 16:18:04 [deepseek-v4] 1. `relay.ts` – `listenService()` opens listeners on ports 8081–8083. It also calls
2026-05-03 16:18:04 [deepseek-v4] `acceptThread()` which only accepts on `sloppyListener` (port 8081) in a loop.
2026-05-03 16:18:04 [deepseek-v4] 2. `slopprompt.ts` – Exports its own `listenService()` that also opens the same
2026-05-03 16:18:04 [deepseek-v4] three ports (duplicate). The `slopPrompt()` function, when called, also opens port 8081 and 8082
2026-05-03 16:18:04 [deepseek-v4] via `listenPort()` and then races an accept promise against the user input and existing connection
2026-05-03 16:18:05 [deepseek-v4] read promises.
2026-05-03 16:18:05 [deepseek-v4] 3. Bot connector (`sloppybot.ts`) – Opens a TCP connection to port 8081 and sends NDJSON
2026-05-03 16:18:05 [deepseek-v4] messages. The relay receives them via `readNamedConnection()`, which stores promises in
2026-05-03 16:18:05 [deepseek-v4] `receivePromises`.
2026-05-03 16:18:05 [deepseek-v4] 4. Race handling – In `slopPrompt()`, a `Promise.race()` races the stdin reader, a timer,
2026-05-03 16:18:05 [deepseek-v4] the listener accept promise, and all `receivePromises`. When a connection arrives, it’s stored in
2026-05-03 16:18:05 [deepseek-v4] `slopConnections` and a new read promise is created.
2026-05-03 16:18:05 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-03 16:18:05 [deepseek-v4] Major issues
