2026-05-04 08:29:06 [nitro@ryzen5] how is llm column calculated in accounts output
2026-05-04 08:29:24 [deepseek-v4] The term "LLM" in accounting outputs is not a universal standard, but in many accounting software
2026-05-04 08:29:24 [deepseek-v4] systems (e.g., Tally ERP, QuickBooks, or custom ERP reports), LLM commonly stands for
2026-05-04 08:29:24 [deepseek-v4] "Last Ledger Month" or "Last Month's Ledger" – representing the closing balance of
2026-05-04 08:29:24 [deepseek-v4] an account as of the end of the previous month.
2026-05-04 08:29:24 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 08:29:24 [deepseek-v4] Calculation (assuming Last Month’s Ledger)
2026-05-04 08:29:24 [deepseek-v4] 1. Identify the time period – The LLM column refers to the immediately preceding complete
2026-05-04 08:29:24 [deepseek-v4] month (e.g., if the report is for February, LLM shows January’s closing balance).
2026-05-04 08:29:24 [deepseek-v4] 2. Opening balance – Use the opening balance of the current financial period (or month).
2026-05-04 08:29:24 [deepseek-v4] 3. Net transactions – Sum all debit and credit entries for the account from the opening
2026-05-04 08:29:24 [deepseek-v4] date up to the end of the previous month.
2026-05-04 08:29:24 [deepseek-v4] 4. Closing balance – Compute the closing balance for that preceding month:
2026-05-04 08:29:24 [deepseek-v4]    `Closing Balance = Opening Balance + Debits – Credits`
2026-05-04 08:29:24 [deepseek-v4]    (or appropriate sign convention based on account type).
2026-05-04 08:29:24 [deepseek-v4] 5. LLM value – That closing balance becomes the LLM figure displayed in the current month’s
2026-05-04 08:29:24 [deepseek-v4] report.
2026-05-04 08:29:24 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 08:29:24 [deepseek-v4] 	Example:* For a Sales account with an opening balance of ₹0 in April, if March sales total
2026-05-04 08:29:24 [deepseek-v4] ₹10,000 (credit) and no debits, the LLM column in the April report would show ₹10,000 (the March
2026-05-04 08:29:24 [deepseek-v4] closing balance).
2026-05-04 08:29:24 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 08:29:24 [deepseek-v4] Alternative interpretations
2026-05-04 08:29:24 [deepseek-v4] - Ledger Line Mark – A system-generated flag indicating the last transaction line for that
2026-05-04 08:29:25 [deepseek-v4] account.
2026-05-04 08:29:25 [deepseek-v4] - Loan Loss Model – In banking/finance, LLM may refer to a provisioning model, but that is
2026-05-04 08:29:25 [deepseek-v4] rare in standard accounts output.
2026-05-04 08:29:25 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 08:29:25 [deepseek-v4] For a precise answer, please confirm:
2026-05-04 08:29:25 [deepseek-v4] - Which software or report you are using (e.g., Tally, QuickBooks, Zoho Books).
2026-05-04 08:29:25 [deepseek-v4] - Whether the column appears in a trial balance, ledger report, or profit & loss statement.
2026-05-04 08:29:25 [deepseek-v4] 
