2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] Based on the provided code from `relay.ts`, the LLM column in the accounts output
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] (generated by the `/account` command) is not "Last Ledger Month". Instead, it represents
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] the number of language models available from that account's connected provider.
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] Calculation
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] In the `onAccount` function (lines near `accountCommand` scheduling), the table row is built as:
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] const count = (models?.length) | 0;
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] echo_row(i, key, emoji, count, price(lode.credit), link);
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] The headers are defined as:
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] echo_row("id","name","brand","llm","credit","topup");
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] So the `llm` column corresponds to the variable `count`, which is derived from:
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] models = endpoint?.modelList || [];
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] count  = models.length
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] Where `endpoint` is the connected API object for that account (e.g., OpenAI, DeepSeek, Google,
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] Anthropic). After connecting to an account via `connectAccount(account)`, the `modelList` property
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] is populated by listing all deployable models from that provider's API (e.g., `/models` endpoint).
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] Example flow
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] 1. `enumerateModels()` iterates over all configured accounts (from `modelAccounts`).
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] 2. `connectAccount(account)` calls the appropriate connector (e.g., `connectOpenAI`,
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] `connectDeepSeek`) which fetches the model list.
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] 3. The list is stored in `endpoint.modelList`.
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] 4. Later, when `/account` is invoked, `onAccount` reads `endpoint?.modelList` and displays its
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] length as the `llm` column.
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] Thus, the column tells you how many distinct LLM identifiers are available under that
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] account – not a monetary or ledger value.
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] If you need confirmation, search the file for
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] `echo_row(i,key,emoji,count,price(lode.credit),link);` and the preceding line `const
2026-05-04 08:31:01 [deepseek-v4] count=(models?.length)|0;` – both appear in the `onAccount` handler.
