2026-05-05 09:58:33 [nitro@ryzen5] general messages are next, maybe i should guard against names that dont match, no code just discuss
2026-05-05 09:58:53 [deepseek-v4] Good idea. Let’s discuss the current situation and the design options.
2026-05-05 09:58:53 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-05 09:58:53 [deepseek-v4] Current Behaviour
2026-05-05 09:58:53 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-05 09:58:53 [deepseek-v4] In `slopbot.ts`, the `onFountain()` function handles two types of incoming lines:
2026-05-05 09:58:53 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-05 09:58:53 [deepseek-v4] 1. Lines starting with `/announce` – only the `foggy` bot acts on them (because of the `if
2026-05-05 09:58:53 [deepseek-v4] (botName=="foggy")` guard).
2026-05-05 09:58:53 [deepseek-v4] 2. All other lines (JSON messages) – every connected bot parses the JSON payload and calls
2026-05-05 09:58:53 [deepseek-v4] `messageSloppy()` for each message, sending it to the same Discord channel.
2026-05-05 09:58:53 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-05 09:58:53 [deepseek-v4] This means that when the relay broadcasts a model response (a “general message”), all three bots –
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] sloppy, riff, and foggy – each send that response to the Discord channel. That results in
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] triple‑posting, which is probably not what you want.
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] The Need for Name Guarding
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] You said “guard against names that don’t match”. I think you mean:
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] - Only one bot should handle general (non‑announce) messages, likely `foggy` (since it’s
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] already the announcer).
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] - The other bots (sloppy, riff) should either ignore general messages or handle different message
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] categories (e.g., status, debug, or specific `from` identifiers).
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] Currently there is no such guard – every bot sends everything. So you need a way to decide which
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] bot is responsible for each message type.
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] Design Options (Discussion, No Code)
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] Option A: Single dispatcher bot
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] Make `foggy` the sole handler for all messages (announce + general). Other bots only serve as
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] bridged sources (e.g., they receive input from Discord and forward it to the relay, but never write
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] back to Discord). This is simple and avoids duplicates, but loses the potential for per‑bot
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] personalities or separate channels.
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] Option B: Route by `from` field
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] The relay already includes a `from` field in each message (e.g., the emoji like `🐋` or the bot
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] name). Each bot could compare its own identifier against the `from` value:
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] - Only send a message to Discord if the `from` matches the bot’s own emoji/name.
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] - For example, `riff` would only relay messages where `from === "🐶"`; `foggy` where `from ===
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] "🐸"`.
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] This gives each bot a dedicated voice and avoids duplicates.
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] Option C: Route by message type
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] Tag messages with a category (e.g., `announce`, `reply`, `status`) and let each bot subscribe to
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] certain categories. `foggy` gets everything, `riff` gets only replies, `sloppy` only status –
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] flexible but adds complexity on both relay and bot sides.
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] Option D: Use a dedicated channel per bot
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] Instead of all bots writing to one channel, give each bot its own Discord channel. Then there’s no
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] conflict – every bot sends everything it receives. This means the user would see three parallel
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] channels with the same content, which might be desired for testing but not for normal operation.
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] What I recommend for now
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] Given you’re testing and want to avoid duplicates, the simplest guard is option A: let only
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] `foggy` send non‑announce messages to Discord. The other bots will still receive and forward user
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] messages to the relay, but when the relay broadcasts a response, only `foggy` writes it out. That
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] matches your statement “making foggy the default message announce for app messages”.
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] You could also combine with option B for clarity: set `sloppy` and `riff` to ignore all
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] broadcasts unless the `from` specifically matches their identifier.
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] Next step
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] If you’d like, I can help you review where in `onFountain()` (or `messageSloppy`) the guard should
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] be placed, without writing code – just discussing the logic. Otherwise, if you’re ready to
2026-05-05 09:58:54 [deepseek-v4] implement, let me know and we can proceed.
