2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] Anthropic, xAI, Z.AI, Moonshot, Ollama) via their respective APIs.
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] - Two separate console workers (`slopbot.ts`) for Discord bots (`foggy`, `sloppy`, `riff`), each
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] communicating with the relay over TCP (port `8081`).
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] - A raw-mode terminal interface (`slopprompt.ts`) that handles Unicode, shortcode replacement, and
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] async I/O.
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] - A configuration system via `forge.json` that persists model metadata, share history, and
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] counters.
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] The logs (`output.txt`, `output2.txt`) show it working end‑to‑end:
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] - Discord user `scudmarks` sends a message → relay sends it to DeepSeek‑V4 → reply is broadcast
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] back to all Discord bots and the terminal.
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] - The relay’s status line (`ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.1 ⛲ deepseek-v4 …`) appears in the terminal and
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] is forwarded as a `> -` line to the bots.
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] Notable Strengths
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] - Provider independence: The code handles both OpenAI‑compatible APIs (DeepSeek, xAI,
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] Moonshot, Ollama) and Google/Anthropic bespoke endpoints.
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] - Tool calling: The relay supports `function` tools (`readtime`, `submitfile`,
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] etc.) and handles recursive calls when a model requests tools.
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] - Cost tracking: Credits are updated per request, and tokens are logged and displayed.
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] - Raw‑mode terminal: `slopprompt.ts` handles backspace, multi‑byte UTF‑8, shortcode
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] expansion, and ANSI sequences on Windows/macOS/Linux.
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] - In‑memory session stack: `/begin` and `/finish` allow pushing/popping conversation
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] context – useful for experimental branching.
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] Potential Improvements / Issues Noticed
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] 1. Verbose Logging in `slopbot.ts`
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] The `splurt` function sends debug output to `console.error` on every message. This is fine for
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] development, but in production you might want to:
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] - Use a configurable log level (e.g., `if (splurtEnabled)`)
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] - Or redirect to a file / discard when not debugging.
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] The logs show `channel dump` and `guild` info being dumped on every incoming message – this
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] adds latency (the `sleep(1500)` after each send is already there) and clutter.
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] 2. Race Condition in `slopPrompt.ts`
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] The `receivePromises` map is updated inside a loop while reading. When a connection closes, the
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] deletion `delete receivePromises[name]` may happen while another `Promise.race` is still awaiting
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] that promise, leading to a `undefined` result. Consider using `AbortController` or a more robust
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] “cancellable promise” pattern.
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] 3. DeepSeek‑V4 “reasoning” tokens
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] The log shows `completiontokensdetails.reasoning_tokens: 204`. The relay prints the
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] reasoning content if `reasonoutloud` flag is on, but currently it seems not to be displayed in the
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] terminal output (only the final answer). That’s configurable (`roha.config.reasonoutloud`).
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] 4. File Sharing / Blob Handling
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] The `commitShares` function reads entire text or image files into memory and sends them as base64.
2026-05-07 11:21:53 [deepseek-v4] This works for small files, but `MaxFileSize` is defined as 512 KB, which might be large for some
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] providers (Gemini has a 20 MB limit, but OpenAI’s vision models accept images up to ~20 MB).
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] Consider respecting per‑model limits stored in `modelSpecs`.
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] 5. Unicode Width Calculation
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] The `stringWidth` and `discordStringWidth` functions have hard‑coded wide/unwide ranges. This is
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] fragile – the Unicode standard evolves (e.g., `🪣` U+1FAD3 was added in Unicode 15.0; your ranges
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] stop at `0x1FAFF` so it’s correctly marked as wide). Consider using a library like `wcwidth` or
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] `@std/unicode` instead.
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] 6. Hard‑coded `rohaTools`
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] The function tools are defined in code, not loaded from a file. This makes it impossible to add new
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] tools without recompiling. A plugin system (e.g., scanning a `tools/` directory) would be more
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] flexible.
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] 7. Error Handling in `relay()`
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] The `try/catch` block is large and catches many specific error messages. Some errors (like `429
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] ratelimiterror`) are handled, but many generic errors just echo the message and stack. This
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] could lead to silent failures. Better to re‑throw or log with severity levels.
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] 8. `pushonshare` flag
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] In `flagNames` the key is `pushonshare` (note the extra `s`). In `emptyConfig` it’s `pushonshare` –
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] but in the actual code, `/push` calls `commitShares` and then checks `if
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] (roha.config.pushonshare)`. There’s a mismatch: the flag name in `flagNames` is `pushonshare` (with
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] 's'), but in the code it's referenced as `roha.config.pushonshare` (missing 's'?). Let’s
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] double-check:
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] - `flagNames` line: `pushonshare : "emit a /push after any /share",`
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] - `emptyConfig` line: `pushonshare:false,`
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] They match. But in `callCommand` under `/push` or `/commit`: the code never reads
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] `roha.config.pushonshare`; it only calls `commitShares`. Did you intend to use it? It seems the
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] flag exists but isn’t activated anywhere else. Maybe it’s a leftover from an earlier version.
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] 9. Logging `outputBuffer` in `flush()`
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] `flush()` writes to `stdout` and also sends the output to all connected bots via `slopBroadcast`.
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] It does both even when the output is from the model’s reply (which already gets sent via
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] `slopBroadcast` inside `relay()`). This results in duplicate messages reaching the bots (the model
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] reply + the echoed `> -` lines). The logs show:
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] [SLOPBOT] result: { message: '{"messages":[{"message":"\\r\\n### I'm doing well…","from":"🐋"}]}' }
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] [SLOPBOT] result: { message: '{"messages":[{"message":"> - ꔀ nitrologic…","from":"slop"}]}' }
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] Both arrive at the bot. That’s by design? It might be noisy.
2026-05-07 11:21:54 [deepseek-v4] 
