2026-05-07 13:38:44 [deepseek-v4] The provided `chroma.pio` appears to be a PIO program for generating the chroma (color burst)
2026-05-07 13:38:44 [deepseek-v4] component of a PAL 576i video signal on a Raspberry Pi Pico 2, clocked at 166.5 MHz with 10656
2026-05-07 13:38:44 [deepseek-v4] cycles per horizontal line.
2026-05-07 13:38:44 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 13:38:44 [deepseek-v4] Observations
2026-05-07 13:38:44 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 13:38:44 [deepseek-v4] - IRQ synchronisation (`wait 1 irq 0` / `irq clear 0`): The program waits for an external
2026-05-07 13:38:44 [deepseek-v4] trigger (IRQ 0) before processing, then clears it. This aligns with line‑by‑line video generation
2026-05-07 13:38:44 [deepseek-v4] where a separate sync program likely fires IRQ 0 at the start of each active line.
2026-05-07 13:38:44 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 13:38:44 [deepseek-v4] - Loop counter (`out x, 32` then `jmp !x start [16]`): The OSR (output shift register) is
2026-05-07 13:38:44 [deepseek-v4] expected to hold a 32‑bit value that is shifted into `x`. If `x` is zero, the program jumps back to
2026-05-07 13:38:44 [deepseek-v4] `start` (with a 16‑cycle delay, possibly to pad to line length). Otherwise it enters the `loop3837`
2026-05-07 13:38:44 [deepseek-v4] routine.
2026-05-07 13:38:44 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 13:38:44 [deepseek-v4] - Output pattern (`out pindirs, 9/8`): The eight `out pindirs` instructions shift 9 or 8
2026-05-07 13:38:44 [deepseek-v4] bits from the OSR (or possibly from a different source – see note below) into the pin direction
2026-05-07 13:38:44 [deepseek-v4] register.
2026-05-07 13:38:44 [deepseek-v4]   Important: In PIO, `out pindirs` sets pin direction (1 = output, 0 = input). It
2026-05-07 13:38:44 [deepseek-v4] does not directly set pin output values. For chroma generation, one typically needs to
2026-05-07 13:38:44 [deepseek-v4] toggle a GPIO output level at the color subcarrier frequency (4.43361875 MHz for PAL). Using
2026-05-07 13:38:44 [deepseek-v4] `pindirs` alone would only enable/disable the pin driver, not produce a waveform unless the pin is
2026-05-07 13:38:44 [deepseek-v4] already configured as open‑drain or pull‑up/pull‑down – which seems unlikely for a clean colour
2026-05-07 13:38:44 [deepseek-v4] burst.
2026-05-07 13:38:44 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 13:38:44 [deepseek-v4]   More common techniques for colour burst in PIO include:
2026-05-07 13:38:44 [deepseek-v4]   - Using `out pins` to set output values directly.
2026-05-07 13:38:44 [deepseek-v4]   - Using `set pins` with side‑set.
2026-05-07 13:38:44 [deepseek-v4]   - Combining `out pindirs` with a known initial output value (e.g., pin high and direction out
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4] toggles the output? – actually direction change does not change the pin level; it only
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4] enables/disables the output driver).
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4]   The pattern of alternating 9 and 8 bits hints at a duty‑cycle variation (9 bits out of a 17‑bit
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4] sequence?), but without a clear understanding of how the OSR is loaded the intent remains
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4] ambiguous.
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4] - Loop body structure: The eight `out pindirs` lines produce a pattern of three `9`s and
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4] five `8`s? Counting:
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4]   out pindirs, 9   (1)
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4]   out pindirs, 8   (2)
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4]   out pindirs, 9   (3)
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4]   out pindirs, 8   (4)
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4]   out pindirs, 9   (5)
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4]   out pindirs, 8   (6)
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4]   out pindirs, 8   (7)
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4]   out pindirs, 8   (8)
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4]   That’s 3×9=27 bits and 5×8=40 bits, totalling 67 bits per iteration. The loop then decrements `x`
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4] and jumps back. If the OSR is 32 bits, shifting 67 bits per iteration would drain the OSR quickly –
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4] implying the OSR must be refilled (perhaps via `pull` hidden in a side‑set or separate state
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4] machine).
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4] - End of line (`set pindirs, 0`): After the loop, all pin directions are set to input. This
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4] likely disables the chroma output, leaving the line in a known state.
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4] Potential Issues
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4] 1. No OSR load – The program assumes the OSR is already filled with data. In a typical PIO
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4] video implementation, the colour burst pattern is pre‑loaded or pulled from TX FIFO. Without
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4] explicit `pull` or `mov` to OSR, the burst will not be generated.
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4] 2. Pin direction vs. pin level – As noted, toggling pin directions does not produce a
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4] colour waveform unless the pins are configured with pull‑ups/-downs that create a voltage change
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4] when direction changes. This is non‑standard and likely to produce incorrect timing.
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4] 3. Clock cycle count – The PAL colour burst lasts about 2.25 µs (10±1 cycles of 4.43 MHz).
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4] At 166.5 MHz, one cycle of the subcarrier is ~37.5 PIO cycles. The loop body of 8 `out`
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4] instructions takes at least 8 cycles (assuming no wait states). If each `out` shifts 9 or 8 bits,
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4] the total cycles per iteration would exceed 8, but the instruction count itself is fixed. The
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4] `[16]` delay after `jmp` is present, but the loop lacks explicit delay instructions to match the
2026-05-07 13:38:45 [deepseek-v4] subcarrier period.
