2026-05-07 13:41:13 [deepseek-v4] specifications.
2026-05-07 13:41:13 [deepseek-v4] - State machine usage: The program handles both vertical sync (the `pulse` loop) and
2026-05-07 13:41:13 [deepseek-v4] horizontal timing (`line` loop). The vertical sync sequence uses a table of pulse widths (likely
2026-05-07 13:41:13 [deepseek-v4] stored in the TX FIFO or OSR pre‑load). The horizontal line loop then repeats for each active line.
2026-05-07 13:41:13 [deepseek-v4] - IRQ handshake: `irq set 0` is issued during the back porch (after HSYNC). This is
2026-05-07 13:41:13 [deepseek-v4] intended to trigger the `chroma` state machine to generate the colour burst. The chroma program
2026-05-07 13:41:13 [deepseek-v4] waits on IRQ 0 at its `start` label, which is a clean synchronisation point (colour burst occurs on
2026-05-07 13:41:13 [deepseek-v4] the back porch).
2026-05-07 13:41:13 [deepseek-v4] - Pin control: The code mixes `set pins`, `set pindirs`, and `out pindirs`. For a mono
2026-05-07 13:41:13 [deepseek-v4] (luma) signal this works because the pin level can be set directly while the direction is held as
2026-05-07 13:41:13 [deepseek-v4] output. The colour burst, however, requires toggling a subcarrier frequency, which the chroma
2026-05-07 13:41:13 [deepseek-v4] program likely does with `out pindirs, 9/8` – a technique that relies on toggling direction
2026-05-07 13:41:13 [deepseek-v4] while the pin output is high (or low) to create a crude waveform. If the mono version uses the same
2026-05-07 13:41:13 [deepseek-v4] GPIO pin(s) for both sync and video, the direction approach may work, but for colour you need
2026-05-07 13:41:14 [deepseek-v4] precise alternating levels.
