2026-05-07 14:53:45 [nitro@ryzen5] how do i irq between state machines on pio?
2026-05-07 14:54:09 [deepseek-v4] To signal between state machines (SMs) within the same PIO block, you use the internal IRQ flags
2026-05-07 14:54:09 [deepseek-v4] (0–7). Each SM can set, clear, or wait on these flags using the `irq` and `wait` instructions.
2026-05-07 14:54:09 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 14:54:09 [deepseek-v4] • Key instructions:*
2026-05-07 14:54:09 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 14:54:09 [deepseek-v4] - `irq set <n>` – set IRQ flag `n` (0–7).
2026-05-07 14:54:09 [deepseek-v4] - `irq clear <n>` – clear IRQ flag `n`.
2026-05-07 14:54:09 [deepseek-v4] - `irq wait <n>` – wait until flag `n` is cleared by another SM or the system (rarely used for
2026-05-07 14:54:09 [deepseek-v4] inter-SM signalling).
2026-05-07 14:54:09 [deepseek-v4] - `wait 1 irq <n>` – wait for IRQ flag `n` to become set.
2026-05-07 14:54:09 [deepseek-v4] - `wait 0 irq <n>` – wait for IRQ flag `n` to become cleared.
2026-05-07 14:54:09 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 14:54:09 [deepseek-v4] • Within the same PIO block:*
2026-05-07 14:54:09 [deepseek-v4] All SMs see the same IRQ flags. IRQ0–3 can also trigger a system interrupt, but you can use any
2026-05-07 14:54:09 [deepseek-v4] flag (0–7) for SM-to-SM communication. IRQ4–7 are also visible to the other PIO block (if
2026-05-07 14:54:09 [deepseek-v4] present), so for intra-block communication you may prefer IRQ0–3 to avoid confusion.
2026-05-07 14:54:09 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 14:54:09 [deepseek-v4] • Simple example (PIO assembly, pioasm syntax):*
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] .program producer
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4]     ; Continuously set IRQ0 after each sample
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] loop:
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4]     out y, 32         ; read 32-bit sample
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4]     mov pins, y       ; output to pins
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4]     irq set 0         ; signal consumer SM
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4]     jmp loop
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] .program consumer
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4]     ; Wait for IRQ0, then read
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] loop:
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4]     wait 1 irq 0      ; block until IRQ0 is set
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4]     irq clear 0       ; clear flag (optional, for next cycle)
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4]     in pins, 32       ; capture input
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4]     push              ; push to RX FIFO
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4]     jmp loop
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] In this scenario, SM0 (producer) sets IRQ0 after writing a sample. SM1 (consumer) waits for that
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] flag, clears it, then processes the data. The `irq clear` is necessary if you want the flag to be
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] reused for the next sample; otherwise, the wait would never block again because the flag remains
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] set.
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] • C SDK usage (if you prefer):*
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] When adding the program with `pioaddprogram()` and starting the state machines, you don't
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] need any special setup – the IRQ flags are automatically available.
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] • Clearing flags:*
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] If you don't explicitly clear an IRQ flag in the consumer, you can also clear it from the producer
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] side (e.g., using `irq clear 0` after the `wait` in the consumer, or using the `irq wait` variant
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] which clears automatically? No, `irq wait` waits for the flag to be cleared, so it's not the same).
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] The simplest pattern is: producer sets → consumer waits, processes, then clears.
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] • Avoiding race conditions:*
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] The hardware guarantees that setting and clearing are atomic from the perspective of other SMs.
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] However, if both SMs try to modify the same flag simultaneously, the result is deterministic (the
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] last instruction wins). For most handshake patterns, one SM sets and the other clears, which avoids
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] conflict.
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] • System interrupts (optional):*
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] If you also need the main CPU to be notified, you can enable a system interrupt on IRQ0 (via
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] `piosetirq0sourcemask` or `irqsetenabled`). But for pure SM-to-SM, that's
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] unnecessary.
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] Would you like a concrete code snippet (e.g., in C or MicroPython) that shows how to set up both
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] state machines with the above programs?
2026-05-07 14:54:10 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 14:54:52 [nitro@ryzen5] between two blocks
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] When communicating between two different PIO blocks (PIO0 and PIO1), you cannot use IRQ0–3
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] because those are local to each block. Instead you must use IRQ4 through IRQ7, which are
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] shared (global) across both PIO blocks.
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] Any state machine in either block can set/clear/wait on IRQ4–7, and the other block’s state
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] machines will see the same flags.
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] • Key points:*
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] - Only IRQ4–IRQ7 are visible to both PIO blocks.
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] - IRQ0–IRQ3 remain private to the respective PIO block.
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] - The instruction syntax is identical: `irq set 4`, `irq clear 5`, `wait 1 irq 6`, etc.
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] - The global flags can also trigger system interrupts (like any other IRQ), but that's optional.
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] • Example (PIO0 producer, PIO1 consumer):*
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] ; PIO0 program (producer)
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] .program producer_pio0
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] loop:
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4]     out y, 32
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4]     mov pins, y
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4]     irq set 4          ; signal PIO1 via global IRQ4
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4]     jmp loop
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] ; PIO1 program (consumer)
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] .program consumer_pio1
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] loop:
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4]     wait 1 irq 4       ; block until IRQ4 is set by PIO0
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4]     irq clear 4        ; clear it for next cycle
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4]     in pins, 32
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4]     push
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4]     jmp loop
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] • C SDK setup:*
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] You load the two programs into their respective PIO blocks (`pio0` and `pio1`), start the state
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] machines, and they will automatically use the global IRQ flags. No extra configuration is needed –
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] the hardware allows any SM to access IRQ4–7 regardless of block.
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] • Caveats:*
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] - If both blocks try to set the same global flag simultaneously, the last instruction to execute
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] (per cycle) wins. For handshake patterns (one sets, the other clears) this rarely causes issues.
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] - The cross-block path is registered (takes 1–2 PIO cycles), but the `wait` instruction will stall
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] until the flag is actually seen. This is fine for typical signalling.
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] If you need more than four shared flags, you can also use GPIOs or an external SM‑to‑SM FIFO (via a
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] chain of state machines and shared pins) – but for simple signalling, IRQ4–7 are the cleanest
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] method.
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] Would you like a complete C code example that sets up both PIO blocks with the above programs?
2026-05-07 14:55:07 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 14:56:19 [nitro@ryzen5] gemini can you confirm irq4 betwen pio on PICO 2 will work
2026-05-07 14:56:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] Yes, I can confirm that using IRQ4 will work for communicating between PIO blocks on the Raspberry
2026-05-07 14:56:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] Pi Pico 2.
2026-05-07 14:56:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 14:56:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Pico 2 uses the RP2350 microcontroller, which actually features three PIO blocks (PIO0,
2026-05-07 14:56:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] PIO1, and PIO2), upgrading from the two blocks found on the original Pico.
2026-05-07 14:56:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 14:56:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] The hardware design maintains the same interrupt sharing rules:
2026-05-07 14:56:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] • IRQ0–IRQ3 remain completely local to their specific PIO block.
2026-05-07 14:56:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] • IRQ4–IRQ7 are globally shared across all three PIO blocks.
2026-05-07 14:56:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 14:56:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] This means a state machine in PIO0 can set IRQ4, and state machines in both PIO1 and PIO2 can wait
2026-05-07 14:56:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] on or clear that exact same flag. The syntax and behaviour remain identical to the original Pico.
2026-05-07 14:56:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 14:58:35 [nitro@ryzen5] is this how i wait for irq 4?
2026-05-07 14:58:36 [nitro@ryzen5] start:	wait	0 irq 4	irq	clear 4
2026-05-07 14:58:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] That syntax is not quite right. In PIO assembly, you cannot combine the `wait` instruction and the
2026-05-07 14:58:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] `irq clear` instruction on the same line. They are two distinct instructions and must be written
2026-05-07 14:58:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] sequentially.
2026-05-07 14:58:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 14:58:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] Additionally, if you are waiting for another state machine to signal this one, you typically want
2026-05-07 14:58:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] to wait for the IRQ to become set (`1`), rather than cleared (`0`).
2026-05-07 14:58:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 14:58:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is the correct way to write it:
2026-05-07 14:58:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 14:58:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 14:58:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] start:
2026-05-07 14:58:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wait 1 irq 4    ; Block until IRQ 4 is SET (1) by the other PIO block
2026-05-07 14:58:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	irq clear 4     ; Clear the flag so it can be used again for the next signal
2026-05-07 14:58:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 14:58:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 14:58:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] • To break down why:*
2026-05-07 14:58:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `wait 1 irq 4` tells the state machine to stall here as long as IRQ 4 is `0`. As soon as the
2026-05-07 14:58:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] other state machine executes `irq set 4`, this instruction completes.
2026-05-07 14:58:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `irq clear 4` immediately resets the flag back to `0`. If you do not clear it, the next time the
2026-05-07 14:58:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] state machine loops back to the `wait` instruction, it will fall straight through without waiting.
2026-05-07 14:58:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 14:59:56 [nitro@ryzen5] how many color clocks will be sent for PAL active line?
2026-05-07 15:00:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] For a standard PAL video signal, the number of color clocks (color subcarrier cycles) during the
2026-05-07 15:00:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] active portion of the line depends on the exact timing standard you are following, but it is
2026-05-07 15:00:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] typically around 230.5 cycles.
2026-05-07 15:00:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 15:00:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is the breakdown of the timing:
2026-05-07 15:00:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 15:00:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Color Subcarrier Frequency (F_sc): 4.43361875 MHz
2026-05-07 15:00:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Total Line Duration: 64 microseconds (μ s)
2026-05-07 15:00:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Active Line Duration: Approximately 52 μ s (the remaining 12 μ s is horizontal blanking,
2026-05-07 15:00:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] including the front porch, sync pulse, and color burst)
2026-05-07 15:00:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 15:00:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Calculating the cycles:*
2026-05-07 15:00:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Total line: 64 μ s × 4.43361875 \text MHz = \mathbf283.75 color clocks per full line.
2026-05-07 15:00:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Active line: 52 μ s × 4.43361875 \text MHz \approx \mathbf230.55 color clocks for the
2026-05-07 15:00:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] visible portion.
2026-05-07 15:00:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 15:00:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Implementing this on PIO:*
2026-05-07 15:00:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because state machines require integer counts for loop iterations and delays, you cannot output
2026-05-07 15:00:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] fractional clocks directly. When generating composite video with a microcontroller, it is standard
2026-05-07 15:00:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] practice to run the PIO clock at a multiple of the color subcarrier frequency to allow for precise
2026-05-07 15:00:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] color phase control (which dictates the hue).
2026-05-07 15:00:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 15:00:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] A very common approach is to run the system or PIO clock at 4 × F_sc (17.734475 MHz).
2026-05-07 15:00:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 15:00:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] At a 4 × F_sc clock rate:
2026-05-07 15:00:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Total line: 283.75 × 4 = \mathbf1135 PIO clock cycles.
2026-05-07 15:00:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Active line: 230.55 × 4 \approx \mathbf922 PIO clock cycles (often rounded to a
2026-05-07 15:00:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] convenient number of pixels for the frame buffer, such as 720 or 768, padded with border timing).
2026-05-07 15:00:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 15:00:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] The exact number of active clocks you choose to send from your PIO will depend on your desired
2026-05-07 15:00:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] horizontal resolution and how much overscan you wish to include in your visible area.
2026-05-07 15:00:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
