2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] This is a compact PIO implementation for PAL video synthesis. By outputting directly to `pindirs`,
2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] you are essentially using the PIO to rapidly toggle the tri-state buffers of the GPIO pins to
2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] switch between high, low, and high-impedance (floating) states, which is a clever way to generate
2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] analog voltage levels for video signals.
2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] I notice a few things in your code that might need consideration regarding the RP2350 architecture
2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] and PIO constraints:
2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. `pindirs` vs `pins`
2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] In your `loop3837` section, you are outputting to `pindirs`. While this works for creating
2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] high-impedance states, remember that `pindirs` affects the entire GPIO bank or the pins assigned to
2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] the state machine. If you are using a resistor ladder (R-2R) for DAC output, ensure that the pins
2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] are configured correctly in your C/MicroPython setup so that the PIO has permission to toggle those
2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] directions.
2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. The `jmp x--` cycle penalty
2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] In PIO, the `jmp` instruction takes 1 cycle, but if the jump is taken, it can sometimes be tight
2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] depending on the instruction flow.
2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Your `out` instructions are 1 cycle each.
2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The `jmp x-- loop3837` will take 1 cycle if the jump is taken (and the decrement happens).
2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] • If you find your timing is drifting slightly over the course of a line, ensure the total number
2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] of cycles in your loop matches the expected pixel clock perfectly.
2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3. RP2350 Specifics (Pico 2)
2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] Since you are targeting the RP2350:
2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Clock speed: 166.5 MHz is a non-standard frequency. Ensure your system clock
2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] (`sysclk`) is explicitly set to this in your `clockconfigure` routine, otherwise, the PIO
2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] timing will be off.
2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] • FIFO usage: You are pulling 32 bits at a time into `x` and then processing `out`
2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] instructions. Ensure your C code is pushing data into the TX FIFO fast enough to prevent a stall.
2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] If the FIFO empties, the PIO will stall, which will cause a "glitch" in your PAL signal timing.
2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] A Suggestion for Structure
2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you are planning to expand this, consider using a `side-set` to handle the sync pulses or
2026-05-07 15:06:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] blanking signals. It can help keep your main `out` loop cleaner.
