2026-05-07 15:07:36 [nitro@ryzen5] ; chroma.pio (c)2026 Simon Armstrong; PAL 576i with 166.5mhz Pico2 PIO = 10656 cycles per line; ~230 color clock per line signalled from vidbit on irq 4.program chromastart:	wait	1 irq 4	irq	clear 4	out	x, 32	jmp	!x start [16]loop3837:	out	pins, 9	out	pins, 8	out	pins, 9	out	pins, 8	out	pins, 9	out	pins, 8	out	pins, 8	out	pins, 8	jmp	x-- loop3837	set	pins, 0.wrap
2026-05-07 15:07:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] This revised version using `out pins` is much more standard for video generation on the Pico, as it
2026-05-07 15:07:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] allows you to output the color burst/chroma signal directly to a resistor ladder DAC.
2026-05-07 15:07:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:07:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] Since you are targeting the RP2350 (Pico 2), here are a few observations regarding the logic flow
2026-05-07 15:07:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] and potential optimizations for this specific state machine:
2026-05-07 15:07:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:07:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. Cycle Counting and Jitter
2026-05-07 15:07:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] In your `loop3837`, the sequence of 8 `out` instructions followed by the `jmp x--` creates a loop
2026-05-07 15:07:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] of 9 cycles.
2026-05-07 15:07:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] • If your goal is to maintain a strict pixel clock, ensure that the total number of cycles
2026-05-07 15:07:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] (including the `wait` and `out x, 32`) perfectly aligns with the PAL line duration.
2026-05-07 15:07:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The `jmp x--` instruction is efficient, but remember that the `jmp` itself takes 1 cycle. If `x`
2026-05-07 15:07:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] is 0, the jump is not taken, and the state machine proceeds to the `set pins, 0` instruction. This
2026-05-07 15:07:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] transition needs to be accounted for in your total line timing.
2026-05-07 15:07:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:07:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. The `out` instruction limitations
2026-05-07 15:07:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] You are using `out pins, 9` and `out pins, 8`.
2026-05-07 15:07:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Ensure that in your PIO configuration (in C/C++), you have set
2026-05-07 15:07:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] `smconfigsetoutpins(&c, pin_base, count)`.
2026-05-07 15:07:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] • If you are using multiple pins, the `out` instruction will map the bits from the OSR (Output
2026-05-07 15:07:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] Shift Register) to the consecutive pins starting at `pin_base`. If your DAC layout isn't strictly
2026-05-07 15:07:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] sequential, you might need to pre-format the data in your C code.
2026-05-07 15:07:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:07:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3. RP2350 `out` behavior
2026-05-07 15:07:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] On the RP2350, the PIO is functionally similar to the RP2040, but if you find yourself needing to
2026-05-07 15:07:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] handle more complex color subcarrier patterns, remember that you have access to the `mov`
2026-05-07 15:07:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] instructions to manipulate the X and Y registers without needing to pull from the FIFO.
2026-05-07 15:07:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:07:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] Proposed Refinement
2026-05-07 15:07:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you find that the C-side transfer is struggling to keep up with the FIFO, you can use the
2026-05-07 15:07:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] `autopull` feature in the PIO configuration. This removes the need to manually manage the FIFO
2026-05-07 15:07:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] thresholds.
2026-05-07 15:07:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:07:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] Here is how you would typically initialize the configuration in C to match your PIO code:
2026-05-07 15:07:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:07:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:07:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] void chroma_program_init(PIO pio, uint sm, uint offset, uint pin_base) {
2026-05-07 15:07:42 [gemini-3.1-lite]     pio_sm_config c = chroma_program_get_default_config(offset);
2026-05-07 15:07:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
