2026-05-07 15:08:22 [nitro@ryzen5] oh i thought those were delay slots in the out instructions
2026-05-07 15:08:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] Ah, I see the confusion! In PIO assembly, the brackets `[number]` at the end of an instruction are
2026-05-07 15:08:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] indeed delay cycles. However, the numbers following the instruction (like the `9` and `8`
2026-05-07 15:08:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] in `out pins, 9`) are bit counts.
2026-05-07 15:08:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:08:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] In your code:
2026-05-07 15:08:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] `out pins, 9`
2026-05-07 15:08:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:08:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] • `out`: The instruction.
2026-05-07 15:08:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] • `pins`: The destination.
2026-05-07 15:08:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] • `9`: The number of bits to shift out of the OSR (Output Shift Register) to the pins.
2026-05-07 15:08:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:08:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] These are not delay slots. The instruction executes in a single cycle (unless you add a
2026-05-07 15:08:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] delay like `out pins, 9 [1]`).
2026-05-07 15:08:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:08:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] Why this matters for your PAL timing:
2026-05-07 15:08:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you intended for those to be delays, your current loop is actually running much faster than you
2026-05-07 15:08:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] might have calculated.
2026-05-07 15:08:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:08:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Each `out pins, X` takes 1 cycle.
2026-05-07 15:08:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Your loop has 8 `out` instructions.
2026-05-07 15:08:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The `jmp` takes 1 cycle.
2026-05-07 15:08:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Total loop duration: 9 cycles per 32 bits (or 8 chunks of data).
2026-05-07 15:08:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:08:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you want to add delays (for example, to stretch the signal to hit your 10656-cycle target), you
2026-05-07 15:08:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] must add the brackets explicitly. For example:
2026-05-07 15:08:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:08:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] `out pins, 9 [2]`  <-- This instruction takes 1 cycle + 2 delay cycles = 3 total cycles.
2026-05-07 15:08:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:08:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] Correcting the logic
2026-05-07 15:08:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you were trying to create "dead time" or specific timing for the color burst/chroma signal, you
2026-05-07 15:08:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] should structure it like this:
2026-05-07 15:08:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:08:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:08:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] loop3837:
2026-05-07 15:08:26 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out pins, 9 [2]  ; Instruction takes 3 cycles total
2026-05-07 15:08:26 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out pins, 8 [2]  ; Instruction takes 3 cycles total
2026-05-07 15:08:26 [gemini-3.1-lite]     ...
2026-05-07 15:08:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:08:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:08:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] A note on the PIO `out` instruction:
2026-05-07 15:08:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] When you execute `out pins, 9`, the PIO automatically takes the next 9 bits from the OSR and puts
2026-05-07 15:08:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] them on the pins for the duration of that instruction. If you want the pins to hold that
2026-05-07 15:08:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] value for longer than one cycle, you must use the delay slots `[n]`.
2026-05-07 15:08:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:08:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Are you trying to match the 10656 cycles exactly?*
2026-05-07 15:08:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] If so, you have a very specific "cycle budget" per line. We can calculate exactly how many delay
2026-05-07 15:08:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] cycles you need to add to each `out` instruction to hit that target precisely. Would you like to
2026-05-07 15:08:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] run the math on that to ensure you hit the PAL timing requirements?
