2026-05-07 15:10:16 [nitro@ryzen5] what is pal color clock?
2026-05-07 15:10:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] In the PAL (Phase Alternating Line) system, the color subcarrier frequency (f_sc) is
2026-05-07 15:10:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] precisely defined as:
2026-05-07 15:10:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:10:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] • 4.43361875 MHz*
2026-05-07 15:10:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:10:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] Why this specific frequency?
2026-05-07 15:10:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] The PAL standard was designed to be "interlaced" with the black-and-white (luminance) signal in a
2026-05-07 15:10:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] way that minimizes visible interference. The math behind the subcarrier is derived from the PAL
2026-05-07 15:10:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] line frequency (f_h = 15,625\text Hz):
2026-05-07 15:10:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:10:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] fsc = \left( 1135.25 + 1⁄4 \right) × fh
2026-05-07 15:10:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:10:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] This "offset" (+ 25 Hz) is what causes the dot-crawl pattern to move/invert every frame,
2026-05-07 15:10:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] effectively cancelling out the visual interference of the color signal in the luminance channel.
2026-05-07 15:10:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:10:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] How this relates to your Pico 2 (166.5 MHz)
2026-05-07 15:10:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] Since you are running your Pico 2 at 166.5 MHz, we can calculate how many PIO cycles
2026-05-07 15:10:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] represent one cycle of the color subcarrier:
2026-05-07 15:10:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:10:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] \textCycles per color clock = \frac166,500,000\text Hz4,433,618.75\text Hz \approx
2026-05-07 15:10:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] \mathbf37.55\text cycles
2026-05-07 15:10:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:10:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] What this means for your PIO code
2026-05-07 15:10:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] Because 37.55 is not an integer, you cannot generate a perfect PAL subcarrier using a simple
2026-05-07 15:10:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] constant delay loop. If you use a fixed delay, you will experience "phase drift," which will cause
2026-05-07 15:10:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] the colors on the screen to shift or "roll" horizontally.
2026-05-07 15:10:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:10:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] To handle this in PIO, you have two main options:
2026-05-07 15:10:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:10:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Phase Accumulation (DDS): Instead of a fixed loop, you store a "phase" value in the PIO
2026-05-07 15:10:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] and use a small lookup table to output the sine wave values.
2026-05-07 15:10:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Cycle Jittering: You alternate your delay values (e.g., alternating between 37 and 38
2026-05-07 15:10:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] cycles) to maintain an average frequency of 37.55 over time.
2026-05-07 15:10:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:10:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Are you trying to generate the color burst and chroma signal by bit-banging a specific
2026-05-07 15:10:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] waveform, or are you using the PIO to shift out a pre-calculated sine wave pattern?*
2026-05-07 15:10:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:10:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you are bit-banging, you will likely need to account for that `.55` remainder to keep the phase
2026-05-07 15:10:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] locked to the horizontal sync pulse.
2026-05-07 15:10:21 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
