2026-05-07 15:11:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] The PAL Subcarrier Check
2026-05-07 15:11:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] You calculated ~230 color clocks per line.
2026-05-07 15:11:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] • 230 \text cycles × 37.55 \text (PIO cycles per clock) \approx 8636 \text PIO cycles.
2026-05-07 15:11:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Your line budget is 10656.
2026-05-07 15:11:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Your current loop is 75 cycles per 8 bits (1 byte).
2026-05-07 15:11:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] • If you process 32 bits (1 `x` loop) per iteration: 75 cycles.
2026-05-07 15:11:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] • To fill 10656 cycles, you would need roughly 142 iterations of that loop.
2026-05-07 15:11:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:11:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] A Few Observations
2026-05-07 15:11:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  The `jmp x--` cycle: Remember that when `x` reaches 0, the `jmp` instruction takes
2026-05-07 15:11:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1 cycle and does not jump. This means your loop will exit "cleanly," but you need
2026-05-07 15:11:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] to ensure the `set pins, 0` instruction is timed to land exactly on the start of the next sync
2026-05-07 15:11:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] pulse or the end of the line.
2026-05-07 15:11:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Bit-banging the Subcarrier: By outputting `1` bit at a time, you are essentially
2026-05-07 15:11:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] generating a square wave. A square wave contains the fundamental frequency (4.43\text MHz) but also
2026-05-07 15:11:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] significant odd harmonics (13.3\text MHz, 22.1\text MHz, etc.). Since you are driving a DAC, these
2026-05-07 15:11:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] harmonics might create "ringing" or aliasing on the video signal unless you have a low-pass filter
2026-05-07 15:11:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] on your output pins.
2026-05-07 15:11:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  The "Jitter" Factor: Since 37.55 is not an integer, even with your 75-cycle loop, you
2026-05-07 15:11:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] are effectively creating a frequency of 166.5\text MHz / 75 \approx 2.22\text MHz (if outputting 1
2026-05-07 15:11:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] bit per 9.3 cycles). You are currently outputting a waveform that is significantly slower than the
2026-05-07 15:11:09 [gemini-3.1-lite] PAL 4.43\text MHz subcarrier.
2026-05-07 15:11:09 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:11:09 [gemini-3.1-lite] • To get closer to 4.43\text MHz:*
2026-05-07 15:11:09 [gemini-3.1-lite] You need your loop to be much tighter. To hit 4.43\text MHz at 166.5\text MHz, you need a cycle
2026-05-07 15:11:09 [gemini-3.1-lite] period of ~37.5. Your current loop is 75 cycles to output 8 bits, which is ~9.3 cycles per bit.
2026-05-07 15:11:09 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:11:09 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you want to output 8 bits of chroma information in one 4.43\text MHz cycle, you actually need
2026-05-07 15:11:09 [gemini-3.1-lite] the whole `out` sequence to complete in ~37 cycles total.
2026-05-07 15:11:09 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
