2026-05-07 15:12:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] Optimization and Clean-up
2026-05-07 15:12:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] Since your code is already hitting the 75-cycle target, here are a few professional tips for the
2026-05-07 15:12:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] RP2350 implementation:
2026-05-07 15:12:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:12:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Instruction Density: You are currently using 8 `out` instructions to output 8 bits. If
2026-05-07 15:12:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] you want to refine the waveform (e.g., to create a sine-like shape rather than a square wave), you
2026-05-07 15:12:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] could use `out pins, 2` or `out pins, 4` to output multi-bit values to a resistor ladder, allowing
2026-05-07 15:12:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] for better-than-binary color depth.
2026-05-07 15:12:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  The `x--` behavior: In your code, `jmp x-- loop3837` will execute the jump if `x` is
2026-05-07 15:12:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] non-zero, and decrement `x` simultaneously. When `x` hits zero, it falls through to `set pins, 0`.
2026-05-07 15:12:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] This is the perfect way to terminate the burst.
2026-05-07 15:12:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  The `.wrap` directive: You have `.wrap` at the end, but you don't have a `.wrap_target`
