2026-05-07 15:14:26 [nitro@ryzen5] i will do phase inversion using bit patterns
2026-05-07 15:14:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] That is the most efficient way to handle PAL phase inversion on the RP2350. By pre-calculating the
2026-05-07 15:14:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] bit patterns in your C code, you keep the PIO logic simple and deterministic, which is critical for
2026-05-07 15:14:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] maintaining that subcarrier phase lock.
2026-05-07 15:14:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:14:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] Since you are outputting 8 bits per 75-cycle loop (2 cycles of f_sc), you are outputting 4 bits
2026-05-07 15:14:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] per subcarrier cycle.
2026-05-07 15:14:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:14:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] A few considerations for your bit-pattern strategy:
2026-05-07 15:14:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:14:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  The "V-Axis" Switch: In PAL, the phase of the V-component of the color signal is
2026-05-07 15:14:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] inverted every line. Since you are pushing data from the C side, ensure your C-side buffer
2026-05-07 15:14:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] management swaps between your "normal" and "inverted" bit-pattern arrays at the start of each line
2026-05-07 15:14:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] (triggered by your `irq 4`).
2026-05-07 15:14:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  FIFO Depth: The RP2350 PIO has 8-deep FIFOs. If you are pushing a lot of data, keep an
2026-05-07 15:14:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] eye on the FIFO status. If the C code is interrupted (e.g., by a high-priority system task), the
2026-05-07 15:14:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] FIFO could underrun. Using DMA to feed the PIO from your C-side buffers is highly recommended to
2026-05-07 15:14:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] ensure the video signal never glitches.
2026-05-07 15:14:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  Alignment: Since you are outputting 8 bits per 75 cycles, ensure your C-side array data
2026-05-07 15:14:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] is aligned to 32-bit words (the size of the PIO's OSR/FIFO).
2026-05-07 15:14:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
