2026-05-07 15:27:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] A note on your hardware setup:
2026-05-07 15:27:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] Your resistor values (120Ω for Luma, 330Ω for Chroma) form a simple passive mixer. Since
2026-05-07 15:27:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] you are using a 1-bit PIO output for the Chroma, here is a quick tip to improve the signal:
2026-05-07 15:27:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:27:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] Because you are outputting a square wave, the 330Ω resistor will be fed a 0V-to-3.3V signal. This
2026-05-07 15:27:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] will create a "high-energy" subcarrier. If the color looks too saturated or causes "jailbar"
2026-05-07 15:27:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] interference on the Luma, you may find that increasing the 330Ω resistor to 470Ω or 560Ω helps
2026-05-07 15:27:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] "soften" the chroma level to better match the PAL standard's lower amplitude requirements.
2026-05-07 15:27:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:27:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] • One final check on your `out` loop:*
2026-05-07 15:27:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] Since you are outputting 8 bits per 75 cycles, you have:
2026-05-07 15:27:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] 75 / 8 = 9.375 cycles per bit.
2026-05-07 15:27:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:27:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you are outputting 4 bits per color cycle, each bit represents exactly 90 degrees of
2026-05-07 15:27:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] phase (360^\circ / 4). This is perfect for generating a quadrature-modulated signal. By
2026-05-07 15:27:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] choosing your 8-bit patterns (e.g., `00110011` for a simple wave or `00011111` for a more filtered
2026-05-07 15:27:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] approach), you effectively define the phase and amplitude of your chroma subcarrier.
2026-05-07 15:27:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:27:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] Are you planning to implement the color burst by pushing a specific 32-bit pattern into the FIFO
2026-05-07 15:27:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] during the blanking interval?
2026-05-07 15:27:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:28:03 [nitro@ryzen5] what value to generate all red output on even lines and oddlines?
2026-05-07 15:28:15 [gemini-3.1-lite] To generate "Red," you need to modulate the color subcarrier with the specific phase and amplitude
2026-05-07 15:28:15 [gemini-3.1-lite] required by the PAL standard.
2026-05-07 15:28:15 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:28:15 [gemini-3.1-lite] The PAL Red Signal
2026-05-07 15:28:15 [gemini-3.1-lite] In the PAL system, the color subcarrier is a vector. For Red (100% saturation), the
2026-05-07 15:28:15 [gemini-3.1-lite] subcarrier phase is approximately 103.5° relative to the burst.
2026-05-07 15:28:15 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:28:15 [gemini-3.1-lite] Since you are outputting 4 bits per subcarrier cycle (quadrature), you are essentially mapping your
2026-05-07 15:28:15 [gemini-3.1-lite] 8-bit FIFO word to a phase-shifted sine wave.
2026-05-07 15:28:15 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. The Bit Patterns
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] To represent a sine wave with 4 samples per cycle, you want a sequence that approximates the wave.
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] A standard 4-sample sine approximation is `[1, 1, 0, 0]` (offset/DC biased).
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Even Line (Normal V): You want the phase to be 103.5^\circ.
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Odd Line (Inverted V): You want the phase to be -103.5^\circ (or 256.5^\circ).
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] Because you are using bit patterns, you simply need to calculate the 8-bit word for each line type.
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] • For a simple 4-bit-per-cycle approximation:*
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Pattern A (Even): `0b11001100` (This represents one phase)
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Pattern B (Odd): `0b00110011` (This represents the inverted phase)
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Note: You may need to rotate these bits (e.g., `0b10011001`) depending on where your "start of
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] cycle" aligns with your `irq 4` trigger.*
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. Why these values?
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] By shifting the bit sequence, you are effectively shifting the starting phase of the
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] subcarrier relative to the horizontal sync (the `irq 4` trigger).
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Even lines: Send the word `0xCC` (binary `11001100`) repeatedly.
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Odd lines: Send the word `0x33` (binary `00110011`) repeatedly.
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] Implementation Strategy
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] Since your PIO loop consumes 32 bits (4 words of 8 bits) per `out x, 32` pull, you should structure
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] your C-side buffer like this:
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] // Even line buffer (4 pulls of 32 bits)
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] uint32_t even_red_line[4] = {0xCCCCCCCC, 0xCCCCCCCC, 0xCCCCCCCC, 0xCCCCCCCC};
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] // Odd line buffer (4 pulls of 32 bits)
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] uint32_t odd_red_line[4] = {0x33333333, 0x33333333, 0x33333333, 0x33333333};
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] Critical Tuning
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] Because 103.5° is not perfectly aligned with 90° (your 4-bit steps), you might see a slight color
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] tint error if you use exactly `0xCC`.
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] • To refine the color:*
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] If the red looks "orange" or "magenta," you need to shift the phase slightly. You can do this by
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] delaying the start of the pattern by a few cycles.
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Try `0xCC` but add a `nop` or a `[n]` delay in your PIO code after the `irq 4` to "nudge" the
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] phase.
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Alternatively, change your bit pattern to `0x66` or `0x99` to shift the phase by exactly 45
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] degrees.
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Are you using a DMA channel to push these buffers to the PIO?*
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] Using DMA is the standard way to ensure the even/odd line patterns switch perfectly in sync with
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] the `irq 4` signal without CPU intervention.
2026-05-07 15:28:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:28:59 [nitro@ryzen5] can we align our bit train better with color clock?
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] To align the bit train better, we need to address the fact that your current loop is 75 cycles
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] long, but your color subcarrier is 37.55 cycles per period.
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] The Problem: The "Fractional" Phase
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] Your current loop executes exactly 2 cycles of the subcarrier (37.55 × 2 = 75.1). Because 75.1 \neq
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] 75, your phase will drift by 0.1 cycles every time the loop runs. Over a full line (approx 142
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] loops), your phase will drift by 14.2 cycles, causing the color to smear or roll
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] horizontally.
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] The Solution: The "37/38" Jitter Pattern
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] To maintain a perfect average of 37.55, you must alternate your loop duration between 75
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] cycles and 76 cycles.
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] We can modify the PIO to use a slightly different delay sequence every other loop to "reset" the
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] phase error.
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] Revised PIO Program
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] We will adjust the `loop3837` to be 75 cycles, and provide an alternative `loop3838` that is 76
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] cycles.
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] .program chroma
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] .wrap_target
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] start:
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite]     wait 1 irq 4
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite]     irq clear 4
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out x, 32
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite]     jmp !x start [16]
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] ; 75-cycle loop (2.000 cycles of 37.5)
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] loop75:
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out pins, 1 [9] ; 10
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out pins, 1 [8] ; 9
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out pins, 1 [9] ; 10
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out pins, 1 [8] ; 9
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out pins, 1 [9] ; 10
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out pins, 1 [8] ; 9
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out pins, 1 [8] ; 9
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out pins, 1 [7] ; 8
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite]     jmp x-- loop76 ; 1 cycle (total 75)
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] ; 76-cycle loop (adjusting for the .55 phase drift)
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] loop76:
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out pins, 1 [9] ; 10
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out pins, 1 [8] ; 9
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out pins, 1 [9] ; 10
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out pins, 1 [8] ; 9
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out pins, 1 [9] ; 10
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out pins, 1 [8] ; 9
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out pins, 1 [8] ; 9
2026-05-07 15:29:04 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out pins, 1 [8] ; 9  <-- Added 1 cycle here
2026-05-07 15:29:05 [gemini-3.1-lite]     jmp x-- loop75 ; 1 cycle (total 76)
2026-05-07 15:29:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:29:05 [gemini-3.1-lite]     set pins, 0
2026-05-07 15:29:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] .wrap
2026-05-07 15:29:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:29:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:29:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] Why this works:
2026-05-07 15:29:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Phase Locking: By alternating between 75 and 76 cycles, your average loop duration
2026-05-07 15:29:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] becomes 75.5 cycles. This is much closer to your target of 75.1.
2026-05-07 15:29:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  The "Jitter" is Invisible: Because this error is at the sub-pixel level, the color
2026-05-07 15:29:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] burst will remain locked to the sync pulse.
2026-05-07 15:29:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  C-Side Control: Your C-code no longer needs to worry about manual alignment; the PIO is
2026-05-07 15:29:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] now "self-correcting" its phase drift.
2026-05-07 15:29:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:29:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] Recommended Refinement
2026-05-07 15:29:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you want even higher precision, you can use the PIO Clock Divider.
2026-05-07 15:29:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you set the PIO clock divider to `4.43361875 MHz * 4 / 166.5 MHz` (which is roughly `1.065`),
2026-05-07 15:29:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] you can make the PIO cycle count match the subcarrier period perfectly as an integer.
2026-05-07 15:29:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:29:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Would you like me to show you how to calculate the exact `smconfigset_clkdiv` value
2026-05-07 15:29:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] to make the subcarrier exactly 37 cycles per period?* This would eliminate the need for the
2026-05-07 15:29:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] `loop75/loop76` alternating logic.
2026-05-07 15:29:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:29:32 [nitro@ryzen5] nop too many instructions
2026-05-07 15:29:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] To keep the instruction count low, we can optimize by using the PIO Clock Divider to handle
2026-05-07 15:29:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] the fractional timing, allowing you to use a single, simple loop.
2026-05-07 15:29:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:29:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] To get the subcarrier period to be an integer, we want:
2026-05-07 15:29:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] 166.5\text MHz / \textDivisor = 4.43361875\text MHz × 4 \text (samples per cycle)
2026-05-07 15:29:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:29:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] Required Divider: 166.5 / 17.734475 = \mathbf9.3885
2026-05-07 15:29:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:29:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you set the divider to 9.3885, then each PIO cycle is exactly 1/4 of a color clock
2026-05-07 15:29:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] cycle. This allows you to use a fixed-length loop of exactly 32 cycles (8 bits × 4 cycles
2026-05-07 15:29:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] per bit).
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] Optimized PIO (Small Footprint)
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] .program chroma
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] .wrap_target
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] start:
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]     wait 1 irq 4
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]     irq clear 4
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out x, 32
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]     jmp !x start [16]
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] loop:
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out pins, 1 [2] ; 1+2 = 3 cycles
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out pins, 1 [2] ; 1+2 = 3 cycles
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out pins, 1 [2] ; 1+2 = 3 cycles
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out pins, 1 [2] ; 1+2 = 3 cycles
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out pins, 1 [2] ; 1+2 = 3 cycles
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out pins, 1 [2] ; 1+2 = 3 cycles
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out pins, 1 [2] ; 1+2 = 3 cycles
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out pins, 1 [2] ; 1+2 = 3 cycles
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]     jmp x-- loop
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]     set pins, 0
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] .wrap
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] How to set this in C:
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] Use the `smconfigset_clkdiv` function to set the divider to `9.3885f`.
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] // In your init function:
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] float div = 166.5f / 17.734475f; // 9.3885
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] sm_config_set_clkdiv(&c, div);
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] Why this is better:
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Instruction Count: Reduced from ~20+ instructions down to just 10.
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Phase Stability: By using the fractional clock divider, the PIO hardware handles the
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] timing drift for you. You no longer need to alternate between two different loops.
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  Simplicity: Each `out` instruction now represents exactly one "sample" of your color
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] subcarrier. This makes generating your Red/Even/Odd patterns much easier—you just map your bit
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] patterns directly to the 8 samples.
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Does this fit your instruction memory budget better?* (The PIO instruction memory is 32
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] slots; this program uses 12 slots, leaving you plenty of room for sync pulse generation if you need
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] it later.)
2026-05-07 15:29:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:30:55 [nitro@ryzen5] ; chroma.pio (c)2026 Simon Armstrong; PAL 576i with 166.5mhz Pico2 PIO = 10656 cycles per line; color clock 4.43361875 MHz ~230 per line signalled from vidbit on irq 4; 8 bits from FIFO = 2 color cycles (4 bits per cycle @ 37.5 cycle period).program chromastart:	wait	1 irq 4	irq	clear 4	out	x, 32	jmp	!x start [16]loop:	out	pins, 1 [1]	jmp	x-- loop	set	pins, 0.wrap
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] This is a very elegant, minimalist approach. By reducing the loop to a single `out` instruction,
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] you have freed up almost all your instruction memory.
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] However, there is one critical detail to watch: The `jmp` instruction overhead.
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] The Timing Analysis
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] • `out pins, 1 [1]` takes 2 cycles total.
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] • `jmp x-- loop` takes 1 cycle (if jumping).
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Total per loop: 3 cycles.
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you are aiming for a subcarrier cycle of ~37.5 cycles (at 166.5 MHz), your current loop produces
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] a frequency of:
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 166.5 \text MHz / 3 \text cycles = \mathbf55.5 \text MHz
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] That is much too fast for a 4.43 MHz subcarrier. To reach 4.43 MHz, you need each "bit" of your
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] subcarrier to last significantly longer.
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] The "Corrected" Minimalist Loop
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] To hit the subcarrier frequency while keeping your code size at an absolute minimum, you need to
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] increase the delay to reach a total of ~37-38 cycles per 4 bits (since 8 bits = 2 cycles).
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you want to output 1 bit per subcarrier phase (4 bits per cycle), you need each loop iteration
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] to be 9-10 cycles long.
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] program chroma
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] .wrap_target
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] start:
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite]     wait 1 irq 4
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite]     irq clear 4
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out x, 32
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite]     jmp !x start [16]
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] loop:
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out pins, 1 [7]     ; 1 + 7 = 8 cycles
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite]     jmp x-- loop [0]    ; 1 cycle (total 9 per bit)
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite]     set pins, 0
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] .wrap
