2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Why this works:*
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Instruction Count: You are using only 7 instructions total.
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Frequency: 9 cycles per bit × 4 bits = 36 cycles per subcarrier cycle. This is
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] extremely close to your target of 37.5.
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  Phase: You can fine-tune the frequency by changing the `[7]` to `[8]` for a few loops
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] if you need to adjust the phase drift.
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:31:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] A suggestion for the "2 cycles per 8 bits" requirement
2026-05-07 15:31:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you want to keep your "8 bits = 2 cycles" logic, you need 8 bits to span ~75 cycles.
2026-05-07 15:31:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Loop: `out pins, 1 [7]` + `jmp x-- loop [0]` = 9 cycles.
2026-05-07 15:31:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] • 8 iterations = 72 cycles.
2026-05-07 15:31:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:31:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] This is incredibly close to your 75-cycle requirement! If you change the delay to `[8]`, you get 10
2026-05-07 15:31:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] cycles per loop, and 8 iterations = 80 cycles.
2026-05-07 15:31:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:31:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Recommendation:*
2026-05-07 15:31:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] Use the `[7]` delay for a 72-cycle period, and use the `clkdiv` in your C code to
2026-05-07 15:31:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] stretch the clock slightly to make that 72-cycle loop hit the exact 75.1-cycle requirement.
2026-05-07 15:31:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
