2026-05-07 15:37:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] Using two pins for Chroma is a smart move. It allows you to create a "3-level" DAC (High,
2026-05-07 15:37:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] Low, and High-Impedance/Mid-point), which is much closer to a true sine wave than a simple square
2026-05-07 15:37:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] wave.
2026-05-07 15:37:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:37:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] The "Two-Pin" Advantage
2026-05-07 15:37:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you connect Pin A and Pin B to your filter network, you can generate four states:
2026-05-07 15:37:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] • 00: 0V (GND)
2026-05-07 15:37:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] • 01: Intermediate voltage (via 330Ω)
2026-05-07 15:37:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] • 10: Intermediate voltage (via 100Ω)
2026-05-07 15:37:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] • 11: 3.3V (High)
2026-05-07 15:37:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:37:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] This creates a staircase approximation of a sine wave. When this passes through your
2026-05-07 15:37:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] 68pF/100Ω filter, the result is a significantly cleaner, lower-distortion sine wave than a single
2026-05-07 15:37:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] pin could ever produce.
2026-05-07 15:37:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
