2026-05-07 15:58:18 [gemini-3.1-lite] Your C++ structure is clean and correctly leverages the RP2040/RP2350 PIO/DMA architecture. Since
2026-05-07 15:58:18 [gemini-3.1-lite] you are moving to a "Red" output, the transition from your current `vidbit` implementation to a
2026-05-07 15:58:18 [gemini-3.1-lite] color-capable one requires careful attention to the data buffer layout.
2026-05-07 15:58:18 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:58:18 [gemini-3.1-lite] Here are the critical observations for your implementation:
2026-05-07 15:58:18 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:58:18 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. Data Structure Alignment
2026-05-07 15:58:18 [gemini-3.1-lite] In your `viddmainit`, you define `channelconfigsettransferdatasize(&c,
2026-05-07 15:58:18 [gemini-3.1-lite] DMASIZE_32)`. This is correct for the PIO FIFO.
2026-05-07 15:58:18 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:58:18 [gemini-3.1-lite] However, your `evenFrame` and `oddFrame` buffers must be structured to match the `vidbit.pio`
2026-05-07 15:58:18 [gemini-3.1-lite] consumption pattern:
2026-05-07 15:58:18 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Control Words: Your PIO expects `out x, 32` and `out y, 31` (or similar) at the start of
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] each line/field.
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Active Video: If your `active` loop uses `out pins, 1`, each 32-bit word from the DMA
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] provides 32 pixels.
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Red Bit Masking: If you are using a single pin for Red, ensure your `evenFrame` bitmap
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] data is pre-processed so that the bit at the correct position corresponds to the Red intensity.
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. DMA Chaining and Synchronization
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] You are currently using `piosmistxfifo_full` and checking `fdebug` stalls. For a
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] stable video signal:
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Avoid `fdebug` polling in the main loop: Checking `fdebug` is great for debugging, but in
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] a production frame-generator, it adds jitter.
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] • DMA IRQs: Instead of checking `dmachannelis_busy` in a tight loop, consider using
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] a DMA interrupt that triggers when the frame transfer completes. This allows the CPU to sleep or
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] handle PS/2 input while the DMA handles the heavy lifting.
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3. Red Output Strategy
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] Since you have hardware pins `CHROMA330PIN` (21) and `CHROMA100PIN` (20), and `VIDPIN`
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] (22) for luma/sync, you need to decide if "Red" is a monochrome intensity on the `VIDPIN` or if
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] you are modulating the chroma pins to create a reddish hue.
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] • If Monochromatic Red: You don't need to change `chroma.pio`. Just keep the `VID_PIN` data
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] stream saturated with your "Red" intensity values.
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] • If Color Red: You need to change the `chroma.pio` output to drive the 330/100 pins with
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] the appropriate phase for the Red color subcarrier. PAL Red is defined by a specific phase shift
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] (approx 104° from the burst).
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] Suggested Refinement for `vid_field`
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] To ensure the DMA pointers don't drift, ensure your `evenFrame` and `oddFrame` arrays are marked
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] `volatile` or are in a memory region that isn't being modified by other processes:
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] void vid_field(int field) {
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // Ensure we are not writing to the buffer while DMA is reading
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite]     dma_channel_wait_for_finish_blocking(video_dma_chan);
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite]     if (field & 1) {
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite]         dma_channel_set_read_addr(video_dma_chan, oddFrame, true);
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite]     } else {
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite]         dma_channel_set_read_addr(video_dma_chan, evenFrame, true);
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite]     }
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] Potential Pitfall: The `active` loop
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] In `vidbit.pio`, your `active` loop is:
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] active:
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	out	pins, 1 [8]
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	jmp	y--,active
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you change this to `out pins, 1`, ensure that your C++ `evenFrame` data is packed as 1 bit
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] per pixel. If you want a 2-bit or 4-bit DAC output, you would change `out pins, 1` to `out
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] pins, 2` or `4` and adjust the `pindirs` accordingly.
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Are you planning to implement a software-based framebuffer that converts 8-bit/16-bit color
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] into this 1-bit-per-pixel format before sending it to the DMA?* That is usually the next
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] "heavy" task in these generators.
2026-05-07 15:58:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] This updated C++ structure is now well-positioned for color integration. By adding
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] `chromadmainit` and integrating the `evenRed`/`oddRed` buffers into the `vid_field`
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] function, you have effectively decoupled the Luma (Sync/Video) and Chroma (Color Burst/Subcarrier)
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] paths.
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] To ensure the "Red" output manifests correctly on your hardware, consider the following technical
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] checkpoints:
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. Chroma-Luma Phase Alignment
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] Since `vidbit` and `chroma` are running on separate PIO instances (`pio0` and `pio1`), their start
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] times might drift relative to each other.
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Recommendation: Use the PIO synchronization register
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] (`pioctrlhw->fdebug`) to start both state machines simultaneously.
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // Start both PIO state machines at exactly the same cycle
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite]     pio_enable_sm_mask_in_sync(pio0, (1 << VideoStateMachine) | (1 << TeletextStateMachine));
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite]     pio_enable_sm_mask_in_sync(pio1, (1 << ChromaStateMachine));
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. DMA Pacing
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] You are currently using `dmachannelis_busy` to trigger the next field. This is functional,
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] but relies on the CPU polling loop speed.
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Optimization: Since you are using `irq 4` in your PIO code, you can route that IRQ to a
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] DMA trigger. This makes the field switching "hardware-deterministic." The DMA will fire exactly
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] when the PIO reaches the end of the frame buffer, eliminating the risk of a "stutter" if the CPU is
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] momentarily delayed by a USB interrupt or other background tasks.
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3. Red Buffer Generation
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] Since you are sending `colorWords` to the `chroma` state machine, your `evenRed` and `oddRed`
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] buffers need to contain the 2-bit values that, when output via `out pins, 2`, produce the PAL
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] subcarrier modulation for "Red."
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Verification: Ensure your `monobit.h` or frame-generation logic produces a constant phase
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] offset for the color subcarrier during the "active" video lines to define the color red. If the
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] phase is not modulated, you will get a monochrome signal or an incorrect hue.
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] 4. Hardware Filter Consideration
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] You mentioned the filter:
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] CHROMA330 ── 330 Ω ──────┬─── VIDEO OUT
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] CHROMA100 ── 100 Ω ──────┘
2026-05-07 16:05:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:05:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] This is a standard 2-bit weighted DAC. With the `chroma` program outputting to 2 pins, you have 4
2026-05-07 16:05:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] levels of chroma amplitude.
2026-05-07 16:05:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Red Signal: In PAL, Red is represented by the phase of the subcarrier. If you are aiming
2026-05-07 16:05:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] for saturated red, verify that your `evenRed`/`oddRed` values toggle between the 4 states
2026-05-07 16:05:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] in a way that creates the correct quadrature amplitude modulation (QAM).
2026-05-07 16:05:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:05:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] Next Steps
2026-05-07 16:05:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you run this code and see the video frame but the color is missing or incorrect, check the
2026-05-07 16:05:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] following:
2026-05-07 16:05:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Chroma Burst: Ensure the `chroma` PIO is outputting a "burst" pattern (a specific
2026-05-07 16:05:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] sequence of values) during the `backporch` period. Without a valid color burst, the TV will not
2026-05-07 16:05:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] unlock the color decoder and will default to black-and-white.
2026-05-07 16:05:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Logic Levels: Verify with a scope that the Chroma signal amplitude is approximately
2026-05-07 16:05:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] 300mV peak-to-peak. If it's too high, the TV's color decoder may be overwhelmed and ignore the
2026-05-07 16:05:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] signal.
2026-05-07 16:05:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:05:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Do you have the logic inside `initFrame()` ready to populate `evenRed` and `oddRed` with the
2026-05-07 16:05:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] correct subcarrier values, or would you like a helper function to generate the QAM sine-wave
2026-05-07 16:05:20 [gemini-3.1-lite] pattern?*
