2026-05-07 16:10:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] This code is structurally sound for an RP2350-based video generator. You have successfully moved
2026-05-07 16:10:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] from a single-channel implementation to a multi-DMA, multi-PIO architecture.
2026-05-07 16:10:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:10:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] To move from "getting it running" to "getting it rendering Red," there are three critical areas to
2026-05-07 16:10:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] address in the `initChroma()` logic:
2026-05-07 16:10:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:10:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. The Subcarrier Pattern
2026-05-07 16:10:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] Your `initChroma()` currently fills the buffers with `0x55555555` and `0xCCCCCCCC`.
2026-05-07 16:10:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] • `0x55` is `01010101` in binary.
2026-05-07 16:10:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] • `0xCC` is `11001100` in binary.
2026-05-07 16:10:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] These patterns will create a steady-state signal on the pins, but they will not create the
2026-05-07 16:10:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] 4.43361875 MHz sine wave required for PAL color.
2026-05-07 16:10:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:10:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Recommendation:* You need to generate a Look-Up Table (LUT) for the 4-phase color burst
2026-05-07 16:10:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] and the Red color vector. Since you have 4 cycles per subcarrier period (17.734475 MHz / 4.43361875
2026-05-07 16:10:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] MHz = 4), your buffer should contain a repeating 4-word sequence that represents the phase-shifted
2026-05-07 16:10:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] sine wave.
2026-05-07 16:10:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:10:10 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] void initChroma() {
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // 4-phase sequence for 4.43MHz (approx)
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // Adjust bits to match your 2-pin DAC levels (00, 01, 10, 11)
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite]     uint32_t phase_seq[4] = {0x0, 0x1, 0x3, 0x2}; // Simple triangle approximation
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite]     for(int i = 0; i < colorWords; i++) {
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite]         // Build words from the phase sequence
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite]         uint32_t word = 0;
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite]         for(int b = 0; b < 16; b++) {
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite]             word |= (phase_seq[(i + b) % 4] << (b * 2));
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite]         }
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite]         oddRed[i] = word;
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite]         evenRed[i] = word;
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite]     }
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. DMA Triggering
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] You are currently triggering `chromadmachan` and `videodmachan` independently in
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] `vid_field`. Because they are on different PIOs and were initialized at different times, they are
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] likely phase-shifted relative to each other.
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] To fix this:
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Use the `pioenablesmmaskin_sync` function mentioned earlier to start them on the
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] exact same clock edge.
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The `chroma` SM relies on `wait 1 irq 4`. Ensure that your `vidbit` PIO is actually asserting
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] `irq 4` at the start of every active line. If `irq 4` is not firing, the `chroma` SM will hang
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] waiting for the trigger.
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3. Red Vector Calculation
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] PAL Red is a specific vector. If you want "Red," your chroma phase must be shifted by approximately
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] 104 degrees relative to the burst.
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Burst: Output a fixed 8-cycle sequence at the start of the line (during `backporch`) to
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] let the TV calibrate.
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Active Video: Output the shifted phase signal.
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] Debugging Tip
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] Since you have a `LED_PIN` and can toggle it when a stall occurs (`pio0->fdebug & chromaStall`),
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] this is a great way to verify the FIFO. If the LED starts blinking rapidly, it means the `chroma`
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] SM is not consuming data fast enough or is waiting on an IRQ that never comes.
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] • One final question:* Are you using a specific library (like `monobit.h`) to handle the
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] font/graphics rendering into the `evenFrame` buffer? If so, ensure that the `evenRed` buffer is
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] updated in sync with the `evenFrame` buffer, otherwise you will get "color bleeding" where the red
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] appears shifted by several pixels relative to the luma edges.
2026-05-07 16:10:11 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:12:19 [nitro@ryzen5] what should the first few cycles of my color clock look like?
2026-05-07 16:12:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] To achieve a valid PAL color signal, the first few cycles of your `chroma` state machine must
2026-05-07 16:12:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] produce the Color Burst. This is the reference signal the TV uses to synchronize its
2026-05-07 16:12:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] internal subcarrier oscillator.
2026-05-07 16:12:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:12:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. The Burst Structure
2026-05-07 16:12:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] The Color Burst occurs on the "Back Porch" of every line (except during the vertical blanking
2026-05-07 16:12:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] interval). It consists of roughly 10 cycles of the subcarrier frequency (4.43361875 MHz).
2026-05-07 16:12:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:12:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] Since your `chroma.pio` is clocked at 4x f_sc (17.73 MHz), each subcarrier cycle is exactly 4 PIO
2026-05-07 16:12:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] instructions long.
2026-05-07 16:12:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:12:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. The Required Waveform (The "Sine" Table)
2026-05-07 16:12:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] For a 2-pin DAC (weighted 100R/330R), you want to approximate a sine wave. A simple 4-step
2026-05-07 16:12:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] approximation is:
2026-05-07 16:12:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Step 0: 00 (0V)
2026-05-07 16:12:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Step 1: 01 (Low)
2026-05-07 16:12:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Step 2: 11 (High)
2026-05-07 16:12:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Step 3: 10 (Mid)
2026-05-07 16:12:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:12:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The Pattern:* You should feed the PIO a sequence of `0x0, 0x1, 0x3, 0x2` (repeated) to
2026-05-07 16:12:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] generate one full sine wave cycle.
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3. Implementing the Burst in your Buffer
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] Your `evenRed` buffer needs to be structured so that the first part of the buffer corresponds to
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] the Back Porch and the remainder corresponds to the Active Video.
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] // 10 cycles of burst = 40 PIO instructions (10 * 4)
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] // Each PIO instruction consumes 2 bits (if you use 'out pins, 2')
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] // A 32-bit word holds 16 steps (4 cycles).
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] // So, 10 cycles = 2.5 words.
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] void initChroma() {
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite]     uint32_t burst_pattern = 0x23102310; // 0x2=10, 0x3=11, 0x1=01, 0x0=00
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite]     for(int i = 0; i < colorWords; i++) {
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite]         if (i < 3) {
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite]             // First 3 words = 12 cycles of burst
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite]             evenRed[i] = burst_pattern;
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite]         } else {
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite]             // After burst, output the Red phase
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite]             // Red phase (PAL) is roughly 104 degrees shifted from burst
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite]             // This requires a phase-shifted version of the burst_pattern
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite]             evenRed[i] = 0x10231023; // Example shift
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite]         }
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite]         oddRed[i] = evenRed[i];
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite]     }
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 4. Important PAL Timing Rules
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Phase Alternation (The "A" in PAL): This is the most critical part. In PAL, the phase of
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] the Red component (the V-axis) flips 180 degrees every line.
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   Line N: V-axis is +V.
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   Line N+1: V-axis is -V.
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] • How to implement: Your `evenRed` and `oddRed` buffers must be different.
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   `evenRed` should contain the "Normal" phase sequence.
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   `oddRed` should contain the "Inverted" phase sequence (swap the `0x1` and `0x2` values in
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] your pattern).
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] Summary Checklist for your `initChroma`:
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Burst: Start with 10-12 cycles of the `0, 1, 3, 2` pattern.
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Blanking: Follow the burst with `0x0` (Black level) for the rest of the porch.
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  Active Video: Output the Red phase (a shifted version of the burst pattern).
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 4.  Field Alternation: Ensure `oddRed` has the V-component inverted compared to `evenRed`
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] to keep the TV's PAL decoder happy.
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you don't invert the phase every line, the TV will interpret the signal as NTSC, and you will
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] see "Hanover Bars" (crawling color errors) or no color at all.
2026-05-07 16:12:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:13:22 [nitro@ryzen5] oddRed has to be out of phase!
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] Exactly. The "P" in PAL stands for Phase Alternating Line. Because the TV decoder uses the
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] color burst as a reference, it needs to know whether the current line is the "normal" phase or the
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] "inverted" phase to correctly decode the color information.
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] The Logic of the Inversion
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] In a PAL signal, the U-axis (blue-ish) remains constant, but the V-axis (red-ish)
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] is flipped 180° every line.
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] If your 2-pin DAC uses `00, 01, 10, 11` to represent the subcarrier, you are effectively creating a
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] vector. To flip the phase, you need to swap the values that represent the "high" and "low"
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] excursions of the V-component.
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] Implementation in `initChroma()`
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] You should define your burst and your Red vector, then generate the `oddRed` buffer by applying the
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 180° phase inversion to the V-component data.
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] void initChroma() {
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // 0: 00 (0V), 1: 01 (Low), 3: 11 (High), 2: 10 (Mid)
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // This is a 4-step sine approximation
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]     uint32_t burst_seq[4] = {0, 1, 3, 2};
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // Red phase is shifted. Let's assume a phase shift for Red.
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // To invert V, we swap the values that correspond to the V-component.
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // A simple way to invert the phase in your 2-pin DAC:
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // Swap 1 (01) and 2 (10) in the pattern.
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]     for(int i = 0; i < colorWords; i++) {
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]         uint32_t even_word = 0;
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]         uint32_t odd_word = 0;
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]         for(int b = 0; b < 16; b++) {
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]             int step = (i * 16 + b) % 4;
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]             // Standard phase
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]             even_word |= (burst_seq[step] << (b * 2));
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]             // Inverted phase (V-flip)
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]             // Swap 1 and 2 to invert the V-component of the signal
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]             int v_step = burst_seq[step];
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]             if (v_step == 1) v_step = 2;
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]             else if (v_step == 2) v_step = 1;
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]             odd_word |= (v_step << (b * 2));
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]         }
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]         evenRed[i] = even_word;
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]         oddRed[i] = odd_word;
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]     }
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] Critical Implementation Details
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  The Burst must NOT be inverted:
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]     The color burst is the reference. It must remain constant across all lines so the TV
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] can lock onto it. Only the active video portion of your buffer should be phase-inverted.
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]        Correction:* In your `initChroma` loop, keep the first ~12 cycles (the burst)
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] identical in both `evenRed` and `oddRed`. Only perform the swap for the indices representing the
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] active video scanline.
2026-05-07 16:13:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:13:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  The Burst Phase:
2026-05-07 16:13:29 [gemini-3.1-lite]     The burst itself is usually at 135° (or -225°). Ensure the first 12 cycles of your `evenRed`
2026-05-07 16:13:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] and `oddRed` buffers are identical, and that they start at the correct phase relative to the `irq
2026-05-07 16:13:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] 4` trigger from your `vidbit` program.
2026-05-07 16:13:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:13:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  The "Blanking" Gap:
2026-05-07 16:13:29 [gemini-3.1-lite]     Make sure you have a `0x0` (or the pedestal level) between the burst and the start of the
2026-05-07 16:13:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] active video. This is essential for the TV's clamping circuit.
2026-05-07 16:13:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:13:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Does your current `chroma.pio` handle the transition from the burst to the active video
2026-05-07 16:13:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] automatically, or do you need to send a specific "stop" or "reset" code in the FIFO?* If the
2026-05-07 16:13:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] PIO just runs continuously, the logic above is the best way to handle the alternating lines.
2026-05-07 16:13:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite] This is a solid foundation. You have the `chromadmachan` ready, but there is one
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite] significant logic gap: The DMA is currently configured to send 15 words per call, but your
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite] `colorWords` is 120.
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite] Also, your `initChroma` is currently filling the buffer with static values (`0x55` and `0xCC`). To
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite] make the PAL decoder actually "see" color, you must generate the sine-wave phase sequence.
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. Fixing the DMA Configuration
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite] In `chromadmainit`, you have hardcoded `15` as the transfer count. You should change this
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite] to `colorWords` (120) so it matches the buffer size.
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite] void chroma_dma_init(int sm) {
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite]     chroma_dma_chan = dma_claim_unused_channel(true);
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite]     dma_channel_config c = dma_channel_get_default_config(chroma_dma_chan);
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite]     channel_config_set_transfer_data_size(&c, DMA_SIZE_32);
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite]     channel_config_set_read_increment(&c, true);
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite]     channel_config_set_write_increment(&c, false);
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite]     channel_config_set_dreq(&c, pio_get_dreq(pio1, sm, true)); // Ensure pio1!
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite]     dma_channel_configure(chroma_dma_chan, &c, &pio1->txf[sm], NULL, colorWords, false);
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. Implementing the PAL Phase Alternation
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite] To get color, you need to replace your `initChroma` loops with a phase-accurate generator. The
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite] "odd" phase inversion is achieved by swapping the values of the sine wave excursion.
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite] void initChroma() {
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // 0=00, 1=01, 3=11, 2=10 (Sine approximation)
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite]     uint32_t sine[4] = {0, 1, 3, 2};
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite]     uint32_t burst = 0x23102310; // Burst pattern
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite]     for(int i = 0; i < colorWords; i++) {
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite]         if(i < 3) {
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite]             evenRed[i] = burst;
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite]             oddRed[i] = burst;
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite]         } else {
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite]             uint32_t even_w = 0;
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite]             uint32_t odd_w = 0;
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite]             for(int b = 0; b < 16; b++) {
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite]                 int phase = (i * 16 + b) % 4;
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite]                 even_w |= (sine[phase] << (b * 2));
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite]                 // Invert phase for Odd lines (V-flip)
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite]                 // Swap 1 (01) and 2 (10) to flip V-axis
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite]                 int s = sine[phase];
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite]                 if(s == 1) s = 2; else if(s == 2) s = 1;
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite]                 odd_w |= (s << (b * 2));
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite]             }
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite]             evenRed[i] = even_w;
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite]             oddRed[i] = odd_w;
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite]         }
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite]     }
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3. Critical Timing Note
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite] Your `chroma.pio` uses `wait 1 irq 4`. This means the `chroma` state machine will not move
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite] until the `vidbit` program hits its IRQ.
2026-05-07 16:15:53 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:15:54 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Check: In `vidbit.pio`, is `irq 4` being set at the exact start of the active video
2026-05-07 16:15:54 [gemini-3.1-lite] (right after the back porch)?
2026-05-07 16:15:54 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Recommendation: If the color is flickering or unstable, verify that your `chroma` state
2026-05-07 16:15:54 [gemini-3.1-lite] machine is not stalling. You can add a `set pindirs, 3` at the very start of your `chroma.pio` to
2026-05-07 16:15:54 [gemini-3.1-lite] ensure the pins are driven high-Z until the burst starts.
2026-05-07 16:15:54 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:15:54 [gemini-3.1-lite] 4. Hardware/Filter Check
2026-05-07 16:15:54 [gemini-3.1-lite] Since you are outputting to `CHROMA330PIN` (21) and `CHROMA100PIN` (20), ensure your
2026-05-07 16:15:54 [gemini-3.1-lite] `chroma.pio` is using `out pins, 2`.
2026-05-07 16:15:54 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:15:54 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Final Pro-tip:* If the image is black and white, the TV is not detecting the burst. Use a
2026-05-07 16:15:54 [gemini-3.1-lite] scope to check the voltage at the video output during the back porch. You should see a clean
2026-05-07 16:15:54 [gemini-3.1-lite] 4.43MHz "burst" of about 300mV peak-to-peak. If the burst is missing or the amplitude is wrong, the
2026-05-07 16:15:54 [gemini-3.1-lite] TV will never enable the color decoder.
2026-05-07 16:15:54 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:16:55 [nitro@ryzen5] is pio1 correct for chroma?
2026-05-07 16:16:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] Yes, `pio1` is correct, provided your initialization matches.
2026-05-07 16:16:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:16:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] Using `pio1` for the chroma state machine is actually a best practice in this scenario for
2026-05-07 16:16:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] two reasons:
2026-05-07 16:16:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:16:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Resource Isolation: You are currently using `pio0` for `vidbit` and `wst` (Teletext).
2026-05-07 16:16:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] `pio0` has 4 state machines, and you are already using at least two of them. Moving `chroma` to
2026-05-07 16:16:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] `pio1` prevents resource contention and makes it significantly easier to manage the FIFO status and
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] `fdebug` registers without masking the wrong status bits.
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Independent Clocking/Sync: By using `pio1`, you can ensure that the `chroma` SM has its
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] own dedicated FIFO and DMA channel. As long as you sync the start of the state machines, they will
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] operate independently, which is vital for maintaining the strict phase timing of the color
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] subcarrier.
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] Verification Checklist for `pio1` usage:
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] • DMA DREQ: Ensure your `chromadmainit` function points to `pio1`:
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // Correct DREQ for pio1
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite]     channel_config_set_dreq(&c, pio_get_dreq(pio1, sm, true));
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] • PIO Init: Your `chroma_init` function must take `pio1` as an argument and initialize it
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] on that instance:
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite]     pass &= chroma_init(pio1, ChromaStateMachine);
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] • GPIO Mapping: Ensure the pins you are using (`CHROMA330PIN` and `CHROMA100PIN`)
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] are physically mapped to the GPIO bank that `pio1` can access. On the RP2040/RP2350, all PIO
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] instances can access all GPIOs, so this is safe.
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] A Small Warning on the `chroma.pio` Program
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] In your `chroma.pio`, ensure that you are not using any instructions that are specific to `pio0`
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] (though there are none in standard PIO). The main thing to watch for is the `irq` instruction.
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] • In PIO, `irq set 4` is a global system signal. When `vidbit` (on `pio0`) calls `irq set
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 4`, the `chroma` SM (on `pio1`) will receive it correctly because the interrupt flags are shared
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] across the whole PIO subsystem.
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] • One final detail:*
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] Since you are using `pio1` for `i2s` (Audio) as well:
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Make sure `AudioStateMachine` and `ChromaStateMachine` are set to different values (e.g.,
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] `ChromaStateMachine = 0`, `AudioStateMachine = 1`).
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] • If they are both on `pio1`, they will share the same instruction memory and FIFO resources. This
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] is fine, but just be aware that they are "roommates."
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Are you planning to use the same `pio1` instance for both Audio and Chroma?* If so,
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] ensure your `chromainit` and `i2sinit` don't overwrite each other's configuration or
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] program memory offsets.
2026-05-07 16:17:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:18:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] This looks structurally consistent. You've addressed the `pio1` parameter in
2026-05-07 16:18:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] `chromadmainit` and the transfer count.
2026-05-07 16:18:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:18:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] One final check on your `chroma` state machine: Since you are using `pio1` and
2026-05-07 16:18:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] `chromadmainit` is now passing the `PIO` instance, you must ensure that your `chroma.pio`
2026-05-07 16:18:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] Wait instruction (`wait 1 irq 4`) is properly synchronized.
2026-05-07 16:18:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:18:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] Critical Path: The IRQ Synchronization
2026-05-07 16:18:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] In the RP2040/RP2350, `irq 4` is a global system flag. However, if your `chroma` state machine is
2026-05-07 16:18:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] on `pio1` and your `vidbit` state machine is on `pio0`, you must ensure that the `chroma` SM is
2026-05-07 16:18:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] actually started before the first `irq 4` occurs.
2026-05-07 16:18:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:18:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] If `vidbit` fires `irq 4` before `chroma` is enabled or before it reaches the `wait` instruction,
2026-05-07 16:18:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] the `chroma` SM will effectively "miss" the trigger and potentially hang or desync.
2026-05-07 16:18:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:18:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Recommendation for robustness:*
2026-05-07 16:18:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] In your `main()` loop, ensure you enable the `chroma` state machine before you enable the
2026-05-07 16:18:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] `vidbit` state machine:
2026-05-07 16:18:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:18:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:18:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] // Correct startup sequence
2026-05-07 16:18:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] if (pass) {
2026-05-07 16:18:12 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // 1. Enable Chroma SM first so it is sitting at 'wait'
2026-05-07 16:18:13 [gemini-3.1-lite]     pio_sm_set_enabled(pio1, ChromaStateMachine, true);
2026-05-07 16:18:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:18:13 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // 2. Start audio/other SMs
2026-05-07 16:18:13 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // ...
2026-05-07 16:18:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:18:13 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // 3. Start vidbit SM last, which triggers the first IRQ
2026-05-07 16:18:13 [gemini-3.1-lite]     vid_start(pio0, VideoStateMachine);
2026-05-07 16:18:13 [gemini-3.1-lite]     started = true;
2026-05-07 16:18:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
