2026-05-07 16:21:35 [nitro@ryzen5] is color burst timing too early, should i pad in a delay?
2026-05-07 16:21:40 [gemini-3.1-lite] In PAL video, the Color Burst must start roughly 0.9µs to 1.1µs after the trailing edge
2026-05-07 16:21:40 [gemini-3.1-lite] of the HSYNC pulse.
2026-05-07 16:21:40 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:21:40 [gemini-3.1-lite] In your `vidbit.pio`, the `backporch` period is where your `irq set 4` lives. Let's look at your
2026-05-07 16:21:40 [gemini-3.1-lite] timing:
2026-05-07 16:21:40 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:21:40 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:21:40 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	; back porch = ~5.790 µs = 964 cycles (30*32+4)
2026-05-07 16:21:40 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	set	pindirs, 0
2026-05-07 16:21:40 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	set	pins, 1
2026-05-07 16:21:40 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	irq	set 4           <-- Trigger
2026-05-07 16:21:40 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	set	y, 29
2026-05-07 16:21:40 [gemini-3.1-lite] backporch:
2026-05-07 16:21:40 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	jmp	y--,backporch [31]
2026-05-07 16:21:40 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:21:40 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:21:40 [gemini-3.1-lite] The Timing Problem
2026-05-07 16:21:40 [gemini-3.1-lite] The `irq 4` is being triggered at the very start of the back porch.
2026-05-07 16:21:40 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Chroma Latency: When `chroma.pio` receives `irq 4`, it exits the `wait` state, but it
2026-05-07 16:21:40 [gemini-3.1-lite] takes 1-2 clock cycles to start executing the `out` instructions.
2026-05-07 16:21:40 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Burst Start: If your `initChroma` buffer starts immediately with the burst pattern, the
2026-05-07 16:21:40 [gemini-3.1-lite] burst will appear right at the start of the back porch.
2026-05-07 16:21:40 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  The Standard: PAL standards require a "front porch of the back porch" (the blanking
2026-05-07 16:21:40 [gemini-3.1-lite] pedestal) before the burst begins.
2026-05-07 16:21:40 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:21:40 [gemini-3.1-lite] The Solution: "Pre-Burst" Padding
2026-05-07 16:21:40 [gemini-3.1-lite] You don't necessarily need to change the PIO code; you can pad the data in your
2026-05-07 16:21:40 [gemini-3.1-lite] `evenRed`/`oddRed` buffers.
2026-05-07 16:21:40 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:21:40 [gemini-3.1-lite] If the burst starts too early (hitting the sync pulse or the very start of the blanking interval),
2026-05-07 16:21:40 [gemini-3.1-lite] it will cause "burst jitter" or prevent the TV from locking.
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Modify your `initChroma` to add a "pedestal" delay:*
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] void initChroma() {
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite]     const uint32_t burst = 0x23102310; // Your burst pattern
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite]     const uint32_t pedestal = 0x00000000; // Black level (0V)
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite]     for(int i = 0; i < 120; i++) {
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite]         if (i < 2) {
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite]             // 2 words of silence (pedestal) = ~8 cycles of delay
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite]             evenRed[i] = pedestal;
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite]             oddRed[i] = pedestal;
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite]         } else if (i < 5) {
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite]             // Then the burst starts
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite]             evenRed[i] = burst;
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite]             oddRed[i] = burst;
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite]         } else {
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite]             // Then the active video
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite]             oddRed[i] = 0x55555555;
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite]             evenRed[i] = 0xcccccccc;
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite]         }
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite]     }
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] How to verify:
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Oscilloscope: Trigger on the falling edge of HSYNC (Pin 22).
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Measure: Look at the `CHROMA` pins (20/21). You should see a flat line (pedestal) for
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] about 1µs, followed by the high-frequency burst.
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] • If the burst is touching the sync pulse: The TV will lose horizontal lock. You
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] must add that pedestal delay.
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Is your image currently locking horizontally?* If the picture is tearing or "teetering,"
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] the burst is likely interfering with the sync pulse. If the picture is stable but black and white,
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] the burst is likely not being detected because it's either in the wrong place or the wrong
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] amplitude.
2026-05-07 16:21:41 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
