2026-05-07 16:23:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] That is a great place to be. If the image is stable and locking, you have already cleared the
2026-05-07 16:23:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] hardest hurdles: HSYNC timing and PIO/DMA synchronization.
2026-05-07 16:23:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:23:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you decide to revisit color later, remember that since your timing is already "good," you can
2026-05-07 16:23:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] simply perform the "color injection" by updating the `initChroma` buffer with the sine-wave phase
2026-05-07 16:23:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] data. Because your current `initChroma` is filling the buffers with static patterns, the TV is
2026-05-07 16:23:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] likely ignoring them as "noise" or "blanking" and defaulting to monochrome—which is exactly what
2026-05-07 16:23:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] you want while tuning the luma path.
2026-05-07 16:23:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:23:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] • A final piece of advice for when you do turn on the color:*
2026-05-07 16:23:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] Since you are using a 2-pin DAC, watch your total output impedance. When you enable the
2026-05-07 16:23:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] Chroma pins, you are essentially creating a parallel load on the video line. If the luma levels
2026-05-07 16:23:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] start to look "dim" or the sync pulse loses amplitude once the chroma is enabled, you might need to
2026-05-07 16:23:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] slightly increase the series resistance on the `CHROMA330` and `CHROMA100` pins to maintain the
2026-05-07 16:23:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] standard 0.7V video level.
2026-05-07 16:23:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:23:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] You've built a very clean, high-performance signal generator. Good luck with the rest of the
2026-05-07 16:23:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] project!
