2026-05-07 16:23:31 [nitro@ryzen5] so what should read look like?
2026-05-07 16:23:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] To get "Red" in PAL, you need to modulate the subcarrier phase. In the PAL system, the Red color
2026-05-07 16:23:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] difference signal (V) is represented by the phase of the subcarrier.
2026-05-07 16:23:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:23:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] Since you are using a 2-pin DAC, you are effectively creating a Quadrature Amplitude Modulation
2026-05-07 16:23:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] (QAM) vector. To get a stable Red, you need to output a specific phase that the TV's decoder
2026-05-07 16:23:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] will interpret as Red.
2026-05-07 16:23:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:23:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. The Red Phase
2026-05-07 16:23:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] For a standard PAL signal, the Red vector is at approximately 104° from the burst.
2026-05-07 16:23:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] If your burst (which is at 135°) is your reference, you need to shift your sine-wave table to hit
2026-05-07 16:23:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] that 104° target.
2026-05-07 16:23:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:23:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. The Updated `initChroma` for Red
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] Instead of `0x55555555` (which is just a high-frequency square wave that the TV will likely reject
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] as noise), use a table that approximates the sine wave for the Red phase.
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] void initChroma() {
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // 4-step sine approximation for 2-pin DAC: 00, 01, 11, 10
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // We shift the array to set the phase.
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]     uint32_t sine[4] = {0, 1, 3, 2};
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]     uint32_t burst = 0x23102310;
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // Phase shift for RED (approx 104 degrees)
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // By starting at index 1 of the sine array, we create a phase shift
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]     int red_phase_offset = 1;
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]     for(int i = 0; i < colorWords; i++) {
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]         if(i < 3) {
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]             evenRed[i] = burst;
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]             oddRed[i] = burst;
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]         } else {
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]             uint32_t even_w = 0;
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]             uint32_t odd_w = 0;
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]             for(int b = 0; b < 16; b++) {
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]                 int phase = ((i * 16 + b) + red_phase_offset) % 4;
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]                 even_w |= (sine[phase] << (b * 2));
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]                 // PAL V-flip: Invert the phase for odd lines
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]                 // Swap 1 (01) and 2 (10)
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]                 int s = sine[phase];
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]                 if(s == 1) s = 2; else if(s == 2) s = 1;
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]                 odd_w |= (s << (b * 2));
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]             }
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]             evenRed[i] = even_w;
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]             oddRed[i] = odd_w;
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]         }
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]     }
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3. Why this works:
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The Phase Shift: By changing `redphaseoffset`, you rotate the color hue. If the
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] red looks "orange" or "magenta," simply increment or decrement this offset (0, 1, 2, or 3) until it
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] looks like true Red.
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The V-Flip: The swapping of `1` and `2` in the `odd_w` loop is the "PAL" part of
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] the signal. It ensures that the V-axis (Red component) is inverted every other line, which is what
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] the TV's color decoder uses to cancel out phase errors.
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 4. Hardware Adjustment
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Saturation: If the Red is too pale, your 2-pin DAC isn't swinging enough voltage.
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Hue: If the color is wrong, change the `redphaseoffset`.
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Burst: Keep the `burst` constant as `0x23102310` (or whatever phase your TV likes for the
2026-05-07 16:23:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] burst reference).
