2026-05-07 18:05:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:05:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  PIO Architecture: Your use of multiple PIO blocks (`pio0`, `pio1`, `pio2`) to separate
2026-05-07 18:05:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] concerns (Video/WST, Chroma/Audio, Keyboard) is a robust approach for managing the different
2026-05-07 18:05:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] time-bases required for PAL broadcast standards.
2026-05-07 18:05:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Chroma Encoding: The `initChroma` function correctly identifies the need for
2026-05-07 18:05:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] phase-alternating line (PAL) logic by swapping the sine phases (1 and 2) to maintain the "V-flip"
2026-05-07 18:05:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] requirement, which is essential for canceling phase errors in analog PAL decoding.
2026-05-07 18:05:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  DMA Synchronization: Relying on `dmachannelis_busy` to trigger the next field
2026-05-07 18:05:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] is standard practice; however, ensure that the `evenWords` and `oddWords` constants are precisely
2026-05-07 18:05:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] calibrated against your `10656` cycles-per-line budget to prevent jitter at the field transition.
2026-05-07 18:05:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] 4.  Hardware Considerations:
2026-05-07 18:05:57 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   Reconstruction Filter: The use of a 2-pin DAC with specific resistor values
2026-05-07 18:05:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] (100R/330R) is a classic way to achieve the PAL levels. Ensure your PCB trace lengths for these
2026-05-07 18:05:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] pins are matched, as mismatched impedance at 17.7MHz subcarrier frequencies can lead to visible
2026-05-07 18:05:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] ringing.
2026-05-07 18:05:57 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   Boot Selection: The `ReadBootSel` function is a clever way to repurpose the QSPI CS
2026-05-07 18:05:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] pin for user input without requiring an additional GPIO, which is helpful in pin-constrained
2026-05-07 18:05:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] designs.
