2026-05-07 18:07:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] The updated code addresses a few critical points for RP2350 compatibility and synchronization. Here
2026-05-07 18:07:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] are the observations regarding the changes:
2026-05-07 18:07:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:07:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. PIO Stalling Logic Fix
2026-05-07 18:07:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] In your previous snippet, you were checking `pio0->fdebug` for the `chromaStall` flag. Since your
2026-05-07 18:08:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] `chroma_init` function correctly registers the Chroma state machine on `pio1`, the fix in the
2026-05-07 18:08:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] `while(pass)` loop to check `pio1->fdebug` is essential. Without this, the stall detection would
2026-05-07 18:08:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] have been looking at the wrong hardware instance.
2026-05-07 18:08:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:08:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. Clock Divider Precision
2026-05-07 18:08:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] You added `piosmsetclkdiv(pio, sm, 9.3885);` to `chromainit`.
2026-05-07 18:08:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Precision Note: The RP2040/RP2350 PIO clock divider is a fixed-point value with 8
2026-05-07 18:08:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] fractional bits.
2026-05-07 18:08:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] • 9.3885 \approx 9 + 99.5/256.
2026-05-07 18:08:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The actual divisor applied will be 9 + (99/256) \approx 9.3867.
2026-05-07 18:08:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Given that 4 × f_sc for PAL is 17.734475 MHz, and your system clock is 166.5 MHz:
2026-05-07 18:08:00 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   166.5 / 17.734475 = 9.3885
2026-05-07 18:08:00 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   Since the PIO clock divider is 1/256 resolution, you are effectively running at 17.7376
2026-05-07 18:08:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] MHz. This is an error of \approx 0.018\%, which is well within the tolerance of standard PAL
2026-05-07 18:08:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] television tuners.
2026-05-07 18:08:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:08:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3. DMA and Memory Safety
2026-05-07 18:08:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] • DMA Pacing: Ensure that `evenWords` and `oddWords` are defined in `monobit.h` such that
2026-05-07 18:08:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] they represent the full frame buffer size.
2026-05-07 18:08:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Cache Coherency: Since you are using `dmachannelsetreadaddr`, verify that
2026-05-07 18:08:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] your `evenFrame` and `oddFrame` buffers are aligned to 4-byte boundaries (which `uint32_t` arrays
2026-05-07 18:08:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] will be by default). On the RP2350, if you were using DMA to read from buffers that might be
2026-05-07 18:08:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] modified by the CPU, you would need to be mindful of cache, but for a static video signal
2026-05-07 18:08:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] generator, this is perfectly safe.
2026-05-07 18:08:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:08:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 4. Minor Recommendation: ReadBootSel
2026-05-07 18:08:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] The `ReadBootSel` function is clever. One minor edge case to consider: if your code eventually
2026-05-07 18:08:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] processes high-frequency interrupts (like USB or audio buffers), ensure that the `for (volatile int
2026-05-07 18:08:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] i = 0; i < 100; ++i);` loop is sufficient for your specific PCB's capacitance on the QSPI SS line.
2026-05-07 18:08:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you notice "ghost" button presses, increasing that delay slightly is safer than shortening it.
