2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] To calculate the new timing with the added padding word, we must look at the `vidbit` PIO sequence
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] and the `chroma` PIO's execution flow.
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. The `vidbit` Trigger (Luminance Side)
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] In your `vidbit.pio`, you have:
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	set	pins, 1
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	irq	set 4
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] • `set pins, 1` takes 1 cycle.
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] • `irq set 4` takes 1 cycle.
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Total delay from the start of the back porch to the IRQ trigger is 2 cycles.
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] • At 166.5 MHz, this is 12 nanoseconds.
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. The `chroma` Execution (Burst Side)
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] When `irq 4` is set, the `chroma` PIO (running at 17.734475 MHz) receives the signal.
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Wait Latency: The `wait 1 irq 4` instruction in `chroma.pio` takes 1 cycle to
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] detect the IRQ.
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Padding: You added `0` (a padding word) as the first element in `evenRed` and `oddRed`.
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] • DMA Latency: The DMA controller needs to fetch the first word from SRAM to the `chroma`
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] PIO FIFO. This typically takes 3–4 cycles of the system clock.
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3. Total Timing Calculation
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] • System Clock (166.5 MHz): 1 \text cycle = 6.006 \text ns.
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] • PIO Clock (17.73 MHz): 1 \text cycle = 56.38 \text ns.
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Timeline:*
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  T=0 ns: `vidbit` hits `irq 4`.
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  T=6 ns: `chroma` PIO sees IRQ and clears it.
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  T=24 ns: DMA transfer of the first word (the padding `0`) to the FIFO.
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 4.  T=80 ns: `chroma` PIO executes `out pins, 2` for the first word (the padding).
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Result:*
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] The padding word occupies the first 56.38 nanoseconds of the "Burst" window. The
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] actual color burst (the `burst` constant) begins after this padding word is shifted out.
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] Summary
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Start of Burst: The first active color subcarrier edge will appear approximately
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 56–60 ns after the IRQ is triggered.
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Compliance: The PAL spec allows for a start-up delay of 0.9 µs to 1.1 µs. Your current
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] implementation triggers the IRQ almost immediately after the H-sync (effectively at 0.1 µs).
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Adjustment Required: To be fully compliant, you should add a delay of approximately
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 150–160 cycles (at 166.5 MHz) in `vidbit.pio` before the `irq set 4` instruction.
2026-05-07 18:14:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:14:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Example adjustment in `vidbit.pio`:*
2026-05-07 18:14:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:14:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	set	pins, 1
2026-05-07 18:14:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	nop [31] ; 32 cycles
2026-05-07 18:14:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	nop [31] ; 32 cycles
2026-05-07 18:14:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	nop [31] ; 32 cycles
2026-05-07 18:14:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	nop [31] ; 32 cycles
2026-05-07 18:14:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	nop [31] ; 32 cycles
2026-05-07 18:14:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	irq	set 4
2026-05-07 18:14:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:14:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] This adds ~160 cycles (~0.96 µs), which, when combined with your existing logic, will place the
2026-05-07 18:14:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] start of the color burst exactly within the 1.0 µs window required by the PAL standard. The
2026-05-07 18:14:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] "padding word" will then act as the final bit of the "front porch" of the burst, which is perfectly
2026-05-07 18:14:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] acceptable.
2026-05-07 18:14:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:15:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] With the addition of the `0, 0, 0, 0` padding words in your `evenRed` and `oddRed` buffers and the
2026-05-07 18:15:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] existing `irq 4` trigger, you have effectively created a programmable delay for the color
2026-05-07 18:15:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] burst.
2026-05-07 18:15:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:15:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] Timing Analysis with Padding
2026-05-07 18:15:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] At 166.5 MHz, the system clock is 6.006 ns per cycle.
2026-05-07 18:15:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:15:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Trigger Latency: From the `irq set 4` instruction in `vidbit.pio` to the first word
2026-05-07 18:15:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] being shifted out of the `chroma` PIO:
2026-05-07 18:15:29 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   `wait 1 irq 4` (1 PIO cycle)
2026-05-07 18:15:29 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   DMA fetch to FIFO (approx 3-4 system cycles)
2026-05-07 18:15:29 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   `out pins, 2` (1 PIO cycle)
2026-05-07 18:15:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Padding Duration: You have 4 words of padding. Each word in your `chroma.pio` (assuming
2026-05-07 18:15:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] 4 bits/cycle at 17.73 MHz) corresponds to 225.5 ns per word.
2026-05-07 18:15:30 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   4 words × 225.5 ns = 902 ns.
2026-05-07 18:15:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  Total Delay:
2026-05-07 18:15:30 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   The `irq` trigger is at ~12 ns (from the start of back porch).
2026-05-07 18:15:30 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   The padding adds ~902 ns.
2026-05-07 18:15:30 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   Burst Start: The first `burst` word hits the pins at approximately 914 ns
