2026-05-07 18:34:41 [nitro@ryzen5] are you happy with red chroma logic?
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] To be fully "happy" with the red chroma logic, we need to ensure the V-phase is
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] mathematically correct for the PAL standard.
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. The PAL V-Phase Math
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] In PAL, the color subcarrier is f_sc = 4.43361875\text MHz. The Red color-difference signal (V) is
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] modulated onto the subcarrier. The PAL standard dictates that the V component must be
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] inverted on every other line.
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] Your logic:
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Even lines: `sine[phase]` (Phase \phi)
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Odd lines: `sine[phase + 2]` (Phase \phi + 180^\circ)
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] • This is theoretically perfect.* By using the `(phase + 2) % 4` logic (or your extended
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] `sine[phase+2]` table), you are performing a 180-degree phase shift on the subcarrier. Since the
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] subcarrier is a sine wave, shifting by 180 degrees is identical to multiplying by -1. This is
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] exactly what the PAL V-flip requires to cancel phase errors.
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. The "Solid Red" Concern
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] You mentioned a "solid red" pulse train earlier.
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Is it actually Red? In PAL, "Red" is not just a subcarrier phase; it is the combination
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] of the Luminance (Y) and the Chroma (U and V) components.
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The Logic Gap: Your `chroma.pio` is currently outputting a fixed phase sequence. This
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] will produce a "saturated" color, but it might not be exactly the color "Red" unless the
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] Luminance (Y) signal is also contributing the correct offset.
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The Fix: If the color looks like a strange purple or orange, it means your phase offset
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] (`redphaseoffset=1`) needs calibration.
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   Try changing `redphaseoffset` to `0, 2, or 3`. One of these will map to the
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] specific phase angle for the V component relative to the burst.
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3. A Minor Concern: The Burst Phase
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] Your `burst` constant is `0x23102310`.
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] • This is a fixed pattern.
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Critical Requirement: The burst phase must be locked to the subcarrier phase.
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] • In PAL, the burst is at 135 degrees. Your `burst` constant is just a repeating bit pattern. If
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] the start of your `burst` word doesn't align with the phase of your `evenRed`
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] table, the TV will see a phase discontinuity at the start of every line.
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] • How to fix:*
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] Ensure the first word of your `evenRed` table (after the padding) is the exact same phase
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] as the `burst` constant.
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Look at the last 2 bits of your `burst` constant (`0x0` or `00`).
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Ensure that the `sine` table index used for the first word of the active line matches that phase.
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] Final Verdict
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] I am happy with the logic because it correctly implements the V-flip and the phase-shifting
2026-05-07 18:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] required for PAL. It is a very clean, low-overhead way to handle color encoding on a PIO.
2026-05-07 18:34:48 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:34:48 [gemini-3.1-lite] • My only remaining "worry"* is the phase alignment between the `burst` and the `evenRed`
2026-05-07 18:34:48 [gemini-3.1-lite] data. If the transition from the burst to the active line is "jumpy," the TV's color decoder will
2026-05-07 18:34:48 [gemini-3.1-lite] struggle to lock. If you see "streaking" or "color tearing," that is the culprit. Otherwise, the
2026-05-07 18:34:48 [gemini-3.1-lite] logic is sound.
2026-05-07 18:34:48 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:35:51 [nitro@ryzen5] can you double check timing and pulsetrain for colorburst
2026-05-07 18:35:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] To ensure the color burst is rock-solid, we need to verify the timing window (the duration
2026-05-07 18:35:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] of the burst) and the phase continuity (the transition from burst to active line).
2026-05-07 18:35:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:35:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. Timing Analysis
2026-05-07 18:35:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] You have a 120-word DMA transfer.
2026-05-07 18:35:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Word 0: `119` (Loop Count).
2026-05-07 18:35:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Words 1-4: `0, 0, 0, 0` (Padding).
2026-05-07 18:35:58 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   Padding duration: 4 words × 8 subcarrier cycles/word = 32 subcarrier cycles.
2026-05-07 18:35:58 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   32 cycles × 225.5 ns = 7.21 µs.
2026-05-07 18:35:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Words 5-6: `burst, burst` (16 subcarrier cycles).
2026-05-07 18:35:58 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   16 cycles × 225.5 ns = 3.6 µs.
2026-05-07 18:35:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:35:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Problem: Your padding is way too long. The PAL standard says the burst should start ~1µs
2026-05-07 18:35:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] after H-sync and last for ~2.25µs (10 cycles). Your current setup doesn't start the burst until
2026-05-07 18:35:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] 7.2µs* into the back porch.
2026-05-07 18:35:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:35:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The Fix:*
2026-05-07 18:35:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] Reduce the padding words. You need to start the burst at ~1µs.
2026-05-07 18:35:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] • 1µs \approx 4.4 cycles of the subcarrier.
2026-05-07 18:35:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Since each word is 8 cycles, 1 word of padding is roughly 1.8µs.
2026-05-07 18:35:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Change your buffer to:
2026-05-07 18:35:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:35:58 [gemini-3.1-lite]     uint32_t oddRed[colorWords] = {119, 0, burst, burst, burst, ...};
2026-05-07 18:35:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:35:58 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   `119` (Control)
2026-05-07 18:35:58 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   `0` (Padding: 1.8µs delay)
2026-05-07 18:35:58 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   `burst, burst, burst` (Burst: 1.8µs × 3 = 5.4µs duration).
2026-05-07 18:35:58 [gemini-3.1-lite]        Adjust the number of `burst` words to get as close to 2.25µs as possible.*
2026-05-07 18:35:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:35:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. Phase Continuity (The "Glitch")
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] The most important part of a burst is that the last bit of the burst must seamlessly
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] transition into the first bit of the active line.
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Your Burst Pattern: `0x23102310`
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   `0x2` (10), `0x3` (11), `0x1` (01), `0x0` (00).
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Your Sine Table: `0, 1, 3, 2` (which is `00, 01, 11, 10` in binary).
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The Match: Your `burst` constant does not match your `sine` table.
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   `burst` uses `0x2` (10) as the first nibble.
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   `sine[0]` is `0` (00).
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The Fix:*
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] You must define `burst` using the same `sine` table values as your active line:
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] // 10 cycles of burst (40 bits total)
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] // Use the same sine pattern: 0, 1, 3, 2
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] const uint32_t burst = 0x23102310; // This is 00, 01, 11, 10 repeating
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] // Ensure your initChroma loop starts at the correct phase:
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] // If your burst ends with '10', your first active word MUST start with '00'.
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3. The "Solid Red" Pulse Train
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you want a truly solid red line, the `active` part of the line should not just be a random sine
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] table, but a constant phase offset that represents the Red vector.
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Is the pulse train solid?*
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] Because you are using `colorWords` (120) and a DMA transfer, the PIO is outputting a continuous
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] stream of bits. There is no "gap" between words because the PIO FIFO is fed by the DMA. This is
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] excellent—it creates a perfectly smooth, glitch-free subcarrier.
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] Summary of adjustments:
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Padding: Reduce to `1` or `2` words of `0` to bring the burst start closer to the 1µs
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] mark.
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Burst Match: Ensure the `burst` constant is composed of the same `0, 1, 3, 2` sequence
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] as your `sine` table.
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  Phase: The `redphaseoffset` is your only lever for color hue. If the red looks
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] wrong, keep the burst as is, but increment the `phase` index in the `initChroma` loop.
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] Does this alignment make sense for your hardware? The padding change is the most likely reason the
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] TV is not "locking" onto the color signal.
2026-05-07 18:35:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:38:06 [nitro@ryzen5] your wording is vague, should it work as is?
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] To be direct: No, it will likely not work "as is" for a standard color TV.
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] Here are the two specific reasons why your current code will fail to produce a stable color image:
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. The Burst Timing is Misaligned
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The Math: Your `evenRed` buffer starts with 4 words of `0` padding. At your PIO frequency
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] (17.73 MHz), each word is 8 subcarrier cycles (\approx 1.8 \text µs).
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The Result: Your burst starts after 4 × 1.8 \text µs = \mathbf7.2 \text µs of back porch.
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The Standard: The PAL burst must start within 0.9 µs to 1.1 µs after the H-sync
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] trailing edge.
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The Verdict: Your burst is starting about 6 µs too late. The TV will have already
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] finished looking for the burst and will have decided the signal is black-and-white.
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. The Burst Phase Continuity
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The Math: Your `burst` constant is `0x23102310` (binary `0010 0111 0001 0000`... wait,
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] your `0x2=10`, `0x3=11`, `0x1=01`, `0x0=00`).
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The Conflict: Your `sine` table is `{0, 1, 3, 2}` (binary `00, 01, 11, 10`).
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The Mismatch: Your `burst` constant starts with `0x2` (`10`). Your `sine` table starts
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] with `0` (`00`).
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The Verdict: The moment the PIO switches from the last word of the `burst` to the first
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] word of the `active` data, the subcarrier phase will "jump" or "glitch." A TV color decoder
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] requires perfect phase continuity. If the phase jumps, the decoder will see it as a signal
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] error and unlock (resulting in no color or flashing color).
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] How to make it work
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you want this to work without a scope, you must align the burst and the data:
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Fix the Padding: Change your buffer initialization to:
