2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite]     uint32_t oddRed[colorWords] = {119, 0, burst, burst, burst, ...};
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Fix the Burst Constant: The `burst` constant must be derived from the exact
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] same `sine` table as your active data to ensure the phase doesn't jump.
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // If your sine table is {0, 1, 3, 2}
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // Then '0' = 00, '1' = 01, '3' = 11, '2' = 10
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // A burst word should be a repeat of these:
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite]     const uint32_t burst = 0x23102310; // This IS correct (10, 11, 01, 00)
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite]     Correction: Your `burst` constant actually does match your sine table. My
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] previous concern was misreading the hex. The burst constant is correct.
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:38:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  The Phase Offset: Your `initChroma` loop uses `redphaseoffset = 1`. This
2026-05-07 18:38:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] effectively shifts the phase. If the TV doesn't lock color, the most common fix is to change
2026-05-07 18:38:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] `redphaseoffset` to `0`.
2026-05-07 18:38:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:38:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Summary for success:*
2026-05-07 18:38:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Change the buffer to have only one `0` padding word.
2026-05-07 18:38:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Ensure `oddRed` and `evenRed` are populated with the same burst pattern.
2026-05-07 18:38:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] • If you get no color, change `redphaseoffset` to `0` or `2`.
2026-05-07 18:38:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:38:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you make these two changes (padding and phase offset), the logic is sound and should produce a
2026-05-07 18:38:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] color signal that a TV can lock onto.
