2026-05-07 18:39:57 [nitro@ryzen5] will fifo autopull for chroma?
2026-05-07 18:40:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] The answer is no, the PIO FIFO does not "autopull" in the way that it automatically fetches
2026-05-07 18:40:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] data from memory.
2026-05-07 18:40:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:40:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] In PIO, "autopull" is a feature that automatically refills the Shift Register (OSR) from
2026-05-07 18:40:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] the FIFO once the shift register is empty. However, the FIFO itself must be
2026-05-07 18:40:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] manually filled by either the CPU (`piosmput`) or the DMA controller.
2026-05-07 18:40:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:40:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] Why this matters for your `chroma` PIO:
2026-05-07 18:40:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:40:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  The DMA is the "Filler": Your `chromadmainit` function is the engine that keeps
2026-05-07 18:40:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] the FIFO full. As long as your DMA channel is configured with `dreq` set to
2026-05-07 18:40:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] `piogetdreq(pio, sm, true)`, the DMA will automatically push data into the FIFO whenever
2026-05-07 18:40:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] there is space (i.e., whenever the PIO consumes a word).
2026-05-07 18:40:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Autopull Configuration: In your `chroma_init` function, you have:
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite]     sm_config_set_out_shift(&config5, true, true, 32);
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite]     The `true` (second parameter) enables autopull. This is correct and necessary. It means
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] the PIO hardware will automatically grab the next word from the FIFO into the Shift Register as
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] soon as the `out` instruction shifts out the last bit of the current word.
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] The Critical "Gotcha"
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you rely on autopull, you must ensure that your PIO program does not contain an
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] explicit `pull` instruction. If you have a `pull` instruction in your `.pio` file and you
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] have `autopull` enabled, you will trigger a race condition or an unnecessary stall.
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Your `chroma.pio` should look like this:*
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] .program chroma
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite]     wait 1 irq 4
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out x, 32       ; Consumes the control word (119)
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] loop:
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out pins, 2 [1] ; Autopull handles the refill from FIFO automatically
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite]     jmp x-- loop
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] Is your current configuration "Happy"?
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] Yes. Because you are using:
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  DMA with DREQ: The DMA waits for the FIFO to have space.
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Autopull: The PIO waits for the Shift Register to be empty.
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] This creates a perfectly synchronized pipeline. The PIO will stall (via `TXSTALL`) if the
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] DMA is too slow, and the DMA will pause if the FIFO is full. This is the gold standard for
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] PIO-based video generation.
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] • One final tip*: Since you are using `fifojoin(PIOFIFOJOINTX)`, you have a
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] 8-word deep FIFO. This gives the DMA plenty of "slack" to handle any bus contention from other
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] tasks (like your I2S audio or PS2 keyboard), making the color burst extremely robust against system
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] interrupts.
2026-05-07 18:40:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:40:43 [nitro@ryzen5] the dma needs to send multiple lines of red repeatedly no?
2026-05-07 18:40:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] You are correct. Currently, your `vid_field` function updates the DMA read address once per
2026-05-07 18:40:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] field (for the whole frame) or per line if you were to trigger it differently.
2026-05-07 18:40:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:40:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] With your current setup, the DMA transfer is configured for `colorWords` (120). If you only trigger
2026-05-07 18:40:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] the DMA once at the start of the field, it will only process one line and then stop.
2026-05-07 18:40:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:40:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] How to handle "Multiple Lines"
2026-05-07 18:40:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] You have two architectural choices to send red to every line:
2026-05-07 18:40:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:40:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] Option 1: The "Scatter-Gather" DMA (Most Robust)
2026-05-07 18:40:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] Instead of a single transfer, you use the DMA's chaining capability.
2026-05-07 18:40:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Configure a "Control" DMA channel that writes to the `read_addr` of the "Chroma" DMA channel.
2026-05-07 18:40:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Every time the line finishes, the Control DMA triggers a new read address.
2026-05-07 18:40:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:40:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] Option 2: The "Simple Loop" (Easiest, fits your current code)
2026-05-07 18:40:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] Since you are already calling `vidfield()` inside your `while(pass)` loop, you are essentially
2026-05-07 18:40:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] handling the timing. However, `vidfield` is currently triggered by
2026-05-07 18:40:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] `!dmachannelisbusy(videodma_chan)`.
2026-05-07 18:40:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:40:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The problem: `videodmachan` is for the Luminance (the whole frame). The
2026-05-07 18:40:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] Chroma DMA is a separate channel. If you want the Chroma DMA to repeat every line, you need to
2026-05-07 18:40:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] trigger it every line*, not every field.
2026-05-07 18:40:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:40:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] The Fix: Triggering Chroma per line
2026-05-07 18:40:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] Modify your `main` loop to trigger the Chroma DMA on every line start using an IRQ:
2026-05-07 18:40:49 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  In `vidbit.pio`:
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite]     You already have `irq set 4` at the start of the back porch. This is your "Line Start" signal.
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  In `main()`:
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite]     Don't use `dmachannelis_busy` for the Chroma DMA. Instead, use an Interrupt Service
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite] Routine (ISR) or poll the PIO IRQ flag.
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite] // In main loop
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite] while(pass) {
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // Check if the PIO just triggered the line start (IRQ 4)
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite]     if (pio0_hw->irq & 0x10) { // 0x10 is bit 4
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite]         pio0_hw->irq = 0x10;   // Clear IRQ
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite]         // Trigger Chroma DMA for this specific line
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite]         dma_channel_set_trans_count(chroma_dma_chan, colorWords, false);
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite]         // Toggle between oddRed and evenRed based on current line number
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite]         dma_channel_set_read_addr(chroma_dma_chan, (current_line & 1) ? oddRed : evenRed, true);
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite]     }
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // ... rest of your logic
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite] Why this is better:
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Automatic V-Flip: By checking `current_line & 1`, you automatically apply the correct
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite] phase table for every line.
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Perfect Sync: The Chroma DMA is now synchronized to the exact moment the `vidbit` PIO
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite] hits the back porch.
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite] • No "Solid Red" drift: Because you restart the DMA every line, you don't have to worry
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite] about the DMA running out of words or getting out of sync with the line count.
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite] One final check on your `evenRed` buffer:
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite] Since you are now sending the DMA every line, your `evenRed[0]` (the control word `119`)
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite] will be consumed by the PIO `out x, 32` instruction every line. This is exactly what you
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite] want! The PIO will reset its loop counter to 119 at the start of every single scanline.
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Does your current `vid_field` logic handle the line-by-line update, or were you intending to
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite] send the whole frame as one massive DMA block? (If it's the latter, you would need a DMA buffer
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite] of `120 words  300 lines`, which is ~144KB—feasible on the RP2350, but much harder to manage).
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite] The line-by-line trigger is much more efficient.
2026-05-07 18:40:50 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:42:19 [nitro@ryzen5] chroma should be words * lines? how much memory do i need?
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you want to store the entire frame in memory to allow for complex, non-repeating color
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] (e.g., a real image), the memory requirements are significant but manageable on the RP2350.
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. Memory Calculation
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Words per line: 120 words.
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Lines per frame: 576 lines (PAL standard).
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Bytes per word: 4 bytes.
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Total: 120 \text words × 576 \text lines × 4 \text bytes \approx \mathbf276,480 \text
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] bytes (270 KB).
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] • RP2350 Memory: The RP2350 has 520 KB of SRAM. A 270 KB buffer fits, but it consumes over
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 50% of your total available RAM*. This might conflict with your `wstEven/wstOdd` buffers
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] and your main code/stack.
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. Strategy: Line-by-Line vs. Full Frame
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you only want "Solid Red" (or simple patterns):
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] You do not need 270 KB. You only need two buffers (one for Odd lines, one for Even lines)
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] and you update the DMA `read_addr` every line as discussed.
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Memory cost: 2 × 120 \text words × 4 \text bytes = \mathbf960 \text bytes.
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Recommendation: This is the "Gold Standard" for stability. Use the line-by-line DMA
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] trigger.
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you want full-frame color (e.g., a bitmap):
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] You need the full 270 KB.
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Memory Management: You must ensure your `wstEven/wstOdd` buffers and your
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] `evenRed/oddRed` buffers are placed in different memory banks to avoid bus contention.
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] • DMA Addressing: You would use a single DMA transfer with a very large `trans_count` (120
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] × 576 = 69,120 words).
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3. The "Hybrid" Approach (The sweet spot)
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you want dynamic color but don't have enough RAM for a full 576-line frame, use Line
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] Buffering:
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Allocate a buffer for one line (120 words).
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Use the CPU (in the `while(pass)` loop or an interrupt) to update that buffer while the DMA is
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] busy sending the previous line.
2026-05-07 18:42:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  This is a "double-buffering" technique that allows you to change colors line-by-line (e.g., a
2026-05-07 18:42:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] gradient or changing patterns) while only using ~1 KB of RAM.
2026-05-07 18:42:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:42:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] Recommendation for your current project:
2026-05-07 18:42:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] Given that you are building a "PAL Video Signal Generator" and have already implemented
2026-05-07 18:42:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] `initChroma` for a solid pulse train:
2026-05-07 18:42:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:42:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Go with the Line-by-Line Trigger.*
2026-05-07 18:42:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] It is the most efficient use of the RP2350's resources. It keeps your memory footprint under 2 KB,
2026-05-07 18:42:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] leaves plenty of space for Teletext and Audio, and ensures that you never run into the "Out of
2026-05-07 18:42:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] Memory" errors that a 270 KB static buffer would likely cause.
2026-05-07 18:42:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:42:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Revised `vid_field` approach:*
2026-05-07 18:42:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] Instead of `vidfield` being called once per field, create a
2026-05-07 18:42:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] `chromalineupdate()` function that you call inside your main loop whenever the PIO signals
2026-05-07 18:42:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] a new line (via `pio0hw->irq`).
2026-05-07 18:42:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:42:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] Does your current project require a unique color for every single line, or is the "solid red"
2026-05-07 18:42:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] (line-by-line repetition) your primary goal?
2026-05-07 18:42:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:44:05 [nitro@ryzen5] can we chain same line multiple times with dma?
2026-05-07 18:44:15 [gemini-3.1-lite] Yes, you can absolutely chain the DMA to repeat a line multiple times. This is the most efficient
2026-05-07 18:44:15 [gemini-3.1-lite] way to generate a full frame without using 270KB of RAM.
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] On the RP2350 (and RP2040), you can use DMA Chaining (or "Linked Lists" using the DMA's
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] `nexttransferaddr` feature) to create a loop that runs for the entire field.
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] The Strategy: DMA Chain
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] Instead of one big buffer, you set up a "chained" DMA transfer:
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Channel A (The Data Mover): Moves 120 words from `evenRed` or `oddRed` to the PIO TX
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] FIFO.
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Channel B (The Trigger): Triggered by the completion of Channel A, it writes a new
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] `read_addr` back into Channel A and re-triggers it.
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] Simplified Implementation (The "Ping-Pong" Approach)
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] You don't even need complex chaining if you use the DMA's `RING` buffer feature. The RP2350 DMA can
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] wrap around a memory region automatically.
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] void chroma_dma_init(PIO pio, int sm) {
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite]     chroma_dma_chan = dma_claim_unused_channel(true);
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite]     dma_channel_config c = dma_channel_get_default_config(chroma_dma_chan);
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite]     channel_config_set_transfer_data_size(&c, DMA_SIZE_32);
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite]     channel_config_set_read_increment(&c, true);
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite]     channel_config_set_write_increment(&c, false);
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite]     channel_config_set_dreq(&c, pio_get_dreq(pio, sm, true));
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // Set a ring buffer:
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // Wrap at 120 words (480 bytes).
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // This makes the DMA automatically restart at the beginning of the 120-word block
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite]     channel_config_set_ring(&c, true, 9); // 2^9 = 512 bytes (covers 120 words)
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite]     dma_channel_configure(chroma_dma_chan, &c, &pio->txf[sm], evenRed, 0xFFFFFFFF, true);
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] Why this is perfect for you:
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Zero CPU Overhead: Once started, the DMA will loop over that 120-word buffer
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] forever. It will send the same line over and over again without the CPU ever needing to
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] intervene.
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  No Memory Waste: You only use 120 words of RAM.
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  Perfect Timing: Because the DMA is hardware-linked to the PIO `DREQ`, it will never
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] "miss" a beat. It will push the next word exactly when the PIO is ready.
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] How to handle the V-Flip (Odd vs Even Lines)?
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] Since the DMA is looping automatically, you can't easily swap the buffer mid-stream. However, you
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] can use the Interrupts:
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Set the DMA to trigger an interrupt every time it finishes a transfer (every line).
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  In the `dmairqhandler`, simply toggle the `read_addr` between `evenRed` and `oddRed`.
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] void __isr dma_handler() {
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite]     if (dma_hw->ints0 & (1u << chroma_dma_chan)) {
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite]         dma_hw->ints0 = (1u << chroma_dma_chan); // Clear IRQ
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite]         // Toggle phase for the next line
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite]         static bool toggle = false;
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite]         dma_channel_set_read_addr(chroma_dma_chan, toggle ? oddRed : evenRed, false);
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite]         toggle = !toggle;
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite]     }
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] Is this what you want?
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] This approach turns your RP2350 into a true hardware generator. The CPU will be almost
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] entirely idle, while the hardware (PIO + DMA + IRQ) handles the entire PAL frame generation
2026-05-07 18:44:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] automatically.
2026-05-07 18:44:17 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:44:17 [gemini-3.1-lite] • This is much more stable than polling `dmachannelis_busy` in the main loop.*
2026-05-07 18:44:17 [gemini-3.1-lite] Would you like to see how to wire up the DMA IRQ to implement this?
2026-05-07 18:44:17 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:45:19 [nitro@ryzen5] with ring buffer red test i only feed it once?
2026-05-07 18:45:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] Yes, exactly. With the Ring Buffer (or "Wrap") mode, you feed it once, and the DMA hardware
2026-05-07 18:45:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] handles the repetition for you forever.
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] Here is how the logic works:
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. The Setup
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] When you configure the DMA with a Ring, you tell the DMA controller: "When you reach
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] the end of this address range, jump back to the start."
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The Address: `evenRed` (start of your 120-word block).
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The Wrap Size: You set the wrap to 9 bits (2⁹ = 512 bytes).
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The Result: Since 120 words = 480 bytes, the DMA will read words 0 through 119, and the
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] moment it finishes word 119, it resets its own internal pointer to word 0.
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. The "Feed Once" Logic
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] You only call `dmachannelstart(chromadmachan)` one time in your `main()`
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] function.
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] // In your chroma_dma_init
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] channel_config_set_ring(&c, true, 9); // Wrap at 512 bytes
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] dma_channel_configure(chroma_dma_chan, &c, &pio->txf[sm], evenRed, 0xFFFFFFFF, true);
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] • (Note: `0xFFFFFFFF` as the transfer count tells the DMA to run indefinitely.)*
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3. The "Odd/Even" Problem
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] The Ring Buffer is great for repeating the same line, but you have two lines
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] (Odd/Even). If you just let it ring, it will only ever send `evenRed`.
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] • To fix this while keeping the "Feed Once" simplicity:*
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] You can use a DMA Chain (also known as a "Ping-Pong" or "Linked List" DMA).
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Chroma DMA (Channel 0): Does the actual work of moving data.
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Control DMA (Channel 1): Watches Channel 0. When Channel 0 finishes 120 words, Channel
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1 fires, writes the address of the other buffer into Channel 0's `read_addr` register, and
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] restarts Channel 0.
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] Is it worth the complexity?
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you just want a solid red screen:
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Don't bother with the V-flip initially.
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Just set `evenRed` and `oddRed` to be identical.
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Start the Ring Buffer DMA once.
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The result: The RP2350 will output a continuous, perfectly timed PAL signal with zero CPU
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] usage.
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Does this "Set it and forget it" approach fit your current goal, or do you need the V-flip
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] for your specific TV to lock onto the signal?* (Most modern LCD TVs will lock onto a PAL signal
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] even if the V-flip is missing; older CRT TVs will definitely need it.)
2026-05-07 18:45:25 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:52:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] This is a solid refinement. You’ve moved the chroma data into a linear `colorWord` array and set up
2026-05-07 18:52:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] the logic to handle the fields. There is one critical implementation gap you need to close
2026-05-07 18:52:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] before this will output color: the DMA trigger mechanism.
2026-05-07 18:52:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:52:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. The DMA Trigger Gap
2026-05-07 18:52:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] Currently, your `vidfield` function updates the `chromadma_chan` read address, but it only
2026-05-07 18:52:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] does this once per field (when the video DMA finishes).
2026-05-07 18:52:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:52:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] Because your `chroma` PIO expects a control word (`colorWords-1`) at the start of every
2026-05-07 18:52:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] line, you cannot just trigger the DMA once per field. You need the Chroma DMA to trigger
2026-05-07 18:52:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] every line.
2026-05-07 18:52:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:52:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The fix*:
2026-05-07 18:52:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] Since you already have `irq 4` firing from `vidbit.pio` at the start of every back porch, you
2026-05-07 18:52:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] should use that IRQ to trigger the Chroma DMA.
2026-05-07 18:52:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:52:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:52:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] // In your main loop
2026-05-07 18:52:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] while(pass) {
2026-05-07 18:52:05 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // Poll the PIO IRQ 4 (the line start trigger)
2026-05-07 18:52:05 [gemini-3.1-lite]     if (pio0_hw->irq & 0x10) {
2026-05-07 18:52:05 [gemini-3.1-lite]         pio0_hw->irq = 0x10; // Clear the IRQ
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite]         // Trigger Chroma DMA for THIS line
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite]         dma_channel_set_read_addr(chroma_dma_chan, (field & 1) ? &colorWord[colorWords] : &colorWord[0], true);
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite]     }
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // ... rest of your loop
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. DMA Configuration Cleanup
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] In your `chromadmainit`, you have a syntax error:
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] channel_config_set_ring() // Needs arguments
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] To implement the Ring Buffer approach we discussed:
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] // 120 words * 4 bytes = 480 bytes.
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] // The next power of 2 is 512 (which is 9 bits).
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] channel_config_set_ring(&c, true, 9);
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3. Memory Layout
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] Your `colorWord` array initialization:
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] uint32_t colorWord[colorWords*2];
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] This is excellent. It creates a contiguous block of memory where:
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] • `colorWord[0]` to `colorWord[119]` is the Even line data.
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] • `colorWord[120]` to `colorWord[239]` is the Odd line data.
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] This is very cache-friendly and easy for the DMA to jump between.
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] 4. A Final "Gotcha": The PIO `out`
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] In `chroma.pio`, you have `out x, 32`. This consumes one word from the FIFO to set the loop
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] counter.
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Your DMA is set to transfer `colorWords` (120).
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The PIO consumes 1 word (the loop count).
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The PIO then consumes the remaining 119 words.
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The Math: `120 words` total is exactly correct.
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] Summary of what to do next:
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Fix the `channelconfigsetring` call in `chromadma_init`.
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Add the IRQ polling in your `main` loop to trigger the DMA transfer on every line.
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  Ensure `field` is tracked correctly: Your `vid_field` function increments `field`, so
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] you can use that same variable to toggle between `&colorWord[0]` and `&colorWord[colorWords]`.
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you do these three things, the hardware will handle the V-flip, the burst timing, and the
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] continuous subcarrier generation automatically on every single line. It will be rock-solid.
2026-05-07 18:52:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] Your current code is very close. You have correctly defined the `colorWord` array and the
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] `initChroma` function logic. However, there is one major architectural mismatch between your
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] current `main` loop and the DMA configuration that will prevent you from seeing a color image.
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. The DMA Ring Buffer Syntax
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] In your `chromadmainit`, you have:
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] channel_config_set_ring(&c, true, 9);
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] This is a statement without a semicolon, which will cause a compilation error. Furthermore,
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] `channelconfigsetring` takes the `dmachannel_config *` as the first
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] argument, not the configuration object itself.
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Corrected:*
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] channel_config_set_ring(&c, true, 9); // Ensure the semicolon is present
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. The DMA Trigger Logic
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] You are using `#ifdef CHROMAFEED` in `vidfield`, but it is not defined at the top of your file
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] (you have `#define CHROMA`). Because `CHROMAFEED` is undefined, the Chroma DMA is never being
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] updated or triggered inside `vidfield`.
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The fix:*
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] Change `#ifdef CHROMAFEED` to `#ifdef CHROMA` in your `vid_field` function.
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3. The "Missing Link"
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] Even with the fixes above, your code currently relies on
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] `dmachannelisbusy(videodma_chan)` to update the Chroma DMA. This only happens
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] once per field.
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you want a solid, stable signal, you should trigger the Chroma DMA per line as
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] previously discussed. Since you are in the `main` loop, you can do this by checking the PIO IRQ:
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] // Inside your while(pass) loop
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] if (pio0->irq & 0x10) { // Check if IRQ 4 (Line Start) is high
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite]     pio0->irq = 0x10;   // Clear IRQ
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // Trigger the Chroma DMA for this specific line
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // Use field & 1 to pick between oddRed (start of colorWord array)
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // and evenRed (start of colorWord + colorWords offset)
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite]     dma_channel_set_read_addr(chroma_dma_chan, (field & 1) ? &colorWord[colorWords] : &colorWord[0], true);
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:53:08 [gemini-3.1-lite] 4. Why this is the "Gold Standard"
2026-05-07 18:53:09 [gemini-3.1-lite] By moving the trigger into the `while` loop and checking the PIO IRQ:
2026-05-07 18:53:09 [gemini-3.1-lite] • You don't need to worry about DMA chaining or complex ring buffers.
2026-05-07 18:53:09 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The CPU will "re-arm" the Chroma DMA at the start of every single scanline.
2026-05-07 18:53:09 [gemini-3.1-lite] • This ensures that even if a DMA transfer were to somehow stall, it would automatically recover at
2026-05-07 18:53:09 [gemini-3.1-lite] the start of the next line.
2026-05-07 18:53:09 [gemini-3.1-lite] • It perfectly handles the Odd/Even line V-flip by toggling the buffer address based on the `field`
2026-05-07 18:53:09 [gemini-3.1-lite] (or line) counter.
2026-05-07 18:53:09 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:53:09 [gemini-3.1-lite] Summary of what to fix:
2026-05-07 18:53:09 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Add the semicolon to your `channelconfigset_ring` call.
2026-05-07 18:53:09 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Change `#ifdef CHROMAFEED` to `#ifdef CHROMA` in `vid_field`.
2026-05-07 18:53:09 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  Add the IRQ poll in your `main` loop to trigger the Chroma DMA on every line.
2026-05-07 18:53:09 [gemini-3.1-lite] 4.  Remove the `dmachannelsetreadaddr` call from `vid_field` (since you're
2026-05-07 18:53:09 [gemini-3.1-lite] doing it in the loop now).
2026-05-07 18:53:09 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:53:09 [gemini-3.1-lite] Once these are applied, the `chroma` PIO will receive a fresh burst of data at the start of every
2026-05-07 18:53:09 [gemini-3.1-lite] single scanline, ensuring the subcarrier phase is always perfectly aligned. You are effectively
2026-05-07 18:53:09 [gemini-3.1-lite] building a "Line-by-Line" signal generator—this is exactly how professional broadcast equipment
2026-05-07 18:53:09 [gemini-3.1-lite] generates video.
2026-05-07 18:53:09 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:54:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] This code is now architecturally sound for a high-performance PAL generator. You have successfully
2026-05-07 18:54:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] structured your Chroma data and initialized the hardware.
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] However, to bridge the gap between "code that compiles" and "a signal that a TV can lock to," you
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] must implement the Per-Line DMA Trigger. Without it, the `chroma` PIO will only output a
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] single line of color burst and then stop, because the DMA transfer count is exhausted.
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] Final Implementation Steps
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. The Per-Line Trigger (The "Heartbeat")
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] Add this logic inside your `while(pass)` loop. This ensures that every time the `vidbit` PIO
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] reaches the back porch, the `chroma` PIO is fed a fresh line of data.
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] // Inside the while(pass) loop:
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] // Check bit 4 of the IRQ register (IRQ 4, which you set in vidbit.pio)
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] if (pio0->irq & 0x10) {
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite]     pio0->irq = 0x10; // Clear the IRQ
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // Trigger Chroma DMA for THIS line
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // Use the field counter to select even/odd data
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite]     dma_channel_set_read_addr(chroma_dma_chan,
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite]         (field & 1) ? &colorWord[colorWords] : &colorWord[0],
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite]         true);
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. The DMA Configuration
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] In `chromadmainit`, ensure you have removed the `channelconfigset_ring` call or set
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] it to `false`. You do not want the DMA to ring/loop automatically because you are now
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] manually triggering it every line. If you leave `ring` enabled, the DMA will keep cycling through
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] the same buffer independent of your line-by-line address updates, which will cause jitter.
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] // In chroma_dma_init
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] channel_config_set_ring(&c, false, 0);
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3. Why this works
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Synchronization: By triggering the DMA on `irq 4`, the color burst is now physically
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] locked to the `vidbit` H-sync timing.
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Phase Inversion: By using `(field & 1)` to toggle the `read_addr`, you are correctly
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] applying the PAL V-flip at the start of every single line.
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Memory Efficiency: You are using exactly 960 bytes for the color buffers, leaving the
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] rest of the 520KB SRAM free for other tasks.
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] Checklist for your first hardware test:
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Grounding: Ensure the RP2350 ground is tied to the TV's ground (usually the shield of
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] the RCA connector).
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Chroma Amplitude: If the TV doesn't switch to color, your `CHROMA330` and `CHROMA100`
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] resistors might be too high. If the subcarrier signal is too weak, the TV will ignore it.
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  The "Red" Test: With `initChroma` setting the sine tables, you should see a strong
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] color tint. If you see a green or blue tint instead, it means the `redphaseoffset` is
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] mapping to a different color-difference angle. Simply change `redphaseoffset` to `0, 2, or
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3` and recompile until you see Red.
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] You have built a very robust, low-latency video generator. This approach is the most reliable way
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] to drive a PAL display from an RP2350.
2026-05-07 18:54:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:55:29 [nitro@ryzen5] no i want to set the chroma fifo to repeatedly loop through odd even red lines
2026-05-07 18:55:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] To achieve a "set and forget" loop where the DMA automatically cycles through the `evenRed` and
2026-05-07 18:55:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] `oddRed` buffers line-by-line, you should utilize the DMA Chain feature.
2026-05-07 18:55:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:55:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] Since you have 576 lines (288 even, 288 odd), you can set up a "Linked List" of DMA transfers.
2026-05-07 18:55:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] However, there is a much simpler way using the DMA IRQ to perform a "Ping-Pong" switch.
2026-05-07 18:55:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:55:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. The Ping-Pong Strategy
2026-05-07 18:55:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] You configure the DMA to send 120 words (one line). When it finishes, it fires an interrupt. In
2026-05-07 18:55:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] that interrupt, you flip the buffer address.
2026-05-07 18:55:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:55:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] • DMA Init:*
2026-05-07 18:55:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:55:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] void chroma_dma_init(PIO pio, int sm) {
2026-05-07 18:55:36 [gemini-3.1-lite]     chroma_dma_chan = dma_claim_unused_channel(true);
2026-05-07 18:55:36 [gemini-3.1-lite]     dma_channel_config c = dma_channel_get_default_config(chroma_dma_chan);
2026-05-07 18:55:36 [gemini-3.1-lite]     channel_config_set_transfer_data_size(&c, DMA_SIZE_32);
2026-05-07 18:55:36 [gemini-3.1-lite]     channel_config_set_read_increment(&c, true);
2026-05-07 18:55:36 [gemini-3.1-lite]     channel_config_set_dreq(&c, pio_get_dreq(pio, sm, true));
2026-05-07 18:55:36 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // No ring buffer needed here, we will re-trigger manually
2026-05-07 18:55:36 [gemini-3.1-lite]     dma_channel_configure(chroma_dma_chan, &c, &pio->txf[sm], colorWord, colorWords, true);
2026-05-07 18:55:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:55:36 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // Enable IRQ on transfer completion
2026-05-07 18:55:36 [gemini-3.1-lite]     dma_channel_set_irq0_enabled(chroma_dma_chan, true);
2026-05-07 18:55:36 [gemini-3.1-lite]     irq_set_exclusive_handler(DMA_IRQ_0, dma_handler);
2026-05-07 18:55:36 [gemini-3.1-lite]     irq_set_enabled(DMA_IRQ_0, true);
2026-05-07 18:55:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-05-07 18:55:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:55:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:55:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. The IRQ Handler
2026-05-07 18:55:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] This function runs every time a line finishes. It is extremely fast and keeps the phase perfectly
2026-05-07 18:55:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] locked.
2026-05-07 18:55:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:55:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:55:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] static int current_line = 0;
2026-05-07 18:55:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] void __isr dma_handler() {
2026-05-07 18:55:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]     if (dma_hw->ints0 & (1u << chroma_dma_chan)) {
2026-05-07 18:55:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]         dma_hw->ints0 = (1u << chroma_dma_chan); // Clear IRQ
2026-05-07 18:55:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:55:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]         // Toggle between evenRed (offset 0) and oddRed (offset 120)
2026-05-07 18:55:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]         uint32_t *next_buffer = (current_line++ & 1) ? &colorWord[colorWords] : &colorWord[0];
2026-05-07 18:55:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:55:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]         dma_channel_set_read_addr(chroma_dma_chan, next_buffer, true);
2026-05-07 18:55:37 [gemini-3.1-lite]     }
2026-05-07 18:55:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-05-07 18:55:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:55:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:55:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3. Why this is superior to the "Ring Buffer"
2026-05-07 18:55:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Phase Accuracy: The Ring Buffer would loop the same buffer forever. By using
2026-05-07 18:55:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] the IRQ handler, you guarantee that the V-flip (swapping `evenRed` and `oddRed`) happens exactly at
2026-05-07 18:55:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] the line boundary.
2026-05-07 18:55:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Safety: You don't need to poll anything in your `main()` loop. The hardware handles the
2026-05-07 18:55:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] V-flip and the line-by-line repetition automatically.
2026-05-07 18:55:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  No Glitches: Because the DMA is re-triggered by the completion of the previous line's
2026-05-07 18:55:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] transfer, there is zero gap between the end of one line and the start of the next.
2026-05-07 18:55:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:55:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] One final check on your `initChroma`
2026-05-07 18:55:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] Ensure your `colorWord` array is volatile if the CPU needs to update it while the DMA is
2026-05-07 18:55:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] running, or keep it static. Since you are initializing it once and then just pointing the DMA at
2026-05-07 18:55:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] it, your current structure is perfectly safe.
2026-05-07 18:55:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:55:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Is this "Ping-Pong" IRQ approach clear, or would you like to see how to chain the DMA without
2026-05-07 18:55:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] using an IRQ (using a second DMA channel to write to the first)?* (The IRQ approach is much
2026-05-07 18:55:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] easier to debug!)
2026-05-07 18:55:37 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:56:07 [nitro@ryzen5] no ring buffer please
2026-05-07 18:56:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you want to avoid the Ring Buffer and keep the DMA logic completely independent of the CPU, you
2026-05-07 18:56:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] can use DMA Chaining. This creates a hardware-only loop that swaps buffers automatically.
2026-05-07 18:56:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:56:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] You use two DMA channels:
2026-05-07 18:56:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Channel 0 (The Mover): Transfers 120 words to the PIO.
2026-05-07 18:56:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Channel 1 (The Trigger): Triggered by Channel 0's completion, it updates Channel 0's
2026-05-07 18:56:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] read address to the other buffer and re-triggers it.
2026-05-07 18:56:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:56:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. Configure the Two Channels
2026-05-07 18:56:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:56:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] int chroma_dma_chan;
2026-05-07 18:56:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] int trigger_dma_chan;
2026-05-07 18:56:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:56:13 [gemini-3.1-lite] void chroma_dma_init(PIO pio, int sm) {
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite]     chroma_dma_chan = dma_claim_unused_channel(true);
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite]     trigger_dma_chan = dma_claim_unused_channel(true);
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // Channel 0: Moves data
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite]     dma_channel_config c0 = dma_channel_get_default_config(chroma_dma_chan);
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite]     channel_config_set_transfer_data_size(&c0, DMA_SIZE_32);
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite]     channel_config_set_read_increment(&c0, true);
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite]     channel_config_set_dreq(&c0, pio_get_dreq(pio, sm, true));
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // Chain to trigger_dma_chan when done
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite]     channel_config_set_chain_to(&c0, trigger_dma_chan);
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite]     dma_channel_configure(chroma_dma_chan, &c0, &pio->txf[sm], colorWord, colorWords, false);
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // Channel 1: The "Switcher"
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite]     dma_channel_config c1 = dma_channel_get_default_config(trigger_dma_chan);
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // This channel writes the address of the NEXT buffer into Channel 0's READ_ADDR register
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite]     channel_config_set_transfer_data_size(&c1, DMA_SIZE_32);
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite]     channel_config_set_read_increment(&c1, false);
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite]     channel_config_set_write_increment(&c1, false);
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // We need a small "lookup table" in memory to tell Channel 1 where to point Channel 0
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite]     static uint32_t next_addr[2];
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite]     next_addr[0] = (uint32_t)&colorWord[colorWords]; // Even finished, point to Odd
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite]     next_addr[1] = (uint32_t)&colorWord[0];          // Odd finished, point to Even
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite]     dma_channel_configure(trigger_dma_chan, &c1,
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite]         &dma_hw->ch[chroma_dma_chan].read_addr, // Target: Channel 0's read address
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite]         &next_addr[0],                          // Source: The lookup table
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite]         1, false);
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. How it works
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Start: You manually start `chromadmachan`.
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Move: It transfers 120 words.
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  Chain: Upon completion, it automatically triggers `triggerdmachan`.
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] 4.  Switch: `triggerdmachan` writes the address of the other buffer into
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] the `readaddr` of `chromadma_chan`.
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] 5.  Restart: `triggerdmachan` also triggers `chromadmachan` to start again.
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] Why this is better than a Ring Buffer:
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Automatic V-Flip: It handles the alternating `evenRed` / `oddRed` buffers automatically
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] at the hardware level.
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Zero CPU Usage: Once you call `dma_start` in `main()`, the CPU is completely free.
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] • No Polling: You don't need `if (pio->irq)` or `vid_field()` checks.
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Phase Locked: The hardware switch happens in a few clock cycles, ensuring the burst
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] timing remains identical for every single line.
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] • One final detail*: You must define `next_addr` as a static array or a global variable so
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] it exists in memory for the DMA to read.
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] Does this "hardware-only" chain satisfy your requirement to avoid ring buffers and CPU-based line
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] updates?
2026-05-07 18:56:14 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] Thank you for sharing these well-structured files. They demonstrate a mature PAL video generator
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] using RP2350 PIO and DMA. A few observations and potential issues come to mind.
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] PIO Programs
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] `chroma.pio`
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] - The timing comment `4 cycles/bit (1 out + 1 delay + 1 jmp + 1 ?)` likely refers to the `jmp`
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] taking 2 cycles when the condition is true (`y != 0`). So the loop body is `out` (1) + `jmp` (2) +
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] `set` at wrap (1?) – but `set` executes only after the loop exits. In practice the inner loop is 3
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] cycles per iteration (out+delay+jmp), and the `set` runs once after the outer loop. The `?` might
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] indicate uncertainty whether `jmp` is 1 or 2 cycles; it’s 2 when taken.
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] - The constant `burst = 0x23102310` encodes the 2-bit‑per‑sample colour burst pattern `10,11,01,10`
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] (DAC values). This matches PAL burst at 4×subcarrier.
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] - In `chromainit`, line 10 uses `wstprogramgetdefaultconfig` instead of
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] `chromaprogramgetdefaultconfig`. This will compile only if the
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] `chromaprogramgetdefault_config` function symbol is also available (e.g. via a
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] `#define` in the generated header), but it’s safer to use the correct function.
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] `vidbit.pio`
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] - The line‑by‑line timing appears carefully calculated: hsync 786 cycles, back porch 964, active
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] 8642, front porch 258, summing to 10650 cycles – but the comment says 10656. The final `[12]` in
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] `jmp x--,line` plus the three instructions before `.wrap` probably account for the 6‑cycle
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] difference.
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] - The vsync pulse train is generated by a separate small state machine (`pulse`, `phase`) that
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] counts down `x` edge‑widths. The `out y,31` loads the pulse width from FIFO, and the inner `phase`
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] loop generates that many cycles of output high. This is elegant because it allows arbitrary vsync
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] pulse patterns.
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] C++ Host Code (`vidbit.cpp`)
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] Chroma DMA ring size
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] The most significant potential issue is in `chromadmainit`:
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] channel_config_set_ring(&c, true, 9);
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] dma_channel_configure(chroma_dma_chan, &c, &pio->txf[sm], NULL, colorWords*2, false);
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] With `colorWords = 120` and `colorWords*2 = 240` words (= 960 bytes), a ring size of 9 bits means
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] the DMA address wraps after 512 bytes. The transfer will read the first 128 words (512 bytes), then
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] wrap and repeat those same 128 words for the remaining 112 transfers. The last 112 words of
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] `colorWord` are never sent. This is almost certainly unintended.
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] If you want a continuous ring with no wrapping, use `ring = 10` (1024 bytes boundary) – but then
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] the buffer of 960 bytes is smaller than the ring size, so no wrap occurs; the DMA will read
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] addresses 0…959 correctly, but after wrapping (address 1024) it would go out of bounds. For a
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] finite transfer count, ring is unnecessary. If you intended the chroma pattern to loop
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] indefinitely, you should set the transfer count to an infinite value (e.g.,
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] `dmachannelsettranscount(chromadmachan, UINT32_MAX, false)`) and use a ring
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] size that exactly matches the buffer length (e.g., `ring = 10` if buffer is 1024 bytes, or ring = 9
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] for 512 bytes). Given `colorWords=120`, you could reduce it to `colorWords=64` to fit a 512‑byte
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] ring, or increase the buffer to 1024 bytes and use ring=10.
2026-05-07 18:57:27 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] Chroma data generation in `initChroma()`
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] The `sine[8]` table defines phases 0‑3 repeated (0,1,3,2,0,1,3,2). The inner loop builds `even` and
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] `odd` words. The expression `phase = ((i * 16 + b) + redphaseoffset) % 4` implies that for
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] each pixel clock (each 8‑bit word?) the phase is constant across all bits of that word? Actually
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] `i` indexes words, `b` loops through bits 0‑15, and `sine[phase]` selects a 2‑bit value per bit.
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] This suggests two 2‑bit samples per pixel bit? The intended mapping might need clarification – but
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] it’s consistent with 4× subcarrier.
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] Missing definitions
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] The code references `evenWords`, `oddWords`, `evenFrame`, `oddFrame` which are presumably defined
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] in `monobit.h`. Also `evenLines`, `oddLines` used in `initTeletext()`. These are not shown here;
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] likely they define the frame buffer dimensions.
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] General correctness
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] - The `WST` (World System Teletext) integration appears correct: Hamming 8/4 encoding, parity bits,
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] packet structure. The `wstEven` and `wstOdd` arrays are dimensioned `[wstWords]` and
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] `[wstWords-1]`, which matches the two fields (309 vs 310 lines?).
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] - The `ReadBootSel()` function is correctly placed in non‑flash memory to avoid crashes when
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] accessing the QSPI interface.
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] - The use of `[36]`‑like delays in PIO programs to fine‑tune cycle counts is well done.
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] Suggestions
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] 1. Fix the ring size in `chromadmainit` – either remove ring or adjust buffer/ring size to
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] match.
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] 2. Correct the config function name in `chromainit` from
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] `wstprogramgetdefaultconfig` to `chromaprogramgetdefault_config`.
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] 3. Consider adding comments in the PIO programs to document what the `?` cycle is (e.g., `jmp`
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] taken = 2 cycles).
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] 4. The main loop’s stall‑flag check currently toggles the LED on any stall. This might be too
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] coarse for debugging; consider counting stalls instead and printing only when excessive.
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] Overall this is an impressive implementation that leverages PIO and DMA effectively for real‑time
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] video generation. The code is readable and well‑structured. Thank you for sharing it.
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 18:57:28 [deepseek-v4] If you have any specific questions about the algorithms or timing, feel free to ask.
