2026-05-07 19:00:53 [deepseek-v4] A chroma word is 32 bits, which at 4× subcarrier (17.734475 MHz) gives 16 two‑bit samples per word
2026-05-07 19:00:53 [deepseek-v4] (each sample is 2 bits for the DAC). With 128 such words you get 128 × 16 = 2048 samples.
2026-05-07 19:00:53 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 19:00:53 [deepseek-v4] The active line is approximately 51.9 µs long. At 17.734475 MHz that’s about
2026-05-07 19:00:53 [deepseek-v4] 51.9 µs × 17.734 MHz ≈ 920 samples. So yes, 128 words (2048 samples) is more than enough for one
2026-05-07 19:00:53 [deepseek-v4] line.
2026-05-07 19:00:53 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 19:00:53 [deepseek-v4] However, the current `chromadmainit` has a ring‑size mismatch (`ring = 9` wraps at 512
2026-05-07 19:00:53 [deepseek-v4] bytes) and `colorWord[1]` is not initialised, so the output won’t be correct. If you fix those
2026-05-07 19:00:53 [deepseek-v4] issues, a 128‑word buffer is fine.
