2026-05-07 19:05:45 [deepseek-v4] • Explanation*:
2026-05-07 19:05:45 [deepseek-v4] - With ring size 10, the DMA’s internal address wraps after every 1024 bytes. Your buffer is
2026-05-07 19:05:45 [deepseek-v4] exactly 1024 bytes, so the DMA will cycle through indices 0–127 (even line) then 128–255 (odd line)
2026-05-07 19:05:45 [deepseek-v4] repeatedly.
2026-05-07 19:05:45 [deepseek-v4] - The PIO waits for irq 4 each line, reads the first word as repeat count, then outputs the next
2026-05-07 19:05:45 [deepseek-v4] 127 words. The ring ensures that after one line’s data is consumed, the address wraps gracefully
2026-05-07 19:05:45 [deepseek-v4] for the next line.
2026-05-07 19:05:45 [deepseek-v4] - Because the transfer count is `UINT32_MAX`, the DMA never stops; it will keep feeding the PIO as
2026-05-07 19:05:45 [deepseek-v4] long as the PIO requests data.
2026-05-07 19:05:45 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 19:05:45 [deepseek-v4] One more thing: ensure `colorWord[1]` is initialised (currently it’s not). The PIO will output it
2026-05-07 19:05:45 [deepseek-v4] as part of the burst? The first word consumed is repeat count (index 0), then it outputs words from
2026-05-07 19:05:45 [deepseek-v4] index 1 onward. You may want to set `colorWord[1]` to something meaningful (e.g., burst
2026-05-07 19:05:45 [deepseek-v4] continuation). If you leave it uninitialised, it will be random. Consider initialising all words.
