2026-05-07 19:14:19 [nitro@ryzen5] can you check colorburst timing, image displayed is still monochrome, was hoping id would be red
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] To get your RP2350 outputting a red PAL image instead of monochrome, we need to address a few
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] critical timing and phase alignment issues. The TV's "color killer" circuit is currently disabling
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] color decoding because the subcarrier frequency and the PAL burst sequence do not match the
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] expected analog standards.
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is a breakdown of the specific areas causing the monochrome output and how to fix them.
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. PIO Loop Timing & Clock Divisor
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your comment in `chroma.pio` states: `Loop: 4 cycles/bit (1 out + 1 delay + 1 jmp + 1 ?) = 4 cycles
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] per 90-degree phase`.
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] However, the PIO code is currently executing in 3 cycles:
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] loop:
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out	pins, 2 [1]  ; 1 cycle out + 1 cycle delay = 2
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp	x-- loop     ; 1 cycle jmp = 1
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro]                      ; Total = 3 cycles
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because `clkdiv` is set to `9.3885` (17.734475 MHz, which is exactly 4 × f_sc), your loop outputs a
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] phase every 3 cycles instead of 4, dropping the subcarrier frequency to ~1.47 MHz.
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] To achieve 4 cycles per phase, you should increase the delay by 1, and speed up the PIO clock to 16
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] × f_sc (70.9379 MHz) so that 4 PIO cycles equal exactly one 90-degree phase (56.38 ns).
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. DMA Consumption & Word Count (`x`)
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] Currently, `colorWord[0]` is set to `colorWords - 1` (127). This means `x = 127`, so the PIO loop
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] runs 128 times, consuming 256 bits (8 words) per scanline.
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because the DMA ring is 256 words long, the PIO will fall out of sync on the second scanline by
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] reading active pixel data as the next `x` loop counter.
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] A PAL scanline requires color for the back porch plus active video (~57.7μ s). At 4.4336 MHz, this
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] is exactly 64 words of data (64 × 16 \text phases = 1024 \text phases). If we set `x =
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1023`, the PIO will consume exactly 64 words per line.
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3. Hex Literal for the Burst
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] The value `0x23102310` places your desired sequence (2, 3, 1, 0) into hex nibbles. However, `out
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] pins, 2` shifts out raw 2-bit pairs. To output the levels `0, 2, 3, 1` sequentially, the bits must
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] be packed as `01 11 10 00` in binary, which is `0x78` per byte, or `0x78787878`.
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 4. PAL Burst Alternation
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] PAL (Phase Alternating Line) requires the color burst phase to swing \pm 45^\circ on alternate
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] lines. You are currently using the exact same burst for both lines. The TV detects this lack of
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] alternation and disables color. Shifting the sequence by one 90-degree phase on alternate lines
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] creates the required 90-degree difference.
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Fixes
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is the corrected `chroma.pio` file:
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] .program chroma
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] start:
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wait	1 irq 4
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	irq	clear 4
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out	x, 32
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp	!x start [15]
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] loop:
2026-05-07 19:19:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out	pins, 2 [2]     ; 1 out + 2 delay = 3 cycles
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp	x-- loop        ; 1 jmp = 1 cycle. Total = 4 cycles per phase
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	pins, 0
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] .wrap
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] And the corrected C++ initialization logic:
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] // 64 words perfectly covers the ~57.7µs back porch + active video
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] const int colorWords = 64;
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] // 128 words total = 512 bytes (requires DMA ring size 9)
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] alignas(512) uint32_t colorWord[colorWords * 2];
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] void initChroma() {
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	const int red_phase_offset = 1;
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	const uint32_t sine[8] = {0, 2, 3, 1, 0, 2, 3, 1};
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Pack 2-bit pairs for the DAC. 0,2,3,1 = 01 11 10 00 = 0x78
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	const uint32_t burstEven = 0x78787878;
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Shift by 90 degrees (1 phase) for the alternate line. 2,3,1,0 = 00 01 11 10 = 0x1E
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	const uint32_t burstOdd  = 0x1E1E1E1E;
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// --- Even Line ---
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	colorWord[0] = 1023; // Loop 1024 times (consumes exactly 64 words)
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	colorWord[1] = 0;    // Breezeway
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	colorWord[2] = burstEven;
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	colorWord[3] = burstEven;
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// --- Odd Line ---
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	colorWord[colorWords] = 1023;
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	colorWord[colorWords + 1] = 0;
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	colorWord[colorWords + 2] = burstOdd;
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	colorWord[colorWords + 3] = burstOdd;
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	for(int i = 4; i < colorWords; i++) {
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		uint32_t even = 0;
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		uint32_t odd = 0;
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		for(int b = 0; b < 16; b++) {
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			int phase = ((i * 16 + b) + red_phase_offset) % 4;
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			even |= (sine[phase] << (b * 2));
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			// +2 shifts by 180 degrees, which correctly inverts the V-axis for PAL Red
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			odd |= (sine[phase + 2] << (b * 2));
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		}
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		colorWord[i] = even;
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		colorWord[i + colorWords] = odd;
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	}
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] void chroma_dma_init(PIO pio, int sm) {
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	chroma_dma_chan = dma_claim_unused_channel(true);
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	dma_channel_config c = dma_channel_get_default_config(chroma_dma_chan);
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	channel_config_set_transfer_data_size(&c, DMA_SIZE_32);
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	channel_config_set_read_increment(&c, true);
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	channel_config_set_write_increment(&c, false);
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	channel_config_set_dreq(&c, pio_get_dreq(pio, sm, true));
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Ring size 9 = 512 bytes = 128 words. Matches colorWord array exactly.
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	channel_config_set_ring(&c, true, 9);
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	dma_channel_configure(chroma_dma_chan, &c, &pio->txf[sm], NULL, UINT32_MAX, false);
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	dma_channel_set_read_addr(chroma_dma_chan, colorWord, true);
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] bool chroma_init(PIO pio, int sm) {
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	const pio_program_t *program5 = &chroma_program;
2026-05-07 19:19:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	if (!pio_can_add_program(pio, program5)) return false;
