2026-05-07 19:20:59 [nitro@ryzen5] 
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] I see you’ve updated the clock divider to `2.3471f` (producing ~70.94 MHz, i.e. 16× f_sc), which is
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] correct. However, the image remains monochrome because several other critical issues from the
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] original design have not been addressed. Below is a checklist of the remaining problems that must
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] be fixed to get a correct PAL colour burst and eventually a red picture.
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] 1. PIO loop timing (still 3 cycles per phase)
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] Your `chroma.pio` file (not shown in the latest snippet) likely still has the original 3‑cycle
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] loop:
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] loop:
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] 	out	pins, 2 [1]      ; 1+1 = 2 cycles
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] 	jmp	x-- loop         ; 1 cycle = total 3 cycles
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] To obtain exactly 4 cycles per 90° phase (required for a 4.43361875 MHz subcarrier when
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] running at 16× f_sc), you must insert one extra delay:
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] loop:
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] 	out	pins, 2 [2]      ; 1+2 = 3 cycles
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] 	jmp	x-- loop         ; 1 cycle = total 4 cycles
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] 2. Burst hex packing is inverted
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] `burst = 0x23102310` produces the wrong 2‑bit levels on the DAC pins.
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] `out pins, 2` shifts bits LSB first – the two‑bit pairs are taken from the lowest two bits
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] of the word first.
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] For the PAL colour burst you need the sequence `(0, 2, 3, 1)` for the even field and `(2, 3, 1, 0)`
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] for the odd field (swapped by 90°).
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] In binary:
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] - `0` → `00`
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] - `2` → `10`
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] - `3` → `11`
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] - `1` → `01`
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] Packed LSB‑first, the repeating 4‑phase cycle `0,2,3,1` becomes:
2026-05-07 19:22:28 [deepseek-v4] `00 10 11 01` = `0x2B` per byte → `0x2B2B2B2B` as a 32‑bit word.
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] Similarly, the 90°‑shifted cycle `2,3,1,0` = `10 11 01 00` = `0x1E1E1E1E`.
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] Correct your `initChroma`:
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] const uint32_t burstEven = 0x2B2B2B2B;  // 0,2,3,1
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] const uint32_t burstOdd  = 0x1E1E1E1E;  // 2,3,1,0
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] 3. DMA ring buffer size mismatch
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] You set `channelconfigset_ring(&c, true, 10);` which wraps the DMA read address modulo 1024
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] bytes (256 words).
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] But your array `colorWord` has `colorWords*2 = 256` words, which is exactly 1024 bytes.
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] However, the DMA will now transfer `UINT32_MAX` words (4 billion) without a transfer count – it
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] relies on the ring to loop forever. That’s fine, but the count inside each `colorWord[0]`
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] and `colorWord[colorWords]` must be set so that the PIO consumes exactly 64 words per
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] scanline (to leave room for the next IRQ‑triggered restart).
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] Your current count `(colorWords-1)16-1` with `colorWords=128` yields `2031` – far too large. It
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] should be `(6416)-1 = 1023` (because the PIO consumes 16 phases per word, and you need 64 words
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] per scanline).
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] Change:
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] const int colorWords = 64;   // 64 words = 128 uint32_t in array
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] alignas(512) uint32_t colorWord[colorWords * 2];
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] void initChroma(){
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4]     int count = (colorWords * 16) - 1;  // = 1023
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4]     // ...
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4]     colorWord[0] = count;
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4]     colorWord[colorWords] = count;
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] And adjust the DMA ring size to match the new array size (128 words = 512 bytes):
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] channel_config_set_ring(&c, true, 9);   // wrap modulo 512 bytes
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] 4. No alternation of burst phase per field
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] Your `colorWord[]` contains the same burst for both even and odd lines. PAL requires the
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] burst phase to alternate by ±45° (i.e. 90° shift) on each successive line. Since your video frame
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] DMA hands you a new field every VBI, you must swap between the “even burst” and “odd burst”
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] sections of the array.
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] In `vid_field()`, after setting the video DMA address, force the chroma DMA to start at the correct
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] half:
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] void vid_field(int field) {
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4]     if (field & 1) {
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4]         dma_channel_set_read_addr(chroma_dma_chan, &colorWord[colorWords], false);
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4]     } else {
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4]         dma_channel_set_read_addr(chroma_dma_chan, colorWord, false);
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4]     }
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4]     // ... rest of your field setup
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] }
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] Then let the DMA ring automatically wrap within the 128‑word array.
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-05-07 19:22:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 19:22:30 [deepseek-v4] 5. (Minor) IRQ synchronisation delay
2026-05-07 19:22:30 [deepseek-v4] In `chroma.pio`, after the `wait 1 irq 4` you immediately clear the IRQ and read `x`. This is fine,
2026-05-07 19:22:30 [deepseek-v4] but ensure the `vidbit.pio` sends the IRQ after the back porch starts, i.e. exactly when
2026-05-07 19:22:30 [deepseek-v4] the chroma should begin. Your `vidbit.pio` already does `irq set 4` right after hsync ends – that’s
2026-05-07 19:22:30 [deepseek-v4] correct.
2026-05-07 19:22:30 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 19:22:30 [deepseek-v4] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-05-07 19:22:30 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 19:22:30 [deepseek-v4] 6. Verify the analog combination network
2026-05-07 19:22:30 [deepseek-v4] Your resistor network combines LUMA and two chroma pins into a single composite video output. The
2026-05-07 19:22:30 [deepseek-v4] levels you generate on the chroma pins (00,01,10,11) must produce a 300 mV‑pp colour subcarrier on
2026-05-07 19:22:30 [deepseek-v4] top of the luma.
2026-05-07 19:22:30 [deepseek-v4] With a 3.3V supply and the values shown (chroma330 = 330Ω, chroma100 = 100Ω, luma150 = 150Ω, output
2026-05-07 19:22:30 [deepseek-v4] 75Ω), the maximum chroma amplitude is around 0.6 Vpp – fine for PAL. But double‑check that the two
2026-05-07 19:22:30 [deepseek-v4] pins are not swapped and that the reconstruction filter (68pF) is included.
2026-05-07 19:22:30 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 19:22:30 [deepseek-v4] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-05-07 19:22:30 [deepseek-v4] 
