2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] Thank you for sharing your source files. It is always fascinating to see how the Raspberry Pi
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] Pico’s PIO and DMA subsystems are utilized for software-defined video generation.
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here are a few observations and thoughts on your implementation:
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] • PIO Synchronization and Timing*
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] The method you have chosen to synchronize the color subcarrier with the video timing is very
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] effective. By having the `vidbit` state machine assert IRQ 4 during the back porch, and the
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] `chroma` state machine wait on that specific IRQ, you ensure the strict phase alignment required
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] for the PAL color burst. The cycle-counting in the PIO assembly is precisely documented, which
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] makes maintaining the 10656 cycles per line highly traceable.
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Hardware and DMA Utilization*
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] You are making excellent use of the RP2350 hardware. Offloading the heavy lifting to DMA channels
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] combined with PIO FIFOs ensures the ARM cores are free to handle higher-level logic (like audio
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] generation and keyboard scanning). The use of a DMA ring buffer for the chroma subcarrier, combined
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] with the `alignas(1024)` directive, is a robust way to guarantee the DMA read pointer wraps
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] correctly without CPU intervention.
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] • PAL Phase Alternation*
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your `initChroma` function correctly handles the alternating phase requirements of the PAL signal
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] (the swinging burst and V-axis inversion) by pre-calculating the phase offsets into the `colorWord`
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] buffer. Approximating the sine wave via a 2-pin DAC (with 00, 01, 10, 11 states) and a
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] reconstruction filter is a classic and efficient technique for microcontroller-based composite
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] video.
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] • World System Teletext (WST)*
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] Including a WST implementation is a highly distinct feature. Your implementation of the Hamming 8/4
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] encoding and the odd parity generation complies nicely with the teletext specification.
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] Pre-calculating the Hamming values into a lookup table is a sensible optimization to save CPU
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] cycles during packet construction.
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] • RP2350 Specifics*
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] It is also notable that your `ReadBootSel` function correctly accounts for the architectural
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] differences between the RP2040 and the RP2350, specifically checking the new QSPI pad status
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] register for the Pico 2.
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:45:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] Please let me know if you would like to discuss any specific aspect of this architecture, or if you
2026-05-08 07:45:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] require any code modifications, optimizations, or additions to your state machines.
2026-05-08 07:45:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:47:49 [nitro@ryzen5] the color burst is apparently too short? can you calculate if it begins at ok offset from porch
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] You are spot on to suspect the color burst length. Let us break down the exact timing of your PIO
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] state machines to calculate both the burst duration and its offset (the "breezeway") from the start
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] of the back porch.
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. Timing Fundamentals
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] Based on your system clock and PIO divider:
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] • System Clock: 166.5 MHz
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Chroma PIO Clock: 166.5 MHz / 2.3471 ≈ 70.938 MHz (14.1 ns per PIO cycle)
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Chroma Loop: 4 PIO cycles per 2-bit sample = 56.4 ns
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Subcarrier Cycle: 4 samples = 16 PIO cycles = 225.5 ns (4.4336 MHz, perfect for PAL)
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] • One 32-bit DMA Word: 16 samples = 64 PIO cycles ≈ 0.902 µs
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. Offset from Porch (Breezeway)
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your `vidbit` state machine triggers `irq 4` at the exact start of the back porch. The
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] `chroma` state machine catches this and incurs the following delays before outputting the first
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] word:
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `wait 1 irq 4` (1 cycle)
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `irq clear 4` (1 cycle)
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `out x, 32` (1 cycle)
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `jmp !x start [16]` (17 cycles — delay is applied even when the branch is not taken)
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] This is a 20-cycle overhead (0.282 µs).
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] After this, `chroma` outputs `colorWord[1]`, which is `0` (blanking), taking exactly 1 word
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] duration (0.902 µs).
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Total Breezeway = 0.282 µs + 0.902 µs = 1.184 µs*.
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] • PAL Standard:* 0.9 µs (± 0.1 µs). Your burst starts slightly late.
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] • The Fix: If you remove the `[16]` delay from the `jmp` instruction in `chroma.pio`, you
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] save 16 cycles (225 ns). Your new breezeway becomes 0.958 µs*, which is almost perfectly
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] aligned with the PAL specification.
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3. Burst Duration
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] You are currently outputting two words of burst:
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `colorWord[2] = burst` (4 subcarrier cycles)
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `colorWord[3] = burst` (4 subcarrier cycles)
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:49:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Total Burst Length = 8 cycles = 1.80 µs*.
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] • PAL Standard:* 10 cycles ± 1 cycle (2.25 µs). Your burst is indeed too short by 2 cycles.
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 4. Back Porch Blanking Continuation
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] After the burst, the back porch must remain blanked until active video starts. The total PAL back
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] porch is ~5.79 µs.
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] Currently, your `initChroma` loop starts active video at `colorWord[4]`, which occurs just 3.1 µs
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] after the start of the back porch. This means you are outputting active subcarrier during
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] the remainder of the back porch, which will confuse monitors.
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Solution
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] To achieve a perfectly timed breezeway, a 10-cycle burst, and proper back porch blanking, you can
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] adjust your `colorWord` array to span exactly the right number of words before active video begins.
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. Update `chroma.pio` to remove the `[16]` delay:
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] start:
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wait	1 irq 4
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	irq	clear 4
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out	x, 32
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp	!x start      ; Removed [16] delay here
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. Update `initChroma()` to stretch the burst to 2.5 words (10 cycles) and pad the rest of
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] the back porch with zeroes:
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] void initChroma(){
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	int count=(colorWords-1)*16-1;
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	const int red_phase_offset=1;
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	const uint32_t sine[8] = {0, 1, 3, 2, 0 , 1, 3, 2};
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Word 1: Breezeway (0.902 µs)
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Words 2, 3: Full burst (8 cycles)
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Word 4: Half burst (2 cycles) + Half blanking
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Words 5, 6: Remaining back porch blanking
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	colorWord[0] = count;
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	colorWord[1] = 0;
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	colorWord[2] = burst;
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	colorWord[3] = burst;
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	colorWord[4] = burst & 0xFFFF; // Lower 16 bits = 2 subcarrier cycles, upper 16 = 0
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	colorWord[5] = 0;
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	colorWord[6] = 0;
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	colorWord[colorWords] = count;
2026-05-08 07:49:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	colorWord[colorWords+1] = 0;
