2026-05-08 07:53:52 [nitro@ryzen5] latest, still just black and white image
2026-05-08 07:56:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] The removal of the `[16]` delay has perfectly aligned your breezeway, but the persisting
2026-05-08 07:56:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] black-and-white image points to two classic analog video hurdles: the PAL swinging burst
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] requirement and DC-coupling offsets.
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is a breakdown of why the TV's color killer is still engaging and how to adjust your synthesis
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] to fix it.
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. The Missing PAL "Swinging Burst"
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] In your current `initChroma`, the `burst` constant is fixed (`0x23102310`) for both even and odd
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] lines.
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] A standard NTSC TV expects a fixed burst phase, but a PAL TV strictly requires the burst
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] phase to swing by ±45° relative to the -U axis on every alternating line (135° then 225°). If the
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] TV detects a fixed phase, its color killer circuit will assume the signal is either NTSC or noisy
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] monochrome, and it will disable color decoding.
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. The Back Porch DC Offset
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because your `CHROMA100` and `CHROMA330` pins are DC-coupled to the video output through resistors,
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] any sequence you output directly adds to the video line's DC voltage.
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • During blanking, your PIO sets the pins to `00` (0V).
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Your current sine wave uses states `0, 1, 3, 2`. The average DC value of this sequence is heavily
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] weighted towards 3.3V.
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • When the burst fires, it creates a massive +0.6V "bump" on the back porch. The TV uses the back
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] porch to clamp its black level. This massive bump likely exceeds the white level, causing the TV's
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] Automatic Gain Control (AGC) to completely clip or ignore the burst.
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Solution: A Balanced, Alternating Sequence
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] We can solve both problems simultaneously by changing the chroma waveform to a balanced sequence:
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] `0, 1, 1, 0`.
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] This sequence has a much lower DC average (minimizing the back porch bump) and a more standard
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] peak-to-peak amplitude.
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] By defining two different burst words that are shifted by 90° (`0x00110011` and `0x10011001`), they
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] become perfectly symmetric around the mathematical average. The TV's PLL will lock to this average
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] as the -U axis, satisfying the ±45° swing requirement perfectly.
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is the updated `initChroma()` function to implement the swinging burst and balanced active
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] video:
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] const uint32_t burst_even = 0x00110011; // Phase 1: 1, 1, 0, 0
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] const uint32_t burst_odd  = 0x10011001; // Phase 2: 1, 0, 0, 1
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] const int colorWords = 128;
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] alignas(1024) uint32_t colorWord[colorWords*2];
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] void initChroma() {
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	int count = (colorWords - 1) * 16 - 1;
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	const int red_phase_offset = 1;
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Balanced low-DC-offset sine wave: 0, 1, 1, 0
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	const uint32_t sine[8] = {0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 1, 1, 0};
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// --- EVEN LINES ---
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	colorWord[0] = count;
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	colorWord[1] = 0;                   // 0.9us Breezeway
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	colorWord[2] = burst_even;          // Burst (4 cycles)
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	colorWord[3] = burst_even;          // Burst (4 cycles)
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	colorWord[4] = burst_even & 0xffff; // Burst (2 cycles) + Blanking
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	colorWord[5] = 0;                   // Back porch blanking
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	colorWord[6] = 0;                   // Back porch blanking
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// --- ODD LINES ---
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	colorWord[colorWords] = count;
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	colorWord[colorWords+1] = 0;
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	colorWord[colorWords+2] = burst_odd;
2026-05-08 07:56:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	colorWord[colorWords+3] = burst_odd;
2026-05-08 07:56:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	colorWord[colorWords+4] = burst_odd & 0xffff;
2026-05-08 07:56:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	colorWord[colorWords+5] = 0;
2026-05-08 07:56:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	colorWord[colorWords+6] = 0;
2026-05-08 07:56:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:56:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// --- ACTIVE VIDEO ---
2026-05-08 07:56:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	for(int i = 7; i < colorWords; i++) {
2026-05-08 07:56:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		uint32_t even = 0;
2026-05-08 07:56:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		uint32_t odd = 0;
2026-05-08 07:56:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		for(int b = 0; b < 16; b++) {
2026-05-08 07:56:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			int phase = ((i * 16 + b) + red_phase_offset) % 4;
2026-05-08 07:56:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			even |= (sine[phase] << (b * 2));
2026-05-08 07:56:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:56:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			// V-axis inversion for PAL: shift by 2 (180 degrees).
2026-05-08 07:56:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			// Because our -U axis is mathematically at 0 degrees relative
2026-05-08 07:56:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			// to the burst, a 180-degree shift correctly reflects the phase!
2026-05-08 07:56:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			odd |= (sine[phase+2] << (b * 2));
2026-05-08 07:56:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		}
2026-05-08 07:56:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		colorWord[i] = even;
2026-05-08 07:56:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		colorWord[i+colorWords] = odd;
2026-05-08 07:56:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	}
2026-05-08 07:56:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-05-08 07:56:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:56:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-08 07:56:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] Why this works with your DMA Ring
2026-05-08 07:56:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your DMA is configured with `channelconfigset_ring(&c, true, 10)`, which creates a
2026-05-08 07:56:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1024-byte (256-word) wrap boundary. Because your `chroma.pio` consumes exactly 128 words per line
2026-05-08 07:56:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] (1 count word + 127 data words), the DMA pointer will naturally advance to the odd-line data on the
2026-05-08 07:56:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] second line, and wrap cleanly back to the even-line data on the third line. It is an incredibly
2026-05-08 07:56:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] elegant way to handle PAL phase alternation without CPU overhead.
2026-05-08 07:56:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
