2026-05-08 10:26:09 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:26:09 [gpt-5.5-2026] Other notable changes
2026-05-08 10:26:09 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:26:09 [gpt-5.5-2026] - You now have `WST` and `CHROMA` both disabled by default.
2026-05-08 10:26:09 [gpt-5.5-2026] - `viddmainit(VideoStateMachine, true)` forces interlace mode currently.
2026-05-08 10:26:09 [gpt-5.5-2026] - `pal576i.cpp` now contains the interlaced preamble and separate `evenFrame`/`oddFrame`, which is
2026-05-08 10:26:09 [gpt-5.5-2026] cleaner than the earlier single-file layout.
2026-05-08 10:26:09 [gpt-5.5-2026] - `chroma.pio` comment still mentions `clkdiv=9.3885`, but the code uses `2.3471f`. The code value
2026-05-08 10:26:09 [gpt-5.5-2026] makes sense because the PIO instruction loop itself divides by 4 to get the 4× subcarrier sample
2026-05-08 10:26:09 [gpt-5.5-2026] rate.
2026-05-08 10:26:09 [gpt-5.5-2026] - LittleFS config looks inconsistent: `PICOFLASHSIZEBYTES` is set to 1 MB, but
2026-05-08 10:26:09 [gpt-5.5-2026] `FSBASEADDRESS=0x10200000` is 2 MB into XIP space, and `blockcount=512` is 2 MB of
2026-05-08 10:26:09 [gpt-5.5-2026] filesystem. That is separate from video, but worth checking before using flash storage.
2026-05-08 10:26:09 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:26:09 [gpt-5.5-2026] The immediate chroma checklist would be:
2026-05-08 10:26:09 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:26:09 [gpt-5.5-2026] 1. Re-enable `#define CHROMA`.
2026-05-08 10:26:09 [gpt-5.5-2026] 2. Put chroma on the same PIO block as video, preferably `pio0` SM2/SM3.
2026-05-08 10:26:09 [gpt-5.5-2026] 3. Keep I2S/PS2 elsewhere.
2026-05-08 10:26:09 [gpt-5.5-2026] 4. Then re-test color burst.
2026-05-08 10:26:09 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:27:19 [nitro@ryzen5] The RP2350's PIO IRQ system allows state machines (SMs) to synchronize with each other and the main CPU, featuring 8 interrupt flags per PIO block, with 0-3 acting as local flags and 4-7 acting as global flags
2026-05-08 10:28:43 [gpt-5.5-2026] You are right — thanks for the correction. On RP2350, using IRQ 4 as a global PIO IRQ means the
2026-05-08 10:28:43 [gpt-5.5-2026] `pio0` video SM can legitimately wake the `pio1` chroma SM. My earlier “wrong PIO block” diagnosis
2026-05-08 10:28:43 [gpt-5.5-2026] was too RP2040-minded.
2026-05-08 10:28:43 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] With that corrected, I would now put the likely causes in this order:
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] 1. In the latest shared file, `CHROMA` is disabled
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] The current `vidbit.cpp` has:
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] //#define CHROMA
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] So this build will definitely be monochrome. I assume that may just be your current test state, but
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] worth calling out first.
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] 2. Chroma GPIOs are not initialised or driven as outputs
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] Inside `chroma_init()` you configure the SM pin mappings:
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] - `smconfigsetsetpins(...)`
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] - `smconfigsetoutpins(...)`
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] But I do not see:
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] - `piogpioinit(pio, CHROMA100_PIN)`
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] - `piogpioinit(pio, CHROMA330_PIN)`
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] - output direction setup for those two pins
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] Your `chroma.pio` only does:
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] out	pins, 2 [2]
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] ...
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] set	pins, 0
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] It never does `set pindirs`. So unless the pin directions are set from C, the chroma pins can
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] remain as GPIO/SIO inputs rather than PIO-driven outputs.
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] This alone would give exactly: good luma image, no colour, no visible chroma.
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] So after re-enabling `CHROMA`, make sure GP20/GP21 are switched to the relevant PIO function and
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] their PIO directions are set output.
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] 3. The chroma line loop is much too long with `colorWords = 128`
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] This is a big timing issue.
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] Your chroma clocking is effectively:
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] - system clock: `166.5 MHz`
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] - PIO clkdiv: `2.3471`
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] - chroma PIO clock: about `70.94 MHz`
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] - output loop: 4 PIO cycles per 2-bit sample
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] - effective chroma sample rate: about `17.734 MHz`, i.e. `4 × fsc`
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] - one 32-bit word = 16 samples = 4 colour subcarrier cycles
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] - one chroma data word duration: about `0.902 µs`
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] Current count:
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] int count = (colorWords - 1) * 16 - 1;
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] With `colorWords = 128`, that means the PIO outputs:
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:44 [gpt-5.5-2026] - `127 × 16 = 2032` samples
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] - duration: `2032 / 17.734475 MHz ≈ 114.6 µs`
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] But one PAL line is only:
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] - `10656 / 166.5 MHz = 64.0 µs`
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] So the chroma SM is outputting for about 1.79 lines after every burst trigger. It will not
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] be back at `wait 1 irq 4` in time for the next line’s burst. It will see a stale global IRQ later,
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] clear it, and start the next chroma sequence badly displaced in the line.
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] That is very likely to keep a TV’s colour PLL / colour killer from accepting the signal.
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] For reference:
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] - back porch + active luma is about `5.79 + 51.90 = 57.69 µs`
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] - at 4× PAL subcarrier, that is about `1023 samples`
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] - `1023 samples / 16 ≈ 64 words`
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] So your chroma payload should be closer to 64 data words, not 127 data words.
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] A clean first test would be to make the chroma per-line stride roughly 64 words total, or 1 count
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] word plus 63-ish data words, then use a 128-word ring for even/odd alternation. That will slightly
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] shorten chroma at the far right edge, but it should let the burst happen on every line at the
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] correct time.
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] If you want full active width, 1 count word + 64 data words is closer, but then the line stride
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] becomes 65 words, which no longer fits the simple power-of-two DMA ring alternation scheme. That
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] would need a different DMA wrap/chaining layout.
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] 4. Your burst offset and length are now basically reasonable
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] With the `[16]` removed from `chroma.pio`, the burst timing looks much better.
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] Approximate timing from `irq set 4` at start of back porch:
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] - wait/clear/out/jmp overhead: about `4 chroma PIO cycles ≈ 0.056 µs`
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] - `colorWord[1] = 0`: one word blank = `0.902 µs`
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] So first burst word begins at roughly:
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] - `0.056 + 0.902 = 0.958 µs`
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] That is a good PAL breezeway.
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] Burst length:
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] - `colorWord[2]`
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] - `colorWord[3]`
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] - low half of `colorWord[4]`
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] That gives:
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] - `4 + 4 + 2 = 10` subcarrier cycles
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] Also good.
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] So I would not focus on burst length now. The burst is probably correctly sized, but either not
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] being output at all, or not being output once per line in the right place due to the too-long
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] chroma loop.
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] 5. Summary of likely fixes
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] In priority order:
2026-05-08 10:28:45 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:46 [gpt-5.5-2026] 1. Re-enable `CHROMA`.
2026-05-08 10:28:46 [gpt-5.5-2026] 2. Initialise GP20/GP21 for the chroma PIO.
2026-05-08 10:28:46 [gpt-5.5-2026] 3. Set GP20/GP21 PIO pin directions to output.
2026-05-08 10:28:46 [gpt-5.5-2026] 4. Reduce chroma line output from 127 data words to about 63–64 data words.
2026-05-08 10:28:46 [gpt-5.5-2026] 5. Keep IRQ 4; your RP2350 global IRQ usage is valid.
2026-05-08 10:28:46 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:28:46 [gpt-5.5-2026] The most suspicious combination is currently: chroma pins not driven + chroma line longer than
2026-05-08 10:28:46 [gpt-5.5-2026] a PAL line. Either one can produce monochrome; together they almost certainly will.
2026-05-08 10:28:46 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-08 10:30:32 [nitro@ryzen5] hi kimi , what does 	sm_config_set_out_pins(&config5, CHROMA100_PIN, 2); do exactly
2026-05-08 10:31:13 [kimi-k2.6] `smconfigsetoutpins(&config5, CHROMA100_PIN, 2)` tells the PIO state machine which
2026-05-08 10:31:13 [kimi-k2.6] physical GPIO pins to drive when executing an `out pins, N` instruction.
2026-05-08 10:31:13 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:31:13 [kimi-k2.6] Specifically for your case:
2026-05-08 10:31:13 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:31:13 [kimi-k2.6] ╭───────────────┬────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-05-08 10:31:13 [kimi-k2.6] │  Parameter    │  Value                 │  Meaning                                                      │
2026-05-08 10:31:13 [kimi-k2.6] ├───────────────┼────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-05-08 10:31:13 [kimi-k2.6] │  `out_base`   │  `CHROMA100_PIN` (20)  │  The first GPIO pin affected is **GP20**                      │
2026-05-08 10:31:13 [kimi-k2.6] │  `out_count`  │  `2`                   │  The instruction spans **2 consecutive pins**: GP20 and GP21  │
2026-05-08 10:31:13 [kimi-k2.6] ╰───────────────┴────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-05-08 10:31:13 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:31:13 [kimi-k2.6] What it sets in hardware
2026-05-08 10:31:13 [kimi-k2.6] It writes the `PINCTRL` register fields for this state machine:
2026-05-08 10:31:13 [kimi-k2.6] • `OUT_BASE` (bits 19:15) = 20
2026-05-08 10:31:13 [kimi-k2.6] • `OUT_COUNT` (bits 24:20) = 2
2026-05-08 10:31:13 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:31:13 [kimi-k2.6] What happens at runtime
2026-05-08 10:31:13 [kimi-k2.6] When your PIO program executes:
2026-05-08 10:31:13 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:31:13 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:31:13 [kimi-k2.6] out	pins, 2 [2]
2026-05-08 10:31:13 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:31:14 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:31:14 [kimi-k2.6] The state machine takes the bottom 2 bits from the output shift register and drives them
2026-05-08 10:31:14 [kimi-k2.6] onto:
2026-05-08 10:31:14 [kimi-k2.6] • Bit 0 → GP20 (CHROMA100_PIN)
2026-05-08 10:31:14 [kimi-k2.6] • Bit 1 → GP21 (CHROMA330_PIN)
2026-05-08 10:31:14 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:31:14 [kimi-k2.6] What it does NOT do
2026-05-08 10:31:14 [kimi-k2.6] Importantly, this function only maps the destination of the `out pins` instruction. It does
2026-05-08 10:31:14 [kimi-k2.6] not:
2026-05-08 10:31:14 [kimi-k2.6] 1.  Select the pin function. You still need `piogpioinit(pio, 20)` and
2026-05-08 10:31:14 [kimi-k2.6] `piogpioinit(pio, 21)` so those pins are connected to the PIO block rather than the SIO
2026-05-08 10:31:14 [kimi-k2.6] (CPU-controlled GPIO).
2026-05-08 10:31:14 [kimi-k2.6] 2.  Set pin direction. You still need to configure those pins as outputs. You can do this
2026-05-08 10:31:14 [kimi-k2.6] from C with:
2026-05-08 10:31:14 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:31:14 [kimi-k2.6]     pio_sm_set_consecutive_pindirs(pio, sm, CHROMA100_PIN, 2, true);
2026-05-08 10:31:14 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:31:14 [kimi-k2.6]     or from PIO with a `set pindirs, 3` instruction. Your current `chroma.pio` does not contain any
2026-05-08 10:31:14 [kimi-k2.6] `pindirs` instructions, so if you have not set directions from C, GP20/GP21 may still be input pins
2026-05-08 10:31:14 [kimi-k2.6] even though the state machine is trying to drive them.
2026-05-08 10:31:14 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:31:14 [kimi-k2.6] Relationship to your other mapping
2026-05-08 10:31:14 [kimi-k2.6] You also have:
2026-05-08 10:31:14 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:31:14 [kimi-k2.6] sm_config_set_set_pins(&config5, CHROMA100_PIN, 2);
2026-05-08 10:31:14 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:31:14 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:31:14 [kimi-k2.6] This means both `out pins` and `set pins` in your PIO program affect the same two
2026-05-08 10:31:14 [kimi-k2.6] pins (GP20–GP21). So when `chroma.pio` executes `set pins, 0` to blank the output, it also drives
2026-05-08 10:31:14 [kimi-k2.6] both GP20 and GP21 low.
2026-05-08 10:31:14 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:33:03 [nitro@ryzen5] so what is missing if i want the always output
2026-05-08 10:33:04 [nitro@ryzen5] bool chroma_init(PIO pio, int sm) {	const pio_program_t *program5 = &chroma_program;	if (!pio_can_add_program(pio, program5)) return false;	uint offset5 = pio_add_program(pio, program5);	pio_sm_config config5=chroma_program_get_default_config(offset5);	sm_config_set_set_pins(&config5, CHROMA100_PIN, 2);	sm_config_set_out_pins(&config5, CHROMA100_PIN, 2);	sm_config_set_out_shift(&config5, true, true, 32);	sm_config_set_fifo_join(&config5, PIO_FIFO_JOIN_TX);	pio_sm_set_clkdiv(pio,sm,2.3471f);	int pioStatus = pio_sm_init(pio, sm, offset5, &config5);	pio_sm_set_enabled(pio, sm, true);	return true;}
2026-05-08 10:33:37 [kimi-k2.6] You are missing two things: selecting the pin function and setting the direction.
2026-05-08 10:33:37 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:33:37 [kimi-k2.6] `smconfigsetoutpins` only tells the state machine which pins to drive when
2026-05-08 10:33:37 [kimi-k2.6] an `out pins` or `set pins` instruction runs. It does not connect those pins to the PIO
2026-05-08 10:33:37 [kimi-k2.6] block, nor does it make them outputs.
2026-05-08 10:33:37 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:33:37 [kimi-k2.6] Add this before `piosminit`:
2026-05-08 10:33:37 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:33:37 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:33:37 [kimi-k2.6] pio_gpio_init(pio, CHROMA100_PIN);
2026-05-08 10:33:37 [kimi-k2.6] pio_gpio_init(pio, CHROMA330_PIN);
2026-05-08 10:33:37 [kimi-k2.6] pio_sm_set_consecutive_pindirs(pio, sm, CHROMA100_PIN, 2, true);
2026-05-08 10:33:37 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:33:37 [kimi-k2.6] 
