2026-05-08 10:38:11 [kimi-k2.6] The remaining critical problem: `colorWords = 128`
2026-05-08 10:38:11 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:38:11 [kimi-k2.6] Your chroma state machine still outputs for ~115 µs per line, but a PAL line is only 64
2026-05-08 10:38:11 [kimi-k2.6] µs. Because the chroma loop overruns the line boundary, the SM is never at `wait 1 irq 4` when
2026-05-08 10:38:11 [kimi-k2.6] the next line’s back porch fires. The colour burst ends up sprayed across random parts of the
2026-05-08 10:38:11 [kimi-k2.6] picture instead of sitting cleanly in the back porch, so the TV’s colour killer stays off.
2026-05-08 10:38:11 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:38:11 [kimi-k2.6] The arithmetic:
2026-05-08 10:38:11 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:38:11 [kimi-k2.6] • Chroma PIO clock: `166.5 MHz / 2.3471 ≈ 70.94 MHz` (14.1 ns/cycle)
2026-05-08 10:38:11 [kimi-k2.6] • One sample: 4 PIO cycles = 56.4 ns
2026-05-08 10:38:11 [kimi-k2.6] • One 32-bit word: 16 samples = 902 ns
2026-05-08 10:38:11 [kimi-k2.6] • `colorWords = 128` words per half-buffer = 115.5 µs
2026-05-08 10:38:11 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:38:11 [kimi-k2.6] PAL back porch + burst + active video + front porch together span roughly 59 µs of
2026-05-08 10:38:11 [kimi-k2.6] chroma-relevant time. You need the entire chroma burst-to-active sequence to fit inside that window
2026-05-08 10:38:11 [kimi-k2.6] so the SM is back waiting for IRQ 4 before the next line starts.
2026-05-08 10:38:11 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:38:11 [kimi-k2.6] The fix: shrink the line buffer to 64 words
2026-05-08 10:38:11 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] `64 words × 902 ns = 57.7 µs`. That is a near-perfect fit for one PAL line.
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] Because your even/odd double-buffer layout relies on a power-of-two DMA ring, you can keep the
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] scheme by changing the ring to 512 bytes (128 words total):
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] ╭─────────────────┬─────────────────┬───────────────╮
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] │  What           │  Old            │  New          │
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] ├─────────────────┼─────────────────┼───────────────┤
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] │  `colorWords`   │  `128`          │  `64`         │
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] │  DMA ring size  │  `10` (1024 B)  │  `9` (512 B)  │
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] │  Buffer words   │  `256`          │  `128`        │
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] │  Line duration  │  `115.5 µs`     │  `57.7 µs`    │
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] ╰─────────────────┴─────────────────┴───────────────╯
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] Code changes needed:
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] const int colorWords = 64;   // was 128
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] void initChroma() {
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 	int count = (colorWords - 1) * 16 - 1;   // now 1007
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 	const int red_phase_offset = 1;
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 	const uint32_t sine[8] = {0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 1, 1, 0};
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 	// Even line
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 	colorWord[0] = count;
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 	colorWord[1] = 0;
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 	colorWord[2] = burst0;
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 	colorWord[3] = burst0;
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 	colorWord[4] = burst0 & 0xffff;
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 	colorWord[5] = 0;
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 	colorWord[6] = 0;
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 	for (int i = 7; i < colorWords; i++) {   // fills words 7..63
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 		uint32_t even = 0, odd = 0;
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 		for (int b = 0; b < 16; b++) {
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 			int phase = ((i * 16 + b) + red_phase_offset) % 4;
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 			even |= (sine[phase] << (b * 2));
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 			odd  |= (sine[phase + 2] << (b * 2));
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 		}
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 		colorWord[i] = even;
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 		colorWord[i + colorWords] = odd;
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 	}
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] }
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] And in `chromadmainit`:
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 	channel_config_set_ring(&c, true, 9);   // was 10; 512-byte wrap
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] That gives you:
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] • Word 0: count
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] • Words 1–6: breezeway + burst + back porch blanking (~5.4 µs)
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] • Words 7–63: active chroma (~51.4 µs)
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] Total: 57.7 µs, well inside the 64 µs line. The state machine will finish, hit `.wrap`, and
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] be waiting at `wait 1 irq 4` with time to spare before the next line’s back porch begins.
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] If you make those two changes (`colorWords = 64`, ring = `9`, and the loop bound `i < colorWords`),
2026-05-08 10:38:12 [kimi-k2.6] the burst should finally land in the right place every line and the colour killer should unlock.
2026-05-08 10:38:13 [kimi-k2.6] 
