2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] Why `colorWords = 64` works
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] ╭───────────────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] │  Parameter                            │  Value                             │
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] ├───────────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] │  Chroma PIO clock                     │  `166.5 MHz / 2.3471 ≈ 70.94 MHz`  │
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] │  Cycles per 2-bit sample              │  `4`                               │
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] │  Cycles per 32-bit word (16 samples)  │  `64`                              │
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] │  Time per word                        │  `≈ 0.902 µs`                      │
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] │  **Words per line**                   │  **64** (1 count + 63 data)        │
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] │  **Total line duration**              │  **`64 × 0.902 ≈ 57.7 µs`**        │
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] ╰───────────────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] That leaves ~6 µs of margin inside the 64 µs line for the SM to wrap and get back to `wait 1 irq
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] 4`.
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] Important bug in your current DMA setup
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] Your existing code has:
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] channel_config_set_ring(&c, true, 10);
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] `true` means wrap the write address. Because your write address is the fixed PIO FIFO
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] register (`&pio->txf[sm]`) and write increment is disabled, the ring does nothing. The DMA read
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] pointer just runs off the end of `colorWord` into whatever follows it in RAM.
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] Use `false` to ring the read address, and size `9` for the new 512-byte (128-word)
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] buffer.
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] Progressive vs interlaced timing
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] The chroma SM sees exactly one `irq 4` per line whether you are in lace or non-lace mode. The DMA
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] ring alternates automatically because the SM consumes exactly 64 words per trigger, so the read
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] pointer lands on the next line’s count word every time. The even/odd phase tables therefore still
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] provide the correct PAL swinging burst in progressive mode.
2026-05-08 10:48:10 [kimi-k2.6] 
