2026-05-08 11:07:24 [nitro@ryzen5] i was hoping to see red pixels but colorburst is not quite working yet
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] The most likely reason you are seeing a stable monochrome picture is that the chroma state machine
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] is stalled at the very first instruction, waiting for an IRQ that never arrives on its PIO block.
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] • The issue: `irq` does not cross PIO boundaries.*
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] In `vidbit.pio` the line generator runs on PIO0 and does:
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] irq set 4
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] In `chroma.pio` your chroma generator runs on PIO1 and does:
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] wait 1 irq 4
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] On the RP2350 each PIO has its own set of 8 IRQ flags. An `irq set` on PIO0 is invisible to a state
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] machine on PIO1, so the chroma SM sits at `start` forever and never outputs the burst or colour
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] subcarrier. The FIFO fills but the pins stay at `0`, which is why the picture is perfectly stable
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] black-and-white.
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] • The fix*
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] Move the chroma SM onto PIO0 (for example SM 1) so it shares the same IRQ bank as the video
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] SM. Since `WST` is currently disabled, SM1 on PIO0 is free.
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] In `vidbit.cpp` change the two chroma calls in `main()` from `pio1` to `pio0`:
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] pass &= chroma_init(pio0, ChromaStateMachine);
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] chroma_dma_init(pio0, ChromaStateMachine);
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] And update the stall check in the main loop to watch PIO0 instead of PIO1:
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] if (pio0->fdebug & chromaStall) {
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6]     pio0->fdebug = chromaStall;
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6]     led = !led;
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] }
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] That is the minimum change needed to get the two state machines talking.
2026-05-08 11:15:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 11:15:54 [kimi-k2.6] • Secondary notes once it is running*
2026-05-08 11:15:54 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 11:15:54 [kimi-k2.6] - Clock-divider comment: The comment in `chroma.pio` mentions `clkdiv=9.3885`, but the C
2026-05-08 11:15:54 [kimi-k2.6] code uses `2.3471f`. The code is correct: with your 4-cycle loop the PIO must run at ~70.94 MHz
2026-05-08 11:15:54 [kimi-k2.6] (166.5 / 2.3471) to hit PAL f_sc. The comment appears stale.
2026-05-08 11:15:54 [kimi-k2.6] - Burst / colour amplitude: Your current `sine[]` table only produces values `0` and `1`,
2026-05-08 11:15:54 [kimi-k2.6] so the 2-bit DAC is only emitting `00` and `01`. That drives only the 100 Ω pin while the 330 Ω pin
2026-05-08 11:15:54 [kimi-k2.6] stays low. You will get a much stronger burst and saturation if you use all four DAC levels (`00`,
2026-05-08 11:15:54 [kimi-k2.6] `01`, `10`, `11`) so both pins carry the reconstruction waveform.
2026-05-08 11:15:54 [kimi-k2.6] - Interlace vs. progressive: In non-lace mode the chroma ring buffer alternates even/odd
2026-05-08 11:15:54 [kimi-k2.6] burst phases every line because the DMA just streams through the 128-word table. Most PAL decoders
2026-05-08 11:15:54 [kimi-k2.6] will still lock, but if you see Hanover-bar artefacts on a progressive frame you can double the
2026-05-08 11:15:54 [kimi-k2.6] table so the phase repeats identically on consecutive lines.
2026-05-08 11:15:54 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 11:15:54 [kimi-k2.6] With the SM moved to PIO0 the IRQ handshake should work and you should see the red tint appear
2026-05-08 11:15:54 [kimi-k2.6] across the active picture.
2026-05-08 11:15:54 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-08 11:22:39 [nitro@ryzen5] can you check the timing for start of my wst bit train
2026-05-08 11:22:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] To check the timing for the start of your WST bit train, we must look at the synchronization
2026-05-08 11:22:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] between the `vidbit` program and the `wst` program, as they share the same physical output pin and
2026-05-08 11:22:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] are coordinated via `irq 0`.
2026-05-08 11:22:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:22:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] Timing Analysis
2026-05-08 11:22:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:22:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  The Trigger (`vidbit` side):
2026-05-08 11:22:45 [gemini-3.1-lite]     In `vidbit.pio`, the `backporch` routine sets `irq 0` exactly at the start of the back porch
2026-05-08 11:22:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] period:
2026-05-08 11:22:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:22:45 [gemini-3.1-lite]     backporch:
2026-05-08 11:22:45 [gemini-3.1-lite]         set	pindirs, 0
2026-05-08 11:22:45 [gemini-3.1-lite]         set	pins, 1
2026-05-08 11:22:45 [gemini-3.1-lite]         irq	set 0      ; Trigger point
2026-05-08 11:22:45 [gemini-3.1-lite]         set	y, 28
2026-05-08 11:22:45 [gemini-3.1-lite]     backporch_loop:
2026-05-08 11:22:45 [gemini-3.1-lite]         jmp	y--,backporch_loop [31]
2026-05-08 11:22:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:22:45 [gemini-3.1-lite]     This signal is sent after the HSYNC pulse, providing the necessary lead time for the teletext
2026-05-08 11:22:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] data to begin appearing after the back porch duration.
2026-05-08 11:22:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:22:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  The Response (`wst` side):
2026-05-08 11:22:45 [gemini-3.1-lite]     In `wst.pio`, the state machine waits for that IRQ:
2026-05-08 11:22:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]     start:
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]         wait	1 irq 0    ; Synchronize
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]         irq	clear 0
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]         out	x, 32      ; Get bit count from FIFO
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]         jmp	!x start [16]
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]     loop:
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]         out	pindirs, 1 [22]
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]         ...
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] Latency Considerations
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Synchronization Delay: The `wait 1 irq 0` instruction is precise, but there is a 1-cycle
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] latency from the moment `irq set 0` is executed in the `vidbit` SM until the `wst` SM recognizes it
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] and clears it.
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Pipeline/Instruction Latency:
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   The `wst` SM executes `out x, 32` after clearing the IRQ. This is 1 cycle.
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]        The `jmp !x start [16]` adds 16 cycles of delay to ensure the data stream doesn't start
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] during* the back porch black level.
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   Total start-up overhead: ~18 cycles. At 166.5 MHz, this is approximately 108
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] nanoseconds.
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] Observations
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Bit Rate Alignment: Your `wst` program uses `out pindirs, 1 [22]`. This provides 23
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] cycles of delay + 1 cycle of execution = 24 cycles per bit.
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   166.5\text MHz / 24 = 6.9375\text Mbps.
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   This is very close to the standard WST teletext rate (6.9375 Mbps), which is excellent.
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  The "Start" Offset: If your teletext characters appear slightly shifted horizontally on
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] the screen, you should adjust the `[16]` delay in the `jmp !x start [16]` line of `wst.pio`.
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   Increasing this value will push the WST packet further to the right (later in the line).
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   Decreasing it will pull it left.
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  Potential Hazard: You are driving `pindirs` in `wst.pio` to output the data. Ensure
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] that `vidbit.pio` is not actively driving the `VID_PIN` (set to input/high-impedance) during the
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] time `wst` is transmitting, or you will have bus contention. In your current code, `vidbit` sets
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] `pindirs, 0` during the back porch, which is correct, but ensure the `active` logic in `vidbit`
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] doesn't accidentally re-assert the pin while `wst` is mid-packet.
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Recommendation:*
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] The synchronization logic is robust because it uses the hardware IRQ. If the teletext signal is
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] failing to lock, verify that the `x` register (bit count) is being populated correctly from the
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] FIFO, as `out x, 32` will stall the `wst` SM if the DMA hasn't filled the FIFO in time for the IRQ
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] trigger. Ensure your `wstdmainit` triggers early enough.
2026-05-08 11:22:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:27:27 [nitro@ryzen5] i expect the timing is slightly wrong and would like to solve it without guess work
2026-05-08 11:27:33 [gemini-3.1-lite] To solve this without guesswork, you must calculate the exact cycle budget from the HSYNC falling
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] edge to the first data bit, then align that with the PAL specification.
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. The PAL Timing Reference
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] In a 576i signal at 166.5 MHz, the WST (Teletext) clock run-in must start precisely after the back
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] porch.
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Target: The first bit of the Teletext packet should occur exactly 10.4 µs after
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] the start of the HSYNC (or ~5.7 µs after the start of the back porch).
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] • System Clock: 166.5 MHz = 166.5 cycles per µs.
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Required Delay: 5.7 µs × 166.5 = 949.05 cycles.
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. The Current Cycle Budget
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] Let's trace the cycles in `vidbit.pio` from the trigger to the `wst` start:
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  `vidbit` IRQ Latency:
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   `irq set 0` executes.
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   `set y, 28` (1 cycle).
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   `backporchloop`: `jmp y--, backporchloop [31]` runs 29 times. Each loop is 32
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] cycles. Total: 29 × 32 = 928 cycles.
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   Total `vidbit` delay before the SM finishes the back porch: 929 cycles.
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  `wst` Startup Latency:
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   `wait 1 irq 0`: Latches on the cycle the IRQ is set.
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   `irq clear 0`: 1 cycle.
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   `out x, 32`: 1 cycle.
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   `jmp !x start [16]`: 1 cycle + 16 delay = 17 cycles.
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   Total `wst` overhead: 19 cycles.
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Current Total:* 929 + 19 = 948 cycles.
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Result: You are at ~948 cycles, which is 5.69 µs. This is remarkably close to
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] your target of 5.7 µs.
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3. The "No Guesswork" Solution
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] To eliminate guesswork, do not rely on the `[16]` delay in the `jmp` instruction, as it is coupled
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] to the instruction pipeline. Instead, use a NOP-slide or a precise `y` counter in
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] `wst.pio`.
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Modify `wst.pio` to be deterministic:*
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] .program wst
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] start:
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite]     wait 1 irq 0    ; Synchronize
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite]     irq clear 0
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out x, 32       ; Get bit count
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite]     ; Precise delay block:
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite]     ; Total cycles needed = (Target_Cycles) - (Cycles_already_spent)
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite]     ; If we need 949 and have 929, we need exactly 20 cycles here.
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite]     set y, 4        ; 1 cycle
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] delay:
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite]     jmp y--, delay  ; 4 loops * 4 cycles/loop = 16 cycles
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite]     nop             ; 1 cycle
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite]     nop             ; 1 cycle
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite]     ; --- Start transmission here ---
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] loop:
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out pindirs, 1 [22] ; 24 cycles per bit
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite]     jmp x-- loop
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] 4. Verification Steps
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Calculate the Delta: Measure the time between the HSYNC falling edge (on an
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] oscilloscope) and the first transition of the WST packet.
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Adjust the Delay Block: If you are N cycles early, remove N cycles from the `wst` delay
2026-05-08 11:27:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] block. If you are N cycles late, add N cycles.
2026-05-08 11:27:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  DMA Synchronization: Ensure `wstdmainit` is called before `vid_start`
2026-05-08 11:27:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] and that the DMA `DREQ` is correctly linked to `pio0` and the specific state machine. If the FIFO
2026-05-08 11:27:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] is empty when the IRQ triggers, the `out x, 32` will stall, introducing jitter.
2026-05-08 11:27:35 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   Pro-tip: Pre-fill the FIFO with the first 12 words of the header before the
2026-05-08 11:27:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] trigger, or use `piosmsetfifojoin(pio, sm, PIOFIFOJOIN_TX)` to double the
2026-05-08 11:27:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] buffer depth.
2026-05-08 11:27:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:27:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] By using the `set y` counter in the `wst` program, you make the start time independent of the
2026-05-08 11:27:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] `vidbit` loop timing, allowing you to "nudge" the start time by single-cycle increments by changing
2026-05-08 11:27:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] the `set y` value or adding `nop` instructions.
2026-05-08 11:27:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:28:50 [nitro@ryzen5] if i only set the header with no erase some tv may just display blank until full pages?
2026-05-08 11:28:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] Yes, that is correct. Teletext decoders in televisions are stateful. If you send only a header (or
2026-05-08 11:28:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] a header with no valid data) and do not set the "Erase Page" (C4) flag or provide a full page of
2026-05-08 11:28:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] data, the TV will behave in one of three ways depending on its firmware:
2026-05-08 11:28:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:28:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. The "Blank Page" Effect
2026-05-08 11:28:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] Most TV teletext decoders maintain a memory buffer for the page being "received." If you transmit a
2026-05-08 11:28:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] header (e.g., Page 100) but provide no body text, the decoder expects the subsequent packets to
2026-05-08 11:28:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] contain the contents for that page. If it receives no data for that page, it will either:
2026-05-08 11:28:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Keep the previous page: If you were previously viewing a page, it stays on screen.
2026-05-08 11:28:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Show a "Searching" screen: It may display "Page 100" in the top corner but show a blank
2026-05-08 11:28:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] or black screen because the memory buffer for that page is empty (or contains nulls).
2026-05-08 11:28:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:28:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. The Role of the C4 Flag
2026-05-08 11:28:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] In your `wstHeader` function, you currently have:
2026-05-08 11:28:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] `packet[p++] = HAMMING_84[0]; // C4=1: Erase Page`
2026-05-08 11:28:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:28:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] In the WST specification, the C4 Control Bit (bit 4 of the control byte) is the "Erase
2026-05-08 11:28:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] Page" flag.
2026-05-08 11:28:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] • If C4=1: The decoder clears the existing page in memory and prepares to receive new data
2026-05-08 11:28:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] for that page.
2026-05-08 11:28:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] • If C4=0: The decoder treats the incoming data as an update to the existing page.
2026-05-08 11:28:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:28:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] • If you send C4=1 without sending the body packets, you are telling the TV: "Clear
2026-05-08 11:28:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] the current page and wait for new content."* If no content arrives, the TV will clear its memory
2026-05-08 11:28:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] and show you a blank page.
2026-05-08 11:28:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:28:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3. How to avoid the "Blank" issue
2026-05-08 11:28:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you want the TV to display something immediately, you have two options:
2026-05-08 11:28:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:28:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Option A: The "Update" approach (C4=0)*
2026-05-08 11:28:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you already have a page in the TV's memory, set the C4 flag to `0`. The TV will keep what it has
2026-05-08 11:28:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] and only update the specific lines you send. This is how "live" news tickers work.
2026-05-08 11:28:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:28:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Option B: The "Repetitive" approach (The standard way)*
2026-05-08 11:28:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] Teletext is designed to be broadcast in a loop. You shouldn't just send the header once; you should
2026-05-08 11:28:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] cycle through the header and the body packets repeatedly.
2026-05-08 11:28:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] • If you send the header and the body (rows 1–24) repeatedly, the TV will populate the page memory,
2026-05-08 11:28:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] and the image will stabilize.
2026-05-08 11:28:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] • If you are currently only sending the header (as your code implies: `wstHeader(1, 0, 0, title32,
2026-05-08 11:28:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] &wstEven[p]); p += 12;`), you are missing the `wstBody` packets that contain the actual text.
2026-05-08 11:28:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:28:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] How to verify your current output
2026-05-08 11:28:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Check your `initTeletext()`: You are calling `wstHeader` followed by `wstBody`. This is
2026-05-08 11:28:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] correct.
2026-05-08 11:28:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  The "Blanking" Trap: If your `wstBody` contains only spaces or nulls, the TV will show
2026-05-08 11:28:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] a "clean" page. Ensure your `line40` string has visible characters.
2026-05-08 11:28:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  Timing the loop: The TV needs to receive the full page (header + 24 rows) multiple
2026-05-08 11:28:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] times to "lock" the page. If your DMA is only sending one header and one body row and then
2026-05-08 11:28:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] stopping, the TV's decoder will time out and clear the screen because it thinks the transmission is
2026-05-08 11:28:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] broken.
2026-05-08 11:28:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:28:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] • My recommendation:*
2026-05-08 11:28:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] Ensure your `wstEven` and `wstOdd` buffers contain the entire page (Header + 24 rows of
2026-05-08 11:28:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] text), not just the header and one row. If you send the full set of 25 packets (1 header + 24 rows)
2026-05-08 11:28:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] repeatedly via the DMA loop, the TV will "paint" the page reliably. If you send only a partial
2026-05-08 11:28:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] page, the decoder will likely reject it as incomplete or corrupted.
2026-05-08 11:28:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:30:32 [nitro@ryzen5] how many scnalines can i use per frame?
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] In a standard PAL 625-line system, you have a specific budget of lines per field. Since you are
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] generating a 576i (interlaced) or 288p (progressive) signal, the limit is strictly defined by the
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] Vertical Blanking Interval (VBI) and the Active Picture area.
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. The PAL 625-Line Budget
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] A full PAL frame consists of 625 lines (312.5 per field).
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] • VBI (Vertical Blanking Interval): Typically occupies lines 1–22 and 313–335. This is
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] where your VSYNC, equalization pulses, and WST (Teletext) data live.
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Active Picture: This leaves 576 lines (288 per field) for visible video data.
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. Your Constraints
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] In your code, you have defined:
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] const int evenLines=305;
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] const int oddLines=304;
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] This is a total of 609 lines per frame. You are currently "missing" about 16 lines compared
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] to a broadcast 625-line signal.
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3. How many can you actually use?
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] You can technically use as many lines as your `lineFrame` buffer can hold, but you are limited by
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] the DMA transfer size and the vertical retrace time.
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Maximum Lines: You can safely use up to 576 lines (288 lines per field) for
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] active video.
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The "Safe" Zone: If you are targeting standard televisions, you should respect the VBI.
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   Lines 1–22 (Field 1) and 313–335 (Field 2): Reserve these for VSYNC/HSYNC/WST.
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   Lines 23–310 (Field 1) and 336–623 (Field 2): Use these for your active display.
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] 4. Technical Limitations
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] • DMA Transfer Limit: The RP2350 DMA `trans_count` is a 16-bit value, so you are well
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] within the limit for line count (you could theoretically drive thousands of lines if you had the
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] SRAM).
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] • SRAM Budget: Each line takes `lineWords` (27 words = 108 bytes).
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   576 lines × 108 bytes ≈ 62 KB.
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   This fits easily in the RP2350's 520KB of SRAM.
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Vertical Retrace: If you add more lines, you must ensure your `vidbit` state machine
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] finishes the `line` loop and returns to the `vsync` state before the TV's horizontal sweep is lost.
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you exceed the field time (20ms for 50Hz), the TV will lose "vertical lock" and the image will
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] roll.
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] 5. Recommendation for "No Guesswork"
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you want to fill the screen correctly for a standard PAL display:
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Standardize your field count: Set your field to 312 lines (even) and 313
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] lines (odd).
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   312 + 313 = 625 total lines.
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Adjust your `initFrame`:
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   Use lines 1–22 for VBI/WST.
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   Use lines 23–310 for active video.
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  Check the "Visible" Area: Most older CRT TVs overscan. If you draw on lines 23 or 310,
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] they may be cut off. Line 30–300 is the "safe" active area.
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Warning on Interlacing:*
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you use `video_lace=true`, remember that the lines in the `evenFrame` and `oddFrame` are
2026-05-08 11:30:38 [gemini-3.1-lite] offset. The `oddFrame` lines must be shifted vertically by 0.5 lines relative to the
2026-05-08 11:30:39 [gemini-3.1-lite] `evenFrame` to achieve true interlacing. If you are just doing "progressive scan" in an interlaced
2026-05-08 11:30:39 [gemini-3.1-lite] container (288p), you can keep the lines identical, but ensure your `initFramei` function correctly
2026-05-08 11:30:39 [gemini-3.1-lite] maps the 288 rows into the 576-line interlaced structure.
2026-05-08 11:30:39 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:30:39 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Summary: You can use up to 576 lines for active display, but keep your total
2026-05-08 11:30:39 [gemini-3.1-lite] field count to 312/313 lines* to maintain the 50Hz vertical refresh rate required by PAL
2026-05-08 11:30:39 [gemini-3.1-lite] displays.
2026-05-08 11:30:39 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:31:36 [nitro@ryzen5] if i only do 20 lines of wst, should i repeat for odd frame, then send remaining rows?
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] To maintain a stable Teletext page, you must treat the transmission as a continuous, cyclic
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] broadcast. A Teletext decoder does not "wait" for a full page to be sent before it starts
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] processing; it captures packets as they arrive.
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. The Strategy: Continuous Cycling
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] Do not try to split the page across fields in a way that "waits" for the next field to finish the
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] page. Instead, broadcast a complete page (Header + 24 rows) in every field.
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you have 20 lines available in the VBI (e.g., lines 7 through 26), you should:
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Packet 1: Header (Row 0).
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Packets 2–20: Rows 1 through 19.
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Next Field: Start again at Row 20, or simply repeat the Header and Rows 1–19.
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. Why you shouldn't "Wait"
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] Teletext decoders are "stateless" regarding the timing of the next packet. If you send 20 lines,
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] stop, and wait for the next field to send the remaining 4 lines:
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  The Decoder's Buffer: The TV will receive the Header and 19 rows, update its internal
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] memory, and then see a "gap" where it expects more data.
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  The Timeout: If the decoder doesn't receive the full 24 rows within a certain timeframe
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] (usually 1–2 field periods), it may assume the signal is lost or the page is incomplete and clear
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] the buffer.
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  The Refresh Rate: By sending the full page (or a subset) in every field, you ensure the
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] TV's screen is updated 50 times per second (or 25 times per second for a full page refresh), which
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] makes the text flicker-free and stable.
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3. The "No Guesswork" Implementation
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] Since you have 20 lines available per field:
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Field 1 (Even): Send Header + Rows 1–19.
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Field 2 (Odd): Send Header + Rows 20–24 + (Optional: Repeat Rows 1–15).
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Wait, what about the Header?*
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] Always send the Header (Row 0) in every field. The Header contains the page number and
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] control bits. If you omit the header in one field, the TV decoder will not know which page the
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] subsequent rows belong to.
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] 4. How to structure your `wst` buffers
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] Instead of splitting the page, make the page "fit" into your 20-line window:
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  If your page is 24 rows: You cannot send a full 24-row page in 20 lines. You must
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] interleave them.
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  The Cycle:
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   Field 1: Header, Rows 1–19.
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   Field 2: Header, Rows 20–24, and then repeat Rows 1–15.
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  The Result: The TV will receive the full page after 2 fields (40ms). This is perfectly
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] acceptable for Teletext.
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] 5. Summary Checklist for Success
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Always include the Header (Row 0) in every field's WST block.
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Cycle the Rows: If you have more rows than lines available in the VBI, just keep a
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] "pointer" to the next row to be sent. Send 19 rows + header, then in the next field, send the next
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] 19 rows + header.
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Don't send blank lines: If you have 20 lines available, use all 20 for data. If you have
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] no more data to send for that page, repeat the header or send "null" packets (packets with a
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] specific Hamming-encoded identifier that the TV ignores).
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Crucial:* Check your `wstEven` and `wstOdd` array sizing. If you change the number of
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] lines, ensure your `dmachannelsettranscount` is updated to match the exact number
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] of words in your buffer, or the DMA will keep pushing data into the PIO until the next field,
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] causing the teletext to "bleed" into your active video area.
2026-05-08 11:31:42 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] To ensure your Teletext transmission is robust and doesn't result in a blank screen, you need to
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] transition from a "static buffer" approach to a cyclic update approach.
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. The Critical Flaw in `initTeletext()`
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] Currently, your `initTeletext()` function calculates the buffers once at startup. Because
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] you use a global `row` variable that increments, the buffers are populated with different rows in
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] the `even` vs `odd` fields, but then they never change.
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] If the TV misses a packet (which happens, as Teletext is not error-corrected beyond Hamming 8/4),
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] it will never receive that row again until a reboot.
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. The Solution: Dynamic Buffer Update
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] Instead of filling the buffers once, you should update the buffers in the main loop or use a
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] "ping-pong" buffer strategy. However, for a simple implementation, you can update the buffers
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] before triggering the DMA for the next field.
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Refactor `initTeletext` to be a "Row-Updater":*
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] // Change row to be a global or static
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] static int currentRow = 1;
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] void updateTeletextBuffer(uint32_t* buffer, bool isOdd) {
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]     int p = 7; // Skip 7 lines of preamble
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // Always write Header
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]     buffer[p++] = 383;
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]     wstHeader(1, 0, (isOdd ? 1 : 0), title32, &buffer[p]);
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]     p += 12;
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // Write the current row
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]     buffer[p++] = 383;
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]     wstBody(1, currentRow, line40, &buffer[p]);
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // Increment row for next field
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]     currentRow++;
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]     if (currentRow > 24) currentRow = 1;
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3. Integrate into the Main Loop
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] In your `while(pass)` loop, you are already checking `if
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] (!dmachannelisbusy(videodma_chan))`. This is the perfect place to update the WST
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] buffers.
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] while(pass) {
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]     if (!dma_channel_is_busy(video_dma_chan)) {
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]         // Before starting the next field, update the WST buffer
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]         if (field & 1) {
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]             updateTeletextBuffer(wstOdd, true);
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]             vid_fieldi(field++);
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]         } else {
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]             updateTeletextBuffer(wstEven, false);
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]             vid_fieldi(field++);
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]         }
2026-05-08 11:33:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]     }
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // ... rest of your loop
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 4. Why this fixes the "Blank Screen"
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Continuous Refresh: By updating the buffer every field, you are effectively
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] broadcasting the entire page (Row 0, Row 1, Row 2... Row 24) over the course of 24 fields (approx
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 0.5 seconds).
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Robustness: If the TV misses a packet, it will receive it again shortly.
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  No "Partial Page" issues: You are always sending a valid Header (Row 0) and one valid
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] Body row, which is the minimum compliant Teletext transmission.
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 5. A Note on your `wst.pio` Timing
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] In your `wst.pio`, you have:
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] start:
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite]     wait 1 irq 0
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite]     irq clear 0
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out x, 32
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite]     jmp !x start [16]
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you find that the TV is not locking onto the Teletext signal at all (i.e., the "Teletext" icon
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] doesn't even appear on the TV), the timing is almost certainly the issue.
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The "No Guesswork" Test: Change `jmp !x start [16]` to `jmp !x start [0]`. If the TV
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] starts showing garbled characters, you are too early. If it shows nothing, you are either way off
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] or the sync is failing.
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Verify the Clock Run-in: Your `wstHeader` starts with `0x55, 0x55, 0x27`. `0x55` is
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] `01010101` in binary. This is the Clock Run-in. The TV uses these bits to sync its internal
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] bit-clock. If your `wst.pio` start-time is off by even 1-2 cycles, the TV's decoder will fail to
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] lock to this bit-pattern.
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Final Advice:* If you have access to a Logic Analyzer (even a cheap $10 USB one), look at
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] the `VID_PIN` during the back porch. You should see a clean burst of transitions starting exactly
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] after the back porch. If the `0x55` pattern is not perfectly aligned with the start of the data
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] burst, the TV will ignore the packet entirely.
2026-05-08 11:33:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] The logic in your `updateTeletext()` function is moving in the right direction, but there is a
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] critical race condition in your implementation that will likely cause the TV to display
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] corrupted teletext or fail to lock.
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. The Race Condition
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] You are updating the `wstEven` and `wstOdd` buffers inside the `while(pass)` loop while the DMA is
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] potentially still reading from them.
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The Issue: The DMA channel does not know you have updated the buffer. It will continue to
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] read the old data until it completes the current transfer, and it has no way to know the
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] data changed mid-stream.
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The Fix: You need to ensure the buffer update happens only when the DMA is idle.
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] Since you are checking `if (!dmachannelisbusy(wstdmachan))`, you are safe, but
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] you must ensure your `updateTeletext()` finishes before you trigger the next
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] `dmachannelsetread_addr`.
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. The "No Guesswork" Buffer Strategy
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] To avoid tearing, use Double Buffering.
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Maintain two sets of buffers: `wstEvenA`, `wstEvenB`.
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Update `wstEvenB` while the DMA reads `wstEvenA`.
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  Swap them.
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] However, for a simpler fix, just ensure `updateTeletext` is called before the `vid_fieldi`
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] call:
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] if (!dma_channel_is_busy(video_dma_chan)) {
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // 1. Update the buffers while DMA is NOT busy
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]     updateTeletext();
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // 2. Trigger the next field
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]     if(video_lace){
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]         vid_fieldi(field++);
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]     }else{
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]         vid_field(field++);
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite]     }
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-05-08 11:34:46 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3. Missing `row` Definition
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] In your provided code, `int row` is used in `updateTeletext()` but it is not declared as a global
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] or static variable. If it's a local variable inside `updateTeletext`, it resets to its initial
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] value every time the function is called, meaning you are only ever sending Row 1.
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Fix:*
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] // Add this at the top of your file (outside any function)
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] static int row = 1;
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 4. The WST Synchronization (The "No Guesswork" Timing)
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] You asked how to solve the timing without guesswork. The "guesswork" comes from the `[16]` delay in
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] your PIO. To solve this mathematically:
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Calculate the HSYNC-to-Data offset:
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   PAL HSYNC starts at T=0.
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   The Teletext data should start exactly 10.4 µs after the HSYNC leading edge.
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   At 166.5 MHz, 10.4 × 166.5 = 1731.6 cycles.
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Count your cycles in `vidbit.pio`:
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   From `irq set 0` in `vidbit.pio` to the start of the data in `wst.pio` is your current
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] "start" time.
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   If your oscilloscope shows the data starting at Tmeasured, and you want Ttarget =
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1732 cycles:
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   New Delay = Current Delay + (Target - Measured).
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The "No Guesswork" PIO modification:*
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] Replace the `jmp !x start [16]` in `wst.pio` with a programmable delay loop:
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] .program wst
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] start:
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite]     wait 1 irq 0
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite]     irq clear 0
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out x, 32           ; Get count
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite]     set y, 7            ; 1 cycle (adjust this constant)
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] delay:
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite]     jmp y--, delay      ; 8 * 4 = 32 cycles
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite]     nop                 ; 1 cycle
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite]     ; Start transmission
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] loop:
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite]     out pindirs, 1 [22] ; 24 cycles
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite]     ...
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] By changing `set y, 7` to `set y, 6` or `set y, 8`, you shift the entire packet by exactly 4
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] cycles (approx 24ns). This is the standard way to align VBI data without trial-and-error.
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] Summary Checklist
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Declare `static int row = 1;` globally so it persists between calls.
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Move `updateTeletext()` to be called immediately before `vid_fieldi()`.
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  Check your `dmachannelsettranscount`: In `updateTeletext`, if you
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] change the structure of the data, ensure the count matches exactly what the DMA expects.
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 4.  Verify the Hamming parity: Ensure your `wstHeader` and `wstBody` are sending the
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] correct number of bits. The TV will ignore the packet if the Hamming check fails.
2026-05-08 11:34:47 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-08 13:21:26 [claude-sonnet-4-6] This is a well-structured PAL composite video generator for the RP2350. A few observations worth
2026-05-08 13:21:26 [claude-sonnet-4-6] noting:
2026-05-08 13:21:26 [claude-sonnet-4-6] 
2026-05-08 13:21:26 [claude-sonnet-4-6] • Architecture*
2026-05-08 13:21:26 [claude-sonnet-4-6] The three-PIO split is clean — `pio0` for luma and teletext, `pio1` for chroma and audio, `pio2`
2026-05-08 13:21:26 [claude-sonnet-4-6] for PS/2. That third PIO is one of the RP2350 advantages over RP2040 and it's being used well here.
2026-05-08 13:21:26 [claude-sonnet-4-6] 
2026-05-08 13:21:26 [claude-sonnet-4-6] • Chroma timing*
2026-05-08 13:21:26 [claude-sonnet-4-6] The clock divider of `2.3471` gives roughly `166500 / 2.3471 / 16 ≈ 4431.7 kHz` per packed word,
2026-05-08 13:21:26 [claude-sonnet-4-6] which is a good approximation of the PAL subcarrier at `4.43361875 MHz`. The ring buffer size of
2026-05-08 13:21:26 [claude-sonnet-4-6] `2^9 = 512 bytes` aligns correctly with the `colorWords2  4 = 512 byte` array, and
2026-05-08 13:21:26 [claude-sonnet-4-6] `alignas(1024)` satisfies that constraint.
2026-05-08 13:21:26 [claude-sonnet-4-6] 
2026-05-08 13:21:26 [claude-sonnet-4-6] • Potential concern*
2026-05-08 13:21:26 [claude-sonnet-4-6] `irq set 4` fires in the video PIO on `pio0` at back porch, but the chroma SM is on `pio1`.
2026-05-08 13:21:26 [claude-sonnet-4-6] Inter-PIO IRQ visibility on RP2350 is worth verifying — without seeing `chroma.pio` it's unclear
2026-05-08 13:21:26 [claude-sonnet-4-6] whether phase lock between luma and chroma is being maintained across fields or whether the chroma
2026-05-08 13:21:26 [claude-sonnet-4-6] is free-running.
2026-05-08 13:21:26 [claude-sonnet-4-6] 
2026-05-08 13:21:26 [claude-sonnet-4-6] • Minor note*
2026-05-08 13:21:26 [claude-sonnet-4-6] `wstdmainit` configures an initial transfer count of `15` but that value is always
2026-05-08 13:21:27 [claude-sonnet-4-6] overridden before the channel starts in `vid_fieldi`, so it's harmless but slightly misleading.
2026-05-08 13:21:27 [claude-sonnet-4-6] 
2026-05-08 13:21:27 [claude-sonnet-4-6] The missing headers `pal576i.h` and `monobit.h` presumably define the frame buffers and word counts
2026-05-08 13:21:27 [claude-sonnet-4-6] — sharing those would complete the picture.
2026-05-08 13:21:27 [claude-sonnet-4-6] 
2026-05-08 13:23:07 [claude-sonnet-4-6] The headers complete the picture well. A few things worth noting:
2026-05-08 13:23:07 [claude-sonnet-4-6] 
2026-05-08 13:23:07 [claude-sonnet-4-6] • Memory footprint*
2026-05-08 13:23:07 [claude-sonnet-4-6] The frame buffers are substantial. Progressive: `8239 × 4 = ~32KB`. Interlaced even + odd fields:
2026-05-08 13:23:07 [claude-sonnet-4-6] `(8270 + 8243) × 4 = ~66KB` combined. The RP2350 has 520KB SRAM so there's headroom, but it's worth
2026-05-08 13:23:07 [claude-sonnet-4-6] keeping track of as the project grows.
2026-05-08 13:23:07 [claude-sonnet-4-6] 
2026-05-08 13:23:07 [claude-sonnet-4-6] • Preamble size difference*
2026-05-08 13:23:07 [claude-sonnet-4-6] Progressive uses `linePreamble=4` while interlaced uses `evenPreamble=35`. That jump reflects the
2026-05-08 13:23:07 [claude-sonnet-4-6] more complex interlaced vsync sequence needing equalization and serration pulses correctly timed
2026-05-08 13:23:07 [claude-sonnet-4-6] across the half-line boundary — that detail is easy to get wrong.
2026-05-08 13:23:07 [claude-sonnet-4-6] 
2026-05-08 13:23:07 [claude-sonnet-4-6] • PetASCII*
2026-05-08 13:23:07 [claude-sonnet-4-6] The presence of `PetAscii()` and a font sized for C64 character rendering is a nice touch. Combined
2026-05-08 13:23:07 [claude-sonnet-4-6] with the teletext support it suggests a fairly capable text display layer.
2026-05-08 13:23:07 [claude-sonnet-4-6] 
2026-05-08 13:23:07 [claude-sonnet-4-6] • `fs.h`*
2026-05-08 13:23:07 [claude-sonnet-4-6] The LittleFS stub implies flash filesystem support is planned. Given the RP2350's XIP flash, that
2026-05-08 13:23:07 [claude-sonnet-4-6] would be a natural fit for storing teletext page content or character sets without exhausting RAM.
2026-05-08 13:23:07 [claude-sonnet-4-6] 
2026-05-08 13:23:08 [claude-sonnet-4-6] • `ps2_init` taking a `PIO`*
2026-05-08 13:23:08 [claude-sonnet-4-6] The signature accepts any PIO instance, but `vidbit.cpp` passes `pio2` which only exists on RP2350.
2026-05-08 13:23:08 [claude-sonnet-4-6] Worth a compile-guard if the code ever needs to target RP2040 as a fallback.
2026-05-08 13:23:08 [claude-sonnet-4-6] 
2026-05-08 13:23:08 [claude-sonnet-4-6] The architecture is shaping up coherently. Is `monobit.cpp` or `pal576i.cpp` the next piece?
2026-05-08 13:23:08 [claude-sonnet-4-6] 
