2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] The WST PIO should wait exactly the duration of the back porch after receiving IRQ 0 before
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] starting to transmit teletext data.
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] Why that delay?
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] - IRQ 0 is asserted at the start of the back porch (see `irq set 0` in `vidbit.pio`).
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] - Teletext data must begin at the same horizontal position as active video on normal lines.
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] - Active video in `vidbit.pio` starts immediately after the back porch ends.
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] - Therefore the WST PIO should delay by the full back‑porch length, then output its bitstream.
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] Current back‑porch length
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] From `vidbit.pio`:
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] set y, BACKPORCH_WAIT32   ; 28
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] backporch:
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4]     jmp y--, backporch [31]
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] Each iteration consumes `1 + 31 = 32` cycles.
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] Y runs from 28 down to 0 inclusive → 29 iterations → 928 cycles (the comment `30*32+4` is
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] outdated; your code now gives 928).
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] The bug in `wst.pio`
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] The current program does this:
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] wait 1 irq 0
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] irq clear 0
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] out x, 32
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] jmp !x start [16]        ; if x==0, wrap; else fall through
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] ;set y, 28               ; commented out
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] ;delay:
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] ;    jmp y--, delay [31]
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] loop:
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4]     out pindirs, 1 [22]
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4]     jmp x-- loop
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] Because the delay loop is commented out, the code starts transmitting the first bit immediately
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] after the IRQ – i.e. at the very beginning of the back porch. That’s about 928 cycles too
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] early, so the teletext waveform lands in the middle of the colour burst / back porch, causing the
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] dropout you see.
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] Fix: uncomment and restructure the delay
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] Add an unconditional delay loop before the bit output, and keep the `jmp !x start` after it (so
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] zero‑length lines still wrap correctly):
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] start:
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4]     wait 1 irq 0
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4]     irq clear 0
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4]     out x, 32
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4]     set y, 28              ; 29 * 32 = 928 cycles
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] delay:
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4]     jmp y--, delay [31]    ; match back porch length
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4]     jmp !x start [16]      ; if x==0, skip transmission
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4] loop:
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4]     out pindirs, 1 [22]
2026-05-09 22:49:45 [deepseek-v4]     jmp x-- loop
2026-05-09 22:49:46 [deepseek-v4]     set pindirs, 0
2026-05-09 22:49:46 [deepseek-v4] .wrap
2026-05-09 22:49:46 [deepseek-v4] 
