2026-05-09 22:53:22 [nitro@ryzen5] does this now start too late?
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] You revised `vidbit.pio` to move `irq set 0` from the middle of the back porch to after the
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] back porch loop. Now the IRQ fires exactly at the moment when active video (or teletext) should
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] begin.
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] Is this too late?
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] No. This is now the correct timing for WST:
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] - Back porch ends → `irq set 0` fires → WST PIO is triggered → it starts transmitting the
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] clock‑run‑in and data immediately.
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] Previously, the IRQ happened too early (inside the back porch), so the WST PIO had to add a
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] 928‑cycle delay to compensate. That commented‑out delay in `wst.pio` was meant to match the old
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] (longer) back porch.
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] What you must do in `wst.pio`
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] Remove the delay entirely (keep only the `wait 1 irq 0` / `irq clear 0` / `out x, 32` / then
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] transmit). The line that outputs `set pindirs, 1 [22]` should run immediately after reading
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] the bit count, because the IRQ now marks the start of the active window.
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] Your updated `wst.pio` should look like this:
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] start:
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4]     wait 1 irq 0
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4]     irq clear 0
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4]     out x, 32
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4]     jmp !x start [16]      ; skip if zero bits
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] loop:
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4]     out pindirs, 1 [22]
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4]     jmp x-- loop
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4]     set pindirs, 0
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] .wrap
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] Check total line length
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] With the IRQ at the end of the back porch, the active‑window start is fixed. The 384‑bit WST packet
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] takes `24 cycles/bit × 384 = 9216 cycles`. Your line budget is 10656 cycles.
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] - hsync + back porch + front porch = 786 + 928 + 258 = 1972 cycles
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] - Remaining for active = 8684 cycles
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] - WST takes 9216 cycles → this will overflow by 532 cycles.
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] That means the WST packet cannot fit within a single standard PAL line when using the
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] 10656‑cycle line length. You must either:
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] 1. Shorten the WST packet (e.g., omit trailing bits or use a shorter line format).
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] 2. Reduce the horizontal blanking (make back porch or hsync shorter) to free up cycles.
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] 3. Use a longer line (change to non‑standard PAL, but then sync may fail).
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] The original design with the delay inside the back porch effectively stole those 928 cycles
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] from the back‑porch period and added them to the active period, allowing the WST packet to
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] fit. By moving the IRQ later, you have re‑assigned those cycles back to blanking, and now the
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] active window is too short for the full 384 bits.
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] Recommended solution
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] Keep the IRQ at the start of the back porch (as it was originally), and restore the delay
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] loop in `wst.pio` to shift the transmission to the correct time. That way the total line length
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] remains 10656 cycles and the teletext packet fits naturally.
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] If you still want the IRQ at the end of back porch, you must change the WST payload size (e.g., use
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] only 360 bits, or increase the line length to ~11500 cycles — but then the video timing will drift
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] from PAL spec).
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] • Bottom line:*
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] - Your change makes the IRQ fire at the correct start of active video, but now the active window is
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] too short for the standard 384‑bit teletext packet.
2026-05-09 22:53:49 [deepseek-v4] - The original design with IRQ early + delay in WST PIO worked because it effectively
2026-05-09 22:53:50 [deepseek-v4] extended the active period by using part of the blanking interval for data.
2026-05-09 22:53:50 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-09 22:53:50 [deepseek-v4] Recommend reverting the IRQ position to the middle of back porch and re‑enabling the delay in
2026-05-09 22:53:50 [deepseek-v4] `wst.pio`.
