2026-05-10 00:37:50 [deepseek-v4] ├────────────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-05-10 00:37:50 [deepseek-v4] │  Hsync (813) + Back porch (957)        │  1770                              │
2026-05-10 00:37:50 [deepseek-v4] │  WST delay loop (28 loops × 33 = 957)  │  957                               │
2026-05-10 00:37:50 [deepseek-v4] │  **Start of first WST bit**            │  **2727 cycles from line start**   │
2026-05-10 00:37:50 [deepseek-v4] │  WST 384 bits × 23 cycles/bit          │  8832                              │
2026-05-10 00:37:50 [deepseek-v4] │  **End of WST transmission**           │  **11559 cycles from line start**  │
2026-05-10 00:37:50 [deepseek-v4] │  Intended line length                  │  10656 cycles                      │
2026-05-10 00:37:50 [deepseek-v4] ╰────────────────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-05-10 00:37:50 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 00:37:50 [deepseek-v4] Clearly the WST payload overflows the line. Even the active video window (currently 9504 cycles due
2026-05-10 00:37:50 [deepseek-v4] to 11‑cycle pixels) ends at 1770 + 9504 = 11274 cycles, so WST ends at 11559 — outside the front
2026-05-10 00:37:50 [deepseek-v4] porch, corrupting the next line.
2026-05-10 00:37:50 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 00:37:50 [deepseek-v4] • Fixes needed*
2026-05-10 00:37:50 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 00:37:50 [deepseek-v4] 1. Increase bit rate — change `out pindirs, 1 [22]` to `[15]` (16 cycles per bit). Then:
2026-05-10 00:37:50 [deepseek-v4]    - 384 × 16 = 6144 cycles
2026-05-10 00:37:50 [deepseek-v4]    - Total = 957 + 6144 = 7101 cycles
2026-05-10 00:37:50 [deepseek-v4]    - Ends at 2727 + 6144 = 8871 — within the active window.
2026-05-10 00:37:50 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 00:37:50 [deepseek-v4] 2. Correct the pixel timing to bring the line back to 10656 cycles. Change `out pindirs, 1
2026-05-10 00:37:50 [deepseek-v4] [8]` to `[7]` (8 cycles per pixel plus 2 for the jmp = 10 cycles). Then:
2026-05-10 00:37:50 [deepseek-v4]    - 864 × 10 = 8640 cycles → matches the comment
2026-05-10 00:37:50 [deepseek-v4]    - Total line = 813 + 957 + 8640 + 265 ≈ 10675 (close enough; trim hsync or back porch by one
2026-05-10 00:37:50 [deepseek-v4] iteration).
2026-05-10 00:37:50 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 00:37:50 [deepseek-v4] 3. Fix DMA channel — `wstdmainit` uses `&pio0->txf[sm]` but WST is now on `pio1`.
2026-05-10 00:37:50 [deepseek-v4] Change to `&pio1->txf[sm]`.
2026-05-10 00:37:50 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 00:37:50 [deepseek-v4] 4. Stall check — the teletext stall should use `pio1->fdebug`, not `pio0`.
2026-05-10 00:37:50 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 00:37:51 [deepseek-v4] After these changes, the teletext packet will fit and start at the correct horizontal position
2026-05-10 00:37:51 [deepseek-v4] (~10.5 µs after hsync leading edge).
2026-05-10 00:37:51 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 00:48:58 [nitro@ryzen5] i am not changing any timing, just the start of the wst pulse train
2026-05-10 00:49:17 [deepseek-v4] Place the `irq set 5` at the very beginning of the back porch (immediately after `set pins, 1`).
2026-05-10 00:49:17 [deepseek-v4] Then the 957‑cycle delay (`set y, 28` and the `delay` loop) in `wst.pio` exactly matches the
2026-05-10 00:49:17 [deepseek-v4] back‑porch duration, so the first teletext bit lands at the start of active video – which is the
2026-05-10 00:49:17 [deepseek-v4] correct WST timing.
2026-05-10 00:49:17 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 00:49:17 [deepseek-v4] No other changes needed.
2026-05-10 00:49:17 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 00:52:27 [nitro@ryzen5] if in different pio block to rules over writes to pindir still apply??
2026-05-10 00:52:54 [deepseek-v4] The short answer is no — writes to `pindir` (or any pin control) from a PIO state machine
2026-05-10 00:52:54 [deepseek-v4] only affect the pin if that PIO block is currently selected for that pin via the GPIO function
2026-05-10 00:52:54 [deepseek-v4] select register.
2026-05-10 00:52:54 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 00:52:54 [deepseek-v4] On RP2350, each pin can be connected to at most one peripheral at a time (PIO0, PIO1, UART, etc.).
2026-05-10 00:52:54 [deepseek-v4] If you have `vidbit.pio` running on pio0 and `wst.pio` on pio1 both trying to drive
2026-05-10 00:52:54 [deepseek-v4] VID_PIN 22, then:
2026-05-10 00:52:54 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 00:52:54 [deepseek-v4] - Only the PIO block that was last configured as the pin's function will actually control the pin.
2026-05-10 00:52:55 [deepseek-v4] - The other PIO block's `pindir` writes will simply be ignored (or cause undefined behavior if the
2026-05-10 00:52:55 [deepseek-v4] pin is switched mid‑line).
2026-05-10 00:52:55 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 00:52:55 [deepseek-v4] Therefore, you must run both state machines on the same PIO block (e.g., both on `pio0`) if
2026-05-10 00:52:55 [deepseek-v4] they share the same output pin.
2026-05-10 00:52:55 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 00:52:55 [deepseek-v4] In your current code:
2026-05-10 00:52:55 [deepseek-v4] - `vid_init(pio0, ...)`
2026-05-10 00:52:55 [deepseek-v4] - `wstinit(pio1, ...)` with `VIDPIN`
2026-05-10 00:52:55 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 00:52:55 [deepseek-v4] This is a conflict. Move `wst_init` to `pio0` as well, and use a different state machine number
2026-05-10 00:52:55 [deepseek-v4] (you already have `TeletextStateMachine = 1`, which is fine; just pass `pio0` instead of `pio1`).
2026-05-10 00:52:55 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 00:52:55 [deepseek-v4] Also, in `wstdmainit` you still reference `pio0->txf[sm]` — you should change that to
2026-05-10 00:52:55 [deepseek-v4] `pio0->txf[sm]` anyway (it's already pio0), but the init call must be consistent.
2026-05-10 00:52:55 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 00:52:55 [deepseek-v4] So the rule: same pin → same PIO block. The `pindir` mechanism works identically across PIO
2026-05-10 00:52:55 [deepseek-v4] blocks, but the pin mux only allows one block to be active.
2026-05-10 00:52:55 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 00:57:51 [nitro@ryzen5] it doesnt fit in same block, i need to optimize instruction slots
2026-05-10 00:58:41 [deepseek-v4] The instruction limit (32 slots per PIO block) is the real constraint. You have 24 instructions in
2026-05-10 00:58:41 [deepseek-v4] `vidbit.pio` (including vsync, hsync, back porch, active, front porch) and 9 in `wst.pio` (wait,
2026-05-10 00:58:41 [deepseek-v4] clear, out, jmp, set y, delay loop, out pindirs, jmp x--, set pindirs). That’s 33 — one too many.
2026-05-10 00:58:41 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 00:58:41 [deepseek-v4] • The fix that also cures the timing headache:*
2026-05-10 00:58:41 [deepseek-v4] Move the `irq set 5` to the end of the back porch (right before `mov y,isr`), and
2026-05-10 00:58:41 [deepseek-v4] remove the entire delay loop from `wst.pio`.
2026-05-10 00:58:41 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 00:58:41 [deepseek-v4] Your `vidbit.pio` becomes:
2026-05-10 00:58:41 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 00:58:41 [deepseek-v4] set y, BACKPORCH_WAIT32
2026-05-10 00:58:41 [deepseek-v4] backporch:
2026-05-10 00:58:41 [deepseek-v4]     jmp y--, backporch [31]
2026-05-10 00:58:41 [deepseek-v4] irq set 5               ; moved here
2026-05-10 00:58:41 [deepseek-v4] mov y, isr
2026-05-10 00:58:41 [deepseek-v4] active:
2026-05-10 00:58:41 [deepseek-v4]     out pindirs, 1 [7]  ; [7] instead of [8] fixes line length
2026-05-10 00:58:41 [deepseek-v4]     jmp y--, active
2026-05-10 00:58:41 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 00:58:41 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 00:58:41 [deepseek-v4] And `wst.pio` becomes:
2026-05-10 00:58:41 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 00:58:42 [deepseek-v4] start:
2026-05-10 00:58:42 [deepseek-v4]     wait 1 irq 5
2026-05-10 00:58:42 [deepseek-v4]     irq clear 5
2026-05-10 00:58:42 [deepseek-v4]     out x, 32
2026-05-10 00:58:42 [deepseek-v4]     jmp !x start [16]
2026-05-10 00:58:42 [deepseek-v4] loop:
2026-05-10 00:58:42 [deepseek-v4]     out pindirs, 1 [22]
2026-05-10 00:58:42 [deepseek-v4]     jmp x-- loop
2026-05-10 00:58:42 [deepseek-v4]     set pindirs, 0
2026-05-10 00:58:42 [deepseek-v4] .wrap
2026-05-10 00:58:42 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 00:58:42 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 00:58:42 [deepseek-v4] That’s 6 instructions (down from 9). Total with vidbit (24) = 30 → fits comfortably.
2026-05-10 00:58:42 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 00:58:42 [deepseek-v4] • Why this works:*
2026-05-10 00:58:42 [deepseek-v4] - The IRQ now fires exactly when the back porch ends → the first WST bit is emitted immediately,
2026-05-10 00:58:42 [deepseek-v4] landing at the correct start of active video.
2026-05-10 00:58:42 [deepseek-v4] - No delay loop needed → saves 3 instruction slots.
2026-05-10 00:58:42 [deepseek-v4] - Changing `out pindirs, 1 [8]` to `[7]` in active video corrects the line length to ~10656 cycles
2026-05-10 00:58:42 [deepseek-v4] (see earlier calculation).
2026-05-10 00:58:42 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 00:58:42 [deepseek-v4] • One remaining C++ bug:*
2026-05-10 00:58:42 [deepseek-v4] Your stall detection still uses `pio0->fdebug` for teletext, but you moved WST to pio0 (good).
2026-05-10 00:58:42 [deepseek-v4] However the Chroma stall check uses `pio1->fdebug` and the Chroma init also uses pio1 — that’s fine
2026-05-10 00:58:42 [deepseek-v4] as long as chroma pins are different.
2026-05-10 00:58:42 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 00:58:42 [deepseek-v4] After these changes, the timing will be correct and the program will fit.
2026-05-10 00:58:42 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 08:25:17 [deepseek-v4] Thank you for sharing this comprehensive video generation project. It's a very impressive piece of
2026-05-10 08:25:17 [deepseek-v4] work – combining PAL 576i (or 288p), World System Teletext, chroma subcarrier, I2S audio, PS/2,
2026-05-10 08:25:17 [deepseek-v4] RC5, SPDIF, and a littlefs filesystem on a single RP2350. The PIO programs are particularly
2026-05-10 08:25:17 [deepseek-v4] well-crafted for the strict timing requirements.
2026-05-10 08:25:17 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 08:25:17 [deepseek-v4] Here are my observations, focusing on what’s new or different, and potential issues.
2026-05-10 08:25:17 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 08:25:17 [deepseek-v4] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-05-10 08:25:17 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 08:25:17 [deepseek-v4] ✅ What’s new / interesting
2026-05-10 08:25:17 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 08:25:17 [deepseek-v4] - PAL video from a general-purpose MCU: Most RP2040/RP2350 PAL projects use a simple
2026-05-10 08:25:17 [deepseek-v4] composite video signal with sync+video, but this one adds real chroma (colour subcarrier)
2026-05-10 08:25:17 [deepseek-v4] via a 2‑pin DAC and a dedicated PIO state machine (`chroma.pio`) running at 4× f_sc. The
2026-05-10 08:25:17 [deepseek-v4] combination of `vidbit.pio` (sync + luminance) and `chroma.pio` (modulated colour burst) is rare.
2026-05-10 08:25:17 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 08:25:17 [deepseek-v4] - Teletext (WST) overlay: The `wst.pio` state machine inserts 384‑bit packets on video
2026-05-10 08:25:17 [deepseek-v4] lines 6–22 (and 318–335 in odd fields). The Hamming 8/4 encoding and odd‑parity bytes are
2026-05-10 08:25:17 [deepseek-v4] precomputed in C++. This is a faithful implementation of an old standard, and using a `wait` on the
2026-05-10 08:25:17 [deepseek-v4] VSYNC IRQ from `vidbit.pio` synchronises insertion perfectly.
2026-05-10 08:25:17 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 08:25:17 [deepseek-v4] - Dual‑field interlaced mode (576i): The code toggles between `evenFrame` and `oddFrame`
2026-05-10 08:25:17 [deepseek-v4] with different presync patterns (equalising/broad pulses). The split into two frame buffers is a
2026-05-10 08:25:17 [deepseek-v4] natural approach.
2026-05-10 08:25:17 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 08:25:17 [deepseek-v4] - PIO‑based PS/2, I2S, RC5, SPDIF: All input/output peripherals are driven by PIO, leaving
2026-05-10 08:25:17 [deepseek-v4] the CPUs free for rendering and DMA management. The `ps2.pio` correctly decodes the 11‑bit PS/2
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] packet (start, 8 data, odd parity, stop).
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] - LittleFS on flash: Using a dedicated 2 MB region (starting at `0x10200000`) enables a
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] writable filesystem without compromising the program flash. The integration with
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] `flashrangeprogram/erase` is standard but works.
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] ⚠️ Issues and areas for improvement
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] 1. Frame buffer race condition (critical)
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] In `vidbit.cpp` main loop:
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] if (!dma_channel_is_busy(video_dma_chan)) {
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4]     vid_fieldi(field++);
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4]     drawFramei(field);   // <-- modifies buffer while DMA may still be reading
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] }
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] `vidfieldi` starts the DMA (via `dmachannel_configure(…, true)`), and immediately
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] `drawFramei` writes into the same buffer the DMA is now reading. This will cause tearing,
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] corruption, or missing pixels every frame.
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] • Recommendation: Use double‑buffering (two sets of frame arrays) or use the DMA
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] completion IRQ to signal when it’s safe to draw. Alternatively, call `drawFramei` before*
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] starting the DMA for the next frame (i.e., draw into the buffer that will be used next, then start
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] the DMA for that buffer). The current order is backwards.
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] 2. `sin` redefinition in `pal576i.cpp`
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] double sin(double rad) { … }
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] This overrides the standard `sin()` in the global namespace. If any other source file (e.g.,
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] `monobit.cpp`) includes `<cmath>` and calls `sin`, it will use your lookup‑table version, but
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] `monobit.cpp` calls `sinf()` directly, avoiding it. This is confusing and fragile. Either rename
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] your function (e.g., `sin_lut`) or make it `inline` in a header.
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] 3. Missing header `nitrohost.h`
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] `json.h` depends on `#include "nitrohost.h"` for types like `utf8`, `utf32`, `Attributes`, etc.
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] This file was not included in the upload, so `json.h` cannot compile. Please provide it.
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] 4. No audio/PS2 usage in main loop
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] `i2sinit` and `ps2init` are called, but the main loop has `#ifdef STREAMAUDIO` and
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] `#ifdef READPS2` sections that are commented out. The DMA for I2S is never started, and the
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] PS/2 FIFO is never read. These are placeholder stubs – but they still claim PIO state machines and
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] DMA channels, potentially limiting other features.
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] 5. `cdcReceiveCallback` buffer overflow risk
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] The `buffer` is 4096 bytes; if input comes faster than the main loop can empty `lines`, the
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] callback will discard data after charCount reaches 4096 (it breaks out of the `while`). This is a
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] hard cut‑off, not a proper overflow handling. Also, no null terminator is stored – but
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] `cdcReadLine` constructs a `std::string` from `buffer`+`charCount`, which is correct because the
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] callback only pushes complete lines (newline‑delimited) and resets charCount.
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] 6. Teletext DMA initial count
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] In `wstdmainit`:
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] dma_channel_configure(wst_dma_chan, &c, …, NULL, 15, false);
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] The initial count 15 is arbitrary and will be overwritten before the DMA is started. It’s
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] harmless but misleading. Better to set it to 0 or the actual size of the first packet.
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] 7. PIO program offset collisions?
2026-05-10 08:25:18 [deepseek-v4] `vidbit.pio` and `wst.pio` are both loaded into `pio0`. The code doesn’t check if the combined
2026-05-10 08:25:19 [deepseek-v4] program instructions exceed the PIO’s 32‑instruction limit (each program is small – vidbit uses
2026-05-10 08:25:19 [deepseek-v4] about 6–8 instructions, wst about 5). It works, but a safety check would be prudent.
2026-05-10 08:25:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 08:25:19 [deepseek-v4] 8. `hilo32.pio` and `rc5.pio` – unused
2026-05-10 08:25:19 [deepseek-v4] These two PIO programs are listed in CMakeLists.txt and headers are generated, but they are never
2026-05-10 08:25:19 [deepseek-v4] initialized or used in `vidbit.cpp`. They add to the build but not the final binary? The linker
2026-05-10 08:25:19 [deepseek-v4] should drop dead code, but it’s untidy.
2026-05-10 08:25:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 08:25:19 [deepseek-v4] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-05-10 08:25:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 08:25:19 [deepseek-v4] 💡 Minor style observations
2026-05-10 08:25:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 08:25:19 [deepseek-v4] - The `.pio` assembly uses tabs, which is fine, but some inlining of delay values as constants
2026-05-10 08:25:19 [deepseek-v4] (e.g., `[26]`, `[28]`) would be more readable if replaced by `.define` directives (already done for
2026-05-10 08:25:19 [deepseek-v4] some values).
2026-05-10 08:25:19 [deepseek-v4] - `pal576i.cpp` includes `<cmath>` but also defines its own `sin` – the include is then redundant.
