2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] The shared files constitute a complete embedded project—vidbit—for an RP2350-based PAL
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] video signal generator with additional teletext, chroma, audio, and PS/2 input features. Below is a
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] professional review of the content, highlighting novel aspects, potential improvements, and
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] observations.
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] Project Overview
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] - Purpose: Generate PAL 576i (and 288P) video with software-rendered graphics, World System
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] Teletext (WST), colour subcarrier, I²S audio output, and PS/2 keyboard input.
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] - Hardware Target: Raspberry Pi Pico 2 (RP2350) with a custom DAC ladder for luminance and
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] chrominance.
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] - Build System: CMake with Ninja (Windows `.bat`) and shell scripts (Linux `.sh`). All PIO
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] programs are compiled via `picogeneratepio_header`.
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] Standout Elements
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] 1. PIO Programs
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] - vidbit.pio – Line-synchronised video timing using a main line loop, sync pulses, back
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] porch, active video (864 pixels), and front porch. Uses `out pindirs` with variable delay to drive
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] a single-bit output (luma).
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] - chroma.pio – 2-bit DAC output for PAL colour burst and modulated chroma. Runs at ~17.7
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] MHz (4× f_sc) and generates two full colour cycles per 8‑bit FIFO entry. The approach of encoding
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] 2‑bit phase pairs is novel and efficient.
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] - wst.pio – Teletext data insertion triggered by an IRQ from the video PIO. Each word
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] carries 384 bits at 6.9375 Mbps. This integration is rarely seen in hobbyist projects.
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] - rc5.pio – Pulse-width measurement for an infrared sensor (flame detector) using 32‑bit
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] counters. Interesting addition for data acquisition.
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] - i2s.pio – 16‑bit stereo I²S output at 48 kHz, using bit‑banged timing at 150 MHz.
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] - ps2.pio – PS/2 keyboard decoder with parity and stop‑bit handling.
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] 2. DMA‑Driven Rendering
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] - The main loop uses DMA to stream pre‑rendered framebuffers (`evenFrame`, `oddFrame`, or
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] `progressiveFrame`) into the PIO TX FIFO.
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] - Teletext data is also DMA’d from separate arrays (`wstEven`, `wstOdd`) and synchronised via video
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] line IRQs.
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] - Chroma data is transferred via a ring‑buffered DMA channel (`colorWord` array) to maintain
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] continuous subcarrier phase.
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] 3. Rendering and Typography
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] - monobit.cpp – Implements a 864‑pixel wide monochrome framebuffer with C64‑style bitmap
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] font. Functions `rect`, `plot`, `text` operate directly on the framebuffer.
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] - pal576i.cpp – Interlaced rendering using two separate framebuffers (even/odd fields).
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] Drawing functions are duplicated with `i` suffix (`ploti`, `recti`). This duplication could be
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] refactored into a field‑aware abstraction.
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] 4. Filesystem and Utility
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] - fs.cpp – LittleFS file system on external (or internal) flash at `0x10200000` (2 MiB
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] offset). Provides wear‑leveled storage.
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] - json.h – A custom JSON parser / serializer (ECMA‑404) used for configuration or data
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] exchange. Note: the implementation uses deprecated `wstringconvert` (C++17) and lacks proper
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] UTF‑8 handling for `wchart`. Consider replacing with a standards‑compliant library (e.g.,
2026-05-10 14:41:17 [deepseek-v4] `nlohmann/json` or a hand‑rolled UTF‑8 parser).
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] 5. Build and Hardware
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] - build.bat / build.sh – Cross‑platform CMake invocation. The `.bat` copies `.uf2` to `E:`
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] drive (likely Pico mounted as USB mass storage). The `.sh` copies to `/Volumes/RP2350` (macOS).
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] - CMakeLists.txt – Correctly includes SDK, PIO generators, and disables deprecated reset
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] methods. Note: `PICOSTDIOUSBENABLERESETVIAVENDOR_INTERFACE` is spelled with
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] `INTERFACE` (should be `INTERFACE`? The SDK uses `INTERFACE`, but both are defined – harmless).
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] Potential Issues & Suggestions
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] Code Quality
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] - Global mutable state – Large arrays (`evenFrame`, `oddFrame`, `colorWord`) are defined as
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] `uint32_t [...]` without `constexpr` or `const`. Their contents are modified at runtime. Ensure
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] alignment (`alignas(1024)`) for DMA is respected (already done for `colorWord`).
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] - Spin lock in interrupt – `cdcReceiveCallback` calls `spinlockblocking` from a USB
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] callback. This is safe as long as the lock is not held for long, but the critical section should be
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] minimal. The current buffer handling may drop data if the lock is contended.
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] - Deprecated `std::wstringconvert` – `json.h` uses
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] `std::wstringconvert<std::codecvtutf8<wchart>>`. This class is deprecated in C++17 and
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] removed in C++20. Replace with a simple UTF‑8 to UTF‑32 conversion (e.g., manual byte‑by‑byte
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] decode) or use a library.
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] - Potential name collision – `NotAnInt` and `NotANumber` are macros defined in `json.h`;
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] they could conflict with other code. Use `constexpr` or `enum` instead.
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] Hardware / PIO
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] - IRQ handshake mismatch – `vidbit.pio` sets IRQ 0 at the start of active video (`irq set
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] 0`). `wst.pio` waits for IRQ 0. `chroma.pio` waits for IRQ 4 and clears IRQ 4. There is no mention
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] of IRQ 4 being set elsewhere. Either `chroma.pio` is intended to be triggered by a different
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] mechanism, or the IRQ number is wrong. Consider adding a `wait`/`irq` handshake in the video PIO
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] for chroma.
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] - `vidinit` sets `smconfigsetset_pins` with 1 pin, but `vidbit.pio` uses both `set
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] pins` and `set pindirs` on the same pin – this is fine because `pindirs` controls direction, `pins`
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] controls output level.
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] - Teletext line count – `wstEven` has 305+12*2 = 329 words, but the DMA transfer count is
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] set to `wstWords` (329) for even and `wstWords-1` (328) for odd. This may cause a line length
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] mismatch. Verify that the number of words exactly fills the required lines (including the
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] header/body lines) and that the odd field has one fewer line (304 vs 305).
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] Performance
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] - Chroma generation uses a pre‑computed sine table (`sin4[4096]`). The `sin` function in
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] `pal576i.cpp` uses a lookup with `int i4=rad*2048/M_PI;` – this introduces a multiplication and
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] division per pixel. For real‑time chroma modulation, consider using a direct digital synthesis
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] (DDS) approach with incremental phase.
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] - The main loop busy‑waits for DMA completion and polls debug flags. This is acceptable for a demo
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] but could be improved with DMA interrupt callbacks for lower CPU usage.
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] Conclusion
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] The vidbit project is a sophisticated, feature‑rich video generator that demonstrates deep
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] integration of PIO, DMA, and custom rendering. The code is well‑structured overall, with clear
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] separation of modules. Main areas for refinement are the JSON parser’s portability, the
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] teletext/chroma IRQ coordination, and the rendering duplication between progressive and interlaced
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] modes. The inclusion of teletext and chroma colour is particularly impressive for a
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] microcontroller‑based video system.
2026-05-10 14:41:18 [deepseek-v4] 
