2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] goal of 10 cycles per pixel.
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] Current Timing Analysis
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] In your new code:
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] - 8 × `out pindirs, 3` – each takes 1 cycle → 8 cycles
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] - `out null, 8` – shifts 8 bits to null, still 1 cycle
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] - `jmp y--, train` – 1 cycle
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] - Total loop: 10 cycles
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] Each loop processes 8 pixels (since `out pindirs, 3` sets the three pins for one pixel; you
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] have 8 such instructions).
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] Thus: 10 cycles ÷ 8 pixels = 1.25 cycles per pixel
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] This is not 10 cycles per pixel; it is actually much faster.
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] What You Intended
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] You wrote: “threepin is now 10 cycles per pixel”.
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] To achieve exactly 10 cycles per pixel, you need to stretch each pixel output over 10 PIO
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] cycles. The simplest way is to add a 9‑cycle delay after each `out pindirs, 3`:
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] .program threepin
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4]     pull    block
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4]     mov     y, osr
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] train:
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4]     out     pindirs, 3    [9]   ; 1 + 9 = 10 cycles per pixel
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4]     out     pindirs, 3    [9]
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4]     out     pindirs, 3    [9]
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4]     out     pindirs, 3    [9]
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4]     out     pindirs, 3    [9]
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4]     out     pindirs, 3    [9]
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4]     out     pindirs, 3    [9]
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4]     out     pindirs, 3    [9]
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4]     out     pindirs, 3    [9]
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4]     out     pindirs, 3    [9]   ; 10 pixels total
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4]     set     pindirs, 0          ; 1 cycle (idle between pixels)
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4]     out     null, 2             ; discard alpha bits (1 cycle)
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4]     jmp     y--, train          ; 1 cycle
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] .wrap
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] • Cycle count per 10 pixels:*
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] `10 × 10 + 1 (set) + 1 (out null) + 1 (jmp) = 103 cycles`
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] That is 10.3 cycles per pixel – still 0.3 off.
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] To get exactly 10 cycles per pixel (100 cycles per 10 pixels), you must absorb the `set`,
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] `out null`, and `jmp` overhead into the delays. For example:
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] train:
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4]     out     pindirs, 3    [9]   ; 10 cycles, pixel 1
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4]     out     pindirs, 3    [9]   ; 10 cycles, pixel 2
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4]     ; ... (repeat for pixels 3-10) ...
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4]     out     pindirs, 3    [9]   ; pixel 10
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4]     ; Instead of separate set/null/jmp, do the next pixel's out with a longer delay that includes cleanup?
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] • Better approach:* Use a single `out pindirs, 3` per pixel, each with `[9]` delay (10
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] cycles each). The loop overhead (set to zero, null, jmp) can be included in the last pixel’s delay
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] by using a `nop` or by shifting the timing boundaries. However, simpler: accept 10.3 cycles/pixel
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] and adjust the pixel counter in `vidbit.pio` accordingly (e.g., 864 pixels × 10.3 = 8899 cycles,
2026-05-11 12:52:55 [deepseek-v4] which may still fit within 8642 active cycles if you reduce pixel count slightly).
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4] Alternative: Reduce Bit Width to Fit Exact 10‑Cycle Pixel
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4] If you want exactly 10 cycles per pixel with no overhead, you can change the pixel format to 2
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4] bits per pixel (2 lanes instead of 3), allowing you to fit 16 pixels per 32‑bit word with no
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4] leftover alpha bits. Then:
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4] .program threepin_2bit
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4]     pull    block
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4]     mov     y, osr
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4] train:
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4]     out     pindirs, 2    [9]   ; 10 cycles per pixel
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4]     out     pindirs, 2    [9]
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4]     ; ... repeat 15 more times for 16 pixels
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4]     ; No set/out null needed; jmp only
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4]     jmp     y--, train
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4] .wrap
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4] That yields exactly 10 cycles per pixel (16 × 10 = 160 cycles per word, plus 1 for jmp = 161 cycles
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4] for 16 pixels ≈ 10.0625 – still slightly off). To get exactly 10, you would need to absorb the jmp
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4] into one pixel’s delay.
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4] Given PIO constraints, perfect 10 cycles per pixel is difficult without careful side‑set or
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4] using `nop` instructions. Typically, video systems tolerate a small timing jitter (≤ 1 cycle)
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4] because the pixel clock is derived from the same PIO, not an external source.
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4] Recommendation
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4] If your downstream hardware expects exactly 10 PIO cycles per pixel, the simplest fix is to add
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4] a 9‑cycle delay to each `out` and adjust the pixel count in `vidbit.pio` to match the
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4] slightly longer active period. If you cannot tolerate the 0.3 cycle error, consider using a
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4] different PIO instruction that allows combining output and delay more efficiently (e.g., side‑set
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4] on `out`).
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4] Let me know if you would like a version that uses side‑set to eliminate overhead entirely – I can
2026-05-11 12:52:56 [deepseek-v4] provide that.
