2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] Thank you for sharing this comprehensive collection of PIO state machine programs. The files
2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] present a highly cohesive architecture for audio, video, and peripheral interfacing, clearly
2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] optimized for the Raspberry Pi Pico/Pico 2 running at system clocks of 150MHz and 166.5MHz.
2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here are a few technical observations on the shared content:
2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Video and Synchronization Pipeline*
2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] The approach to generating PAL 576i video is well-structured. By separating the core line timings
2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] into `vidbit.pio` and using hardware IRQs (such as `irq 0` and `irq 4`) to synchronize auxiliary
2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] state machines like `chroma.pio` and `wst.pio`, you ensure tight phase alignment without
2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] overloading a single state machine. The inclusion of `wst.pio` for World System Teletext generation
2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] at 6.9375 Mbps is a particularly interesting addition to the VBI (Vertical Blanking Interval)
2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] pipeline.
2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Peripheral Interfacing*
2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] `rc5.pio` makes excellent use of the bitwise inversion instruction (`mov y, ~x`) to maintain a
2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] high-resolution 32-bit counter, allowing for precise 20ns pulse-width measurements. `ps2.pio`
2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] effectively handles the synchronous clock/data protocol entirely within the state machine,
2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] offloading the bit-banging overhead from the main CPU.
2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Audio Implementations*
2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] `i2s.pio` efficiently handles 16-bit stereo output using standard PIO delay cycles to match the
2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] 48kHz target. I do note that `spdif.pio` currently contains a single `nop` instruction acting as a
2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] placeholder, though your comments clearly outline the targeted bi-phase mark decoding and
2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] cycle-per-bit timings for standard sample rates.
2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Data Flow*
2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] The use of blocking pushes and pulls across `pixelpump.pio`, `onebit.pio`, and `threepin.pio`
2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] indicates a robust multi-SM pipeline. Relying on the FIFO backpressure to pace the bit trains is a
2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] highly efficient use of the RP2040/RP2350 architecture.
2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] Please let me know if you would like to discuss the architecture further, if you need assistance
2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] fleshing out the `spdif.pio` decoder, or if you would like me to draft any of the accompanying
2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] C/C++ host configuration functions for these programs.
2026-05-11 12:54:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 12:54:47 [nitro@ryzen5] for pixelpump are comments complete?
2026-05-11 12:55:04 [gemini-3.1-pro] The comments for `pixelpump.pio` provide excellent system-level context, particularly regarding the
2026-05-11 12:55:04 [gemini-3.1-pro] 64µs PAL line timing (10656 cycles at 166.5MHz) and the multi-SM pipeline (`pixelpump -> onebit ->
2026-05-11 12:55:04 [gemini-3.1-pro] threepin`).
2026-05-11 12:55:04 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 12:55:04 [gemini-3.1-pro] There are two minor details you might consider adjusting for absolute completeness and accuracy:
2026-05-11 12:55:04 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 12:55:04 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. Loop Count Discrepancy:
2026-05-11 12:55:04 [gemini-3.1-pro]    The comment states `; vidbit linewidth 864 = 27 words`. However, the PIO instruction `set x, 27`
2026-05-11 12:55:04 [gemini-3.1-pro] followed by a `jmp x-- loop` will actually execute the loop 28 times (counting down from 27 to 0
2026-05-11 12:55:04 [gemini-3.1-pro] inclusive). If the goal is exactly 27 words (864 bits), `x` should be set to 26. You may want to
2026-05-11 12:55:04 [gemini-3.1-pro] update either the comment or the `set` instruction so they perfectly align.
2026-05-11 12:55:04 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 12:55:04 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. Initial Color Train Pass-Through:
2026-05-11 12:55:04 [gemini-3.1-pro]    The comments `; color 0 bit train` and `; color 1 bit train` correctly identify what is being
2026-05-11 12:55:04 [gemini-3.1-pro] handled. However, it might be helpful to briefly note why the SM is doing a `pull` followed
2026-05-11 12:55:04 [gemini-3.1-pro] immediately by a `push` here. Since this SM acts as a pump, explicitly stating that it is passing
2026-05-11 12:55:04 [gemini-3.1-pro] the two 32-bit color/palette definitions through its RX FIFO downstream to initialize
2026-05-11 12:55:04 [gemini-3.1-pro] `onebit.pio`'s `x` and `y` scratch registers makes the interaction between the two files instantly
2026-05-11 12:55:04 [gemini-3.1-pro] clear to a reader.
2026-05-11 12:55:04 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 12:55:04 [gemini-3.1-pro] Otherwise, the comments are highly effective at explaining the state machine's role within the
2026-05-11 12:55:04 [gemini-3.1-pro] larger video architecture.
2026-05-11 12:55:04 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 12:56:47 [nitro@ryzen5] is there other ways to pump the two color registers into onebit.pio ?
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] Yes, there are a few alternative ways to initialize or update the `x` and `y` registers in
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] `onebit.pio` without routing them through `pixelpump.pio` as a pass-through.
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] Since `onebit` is a separate state machine, it has its own independent TX FIFO. Depending on
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] whether these colors are static for the whole frame, or change dynamically, you might consider
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] these approaches:
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 1. Direct TX FIFO Pre-loading (Out-of-Band Setup)*
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] Instead of pushing the palette words into the DMA stream that feeds `pixelpump`, the host CPU can
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] write the two 32-bit color words directly into `onebit`'s TX FIFO during system initialization.
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] • How it works: `onebit.pio` keeps its initial `pull block` and `mov` instructions. Before
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] enabling the DMA channels and starting the state machines, the CPU simply pushes the two words to
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] the `onebit` SM.
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Benefit: You can completely remove the first 6 instructions (the pull/mov/push
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] pass-through logic) from `pixelpump.pio`, saving PIO instruction space and simplifying the DMA
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] buffer structure.
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2. Direct CPU Execution (piosmexec)*
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you want to save instruction space in both state machines, you can remove the
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] initialization logic from `onebit.pio` entirely and force-load the registers from the C/C++ host
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] side.
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] • How it works: While the state machine is disabled, the host CPU pushes a value to the TX
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] FIFO, then uses the SDK's `piosmexec` function to manually execute a `pull` instruction
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] followed by a `mov x, osr` instruction. This is repeated for `y`.
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Benefit: This reclaims the instruction slots used by the setup phase in `onebit.pio`,
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] which is highly beneficial if you need to add more complex logic to its core loop later.
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 3. OUT EXEC for Dynamic Mid-Frame Updates*
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] If your architecture requires changing the two color registers mid-frame (for example, changing the
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] palette per-scanline or mid-scanline), passing them through the FIFO is still the best approach.
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] However, you could use the `out exec` instruction.
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] • How it works: You interleave executable PIO instruction opcodes within your pixel data
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] stream. When the SM encounters these specific words, it executes them as instructions (e.g.,
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] pulling new colors) rather than treating them as pixel data.
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Benefit: Allows for seamless, cycle-accurate dynamic palette swaps without halting the
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] state machine or using IRQs.
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] If the color trains are static for the duration of the state machine's run, Option 1 or 2 is
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] generally preferred, as it cleanly separates the setup data from the high-speed pixel pipeline.
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] Let me know if you would like me to draft the C/C++ initialization functions for any of these
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] approaches.
2026-05-11 12:57:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:05:28 [nitro@ryzen5] how fast can i clock rp2350?
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] The official maximum system clock (`clk_sys`) specified by Raspberry Pi for the RP2350 is 150 MHz.
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] However, much like its predecessor, the silicon is highly tolerant of overclocking.
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your current target of 166.5 MHz is a very mild and safe overclock that should run perfectly stable
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] on default settings. If you need more PIO cycles per pixel or sample, here is what you can
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] generally expect when pushing the RP2350 further:
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Moderate Overclocking (150 MHz to ~250 MHz)*
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] Most RP2350 chips will comfortably reach 200 MHz to 250 MHz at the default core voltage (1.10V).
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] This range is usually plug-and-play and requires nothing more than configuring the system PLL to
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] the desired frequency.
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Aggressive Overclocking (~250 MHz to ~350+ MHz)*
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] To push into the 300 MHz range and beyond, you will typically need to increase the core voltage.
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] The RP2350's internal voltage regulator (VREG) can be adjusted via software (up to 1.30V). Many
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] users have reported stable operation in the 300–350 MHz range with a slight voltage bump.
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Extreme Overclocking (400 MHz+)*
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] While achievable, pushing past 400 MHz enters the "silicon lottery" territory. It requires maximum
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] safe voltage, potentially active cooling, and careful management of peripheral clocks.
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Technical Considerations When Overclocking:*
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Flash Memory Speed: The QSPI flash chip is usually the first bottleneck. Standard flash
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] chips are rated for around 100 MHz to 133 MHz. When pushing the system clock high, you will need to
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] increase the flash clock divider in the XIP (Execute In Place) peripheral to ensure the flash chip
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] isn't driven beyond its limits, otherwise the system will hard fault when fetching instructions.
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] • USB Clock: The USB peripheral strictly requires 48 MHz. The SDK handles this well by
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] keeping the USB PLL separate from the System PLL, but it is worth keeping in mind if you start
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] adjusting PLLs manually.
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Power and Thermal: Higher clocks and voltages will significantly increase the power draw
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] and thermal output of the chip.
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you find yourself needing more than 10656 cycles per line for your PAL video generation, pushing
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] the clock to something like 252 MHz (which is a clean multiple for many video timings) is highly
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] achievable on this hardware.
2026-05-11 13:05:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
