2026-05-11 13:13:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] boundaries for every iteration.
2026-05-11 13:13:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Cycle Counting: The loop is beautifully optimized to exactly 10 cycles per iteration (7
2026-05-11 13:13:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] `out`s + 1 `set` + 1 `out null` + 1 `jmp`). This perfectly mirrors the timing defined in
2026-05-11 13:13:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] `vidbit.pio` (`active: out pindirs, 1 [8]`, which also yields 10 cycles per pixel).
2026-05-11 13:13:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Hardware Behavior: By using `set pindirs, 0`, you are actively driving the pins for 7
2026-05-11 13:13:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] cycles and then releasing them to High-Z (inputs) for the remaining 3 cycles of the pixel duration.
2026-05-11 13:13:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] This is an elegant way to shape the analog output waveform for your reconstruction filter.
2026-05-11 13:13:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:13:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] The pipeline from `pixelpump` to `threepin` is completely mathematically sound. Let me know if you
2026-05-11 13:13:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] would like to look at `wst.pio` or `spdif.pio` next.
