2026-05-11 13:15:30 [nitro@ryzen5] to drive component video pins what is voltage range for PAL televsion
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] For PAL televisions, analog component video typically refers to either YPbPr or RGB
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] (commonly delivered via a SCART connector in PAL regions).
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] Regardless of the format, standard video inputs are always terminated with a 75Ω resistor
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] to ground at the television. This is critical for your Pico 2 design, as your GPIO series resistors
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] will form a voltage divider with this 75Ω load to achieve the correct target voltages.
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here are the standard voltage ranges for both formats:
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. YPbPr (Standard Component Video)
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Y (Luma + Sync): 1.0V peak-to-peak (Vpp)
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro]     *   Sync tip: -0.3V
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro]     *   Black level (Blanking): 0.0V
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro]     *   Peak white: +0.7V
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Pb and Pr (Color Difference): 0.7V peak-to-peak (Vpp)
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro]     *   These signals do not contain sync pulses.
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro]     *   They are centered at 0.0V, swinging from -0.35V to +0.35V.
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Note for Pico 2:* Because the RP2350 operates on a single 0V to 3.3V supply, you cannot generate
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] the negative voltages natively. The standard practice is to bias your DAC output above 0V and use a
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] series capacitor (typically 100µF to 220µF) to AC-couple the Pb and Pr lines, allowing the
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] television to restore the DC baseline.
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. RGB (SCART Component)
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you are targeting older PAL CRT televisions, RGB over SCART is often preferred over YPbPr.
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Red, Green, Blue lines: 0.7V peak-to-peak
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro]     *   Black level: 0.0V
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro]     *   Peak color: +0.7V
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Sync (CSYNC or CVBS line): 1.0V peak-to-peak
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro]     *   Uses the same -0.3V to +0.7V range as the Y line above.
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Blanking / RGB Switch (Pin 16): 1.0V to 3.0V
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro]     *   A DC voltage applied here tells the TV to switch from Composite to RGB mode.
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] Resistor DAC Considerations
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] Since your Pico 2 outputs ~3.3V when a pin is driven high, driving a 0.7V peak white level into a
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 75Ω load requires an equivalent series resistance of approximately 280Ω from the GPIO pin
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] (using the voltage divider formula: `3.3V * (75 / (75 + R)) = 0.7V`).
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] For a multi-bit DAC (like the one hinted at in `chroma.pio` and `threepin.pio`), your parallel
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] resistor network will need to be calculated so that the maximum combined current drops exactly 0.7V
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] across the TV's internal 75Ω resistor.
2026-05-11 13:15:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:16:58 [nitro@ryzen5] happy to generate 0 to 0.7 volts, what resistor on GPIO?
2026-05-11 13:17:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] To generate a 0 to 0.7V signal from a 3.3V GPIO pin into a television, you are essentially creating
2026-05-11 13:17:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] a voltage divider with the television's internal 75Ω termination resistor.
2026-05-11 13:17:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:17:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] The ideal series resistor value is 279Ω.
2026-05-11 13:17:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:17:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is the breakdown of why, and what standard components to use:
2026-05-11 13:17:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:17:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] • The Math*
2026-05-11 13:17:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] Using the standard voltage divider formula:
2026-05-11 13:17:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] `Target Voltage = GPIO Voltage * (TV Resistance / (Series Resistor + TV Resistance))`
2026-05-11 13:17:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] `0.7V = 3.3V * (75Ω / (R + 75Ω))`
2026-05-11 13:17:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] Solving for R gives approximately 278.57Ω.
2026-05-11 13:17:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:17:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Standard Resistor Values to Use*
2026-05-11 13:17:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] Since 279Ω is not a standard resistor value, you have a couple of practical options:
2026-05-11 13:17:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:17:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 270Ω (Standard E12 value): This is the most common choice. It will give you approximately
2026-05-11 13:17:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 0.71V, which is well within the safe tolerance of any PAL television.
2026-05-11 13:17:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 287Ω (Standard E48 value): If you want to be slightly conservative, this gives you
2026-05-11 13:17:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] approximately 0.68V.
2026-05-11 13:17:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:17:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] • A Note on GPIO Internal Resistance*
2026-05-11 13:17:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Raspberry Pi Pico's GPIO pins have a small amount of internal resistance (output impedance),
2026-05-11 13:17:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] which varies depending on the drive strength you configure in software (typically around 20Ω to 30Ω
2026-05-11 13:17:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] at default settings).
2026-05-11 13:17:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:17:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because of this internal resistance, a 270Ω resistor is usually perfect in the real world.
2026-05-11 13:17:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you find the image is slightly too dark, you can safely drop down to a 240Ω or even
2026-05-11 13:17:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 220Ω resistor to compensate for the GPIO pad's internal impedance.
2026-05-11 13:17:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
