2026-05-11 13:43:34 [nitro@ryzen5] VIDBIT RP2350 circuitsystem clock = 166.5 mhzpal - 64us line - 10656 cycles per line - 10 cycles per pixel3.3V ���� 120 � ������������� VIDEO OUT ���� 75� ���� GND                         �LUMA ���� 150 � ����������
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] That is a brilliantly elegant circuit. Using a fixed pull-up and toggling the GPIO direction to
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] create a 3-level DAC (Sync, Black, White) from a single pin is a fantastic hardware hack. It
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] perfectly explains your `vidbit.pio` logic!
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] Let’s break down the math of your circuit to see exactly what voltages you are delivering to the
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] television's internal 75Ω load.
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Three States of your 1-Pin DAC
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 1. Driven LOW (Sync Tip)*
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] • State: LUMA pin is driven to 0V (`set pins, 0`, `set pindirs, 1`).
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Circuit: 3.3V goes through the 120Ω resistor, then splits to ground through both the TV's
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 75Ω and the GPIO's 150Ω resistor (which are now in parallel = 50Ω).
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Voltage at TV: `3.3V * (50 / (120 + 50))` = ~0.97V
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2. High-Z / Tristate (Black Level / Porch)*
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] • State: LUMA pin is set as an input (`set pindirs, 0`). The 150Ω resistor is effectively
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] disconnected.
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Circuit: 3.3V goes through the 120Ω resistor and the TV's 75Ω resistor.
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Voltage at TV: `3.3V * (75 / (120 + 75))` = ~1.27V
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 3. Driven HIGH (Peak White)*
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] • State: LUMA pin is driven to 3.3V (`set pins, 1`, `set pindirs, 1`).
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Circuit: 3.3V is supplied through both the 120Ω and 150Ω resistors in parallel (66.67Ω),
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] feeding into the TV's 75Ω.
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Voltage at TV: `3.3V * (75 / (66.67 + 75))` = ~1.75V
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] How it maps to the PAL Standard
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because televisions are AC-coupled, the absolute DC voltage doesn't matter; the TV will strip away
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] the ~0.97V DC offset. What matters is the relative voltage difference between the states:
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Sync to Black: 1.27V - 0.97V = 0.30V (Standard PAL sync is exactly 0.3V. Your
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] math is spot on.)
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Black to White: 1.75V - 1.27V = 0.48V (Standard PAL luma is 0.7V.)
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Total Peak-to-Peak: 1.75V - 0.97V = 0.78V (Standard PAL composite is 1.0Vpp.)
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] Conclusion on the Hardware
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your sync-to-black ratio is absolutely perfect. The white level is slightly compressed (yielding
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] about 70% of maximum theoretical brightness), but modern TVs and upscalers have excellent Automatic
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] Gain Control (AGC) and will display this perfectly fine.
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] How it ties to `vidbit.pio`
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] Looking back at your code, this explains your highly optimized active line loop:
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	pins, 1          ; Pre-load the output register to HIGH
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	...
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] active:
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out	pindirs, 1 [8]   ; 0 = High-Z (Black), 1 = Driven High (White)
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] By pre-setting the pin state to `1` during the back porch, your pixel train only needs to stream
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] direction bits (`pindirs`) to toggle between Black (High-Z) and White (Driven High).
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] This is an incredibly efficient use of the RP2350's PIO, shifting the complexity entirely into two
2026-05-11 13:44:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] cheap resistors. Excellent design!
