2026-05-11 13:58:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] What you are seeing is exactly the expected hardware behavior. The 'Y' (green) jack on a component
2026-05-11 13:58:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] input expects Luma + Sync. Because it is essentially just a standard black-and-white video signal,
2026-05-11 13:58:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] leaving the Pb and Pr (color difference) lines disconnected means the TV reads a color difference
2026-05-11 13:58:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] of zero, resulting in a perfectly stable grayscale image.
2026-05-11 13:58:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 13:58:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] The fact that it is syncing well confirms that your 120Ω/150Ω tristate DAC is delivering a clean,
2026-05-11 13:58:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] readable sync tip (the 0.3V drop we calculated earlier) and that your PIO timings in `vidbit.pio`
2026-05-11 13:58:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] are well within the tolerances of the TV's sync separator. The TV's Automatic Gain Control (AGC) is
2026-05-11 13:58:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] likely doing the rest of the work to stretch your ~0.48V luma range up to a comfortable viewing
2026-05-11 13:58:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] brightness.
