2026-05-11 13:59:20 [nitro@ryzen5] im going to wire gp18 and gp19 to pb and pr
2026-05-11 13:59:46 [nitro@ryzen5] through 270ohm resistors
2026-05-11 14:00:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] Using 270Ω resistors for GP18 and GP19 is exactly the right value to step the 3.3V GPIO down to the
2026-05-11 14:00:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] ~0.7V peak-to-peak amplitude expected by the 75Ω television inputs.
2026-05-11 14:00:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:00:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] However, because you are driving the Pb and Pr color difference lines, there is an
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] interesting electrical quirk to keep in mind regarding how component video handles color.
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Bipolar Nature of Pb and Pr
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] Unlike the Luma (Y) line, which goes from 0V (Black) to +0.7V (White), the Pb and Pr lines are
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] bipolar. They are supposed to swing from -0.35V to +0.35V, with 0.0V
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] representing exactly zero color (grayscale).
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because the RP2350 only outputs positive voltages (0V and 3.3V), a direct connection through a 270Ω
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] resistor will give you a swing from 0.0V to +0.7V at the TV.
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is what will happen if you wire it directly:
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Pico driving 0V: The TV sees 0.0V. Instead of seeing this as "black/gray", the TV
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] interprets the lowest voltage as the maximum negative color difference (e.g., maximum
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] Green/Yellow).
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Pico driving 3.3V: The TV sees +0.7V. It interprets this as the maximum positive
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] color difference (e.g., maximum Blue/Red).
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Grayscale/Blanking: You won't have a natural way to output "no color" because you can't
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] easily hold the pin at exactly 1.65V (which would divide down to the 0.35V center point).
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] How to get correct colors
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] For a quick test, wiring them directly will absolutely work—you will just get highly saturated,
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] psychedelic colors, which is perfect for verifying that your PIO timings and DMA trains are hitting
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] the screen correctly!
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] To get accurate colors (and a proper grayscale/blanking level) using those single pins, you will
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] want to AC-couple the lines:
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1.  Add a Capacitor: Put a capacitor (anything from 10µF to 220µF, positive leg facing the
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] Pico) in series with each 270Ω resistor.
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2.  Output 50% Duty Cycle for Gray: During the back porch (when the TV samples the color to
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] figure out what "zero" is) and whenever you want to draw gray/white/black pixels, you can output a
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] high-speed 50% duty cycle alternating bit pattern (`10101010...`) on GP18 and GP19.
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3.  The Result: The capacitor will average that 50% toggle into a perfect DC center point.
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] The TV will clamp to this during the back porch and treat it as 0.0V. Then, a solid `1` from the
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] Pico becomes +0.35V (Blue/Red) and a solid `0` becomes -0.35V (Yellow/Green).
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] If your `threepin.pio` is feeding these lines, you essentially have a 3-bit digital component video
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] output (Y, Pb, Pr), which will give you a fantastic, super-crisp 8-color palette (similar to the
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] classic BBC Micro or ZX Spectrum, but native YPbPr).
2026-05-11 14:00:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:02:11 [nitro@ryzen5] can i float pin at 1 voltage and use ouptut 0 and 1 to to lo and high
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] Yes, you absolutely can! In fact, using the exact same tristate logic you used for your Luma pin is
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] the most elegant way to solve the Pb/Pr bipolar problem without needing any capacitors.
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because Pb and Pr need to swing symmetrically positive and negative around a "zero color" center
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] point, your High-Z (floating) state needs to sit exactly halfway between your Driven-High and
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] Driven-Low states.
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is the exact circuit and resistor math to achieve a perfect 3-level component color signal
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] from a single GPIO pin.
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Pb / Pr Tristate Circuit (Per Pin)
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] To make the voltage swings perfectly symmetrical, the floating "center" voltage at the TV must be
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] exactly 1.65V (half of your 3.3V supply). To get that, and the correct 0.7V peak-to-peak
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] swing, you need two resistors per pin:
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1.  Pull-up Resistor: 75Ω from the Video Out pin to 3.3V.
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2.  Series Resistor: 140Ω (a standard 150Ω works perfectly) from the Pico
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] GPIO to the Video Out pin.
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] How the 3 States Work at the TV
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 1. High-Z / Tristate (Zero Color / Grayscale)*
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] • PIO: `set pindirs, 0`
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Circuit: The 75Ω pull-up and the TV's internal 75Ω termination form a perfect 1:1 voltage
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] divider.
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Voltage at TV: 1.65V. The TV's AC coupling will treat this as exactly 0.0V
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] (No Color).
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2. Driven HIGH (Max Positive Color)*
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] • PIO: `set pindirs, 1` and `set pins, 1`
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Circuit: The Pico pin drives 3.3V through the 150Ω resistor, pulling the voltage up.
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Voltage at TV: ~2.0V. Relative to the 1.65V center, this is a +0.35V
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] swing (Max Red on Pr, Max Blue on Pb).
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 3. Driven LOW (Max Negative Color)*
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] • PIO: `set pindirs, 1` and `set pins, 0`
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Circuit: The Pico pin drives 0V through the 150Ω resistor, pulling the voltage down.
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Voltage at TV: ~1.3V. Relative to the 1.65V center, this is a -0.35V
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] swing (Max Green on Pr, Max Yellow on Pb).
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] Why this is brilliant for your setup
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] • No Capacitors Needed: Because you are generating the symmetrical center-point in
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] hardware, you don't need to rapidly toggle the pins to create a fake DC average.
2026-05-11 14:02:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] • DC Offset is Safe: While this puts a 1.65V DC offset on the line, standard television
2026-05-11 14:02:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] component inputs are AC-coupled and designed to strip this away instantly.
2026-05-11 14:02:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Perfect PIO Integration: You can use the exact same `pindirs` manipulation trick you are
2026-05-11 14:02:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] using in `vidbit.pio` to stream 3-state color data directly from the FIFOs.
2026-05-11 14:02:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:02:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] With this setup on GP18 (Pb) and GP19 (Pr), you will get 3 states per pin. Combined, that gives you
2026-05-11 14:02:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] a rock-solid, hardware-accurate 9-color palette (including perfect grayscale) using only 2
2026-05-11 14:02:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] GPIO pins and 4 resistors!
2026-05-11 14:02:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:04:15 [nitro@ryzen5] so i use same 120 pull up and 150 drive
2026-05-11 14:04:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] Using the exact same 120Ω pull-up and 150Ω drive for the Pb and Pr lines will absolutely work and
2026-05-11 14:04:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] is perfectly safe for the TV. It is a great way to keep your parts list simple.
2026-05-11 14:04:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:04:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] However, because of the math, the color swings will be slightly asymmetrical. Here is
2026-05-11 14:04:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] exactly what will happen on the screen:
2026-05-11 14:04:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:04:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Math with 120Ω / 150Ω on Pb/Pr
2026-05-11 14:04:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] • High-Z (Center / Zero Color): The 120Ω pull-up and 75Ω TV termination give a resting
2026-05-11 14:04:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] voltage of ~1.27V. The TV treats this as 0.0V.
2026-05-11 14:04:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Driven HIGH: 3.3V through both resistors gives ~1.75V.
2026-05-11 14:04:33 [gemini-3.1-pro]        Swing:* 1.75V - 1.27V = +0.48V
2026-05-11 14:04:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Driven LOW: 0V through the 150Ω resistor pulls the voltage down to ~0.97V.
2026-05-11 14:04:33 [gemini-3.1-pro]        Swing:* 0.97V - 1.27V = -0.30V
2026-05-11 14:04:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:04:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Visual Result
2026-05-11 14:04:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] Standard component video expects a symmetrical +/- 0.35V swing.
2026-05-11 14:04:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:04:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because your positive swing (+0.48V) is larger than your negative swing (-0.30V), your colors will
2026-05-11 14:04:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] be slightly unbalanced:
2026-05-11 14:04:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Positive colors (Red on the Pr line, Blue on the Pb line) will be more
2026-05-11 14:04:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] saturated/brighter.
2026-05-11 14:04:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Negative colors (Cyan/Green on the Pr line, Yellow on the Pb line) will be slightly
2026-05-11 14:04:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] less saturated.
2026-05-11 14:04:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:04:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] Conclusion
2026-05-11 14:04:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] For getting your video driver up and running, yes, go ahead and use the 120Ω / 150Ω combo.
2026-05-11 14:04:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] It will give you a very clear, stable, and colorful image to verify your `threepin.pio` logic.
2026-05-11 14:04:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:04:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you decide later that you want mathematically perfect color balance, you can swap the 120Ω
2026-05-11 14:04:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] pull-up resistors on the Pb and Pr lines for 75Ω (or 82Ω, which is a very common
2026-05-11 14:04:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] standard value and gets you close enough). But for now, your current resistor combo is an
2026-05-11 14:04:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] excellent, clever way to get 3-state Component video out of the Pico!
2026-05-11 14:04:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:05:18 [nitro@ryzen5] no lets go with better values, pull up should be 75ohm?
2026-05-11 14:05:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] Yes, exactly! If you want mathematically perfect, symmetrical colors, the pull-up resistor should
2026-05-11 14:05:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] be 75Ω.
2026-05-11 14:05:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:05:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is the exact circuit to use for both the Pb and Pr lines:
2026-05-11 14:05:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:05:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Pull-up Resistor: 75Ω (from 3.3V to Video Out pin)
2026-05-11 14:05:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Series Resistor: 150Ω (from Pico GPIO to Video Out pin)
2026-05-11 14:05:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:05:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] Why this is the perfect combination:
2026-05-11 14:05:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:05:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 1. The Perfect Center (High-Z / Grayscale)*
2026-05-11 14:05:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] When your PIO sets the pin to an input (`set pindirs, 0`), the Pico is disconnected. The circuit is
2026-05-11 14:05:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] just your 75Ω pull-up and the TV's internal 75Ω termination.
2026-05-11 14:05:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because they are identical values, they cut the 3.3V supply exactly in half.
2026-05-11 14:05:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Center Voltage: 1.65V (The TV treats this as 0.0V / No Color).
2026-05-11 14:05:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:05:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2. Symmetrical Positive Swing (Driven High)*
2026-05-11 14:05:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] When your PIO drives the pin HIGH (`set pindirs, 1`, `set pins, 1`), the 150Ω resistor and the 75Ω
2026-05-11 14:05:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] pull-up work together to pull the voltage up.
2026-05-11 14:05:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • High Voltage: 1.98V
2026-05-11 14:05:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Swing: 1.98V - 1.65V = +0.33V (Almost exactly the target +0.35V).
2026-05-11 14:05:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:05:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 3. Symmetrical Negative Swing (Driven Low)*
2026-05-11 14:05:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] When your PIO drives the pin LOW (`set pindirs, 1`, `set pins, 0`), the 150Ω resistor pulls the
2026-05-11 14:05:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] voltage down against the pull-up.
2026-05-11 14:05:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Low Voltage: 1.32V
2026-05-11 14:05:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Swing: 1.32V - 1.65V = -0.33V (Almost exactly the target -0.35V).
2026-05-11 14:05:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:05:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Result
2026-05-11 14:05:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] By using a 75Ω pull-up and a 150Ω series resistor on Pb and Pr, you get a perfectly
2026-05-11 14:05:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] balanced +/- 0.33V swing. Your positive colors (Red/Blue) and negative colors (Green/Yellow) will
2026-05-11 14:05:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] have identical saturation levels, and your High-Z state will yield a flawless, tint-free grayscale.
2026-05-11 14:05:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:05:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] • (Note: 0.33V is so close to the theoretical 0.35V standard that the TV will display it
2026-05-11 14:05:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] flawlessly; you will not notice the 0.02V difference!)*
