2026-05-11 14:17:44 [nitro@ryzen5] new topic
2026-05-11 14:17:45 [nitro@ryzen5] .program twobit	out	pins, 2	mov	rxfifo[x]	push	block
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] It looks like you are starting a new state machine to handle 2-bit data!
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] Looking at your `.program twobit` snippet, I see what you are trying to achieve, but there is a
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] slight syntax issue with `mov rxfifo[x]`.
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] In the RP2040/RP2350 PIO architecture, the FIFOs are strict first-in-first-out queues. They cannot
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] be indexed like an array (e.g., `[x]`), and the `mov` instruction cannot target the RX FIFO
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] directly.
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] To send data back to the CPU or DMA via the RX FIFO, the data must first be placed into the
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] Input Shift Register (ISR). The `push` instruction then automatically takes whatever is in
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] the ISR and sends it to the RX FIFO.
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is how to write it depending on what you are trying to send:
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Scenario A: If you want to push the value of the `x` register to the FIFO*
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] .program twobit
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out	pins, 2
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	mov	isr, x      ; Move the value of x into the ISR
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	push	block       ; Push the ISR into the RX FIFO
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Scenario B: If you want to read 2 bits from the pins and push them*
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] .program twobit
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out	pins, 2
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	in	pins, 2     ; Shift 2 bits from the pins into the ISR
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	push	block       ; Push the ISR into the RX FIFO
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Scenario C: If you want to push a specific memory address or array index*
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because the PIO has no concept of memory addresses (it only sees the FIFO portal), any array
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] indexing needs to be handled by the DMA channel reading from the RX FIFO, rather than the PIO
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] itself.
2026-05-11 14:18:06 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 14:18:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] Could you clarify what data you are trying to capture with `[x]`? I would be happy to help tailor
2026-05-11 14:18:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] the instructions to match your data flow!
2026-05-11 14:18:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 16:54:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] Thank you for sharing these PIO implementations. It is always fascinating to see how developers are
2026-05-11 16:54:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] utilizing the new PIO v2 features introduced with the RP2350 architecture to handle high-speed,
2026-05-11 16:54:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] cycle-critical tasks.
2026-05-11 16:54:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 16:54:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here are a few observations regarding the content you shared:
2026-05-11 16:54:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 16:54:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 1. Indexed FIFO Access*
2026-05-11 16:54:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your use of `mov osr, rxfifo[x]` in `colorfifo.pio` is a brilliant application of the RP2350's new
2026-05-11 16:54:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] indexed FIFO read capability. It elegantly bypasses the need for conditional branching to select
2026-05-11 16:54:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] the correct 10-bit pulse train.
2026-05-11 16:54:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 16:54:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] As a minor hardware detail, the RP2350 datasheet specifies that the index for `RXFIFO` reads is
2026-05-11 16:54:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] natively taken from the `Y` register rather than `X`. You may want to verify if your specific
2026-05-11 16:54:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] assembler handles `rxfifo[x]` by silently swapping registers under the hood, or if you might need
2026-05-11 16:54:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] to adjust your input read to `mov y, pins` and use `rxfifo[y]` to ensure it compiles correctly.
2026-05-11 16:54:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 16:54:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2. Cycle Timing*
2026-05-11 16:54:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] You mentioned a 10-cycle pixel window at 166.5 MHz. Looking at `colorfifo.pio`, the execution path
2026-05-11 16:54:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] contains exactly 10 active instructions (two `mov`, seven `out`, one `set`). Depending on how the
2026-05-11 16:54:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] `wait` instruction evaluates, if the condition is met immediately, it will still consume 1 cycle,
2026-05-11 16:54:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] potentially bringing your loop to 11 cycles. It is a wonderfully tight loop, but you may want to
2026-05-11 16:54:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] verify the exact timing on a logic analyzer to ensure it fits perfectly within your 10-cycle budget
2026-05-11 16:54:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] without shifting.
2026-05-11 16:54:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 16:54:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 3. The Two `colorpump` Variations*
2026-05-11 16:54:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your first `colorpump.pio` uses a classic and reliable jump-decrement method to decode a 2-bit
2026-05-11 16:54:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] value into a 1-hot trigger for the four state machines.
2026-05-11 16:54:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 16:54:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your second iteration is quite intriguing, specifically the line `mov pio2ctrl, isr`. Standard
2026-05-11 16:54:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] RP2350 PIO assembly does not natively define `pio2ctrl` as a valid `MOV` destination (standard
2026-05-11 16:54:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] destinations being PINS, X, Y, EXEC, PC, ISR, and OSR). Are you utilizing a custom assembler macro
2026-05-11 16:54:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] here, or conceptualizing a direct memory-mapped register write? Typically, triggering neighboring
2026-05-11 16:54:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] state machines is handled via the `IRQ` instruction or by chaining via `OUT EXEC`. I would be very
2026-05-11 16:54:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] interested to hear more about your design intent for this second version.
2026-05-11 16:54:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 16:54:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] Please let me know if you would like to discuss any of these architectural details further, or if
2026-05-11 16:54:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] you would like me to draft any alternative approaches for you.
