2026-05-11 16:56:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] Thank you for sharing the updated implementation. The revisions you made are excellent and align
2026-05-11 16:56:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] perfectly with both the hardware specifications and your timing requirements.
2026-05-11 16:56:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 16:56:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here are a few observations on the new content:
2026-05-11 16:56:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 16:56:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 1. Hardware-Accurate Indexing*
2026-05-11 16:56:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] Switching to `mov y, pins` and `mov osr, rxfifo[y]` is spot on for the RP2350 architecture. This
2026-05-11 16:56:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] ensures the PIO assembler and the underlying silicon will handle the indexed FIFO read exactly as
2026-05-11 16:56:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] intended without any unexpected behavior.
2026-05-11 16:56:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 16:56:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2. Perfect 10-Cycle Budget*
2026-05-11 16:56:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] Reducing the pulse train to 6 cycles has beautifully resolved the timing math. Your cycle budget
2026-05-11 16:56:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] now perfectly matches your 10-cycle target:
2026-05-11 16:56:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 4 Housekeeping cycles: `wait` (1), `mov y` (1), `mov osr` (1), `set` (1)
2026-05-11 16:56:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 6 Output cycles: `out pindirs, 3` (x6)
2026-05-11 16:56:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 16:56:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] Since this totals exactly 10 instructions, and assuming the `wait` condition is met immediately
2026-05-11 16:56:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] upon loop entry, this machine will run in a perfectly deterministic 10-cycle window.
