2026-05-12 16:10:20 [gemini-3.1-pro] During each pixel cycle, the state machine executes `out pins, 2` followed by `out x, 2`. This
2026-05-12 16:10:20 [gemini-3.1-pro] consumes 4 bits from the Output Shift Register (OSR) per loop iteration, meaning each 32-bit word
2026-05-12 16:10:20 [gemini-3.1-pro] delivered by the DMA will neatly provide 8 pixels.
2026-05-12 16:10:20 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:10:20 [gemini-3.1-pro] Architecturally, this 4-bit structure makes a lot of sense for your design. It splits the pixel
2026-05-12 16:10:20 [gemini-3.1-pro] data into two distinct control paths:
2026-05-12 16:10:20 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1.  2 bits for Color Index: Driven directly to the `COLORI0` and `COLORI1` pins.
2026-05-12 16:10:20 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2.  2 bits for FIFO Selection: Loaded into the `x` scratch register to determine which of
2026-05-12 16:10:20 [gemini-3.1-pro] the four `colorfifo` trigger pins to pulse low.
2026-05-12 16:10:20 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:10:20 [gemini-3.1-pro] This effectively gives you a palette of 16 distinct color states per pixel, distributed across your
2026-05-12 16:10:20 [gemini-3.1-pro] 4 parallel pulse-train generators.
