2026-05-12 16:12:47 [nitro@ryzen5] is colortest correct length for each line of video color?
2026-05-12 16:12:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] Yes, `colorTest` is exactly the correct length for one line of video color.
2026-05-12 16:12:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:12:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is the math based on your code to confirm it:
2026-05-12 16:12:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:12:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. Buffer Size: You defined `colorTest` as `lineWords * 4`. From `monobit.h`, `lineWords`
2026-05-12 16:12:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] is 27.
2026-05-12 16:12:58 [gemini-3.1-pro]     `27  4 = 108` 32-bit words per line.
2026-05-12 16:12:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. Bits per Pixel: As we discussed earlier, your `colorpump.pio` consumes 4 bits per pixel
2026-05-12 16:12:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] (`out pins, 2` and `out x, 2`).
2026-05-12 16:12:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3. Pixels per Word: A 32-bit word divided by 4 bits gives you 8 pixels per word.
2026-05-12 16:12:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] 4. Total Pixels: 108 words × 8 pixels per word = 864 pixels.
2026-05-12 16:12:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:12:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] This perfectly matches your `lineWidth` constant of 864. So `colorTest` will hold exactly one full
2026-05-12 16:12:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] line of color index and FIFO trigger data.
2026-05-12 16:12:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:14:21 [nitro@ryzen5] what should set ring be?
2026-05-12 16:14:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] For the `colorpumpdmainit` configuration, the ring should be removed entirely.
2026-05-12 16:14:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:14:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is why, along with a couple of important details about how the RP2040/RP2350 DMA ring hardware
2026-05-12 16:14:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] works:
2026-05-12 16:14:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:14:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 1. Buffer Size Must Be a Power of 2*
2026-05-12 16:14:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] The DMA hardware ring feature only works on boundaries that are a power of 2.
2026-05-12 16:14:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your `colorTest` buffer is `lineWords  4` (27  4 = 108 words). 108 words is 432 bytes.
2026-05-12 16:14:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because 432 is not a power of 2 (it sits between 256 and 512), you cannot use the hardware ring to
2026-05-12 16:14:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] wrap perfectly back to the start of this specific buffer. If you set it to 9 (512 bytes), the DMA
2026-05-12 16:14:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] will read past the end of your 432-byte array and into uninitialized memory before wrapping.
2026-05-12 16:14:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:14:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2. The Ring Direction Boolean*
2026-05-12 16:14:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] In your current code, you have:
2026-05-12 16:14:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] `channelconfigset_ring(&c, true, 9);`
2026-05-12 16:14:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] The boolean parameter specifies which address gets wrapped. `true` applies the ring to the
2026-05-12 16:14:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] write address. Since you are writing to the PIO TX FIFO (where `write_increment` is already
2026-05-12 16:14:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] `false`), this ring setting essentially does nothing. If you ever want to ring a source buffer,
2026-05-12 16:14:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] that parameter must be `false` (apply to the read address).
2026-05-12 16:14:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:14:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] • (Note: You will want to fix this in `chromadmainit` as well! Change it to
2026-05-12 16:14:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] `channelconfigset_ring(&c, false, 10);` because `colorWord` is 256 words = 1024 bytes =
2026-05-12 16:14:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2^10).*
2026-05-12 16:14:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:14:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] • The Solution for ColorPump:*
2026-05-12 16:14:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] Since you cannot hardware-wrap a 108-word buffer, you should handle the `colorpumpdmachan`
2026-05-12 16:14:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] exactly the way you handle `videodmachan`:
2026-05-12 16:14:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:14:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. Remove the `channelconfigsetring` call in `colorpumpdma_init`.
2026-05-12 16:14:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. Change the initial `dmachannelconfigure` transfer count from `UINT32_MAX` to `108` (or
2026-05-12 16:14:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] `lineWords * 4`).
2026-05-12 16:14:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3. Inside your `vidfield` and `vidfieldi` functions, simply reset the read address and
2026-05-12 16:14:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] transfer count for `colorpumpdmachan` right alongside the main video DMA.
2026-05-12 16:14:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:14:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because `colorpump.pio` uses `wait 1 irq 5` to sync itself to every pixel line, feeding it
2026-05-12 16:14:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] line-by-line via DMA in lockstep with your video frame logic will keep everything perfectly
2026-05-12 16:14:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] synchronized.
2026-05-12 16:14:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:18:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] I see the clever workaround you are aiming for with `colorPumpStride=128`! Bumping the array size
2026-05-12 16:18:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] to a power of 2 is the standard way to force compatibility with the DMA ring hardware.
2026-05-12 16:18:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:18:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] However, there are a few parameter corrections needed to make the DMA ring function properly, and a
2026-05-12 16:18:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] logical "gotcha" regarding the video timing that we should discuss.
2026-05-12 16:18:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:18:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. The Ring Parameters (Both DMA channels)
2026-05-12 16:18:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] The `channelconfigsetring` function takes `(config, write, sizebits)`:
2026-05-12 16:18:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `write`: You currently have this set to `true`. This tells the DMA to wrap the
2026-05-12 16:18:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] write address. Since you are writing to the PIO's TX FIFO (a single, non-incrementing
2026-05-12 16:18:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] address), wrapping it does nothing. You need to wrap the read address (your memory array),
2026-05-12 16:18:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] so this must be `false`.
2026-05-12 16:18:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `size_bits`: This is calculated in bytes, not words.
2026-05-12 16:18:15 [gemini-3.1-pro]     *   Colorpump: 128 words × 4 bytes = 512 bytes. 2⁹ = 512. The size should be
2026-05-12 16:18:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] `9` (not `7`).
2026-05-12 16:18:15 [gemini-3.1-pro]     *   Chroma: `colorWord` is 256 words (128 even + 128 odd) × 4 bytes = 1024 bytes. 2^10
2026-05-12 16:18:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] = 1024. The size should be `10` (not `9`).
2026-05-12 16:18:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:18:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is what those lines should look like:
2026-05-12 16:18:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:18:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] // In chroma_dma_init:
2026-05-12 16:18:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] channel_config_set_ring(&c, false, 10); // 1024 byte ring on read address
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] // In colorpump_dma_init:
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] channel_config_set_ring(&c, false, 9);  // 512 byte ring on read address
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. The "Stride" Drift Issue
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] While making `colorTest` 128 words satisfies the DMA hardware, it introduces a desync with your PIO
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] state machine.
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your `colorpump.pio` is pre-loaded with `863` in the `y` register, meaning it consumes exactly
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 864 pixels (which is exactly 108 words) every time `irq 5` fires for a new video
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] line.
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] If your DMA ring is 128 words long, here is what happens:
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Line 1: PIO consumes words 0 through 107.
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Line 2: PIO consumes words 108 through 127, the DMA wraps to 0, and the PIO consumes
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] words 0 through 87.
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Line 3: PIO consumes words 88 through 127... and so on.
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because 108 does not divide evenly into 128, your color pattern will shift horizontally by 20 words
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] (160 pixels) on every single scanline.
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] How to fix the drift:
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] If your goal is to have a static 1-line color test pattern that repeats perfectly on every
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] scanline, the hardware ring buffer cannot be used because 108 is not a power of 2.
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] You have two main architectural choices:
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1.  Full Field Buffer: Make `colorTest` the exact same size as `progressiveFrame`
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] (`progressiveWordCount`), disable the ring buffer entirely, and restart it in `vidfield`
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] exactly like you do with `videodma_chan`. This uses more RAM but allows you to draw actual 2D
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] color graphics.
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2.  DMA Chaining (Ping-Pong): Keep `colorTest` at 108 words, disable the ring buffer, and
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] use a second "control" DMA channel. When the color DMA finishes transferring 108 words, it chains
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] to the control DMA. The control DMA writes the starting address of `colorTest` back into the color
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] DMA's read address register, resetting it instantly for the next line.
2026-05-12 16:18:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:24:00 [nitro@ryzen5] any other ways of resending a fifo?
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] Since the hardware DMA ring is strictly limited to power-of-2 boundaries, you cannot natively loop
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] a 108-word buffer. However, if you want to repeatedly send a small buffer without allocating a full
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] frame of memory, there are three excellent ways to handle this.
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here are the best alternatives, ranked by how well they fit your current architecture:
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. The "Padded Ring" (Hardware Only, Zero CPU)
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] Since you are already controlling the exact pixel count inside your PIO state machine, you can
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] "trick" the DMA ring into working perfectly by padding your buffer.
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] • The Buffer: Size your `colorTest` array to 128 words (which allows a perfect hardware
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] ring of 2⁹ / 512 bytes). Fill the first 108 words with your color data, and the remaining 20 words
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] with zeros (or any dummy data).
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] • The PIO: Modify `colorpump.pio` so that after it finishes its active loop of 864 pixels
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] (108 words), it enters a second, brief loop. This second loop executes 160 times (the remaining 20
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] words × 8 pixels) doing nothing but `out null, 4` to silently consume the padded data.
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] • The Result: The DMA hardware ring wraps perfectly at 128 words, the PIO stays perfectly
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] synced to your video lines via `irq 5`, and the CPU does absolutely nothing.
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. DMA Chaining (The "Official" Microcontroller Way)
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you strictly want to keep your buffer at 108 words, you can use a second DMA channel to act as a
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] "rewind mechanism."
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] • How it works: You set up an unused DMA channel (let's call it the Control Channel). You
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] configure your Color DMA channel so that when it finishes transferring its 108 words, it
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] automatically triggers the Control Channel.
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] • The Control Channel: This channel is configured to transfer exactly one 32-bit
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] word. It reads the memory address of `colorTest` and writes it directly into the
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] `READADDRTRIG` register of the Color DMA channel.
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] • The Result: As soon as the Color DMA finishes a line, the Control channel instantly
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] resets the Color DMA's read pointer back to the start and re-triggers it. It is entirely handled by
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] the DMA bus matrix, meaning zero CPU overhead.
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3. CPU Line Interrupt
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you don't mind a tiny bit of CPU overhead, you can tie an interrupt to the DMA completion.
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] • How it works: Enable the `DMAIRQ0` interrupt for your Color DMA channel. Every
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] time it finishes sending 108 words, the CPU fires an interrupt handler.
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] • The Handler: Inside the Interrupt Service Routine (ISR), you simply call
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] `dmachannelsetreadaddr()` to reset the pointer back to `colorTest`.
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] • The Caveat: Because your PIO is paced by the `166.5MHz` clock and synced to `irq 5`, the
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] CPU must service this interrupt during the horizontal blanking interval before the next
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] line starts. If the CPU is busy (e.g., parsing a long JSON string), the video will glitch.
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 4. Just Use a Full Frame Buffer
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] It is worth noting that your main video is already using a full frame buffer (`progressiveFrame` is
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] ~32KB). If your ultimate goal is to draw 2D color graphics (rather than just a static test
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] pattern), you will eventually need a full frame buffer for the color data anyway.
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] • If you make `colorTest` the same size as `progressiveFrame`, you completely bypass the need for
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] rings or looping. You just reset the DMA once per frame inside your `vid_field` function, exactly
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] like you are already doing for the monochrome video.
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Recommendation:*
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you want a static test pattern, Method 1 (Padded Ring) is the most elegant because it
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] leverages your existing PIO structure. If you plan to draw actual color graphics later, Method
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 4 (Full Frame) is where you will end up anyway!
2026-05-12 16:24:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:26:00 [nitro@ryzen5] dma chaining sounds like me
2026-05-12 16:27:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] DMA chaining is a fantastic hardware-level approach, and it is definitely the "microcontroller way"
2026-05-12 16:27:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] to handle this.
2026-05-12 16:27:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:27:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] However, there is a famous "gotcha" with RP2040/RP2350 DMA: when a DMA channel finishes
2026-05-12 16:27:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] transferring, its `transcount` goes to 0. If you want to loop a buffer, you can't just chain to
2026-05-12 16:27:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] a channel that rewrites the `readaddr`; you have to rewrite the `trans_count` as well.
2026-05-12 16:27:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:27:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] To do this elegantly without CPU overhead during the active frame, we use a Scatter-Gather
2026-05-12 16:27:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] Control Channel.
2026-05-12 16:27:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:27:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is the trick: We create a second DMA channel (the Control Channel) that reads from a small
2026-05-12 16:27:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] array of `[transcount, readaddr]` pairs. We use the DMA's hardware `RING` feature on the
2026-05-12 16:27:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] write address to force it to toggle perfectly between the Data Channel's `TRANSCOUNT`
2026-05-12 16:27:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] and `READADDR_TRIG` registers.
2026-05-12 16:27:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:27:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is the exact code to implement this in your project.
2026-05-12 16:27:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:27:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. Add the Control Blocks Array
2026-05-12 16:27:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] Add this right near your `colorTest` array. This small buffer will hold the reset instructions for
2026-05-12 16:27:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] every line in the video field.
2026-05-12 16:27:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:27:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:27:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] const int colorpumpStride = lineWords * 4; // 108 words
2026-05-12 16:27:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] alignas(1024) uint32_t colorTest[colorpumpStride];
2026-05-12 16:27:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:27:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] // Control blocks: 2 words per line (Transfer Count, Read Address)
2026-05-12 16:27:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] // progressiveLines (304) * 2 = 608 words (~2.4 KB)
2026-05-12 16:27:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] uint32_t colorpump_ctrl_blocks[progressiveLines * 2];
2026-05-12 16:27:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:27:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] int colorpump_dma_chan;
2026-05-12 16:27:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] int colorpump_ctrl_chan; // The new chaining channel
