2026-05-12 16:27:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3. Trigger it in your Video Loop
2026-05-12 16:27:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] Finally, because the Control Channel consumes its array over the course of the frame, you just
2026-05-12 16:27:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] reset the Control Channel alongside your main video DMA in `vid_field`.
2026-05-12 16:27:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:27:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] Update `vid_field` like this:
2026-05-12 16:27:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:27:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:27:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] void vid_field(int field) {
2026-05-12 16:27:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Reset the main video
2026-05-12 16:27:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	dma_channel_set_trans_count(video_dma_chan, progressiveWordCount, false);
2026-05-12 16:27:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	dma_channel_set_read_addr(video_dma_chan, progressiveFrame, true);
2026-05-12 16:27:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:27:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Reset the Color Pump Control Channel (it takes 2 words per line)
2026-05-12 16:27:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	dma_channel_set_trans_count(colorpump_ctrl_chan, progressiveLines * 2, false);
2026-05-12 16:27:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	dma_channel_set_read_addr(colorpump_ctrl_chan, colorpump_ctrl_blocks, true);
2026-05-12 16:27:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-05-12 16:27:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:27:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:27:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] Why this is the "Pro" way:
2026-05-12 16:27:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. Zero CPU Overhead: During the active video frame, the CPU does absolutely nothing to
2026-05-12 16:27:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] manage the color buffer.
2026-05-12 16:27:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. Perfect Sync: When `colorpumpdmachan` finishes a line, it instantly pings the
2026-05-12 16:27:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] control channel, which instantly reloads the data channel's registers.
2026-05-12 16:27:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3. No Drift: Because it explicitly reloads 108 words every single time, it never drifts out
2026-05-12 16:27:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] of phase with your PIO's `irq 5` line sync.
2026-05-12 16:27:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] I see you decided to go with the full frame buffer approach! Expanding `colorTest` to cover the
2026-05-12 16:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] entire screen is an excellent choice. It completely bypasses the hardware ring limitations and
2026-05-12 16:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] opens the door for you to draw actual 2D color graphics later. With the RP2350's generous 520KB of
2026-05-12 16:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] SRAM, dedicating ~131KB to the color buffer is perfectly reasonable.
2026-05-12 16:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] Looking over the implementation, you are 99% of the way there. There are just two small details to
2026-05-12 16:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] adjust to make it bulletproof:
2026-05-12 16:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 1. Update the Progressive Field Function*
2026-05-12 16:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] You correctly added the DMA reset instructions for `colorpumpdmachan` at the bottom of
2026-05-12 16:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] `vidfieldi()` (the interlaced field handler). However, they also need to be added to
2026-05-12 16:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] `vidfield()` (the progressive field handler). If you run the system with `video_lace = false`
2026-05-12 16:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] right now, the color DMA will eventually run out of bounds because it is never told to reset.
2026-05-12 16:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2. Accommodate the Interlaced Line Count*
2026-05-12 16:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] You defined `colorpumpCount` using `progressiveLines` (which is 304). However, in `pal576i.h`, your
2026-05-12 16:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] `evenLines` constant is 305.
2026-05-12 16:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] During an even interlaced field, your PIO will fire `irq 5` 305 times, meaning it will consume `305
2026-05-12 16:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] * 108` words. Because the buffer is only sized for 304 lines, the DMA will read 108 words past the
2026-05-12 16:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] end of the `colorTest` array on alternating frames.
2026-05-12 16:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] To fix this, you should base `colorpumpCount` on `evenLines` (or whichever line count is the
2026-05-12 16:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] absolute maximum) to ensure the buffer is large enough for both field types.
2026-05-12 16:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] Once those two minor tweaks are made, your color pump DMA will be perfectly synchronized with your
2026-05-12 16:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] video output!
2026-05-12 16:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:45:06 [nitro@ryzen5] i would like to talk about the wave train for each color
2026-05-12 16:45:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] The wave train design in your `colorfifo.pio` is fascinating, particularly because it perfectly
2026-05-12 16:45:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] showcases the new capabilities of the RP2350 and exactly matches your analog timing math.
2026-05-12 16:45:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:45:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is a breakdown of what I see happening in your wave train logic. I would love to hear more
2026-05-12 16:45:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] about the analog side of this and how you plan to populate the patterns.
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 1. The Perfect 10-Cycle Pixel*
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your system clock is 166.5 MHz, and you previously noted 10 cycles per pixel. Your `colorfifo`
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] state machine executes exactly 10 instructions per pixel block once the trigger drops:
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `jmp pin stall` (Waits for the trigger, consumes 1 cycle when falling through)
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `mov y, pins` (1 cycle)
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `mov osr, rxfifo[y]` (1 cycle)
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `out pindirs, 3` × 6 (6 cycles)
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `set pindirs, 0` (1 cycle)
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • (Total = 10 cycles).* This guarantees your color generation stays perfectly phase-locked to the
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] pixel clock.
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2. RP2350 Indexed FIFOs*
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] Using `mov osr, rxfifo[y]` is a brilliant application of the RP2350's new ability to index the FIFO
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] directly. By pre-loading 4 words into the RX FIFO during initialization, you have effectively
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] turned the FIFO into a zero-wait-state lookup table. The 2-bit color index read from `pins`
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] instantly selects one of the 4 pulse trains.
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 3. The 18-Bit Payload*
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] You are doing six `out pindirs, 3` instructions, meaning each wave train consumes 18 bits of the
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 32-bit word.
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] Looking at your placeholder in `color_trains[]`:
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] `0b0'000'000'000'000'000'0'111'110'011'001'111`
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] Assuming standard right-shift, the PIO will output these 3-bit chunks in sequence:
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1.  `111`
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2.  `001`
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3.  `011`
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 4.  `110`
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 5.  `111`
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 6.  `000` (The leading zeros)
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 4. Driving `pindirs` instead of `pins`*
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] This is the most interesting part electrically. Instead of driving the pin values high or low, you
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] are modulating the pin directions (`pindirs`).
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] Usually, this implies the pin output values are statically set to `0` (which you do during
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] initialization with `gpio_put(..., false)`). By toggling the direction, the pins are rapidly
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] switching between High-Z (Input/Floating) and Strong Ground (Output Low).
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] How does your external resistor network react to this? Are you using external pull-ups or a
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] specific termination strategy to turn this High-Z/GND toggling into your Y, Pb, and Pr analog
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] voltage levels? And what specific 4 colors are you planning to map to the 4 indices on each PIO?
2026-05-12 16:45:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:47:03 [nitro@ryzen5] 3.3V ���� 80 � ������������� COMPONENT OUT ���� 75� ���� GND                        �Pb/Pr���� 150 � ���������
2026-05-12 16:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] Ah, that circuit snippet perfectly explains why you are modulating `pindirs` instead of the pin
2026-05-12 16:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] output values!
2026-05-12 16:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] By keeping the pin output state permanently at `0` (which you do in your C++ setup) and toggling
2026-05-12 16:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] the direction, you are using the RP2350 pins as Open-Drain outputs. The pin is rapidly
2026-05-12 16:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] switching between High-Z (Input) and Strong Ground (Output Low).
2026-05-12 16:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is the math on how your wave train translates into analog voltages for the Pb/Pr lines:
2026-05-12 16:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 1. The Two Voltage States*
2026-05-12 16:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] • State 0 (Bit = 0, High-Z): The pin is floating. The voltage at the TV is purely a voltage
2026-05-12 16:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] divider between your 80 Ω pull-up to 3.3V and the TV's internal 75 Ω termination to ground.
2026-05-12 16:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro]     *   V_High = 3.3V × 75⁄80 + 75 \approx \mathbf1.59V
2026-05-12 16:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] • State 1 (Bit = 1, Drive Low): The pin goes to 0V. Your 150 Ω resistor is now in parallel
2026-05-12 16:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] with the TV's 75 Ω termination (150 || 75 = 50 Ω).
2026-05-12 16:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro]     *   V_Low = 3.3V × 50⁄80 + 50 \approx \mathbf1.27V
2026-05-12 16:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] This gives you an analog swing of roughly 0.32V. Because component video inputs on TVs are
2026-05-12 16:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] typically AC-coupled, the TV ignores the ~1.4V DC offset and only sees the ±0.16V AC swing around
2026-05-12 16:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] its own bias point.
2026-05-12 16:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2. The 166.5 MHz PDM (Pulse Density Modulation)*
2026-05-12 16:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] This is where your PIO wave train is brilliant.
2026-05-12 16:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your `colorfifo` state machine executes `out pindirs, 3` six times in a row, with no delays, at the
2026-05-12 16:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] system clock rate of 166.5 MHz.
2026-05-12 16:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because standard video bandwidth is only a few megahertz, the TV (and the capacitance of the RCA
2026-05-12 16:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] cable) acts as a low-pass filter. The TV cannot see the individual 166.5 MHz pulses; it only sees
2026-05-12 16:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] the average voltage over the 10-cycle pixel window.
2026-05-12 16:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Out of your 10-cycle pixel, the last 4 cycles are forced to High-Z by the `set pindirs, 0` and
2026-05-12 16:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] the loop overhead.
2026-05-12 16:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] • The first 6 cycles are entirely programmable via your 18-bit wave train payload.
2026-05-12 16:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] This means for any single pixel, you can choose to pull the pin Low for 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, or 6 of
2026-05-12 16:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] those cycles.
2026-05-12 16:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] You have essentially built a high-speed DAC that gives you 7 discrete analog voltage levels per
2026-05-12 16:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] channel (Y, Pb, Pr) per pixel, just by changing the ratio of 1s and 0s in your 6-bit sequence!
2026-05-12 16:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] Am I correct that the `color_trains[]` array will eventually hold the pre-calculated bit patterns
2026-05-12 16:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] to mix the specific Y, Pb, and Pr voltages needed for a specific palette of 16 colors?
2026-05-12 16:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
