2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] palette!
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] Before we write the wave generation logic, I noticed a small hardware mapping detail in your
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] `vidbit.cpp` that might cause a headache:
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] const uint Y_PIN = 17;
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] const uint PB_PIN = 20;
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] const uint PR_PIN = 21;
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] // ... later in colorpump_init ...
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] sm_config_set_out_pins(&config7, Y_PIN, 3);
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] sm_config_set_set_pins(&config7, Y_PIN, 3);
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because the PIO `out` and `set` instructions affect contiguous pins, starting at `YPIN` (17)
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] for a length of 3 will actually drive GPIOs 17, 18, and 19 (not 17, 20, and 21). You will
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] likely want to either change `PBPIN` and `PR_PIN` to 18 and 19, or adjust your physical wiring
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] to match 17, 18, 19.
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] Building the Wave Trains
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] To populate `color_trains[]`, we need to map a desired intensity (0 to 6) for each of the 3
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] channels into that 18-bit word.
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because of your Open-Drain setup, the logic is inverted:
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] • A `1` drives the pin to Ground (Lower Voltage).
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] • A `0` leaves the pin High-Z (Higher Voltage).
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] Also, to make the analog signal as smooth as possible for the TV's low-pass filter, it is best to
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] "spread out" the 1s and 0s across the 6 cycles rather than clumping them together (e.g., `010101`
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] produces a much cleaner analog voltage than `000111`).
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is a reusable C++ helper function you can drop into `vidbit.cpp` to generate your palette
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] mathematically:
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] // 6-bit patterns for PDM (Pulse Density Modulation)
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] // 1 = Drive Low (Darker/Lower Voltage), 0 = High-Z (Brighter/Higher Voltage)
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] // Patterns are chosen to spread the pulses evenly across the 6 cycles
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] const uint8_t pdm_patterns[7] = {
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro]     0b000000, // Level 0: All High-Z (Max Voltage)
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro]     0b001000, // Level 1: 1 Low
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro]     0b010010, // Level 2: 2 Lows
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro]     0b010101, // Level 3: 3 Lows
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro]     0b101101, // Level 4: 4 Lows
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro]     0b111011, // Level 5: 5 Lows
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro]     0b111111  // Level 6: All Low (Min Voltage)
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] };
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:49:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] // y, pb, pr should be values from 0 to 6
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] uint32_t make_color_train(int y, int pb, int pr) {
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro]     uint32_t train = 0;
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro]     uint8_t y_bits = pdm_patterns[y];
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro]     uint8_t pb_bits = pdm_patterns[pb];
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro]     uint8_t pr_bits = pdm_patterns[pr];
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro]     // The PIO executes 6 'out pindirs, 3'
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro]     // Assuming standard right-shift, cycle 0 uses the lowest 3 bits.
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro]     for (int cycle = 0; cycle < 6; cycle++) {
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro]         uint32_t y_val  = (y_bits >> cycle) & 1;
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro]         uint32_t pb_val = (pb_bits >> cycle) & 1;
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro]         uint32_t pr_val = (pr_bits >> cycle) & 1;
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro]         // Pack the 3 pins into this cycle's 3-bit chunk
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro]         // Bit 0 = Y, Bit 1 = Pb, Bit 2 = Pr
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro]         uint32_t chunk = (pr_val << 2) | (pb_val << 1) | y_val;
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro]         train |= (chunk << (cycle * 3));
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro]     }
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro]     return train;
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] Populating the Palette
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] With that function, you can replace your hardcoded `color_trains[]` array with an initialization
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] routine.
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] For example, if you wanted to build a palette during startup, you could do something like this
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] (assuming 0 is darkest/lowest voltage and 6 is brightest/highest voltage):
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] void init_palette() {
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro]     // Example: 0 = Black, 1 = White, 2 = Red, 3 = Blue, etc.
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro]     // Remember: 0 in the helper means MAX voltage (High-Z).
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro]     // For Y (Luma), 0 is White, 6 is Black.
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro]     // Index 0: Black (Y is pulled low, color diffs are neutral)
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro]     color_trains[0] = make_color_train(6, 3, 3);
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro]     // Index 1: White (Y is High-Z, color diffs are neutral)
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro]     color_trains[1] = make_color_train(0, 3, 3);
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro]     // Index 2: Bright Red (Y medium, Pr high, Pb low)
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro]     color_trains[2] = make_color_train(3, 6, 0);
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro]     // Index 3: Bright Blue (Y medium, Pr low, Pb high)
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro]     color_trains[3] = make_color_train(3, 0, 6);
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro]     // ... define the rest of your 16 colors ...
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] Are you aiming for a specific retro palette (like the Commodore 64, Teletext, or ZX Spectrum), or
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] are you planning to define a custom 16-color palette for your system?
2026-05-12 16:49:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] I see you updated `Y_PIN` to `19`! That perfectly solves the contiguous pin requirement. Now your
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] `out pindirs, 3` instruction will cleanly drive GPIOs 19, 20, and 21 for Y, Pb, and Pr
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] respectively.
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] Since you are ready to tackle the wave trains next, here is a clean way to replace that hardcoded
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] `color_trains[]` array with a programmable palette generator.
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] You can drop these two functions into `vidbit.cpp` (perhaps right above `colortrains[]`). The
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] `makecolor_train` function uses the Pulse Density Modulation (PDM) concept we discussed to
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] spread the 1s and 0s evenly across the 6 active clock cycles.
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] // 6-bit patterns for PDM (Pulse Density Modulation)
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] // 1 = Drive Low (Darker/Lower Voltage), 0 = High-Z (Brighter/Higher Voltage)
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] const uint8_t pdm_patterns[7] = {
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	0b000000, // Level 0: All High-Z (Max Voltage)
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	0b001000, // Level 1: 1 Low
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	0b010010, // Level 2: 2 Lows
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	0b010101, // Level 3: 3 Lows
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	0b101101, // Level 4: 4 Lows
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	0b111011, // Level 5: 5 Lows
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	0b111111  // Level 6: All Low (Min Voltage)
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] };
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] // y, pb, pr should be intensity values from 0 to 6
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] uint32_t make_color_train(int y, int pb, int pr) {
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	uint32_t train = 0;
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	uint8_t y_bits = pdm_patterns[y];
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	uint8_t pb_bits = pdm_patterns[pb];
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	uint8_t pr_bits = pdm_patterns[pr];
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	for (int cycle = 0; cycle < 6; cycle++) {
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		uint32_t y_val  = (y_bits >> cycle) & 1;
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		uint32_t pb_val = (pb_bits >> cycle) & 1;
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		uint32_t pr_val = (pr_bits >> cycle) & 1;
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		// Pack the 3 pins into this cycle's 3-bit chunk
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		// Bit 0 = Y, Bit 1 = Pb, Bit 2 = Pr
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		uint32_t chunk = (pr_val << 2) | (pb_val << 1) | y_val;
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		train |= (chunk << (cycle * 3));
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	}
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	return train;
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] With that in place, you can leave `color_trains[16]` uninitialized at compile time:
2026-05-12 16:53:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:53:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:53:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] uint32_t color_trains[16];
2026-05-12 16:53:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:53:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:53:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] And then populate it dynamically inside your `colorpump_init` function, right before you load the
2026-05-12 16:53:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] FIFOs. Here is a quick example of generating a basic test palette:
2026-05-12 16:53:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:53:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:53:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] void init_test_palette() {
2026-05-12 16:53:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Remember: 0 is highest voltage, 6 is lowest voltage (Ground)
2026-05-12 16:53:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// For Y (Luma), 0 = White, 6 = Black.
2026-05-12 16:53:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// For Pb/Pr, 3 is the neutral/center point.
2026-05-12 16:53:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:53:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	color_trains[0] = make_color_train(6, 3, 3); // Black
2026-05-12 16:53:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	color_trains[1] = make_color_train(0, 3, 3); // White
2026-05-12 16:53:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	color_trains[2] = make_color_train(3, 6, 0); // Red
2026-05-12 16:53:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	color_trains[3] = make_color_train(3, 0, 6); // Blue
2026-05-12 16:53:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	color_trains[4] = make_color_train(3, 6, 6); // Magenta
2026-05-12 16:53:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	color_trains[5] = make_color_train(3, 0, 0); // Green
2026-05-12 16:53:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:53:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Fill the rest with a greyscale ramp for testing
2026-05-12 16:53:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	for(int i = 6; i < 16; i++) {
2026-05-12 16:53:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		int luma = (i - 6) * 6 / 9; // Map remaining 10 slots to 0-6
2026-05-12 16:53:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		color_trains[i] = make_color_train(luma, 3, 3);
2026-05-12 16:53:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	}
2026-05-12 16:53:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-05-12 16:53:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:53:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 16:53:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] Just call `inittestpalette()` at the top of `colorpump_init()` and your PIO state machines
2026-05-12 16:53:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] will automatically pick up the correct 18-bit wave trains.
