2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] This is looking fantastic! You have successfully integrated the Pulse Density Modulation (PDM)
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] generator, and I love the addition of the `noise2()` xorshift function.
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] Filling the `colorTest` full-frame buffer with random noise is the absolute best way to test this
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] stage of the hardware. Because your PIO consumes 4 bits per pixel, each 32-bit random word from
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] `noise2()` will perfectly generate 8 random pixels, each indexing randomly into your 16-color
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] palette. When you boot this up, you should see a rock-solid screen of 16-color static (snow). If
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] the snow doesn't tear or drift, your DMA and PIO timings are mathematically perfect.
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] A Quick Note on YPbPr Analog Colors
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] I see how you set up your test palette (e.g., `(6,6,6)`, `(3,3,3)`, `(0,0,0)`). This will
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] absolutely produce distinct colors on the screen, but when you are ready to tune them into exact
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] colors (like pure black, pure white, pure red), keep in mind how the Component Video
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] (YPbPr) color space works.
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Y (Luma/Brightness): This is your black-and-white image.
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro]     *   `0` (High-Z) = Maximum Voltage = White
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro]     *   `6` (Drive Low) = Minimum Voltage = Black
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Pb and Pr (Color Differences): These signals rest at a neutral center voltage
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] when there is no color (greyscale).
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro]     *   `3` is your neutral point.
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro]     *   If Pb and Pr are both `3`, the pixel is perfectly greyscale.
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro]     *   If Pb goes towards `0` (High-Z), the pixel gets more Blue. If it goes towards `6`,
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] it gets more Yellow.
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro]     *   If Pr goes towards `0` (High-Z), the pixel gets more Red. If it goes towards `6`,
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] it gets more Cyan.
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] So, for a "pure" test palette, you would eventually want to tweak your indices to look like this:
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] color_trains[0] = make_color_train(6, 3, 3); // Pure Black (Min Luma, Neutral Color)
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] color_trains[1] = make_color_train(3, 3, 3); // Pure Grey (Mid Luma, Neutral Color)
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] color_trains[2] = make_color_train(0, 3, 3); // Pure White (Max Luma, Neutral Color)
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] color_trains[3] = make_color_train(4, 6, 0); // Red (Darker luma, Pr High, Pb Low)
2026-05-12 17:04:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] color_trains[4] = make_color_train(2, 0, 6); // Blue (Brighter luma, Pb High, Pr Low)
2026-05-12 17:04:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] color_trains[5] = make_color_train(3, 6, 6); // Green (Mid luma, both Pb and Pr Low)
2026-05-12 17:04:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 17:04:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] • (Note: Your current `(0,0,0)` and `(6,6,6)` will still work for testing, they will just push the
2026-05-12 17:04:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] TV's color decoders to their absolute maximum limits!)*
2026-05-12 17:04:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
