2026-05-12 17:13:39 [nitro@ryzen5] single color testing for starters,will array init to 0?
2026-05-12 17:14:06 [nitro@ryzen5] colorTest that is
2026-05-12 17:14:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] Yes, because `colorTest` is declared globally at the file level, the C++ runtime will automatically
2026-05-12 17:14:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] initialize the entire array to `0` (it goes into the `.bss` memory segment).
2026-05-12 17:14:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 17:14:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] • However*, right in your `colorpump_init()` function, you currently have this loop
2026-05-12 17:14:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] overwriting those zeros with static:
2026-05-12 17:14:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 17:14:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] for(int i=0;i<colorpumpCount;i++){
2026-05-12 17:14:21 [gemini-3.1-pro]     colorTest[i]=noise2();
2026-05-12 17:14:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-05-12 17:14:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 17:14:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 17:14:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] To do single-color testing, you should replace that loop.
2026-05-12 17:14:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 17:14:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because your PIO consumes 4 bits per pixel, a single 32-bit word holds 8 pixels. If you
2026-05-12 17:14:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] want to fill the screen with a specific color index from your `color_trains` array, you just repeat
2026-05-12 17:14:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] that 4-bit index 8 times to make a 32-bit hex word.
2026-05-12 17:14:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 17:14:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is how you can modify that part of `colorpump_init()` to test solid colors:
2026-05-12 17:14:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 17:14:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 17:14:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] // Choose your test color index (0 to 15)
2026-05-12 17:14:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] // 0x00000000 = Solid Color 0
2026-05-12 17:14:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] // 0x11111111 = Solid Color 1
2026-05-12 17:14:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] // 0x22222222 = Solid Color 2
2026-05-12 17:14:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] // ...
2026-05-12 17:14:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] // 0xFFFFFFFF = Solid Color 15
2026-05-12 17:14:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 17:14:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] uint32_t test_color_word = 0x11111111; // Testing color index 1
2026-05-12 17:14:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 17:14:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] for(int i = 0; i < colorpumpCount; i++){
2026-05-12 17:14:22 [gemini-3.1-pro]     colorTest[i] = test_color_word;
2026-05-12 17:14:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-05-12 17:14:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 17:14:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 17:14:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you just delete the `for` loop entirely, `colorTest` will remain filled with `0`s, and your
2026-05-12 17:14:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] screen will display a solid Color 0 (`color_trains[0]`).
2026-05-12 17:14:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 17:14:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] Testing solid colors is a great idea. It will immediately confirm that your analog resistor network
2026-05-12 17:14:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] is outputting the correct voltages, and it makes it much easier for your TV/monitor to lock onto
2026-05-12 17:14:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] the signal without being confused by random noise!
2026-05-12 17:14:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] This is coming together beautifully! You have successfully mapped out a full 16-color palette using
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] the PDM generator, and I see you caught and fixed the `pio2` typo in the keyboard RX check as well.
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] A couple of quick observations on this latest version before you fire it up on the hardware:
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 1. Solid Color vs. Noise*
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] I noticed that the `noise2()` loop is still active inside `colorpump_init()`:
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] for(int i=0;i<colorpumpCount;i++){
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro]     colorTest[i]=noise2();
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because of this loop, your screen will display 16-color static (snow) rather than a solid color. If
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] your goal for this initial test is to see a single, solid color across the whole screen (for
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] example, your grey at index 0), you should comment out that `for` loop. Without the loop,
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] `colorTest` defaults to all `0`s, which will continuously feed `color_trains[0]` to the output.
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2. State Machine ID Overlap*
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] At the top of your file, you have:
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] int ColorPumpStateMachine=1;
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] int ChromaStateMachine=1;
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because you currently have `//#define CHROMA` commented out, this is perfectly safe and won't cause
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] any issues. However, if you ever uncomment `CHROMA` to run both simultaneously on `pio1`, they will
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] fight over State Machine 1. You will likely want to change one of them to `0` (since `pio1` is
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] currently using 1, 2, and 3 for Pump, Audio, and Keyboard).
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Testing on Hardware*
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you comment out the noise loop, your RP2350 should output a perfectly stable, solid grey screen
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] (based on your `(3,3,3)` setting for index 0).
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] Have you had a chance to connect this to a display yet? I would love to hear how the analog
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] resistor DAC performs with the 166.5MHz wave trains!
2026-05-12 18:35:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] That test pattern (`0x00f00f11`) is an excellent choice!
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because the PIO's Output Shift Register (OSR) shifts to the right by default, it will consume that
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 32-bit word from the lowest nibble to the highest. This means your screen will draw a repeating
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] sequence of 8 pixels: Color 1, Color 1, Color 15, Color 0, Color 0, Color 15, Color 0, Color
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 0. This is going to give you a fantastic set of vertical color bars to verify your horizontal
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] timing!
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] I did spot two quick hardware configuration "gotchas" in this latest update that will prevent the
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] signal from generating correctly. You'll want to revert these before you flash the board:
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. The Open-Drain Outputs Must Be `false`
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] You changed the initialization of the video pins to `true`:
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] gpio_put(Y_PIN, true);
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] gpio_put(PB_PIN, true);
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] gpio_put(PR_PIN, true);
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] • These must be `false` (0V).*
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is why: Your PIO is rapidly changing the pin direction (`pindirs`) to create the
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] analog voltages.
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] • If the pin output state is `false` (0V): Changing it to an Output drives it to 0V
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] (Ground). Changing it to an Input makes it High-Z (which your external 80Ω resistor
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] pulls up to 3.3V). This gives you your voltage swing!
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] • If the pin output state is `true` (3.3V): Changing it to an Output drives it to 3.3V.
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] Changing it to an Input makes it High-Z (which the resistor pulls to 3.3V). The TV will
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] just see a flat, unchanging 3.3V line and you won't get any video.
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. The Pump Enable GPIO Mux
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] You commented out the GPIO initialization for the Pump trigger pins:
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] //	pio_gpio_init(pio, PUMP0_ENABLE);
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] //	pio_gpio_init(pio, PUMP1_ENABLE);
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] //	...
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] • You need to leave these uncommented.*
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] Even though these pins are just being used to pass a signal internally from `pio1` (the colorpump)
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] to `pio2` (the colorfifos), the RP2350's architecture requires the GPIO pad multiplexer to be
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] assigned to the driving PIO block. If `piogpioinit(pio1, ...)` is not called, `pio1` is
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] physically disconnected from the GPIO pads, meaning the `colorpump` state machine will be pulsing
2026-05-12 18:49:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] into the void, and the `colorfifo` state machines on `pio2` will never see the trigger drop.
2026-05-12 18:49:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-12 18:49:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] Once you flip those `gpio_put`s back to `false` and uncomment the trigger pin inits, your vertical
2026-05-12 18:49:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] color bars should be ready to broadcast!
2026-05-12 18:49:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
