2026-05-13 09:08:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] It is great that the state machine is running now! By the way, the way you perfectly balanced all
2026-05-13 09:08:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] four branches of `colorpump` to exactly 10 cycles to phase-lock with the 10-cycle `colorfifo` loop
2026-05-13 09:08:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] is a brilliant piece of PIO design.
2026-05-13 09:08:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:08:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] Since it is running but there is no color, the issue now lies in how the data is moving (or not
2026-05-13 09:08:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] moving) through the FIFOs and DMA. There are four missing links in the data pipeline preventing the
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] color signals from reaching the pins.
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is how to fix them:
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. Enable Autopull for `colorpump`
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] In `vidbit.cpp`, your `colorpump_init` function configures the pins but forgets to enable
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] `autopull`. Without this, the state machine never pulls data from the TX FIFO. It just shifts out
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] `0`s endlessly, and your DMA channel stalls after transferring the very first word.
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] Add this line to `colorpump_init`:
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] sm_config_set_out_shift(&config6, true, true, 32); // shift right, autopull ON, 32 bits
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. Empty the OSR after loading the ISR
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] In `colorpump_init`, you manually load `863` into the ISR using a `pull` and `mov`. However, this
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] leaves `863` sitting in the OSR. When the state machine starts its loop, it will accidentally
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] output the bits of `863` as your first 8 pixels, throwing the DMA completely out of sync with your
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] lines.
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] You need to empty the OSR immediately after loading the ISR so that the first pixel triggers a
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] fresh autopull from the DMA. Add the `out` execution here:
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] pio_sm_put_blocking(pio, sm, 863);	// load ISR with count
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] pio_sm_exec(pio, sm, pio_encode_pull(false, false));
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] pio_sm_exec(pio, sm, pio_encode_mov(pio_isr, pio_osr));
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] pio_sm_exec(pio, sm, pio_encode_out(pio_null, 32)); // <--- ADD THIS to empty the OSR
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3. Read from the correct FIFO in `colorfifo.pio`
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] In `vidbit.cpp`, you use `piosmput_blocking` to preload the 4 color trains. This function
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] writes to the TX FIFO.
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] However, in `colorfifo.pio`, your instruction is `mov osr, rxfifo[y]`. Because the RX FIFO is
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] empty, it outputs all `0`s, resulting in no color.
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] The RP2350 allows you to index the TX FIFO directly. Update `colorfifo.pio` to read from `txfifo`:
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] stall:
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp	pin stall
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	mov	y, pins
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	mov	osr, txfifo[y] ; <--- Change rxfifo to txfifo
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out	pindirs, 3
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 4. Fix the State Machine ID and PIO Instance
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] As discussed previously, `colorpump` must be on `pio0` to see `irq 5`, but in your original
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] `vidbit.cpp` it was on `pio1` and shared an ID with Teletext. Make sure you have updated these
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] lines in `vidbit.cpp`:
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] // Change ID to 2 so it doesn't collide with Teletext (which is 1)
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] int ColorPumpStateMachine = 2;
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] And in `main()`:
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] // Initialize on pio0 instead of pio1
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] pass &= colorpump_init(pio0, ColorPumpStateMachine);
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] colorpump_dma_init(pio0, ColorPumpStateMachine);
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] Make these adjustments and your color signal should successfully make it all the way from the DMA
2026-05-13 09:08:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] buffer out to the resistor network!
