2026-05-13 09:18:43 [nitro@ryzen5] yuck
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] I completely understand the "yuck" feeling. Getting multiple PIO state machines, DMA channels, and
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] FIFOs to perfectly align across different hardware blocks is notoriously fiddly, and when it fails
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] silently, it can be very frustrating.
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] Looking through your updated files, I can see exactly why the color signal is still flatlining.
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] There are three specific issues remaining, including a critical hardware bug where your state
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] machine is reading the wrong pins.
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is how to clean it up and get the color flowing:
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. `colorfifo` is reading out-of-bounds memory
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] In `colorfifo.pio`, you are using `mov y, pins` to read the 2-bit color index. The problem is that
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] `mov y, pins` reads all 32 pins starting from your `IN` base. Because `PUMP0_ENABLE` is on
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] GPIO 2, it will frequently be high. This means `y` won't be `0`, `1`, `2`, or `3`—it will be a
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] large number (like `4`, `5`, etc.), causing `rxfifo[y]` to read out of bounds and output garbage or
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] zeroes.
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] To fix this, isolate exactly 2 bits using the `in` instruction:
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] ; colorfifo.pio
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] .program colorfifo
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] stall:
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp	pin stall
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	mov	isr, null	; Clear ISR
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	in	pins, 2		; Shift exactly 2 bits from COLOR_I0/1 into ISR
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	mov	y, isr		; Move the clean 0-3 index into y
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	mov	osr, rxfifo[y]
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out	pindirs, 3
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out	pindirs, 3
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out	pindirs, 3
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out	pindirs, 3
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out	pindirs, 3
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out	pindirs, 3
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	pindirs, 0
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] .wrap
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] • (Note: You can safely remove `.fifo txget`, as the C++ preload method below bypasses the need for
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] custom FIFO configuration).*
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. `colorpump` is still on the wrong PIO block
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] In `vidbit.cpp`, `colorpump` is still being initialized on `pio1`. Because your main video
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] generator is on `pio0`, `colorpump` will never see `irq 5` go high. It is permanently stuck waiting
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] for an interrupt that happens on a different hardware block.
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] You also need to change `ColorPumpStateMachine` to `2` so it doesn't collide with the Teletext
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] state machine.
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] Update your variables at the top of `vidbit.cpp`:
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] int VideoStateMachine=0;
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] int TeletextStateMachine=1;
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] int ColorPumpStateMachine=2; // Changed from 1 to avoid collision
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] And update your `main()` function to place it on `pio0`:
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] #ifdef COLOR
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	pass&=colorpump_init(pio0, ColorPumpStateMachine); // Moved to pio0
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	colorpump_dma_init(pio0, ColorPumpStateMachine);   // Moved to pio0
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] #endif
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3. Clean up the FIFO Preloading (The "Yuck" Fix)
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] Inlining the `piosmexec` calls inside your initialization loop makes the setup code
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] difficult to read. As requested, here is a named, reusable function to handle moving data from the
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] CPU into the PIO's RX FIFO.
2026-05-13 09:19:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] Add this function above `colorpump_init`:
2026-05-13 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] void preload_rxfifo(PIO pio, int sm, uint32_t data) {
2026-05-13 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	pio_sm_put_blocking(pio, sm, data);
2026-05-13 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	pio_sm_exec(pio, sm, pio_encode_pull(false, false));
2026-05-13 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	pio_sm_exec(pio, sm, pio_encode_mov(pio_isr, pio_osr));
2026-05-13 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	pio_sm_exec(pio, sm, pio_encode_push(false, false));
2026-05-13 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-05-13 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] Then, your `colorfifo` loop inside `colorpump_init` becomes much cleaner:
2026-05-13 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
2026-05-13 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			preload_rxfifo(pio2, fifo, color_trains[color_index]);
2026-05-13 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			color_index++;
2026-05-13 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		}
