2026-05-13 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] With `colorpump` moved to `pio0` to share the IRQ, and `colorfifo` safely masking the pin inputs,
2026-05-13 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] your DMA pipeline will successfully drive the resistor network.
2026-05-13 09:19:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:21:06 [nitro@ryzen5] irq 4..7 are global
2026-05-13 09:21:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] You are absolutely right that IRQs 4 through 7 are the internal, shared flags that don't trigger
2026-05-13 09:21:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] system-level CPU interrupts.
2026-05-13 09:21:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:21:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] However, those flags are only shared within a single PIO block.
2026-05-13 09:21:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:21:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] The RP2350 has three physically separate PIO blocks (`pio0`, `pio1`, and `pio2`), and each block
2026-05-13 09:21:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] has its own independent set of 8 IRQ flags. Because `vidbit` is running on `pio0` and `colorpump`
2026-05-13 09:21:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] is running on `pio1`, they are looking at two completely different `irq 5` flags. `colorpump` is
2026-05-13 09:21:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] waiting for `pio1`'s `irq 5` to go high, but `vidbit` is only setting `pio0`'s `irq 5`.
2026-05-13 09:21:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:21:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] Moving `colorpump` to `pio0` is the only way for them to share that specific internal IRQ.
2026-05-13 09:21:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:21:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] Also, definitely double-check the `mov y, pins` instruction in your `colorfifo.pio` that we
2026-05-13 09:21:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] discussed in the previous step. The `mov` instruction ignores the pin count you set in your C++
2026-05-13 09:21:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] config and always reads all 32 pins starting from the base pin. Since your enable pins are right
2026-05-13 09:21:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] next to your index pins, `y` is pulling in those high enable bits, causing the RX FIFO index to go
2026-05-13 09:21:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] out of bounds. Using the `in` instruction to mask exactly 2 bits will solve that.
2026-05-13 09:21:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-13 09:21:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] Let me know if you'd like me to write out any specific code adjustments for this!
2026-05-13 09:21:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
