2026-05-13 09:22:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] RP2040-era assumptions.
2026-05-13 09:22:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-13 09:22:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] Short answer: you should treat RP2350 PIO IRQ 4–7 as chip-level shared/internal PIO flags (not
2026-05-13 09:22:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] confined to one PIO instance) when using the RP2350 extended PIO feature set. So your thought
2026-05-13 09:22:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] is valid, and “must be same PIO block” is not a safe blanket rule on RP2350.
2026-05-13 09:22:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-13 09:22:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] Practical takeaway for your case:
2026-05-13 09:22:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] - If `colorpump` waits on `irq 5` and another SM sets/clears that same internal IRQ, cross-PIO
2026-05-13 09:22:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] sync can work on RP2350.
2026-05-13 09:22:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] - So if color is still absent, the root cause is likely elsewhere (indexing pins, FIFO preload
2026-05-13 09:22:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] semantics, pin direction timing, DMA cadence), not necessarily IRQ domain isolation.
2026-05-13 09:22:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-13 09:22:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] Given your current code, the highest-risk item is still this one in `colorfifo.pio`:
2026-05-13 09:22:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] - `mov y, pins`
2026-05-13 09:22:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] That can capture more than just your 2-bit index in real hardware timing contexts, and then
2026-05-13 09:22:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] `rxfifo[y]` can select unintended entries.
