2026-05-13 09:25:56 [gpt-5.3-codex] Yes — but only in a narrow sense.
2026-05-13 09:25:56 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-13 09:25:56 [gpt-5.3-codex] `mov y, pins` reads the full sampled pin input bus for that PIO, not a 2-bit masked value
2026-05-13 09:25:56 [gpt-5.3-codex] from `smconfigsetinpins(...)`.
2026-05-13 09:25:56 [gpt-5.3-codex] So you cannot configure the SM to make `mov y, pins` automatically return just 0..3.
2026-05-13 09:25:56 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-13 09:25:56 [gpt-5.3-codex] What you can do:
2026-05-13 09:25:56 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-13 09:25:56 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Keep `mov y, pins`, then mask/shift in PIO code before indexing.
2026-05-13 09:25:56 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Or better: use `in pins, 2` and move ISR to Y (clean 2-bit value).
2026-05-13 09:25:56 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-13 09:25:56 [gpt-5.3-codex] So answer: no direct config switch makes raw `mov y, pins` “just work” as 2-bit index. You
2026-05-13 09:25:56 [gpt-5.3-codex] must sanitize it in program flow.
2026-05-13 09:25:56 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
