2026-05-13 09:32:05 [nitro@ryzen5] is there a jmp!pin
2026-05-13 09:32:15 [gpt-5.3-codex] Yes — effectively yes in PIO terms.
2026-05-13 09:32:15 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-13 09:32:15 [gpt-5.3-codex] Use:
2026-05-13 09:32:15 [gpt-5.3-codex] - `jmp pin, target` → jump when pin is 1
2026-05-13 09:32:15 [gpt-5.3-codex] - fall-through path is your “`!pin`” case
2026-05-13 09:32:15 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-13 09:32:15 [gpt-5.3-codex] So `jmp !pin` doesn’t exist as separate mnemonic, but you implement it by swapping
2026-05-13 09:32:15 [gpt-5.3-codex] branch/fall-through (or adding a second jump).
2026-05-13 09:32:15 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-13 09:32:15 [gpt-5.3-codex] Example logic pattern:
2026-05-13 09:32:15 [gpt-5.3-codex] - `jmp pin, is_one`
2026-05-13 09:32:15 [gpt-5.3-codex] - ... here is `pin == 0` path (`!pin`)
2026-05-13 09:32:15 [gpt-5.3-codex] - `jmp done`
2026-05-13 09:32:15 [gpt-5.3-codex] - `is_one:`
2026-05-13 09:32:15 [gpt-5.3-codex] - ... `pin == 1` path
2026-05-13 09:32:15 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
