2026-05-14 11:15:13 [deepseek-v4] Your files present an interesting approach to generating component video with RP2350 PIO state
2026-05-14 11:15:13 [deepseek-v4] machines. I’ll comment on a few aspects I found notable, plus point out potential issues.
2026-05-14 11:15:13 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 11:15:13 [deepseek-v4] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-05-14 11:15:13 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 11:15:14 [deepseek-v4] `colorpump.pio`
2026-05-14 11:15:14 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 11:15:14 [deepseek-v4] - The state machine uses `out pins, 2` then `out x, 2` to set the color index. After that, it
2026-05-14 11:15:14 [deepseek-v4] decrements `x` and jumps to set one of four trigger lines (GP7, GP11, GP13, GP14).
2026-05-14 11:15:14 [deepseek-v4] - Potential problem: The `out x, 2` instruction loads the same source (OSR) that
2026-05-14 11:15:14 [deepseek-v4] was just used for `out pins, 2`. In PIO, `out` shifts bits from OSR. After the first `out pins, 2`,
2026-05-14 11:15:14 [deepseek-v4] the OSR has advanced by 2 bits; the second `out x, 2` reads the next two bits, not the same
2026-05-14 11:15:14 [deepseek-v4] index. If the intention is to use the same index for both the output pins and the jump, you would
2026-05-14 11:15:14 [deepseek-v4] need to reload or use a `mov` instruction beforehand. This misalignment could cause incorrect
2026-05-14 11:15:14 [deepseek-v4] colour selection.
2026-05-14 11:15:14 [deepseek-v4] - Alternative: Use `out x, 2` first, then `mov isr, x` or store the value in `y` before
2026-05-14 11:15:14 [deepseek-v4] outputting to pins.
2026-05-14 11:15:14 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 11:15:14 [deepseek-v4] - The `set pins, 15` after the trigger pulse returns to all‑low (since 15 = 0b1111, all four
2026-05-14 11:15:14 [deepseek-v4] side‑set pins low? Check pin mapping: if side‑set pins are GPIO7,11,13,14, then setting pins = 15
2026-05-14 11:15:14 [deepseek-v4] sets those bits; but you might want to clear all triggers after the pulse. The `nop [1]` and `set
2026-05-14 11:15:14 [deepseek-v4] pins, 15` sequence creates a short low pulse on the selected trigger, but the last state is high on
2026-05-14 11:15:14 [deepseek-v4] all four. That may keep subsequent colourfifo state machines in a `jmp pin stall` loop if they are
2026-05-14 11:15:14 [deepseek-v4] edge‑sensitive or level‑sensitive? Better to set pins back to a known idle state (e.g., all high?
2026-05-14 11:15:14 [deepseek-v4] or all low? depends on active‑low triggering). Clarify the trigger polarity.
