2026-05-14 12:51:21 [deepseek-v4] Your system pushes the RP2350’s PIO to its limit: 166.5 MHz, multiple state machines, PAL timing,
2026-05-14 12:51:21 [deepseek-v4] and several color‑encoding strategies. Below I highlight what I see as the core ideas, potential
2026-05-14 12:51:21 [deepseek-v4] pitfalls, and suggestions for integration.
2026-05-14 12:51:21 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 12:51:21 [deepseek-v4] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-05-14 12:51:21 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 12:51:21 [deepseek-v4] Two distinct color pipelines
2026-05-14 12:51:21 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 12:51:21 [deepseek-v4] 1. `colorpump` + `colorfifo` (4‑color mode, indexed)
2026-05-14 12:51:21 [deepseek-v4] - Goal: Pre‑load 4 colour pulse trains and output one per pixel based on a 2‑bit index from
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] the incoming pixel data.
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] - How it works:
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4]   - `colorpump` reads from its own TX FIFO (presumably filled by the CPU with 2‑bit pixel values
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] per line), writes the 2‑bit index to GPIO pins, then triggers one of four PIO channels
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] (`colorfifo`) via a low pulse on a side‑set pin.
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4]   - Each `colorfifo` holds 4 (or more) 32‑bit words in its RX FIFO. When triggered, it moves
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] `pins[0..1]` into `y`, fetches `rxfifo[y]`, and outputs that 3‑lane pulse train.
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] - Issues:
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4]   - Pin count: 4 separate `colorfifo` state machines each need their own output pins –
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] that’s 12 pins (3 each) plus the trigger pins from `colorpump`. The files mention GP42‑GP47, which
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] fits, but check if they are on the same PIO block to allow `mov y, pins` to read the shared index.
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4]   - Timing: The trigger pulses from `colorpump` must be short enough not to interfere with
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] neighbouring pulses. The current `set pins, 7` etc. are active‑low pulses – ensure they align with
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] the pixel clock.
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4]   - FIFO depth: Each `colorfifo`’s RX FIFO holds 4 entries by default. If you pre‑load 4
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] pulse trains, that’s fine. But if you want more colours (e.g. 16), you’d need more FIFO slots or
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] interleaving.
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] 2. `pixelpump` → `onebit` → `threepin` (serial bit‑plane approach)
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] - Goal: Build a two‑colour (1‑bit) pixel stream by switching between two pre‑loaded
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] waveform `x` and `y` based on the pixel value read from the third state machine.
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] - How it works:
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4]   - `pixelpump` pumps 27 words per line (864 pixels) into the FIFO chain.
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4]   - `onebit` loads two 32‑bit pulse patterns into `y` and `x` (typical PAL waveform pair, e.g. luma
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] levels). For each pixel, it outputs 1 bit, reads that bit from a loopback pin, and pushes the
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] appropriate waveform word into the next FIFO.
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4]   - `threepin` receives these waveform words and outputs 10 (or 7) groups of 3‑bit `pindirs` to
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] drive three pins (e.g. RGB or YPbPr).
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] - Issues:
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4]   - Pipeline depth: Three state machines with FIFO passes – possible stalls if any FIFO
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] becomes full or empty. The `push block / pull block` will eventually pause the chain, but the
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] timing must be exact to keep line rate.
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4]   - Loopback pin: `onebit` uses a pin to read the current pixel state – that pin must be
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] driven by a state machine (likely a fourth) or a CPU‑toggled GPIO. The file says “GPIO 46”, but
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] it’s not clear how that pin is set per pixel.
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4]   - Word length: `threepin` currently outputs 7 groups of 3‑bit directions (21 bits) then
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] skips 11 bits, totalling 32 bits. That matches the 10‑cycle pixel period (7 cycles for waveform, 3
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] dummy). But you have a `set pindirs, 0` after output – that turns off the pins for the remaining
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] cycles, which is correct for analog reconstruction? Ensure the DAC resistors see a clean high‑Z or
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] pull‑down during the inactive part.
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] PAL timing generator (`vidbit.pio`)
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] - This is a solid H‑sync, back porch, active line, front porch loop counting 10656 cycles.
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] - Note the use of `irq set 5` at the end of the back porch – presumably to trigger `colorpump` or
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] the pixel source. This synchronises the pixel pipeline.
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] - However, the pixel pipeline runs on a different state machine. The IRQ5 is set after the back
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] porch, but `colorpump` waits on `irq 5` – this is the correct handshake. But the pixel data must
2026-05-14 12:51:22 [deepseek-v4] already be waiting. The CPU or a DMA controller should fill `pixelpump`’s FIFO before the IRQ
