2026-05-14 13:25:19 [nitro@ryzen5] if i add a grey gpio pin, could it contribute to brightness of luma, with a meaningful R?
2026-05-14 13:25:29 [nitro@ryzen5] 3.3V ���� 120 � ������������� VIDEO:2 OUT ���� 75� ���� GND                         �LUMA ���� 150 � ����������GREY ���� 150 � ����������
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] Yes, adding a second GPIO pin (driven as a digital output) with a resistor to the video output can
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] contribute additional brightness levels. In your circuit, you already have one luma pin (LUMA)
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] through 150 Ω. Adding a second pin (GREY) through another 150 Ω creates a simple two‑bit
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] resistor DAC.
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] How it works
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] Assume:
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] - 3.3 V supply
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] - 120 Ω pull‑up to video out
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] - Two GPIO pins, each with a 150 Ω resistor to the output
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] - When a pin is high (3.3 V), it adds current through its resistor toward the output node.
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] The output voltage for each combination of pin states is determined by the parallel/series network.
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] With both pins high, you get the maximum white level; both low gives black (zero output, because
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] the pull‑up is the only path). The three intermediate states (one pin high, the other low) give two
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] distinct intermediate voltages if the resistors are equal? Actually, if both resistors are equal,
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] the two cases (only LUMA high, only GREY high) produce the same output voltage because the
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] circuit is symmetric. So you would only get three distinct levels: 0, 1, and 2 (with both
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] high). That’s still a meaningful increase from one bit (two levels).
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] Making it more useful: weighted resistors
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] To get four evenly‑spaced levels (i.e., 2‑bit greyscale), use binary‑weighted resistors.
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] For example:
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] - LUMA pin: 150 Ω (most significant bit – contributes roughly 2/3 of the total current when
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] high)
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] - GREY pin: 300 Ω (least significant bit – contributes half the current of LUMA)
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] Then the output voltage will have four distinct steps: 0, V1, V2, V1+V2 (roughly linear). This
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] gives meaningful brightness variation.
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] Example voltage calculation (with 150 Ω and 300 Ω)
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] - Pull‑up: 120 Ω to 3.3 V
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] - When a pin is high, it connects through its resistor to 3.3 V. When low, it’s effectively open
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] (or 0 V through a small output impedance – assume ideal digital output).
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] For the four states:
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] ╭────────────────┬────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────╮
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] │  LUMA (150 Ω)  │  GREY (300 Ω)  │  Equivalent R to 3.3 V (aside from 120 Ω)  │  Output voltage (into 75 Ω load)  │
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] ├────────────────┼────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────┤
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] │  low           │  low           │  only 120 Ω pull‑up                        │  ≈ 0.98 V (sync level?)           │
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] │  low           │  high          │  120 Ω ∥ 300 Ω                             │  ≈ 1.54 V                         │
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] │  high          │  low           │  120 Ω ∥ 150 Ω                             │  ≈ 1.79 V                         │
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] │  high          │  high          │  120 Ω ∥ 150 Ω ∥ 300 Ω                     │  ≈ 2.22 V                         │
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] ╰────────────────┴────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────╯
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] Those are distinct and usable for luma (assuming 75 Ω termination). The steps are not perfectly
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] linear but are meaningful.
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] Conclusion
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] Yes, adding a grey pin through a resistor contributes to brightness. For meaningful levels,
2026-05-14 13:25:44 [deepseek-v4] use binary‑weighted resistors (e.g., 150 Ω and 300 Ω) rather than equal values. Even with
2026-05-14 13:25:45 [deepseek-v4] equal resistors you get three levels, which is still an improvement over one bit.
2026-05-14 13:25:45 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:30:29 [nitro@ryzen5] is 330 ohm ok
2026-05-14 13:30:40 [deepseek-v4] Using 330 Ω for the GREY pin is acceptable and will give a distinct, usable extra brightness
2026-05-14 13:30:40 [deepseek-v4] level, though the step between the two channels will be slightly smaller than with 300 Ω.
2026-05-14 13:30:40 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:30:40 [deepseek-v4] Circuit recap
2026-05-14 13:30:40 [deepseek-v4] - 3.3 V supply
2026-05-14 13:30:40 [deepseek-v4] - 120 Ω pull‑up to video output
2026-05-14 13:30:40 [deepseek-v4] - LUMA pin through 150 Ω to output
2026-05-14 13:30:40 [deepseek-v4] - GREY pin through 330 Ω to output
2026-05-14 13:30:40 [deepseek-v4] - Video output terminated with 75 Ω to GND
2026-05-14 13:30:40 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:30:40 [deepseek-v4] Output voltage levels (ideal, ignoring GPIO output impedance)
2026-05-14 13:30:40 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:30:40 [deepseek-v4] ╭────────────────┬────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────╮
2026-05-14 13:30:40 [deepseek-v4] │  LUMA (150 Ω)  │  GREY (330 Ω)  │  Equivalent parallel resistance to 3.3 V (aside from 120 Ω)  │  Thevenin equivalent (Vth, Rth) to output  │  Voltage across 75 Ω load     │
2026-05-14 13:30:40 [deepseek-v4] ├────────────────┼────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┤
2026-05-14 13:30:40 [deepseek-v4] │  low           │  low           │  120 Ω only                                                  │  3.3 V via 120 Ω                           │  3.3 × 75/(75+120) = 1.29 V   │
2026-05-14 13:30:40 [deepseek-v4] │  low           │  high          │  120 Ω ∥ 330 Ω = 88.0 Ω                                      │  3.3 V via 88.0 Ω                          │  3.3 × 75/(75+88) = 1.55 V    │
2026-05-14 13:30:40 [deepseek-v4] │  high          │  low           │  120 Ω ∥ 150 Ω = 66.7 Ω                                      │  3.3 V via 66.7 Ω                          │  3.3 × 75/(75+66.7) = 1.75 V  │
2026-05-14 13:30:41 [deepseek-v4] │  high          │  high          │  120 Ω ∥ 150 Ω ∥ 330 Ω = 42.6 Ω                              │  3.3 V via 42.6 Ω                          │  3.3 × 75/(75+42.6) = 2.15 V  │
2026-05-14 13:30:41 [deepseek-v4] ╰────────────────┴────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────╯
2026-05-14 13:30:41 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:30:41 [deepseek-v4] Comparison with equal resistors (150 Ω each)
2026-05-14 13:30:41 [deepseek-v4] If both were 150 Ω, the voltages would be:
2026-05-14 13:30:41 [deepseek-v4] - low, low: 1.29 V (same)
2026-05-14 13:30:41 [deepseek-v4] - low, high: 1.71 V
2026-05-14 13:30:41 [deepseek-v4] - high, low: 1.71 V (same) → only 3 distinct levels
2026-05-14 13:30:41 [deepseek-v4] - high, high: 2.13 V
2026-05-14 13:30:41 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:30:41 [deepseek-v4] With 330 Ω, you get four distinct levels (1.29, 1.55, 1.75, 2.15 V), which is a meaningful
2026-05-14 13:30:41 [deepseek-v4] 2‑bit greyscale. The step from low‑only‑LUMA to low‑only‑GREY is 0.20 V, while the step from
2026-05-14 13:30:41 [deepseek-v4] low‑only‑GREY to only‑LUMA is 0.20 V as well. The top step (only‑LUMA to both) is 0.40 V. That’s
2026-05-14 13:30:41 [deepseek-v4] acceptable – the brightness progression is roughly linear.
2026-05-14 13:30:41 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:30:41 [deepseek-v4] Caveats
2026-05-14 13:30:41 [deepseek-v4] - The 330 Ω value is a standard E12 series (or E24) value, so easy to obtain.
2026-05-14 13:30:41 [deepseek-v4] - The lower grey output (1.55 V) is about 0.16 V less than if you used 300 Ω, but still clearly
2026-05-14 13:30:41 [deepseek-v4] different from the other levels.
2026-05-14 13:30:41 [deepseek-v4] - If you want more precise weighting (e.g., binary 2:1), 150 Ω and 300 Ω are closer to ideal, but
2026-05-14 13:30:41 [deepseek-v4] 330 Ω works fine.
2026-05-14 13:30:41 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:30:41 [deepseek-v4] • Conclusion*: Yes, 330 Ω is perfectly OK. It gives four distinct luma levels, improving
2026-05-14 13:30:41 [deepseek-v4] from 2‑level (monochrome) to 4‑level (greyscale) with minimal extra components.
2026-05-14 13:30:41 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:52:56 [nitro@ryzen5] so i set grey pin as always output or just when line is active, or just when i want to drive high?
2026-05-14 13:53:05 [deepseek-v4] You should set both GPIO pins (LUMA and GREY) as outputs permanently – they are always
2026-05-14 13:53:05 [deepseek-v4] driven to a known state. During blanking and sync, both pins are set low (0,0) so that the video
2026-05-14 13:53:05 [deepseek-v4] output stays at the blanking level (~1.29 V). During active video, you drive the appropriate
2026-05-14 13:53:05 [deepseek-v4] combination (00, 01, 10, 11) to produce the four luma levels.
2026-05-14 13:53:05 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:53:05 [deepseek-v4] The PIO state machine that generates the pixel waveform will control the pins every cycle – you
2026-05-14 13:53:05 [deepseek-v4] don’t need to switch the pin direction dynamically. Just configure them as outputs once, and the
2026-05-14 13:53:05 [deepseek-v4] PIO’s `out pins` or `set pins` instructions will set the values for each pixel (including zero
2026-05-14 13:53:05 [deepseek-v4] during blanking).
2026-05-14 13:53:05 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:53:05 [deepseek-v4] In short: always output, but only drive high when you want a brighter greyscale level.
2026-05-14 13:53:05 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:53:23 [nitro@ryzen5] luma pin is a tristate system
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] In a tristate system (where each pin is either output enabled or input high‑Z), the grey
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] pin should not be always output. Instead, control its direction exactly like the luma pin:
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] use the same `out pindirs, N` instruction to enable/disable both outputs simultaneously, and set
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] the pin level with `out pins` (or `set pins`) only when it is enabled.
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] Typical state mapping
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] ╭───────────────────────┬───────────────┬───────────────┬─────────────────────────────────╮
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] │  Video region         │  Luma pin     │  Grey pin     │  Effect on output               │
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] ├───────────────────────┼───────────────┼───────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┤
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] │  Sync                 │  Output low   │  Output low   │  Strong pull‑down → 0 V         │
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] │  Blank                │  High‑Z       │  High‑Z       │  Only pull‑up active → ~1.29 V  │
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] │  Active pixel levels  │  Output high  │  High‑Z       │  Medium bright (~1.75 V)        │
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] │                       │  High‑Z       │  Output high  │  Slightly brighter (~1.55 V)    │
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] │                       │  Output high  │  Output high  │  Maximum bright (~2.15 V)       │
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] ╰───────────────────────┴───────────────┴───────────────┴─────────────────────────────────╯
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] (Values from earlier calculation)
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] PIO pattern adjustment
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] If you previously controlled a single luma pin with 3 bits per pixel (e.g., `out pindirs, 1` + `out
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] pins, 1`), you now need 2 bits for direction and 2 bits for data. Your `threepin` or `colorfifo`
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] output word should include:
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] - Bits for luma direction and level
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] - Bits for grey direction and level
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] Example word layout (low bits first):
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] bit 0: luma enable (pindir)
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] bit 1: luma data  (pins)
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] bit 2: grey enable (pindir)
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] bit 3: grey data  (pins)
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] Then a single `out pindirs, 2` (bits 0 and 2) sets both directions, and `out pins, 2` (bits 1 and
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] 3) sets both levels.
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:53:46 [deepseek-v4] Recommendation
2026-05-14 13:53:47 [deepseek-v4] - Set both pins to high‑Z during blanking (both directions = 0).
2026-05-14 13:53:47 [deepseek-v4] - During sync, drive both output low (both directions = 1, data = 0).
2026-05-14 13:53:47 [deepseek-v4] - During active video, choose the combination of enabled outputs and data to generate desired luma.
2026-05-14 13:53:47 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:53:47 [deepseek-v4] This gives you full control of the tristate DAC while keeping the PIO code simple. Always
2026-05-14 13:53:47 [deepseek-v4] disabling/unused pins avoids unintended current paths.
2026-05-14 13:53:47 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:56:34 [deepseek-v4] Your `vidbit.h` now shows the 300 Ω resistor for GREY – that’s a good binary‑weighted choice
2026-05-14 13:56:34 [deepseek-v4] (150 Ω : 300 Ω = 2:1). The tristate approach is appropriate.
2026-05-14 13:56:34 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:56:34 [deepseek-v4] The new `grey.pio` program is interesting, but it has a few issues:
2026-05-14 13:56:34 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:56:34 [deepseek-v4] 1. Only sets pin direction, never pin data
2026-05-14 13:56:34 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:56:34 [deepseek-v4] out pindirs, 1   ; enable/disable output (direction)
2026-05-14 13:56:34 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:56:34 [deepseek-v4] It never executes `out pins, 1` to set the actual logic level when the pin is enabled. With only
2026-05-14 13:56:34 [deepseek-v4] `pindirs` controlled:
2026-05-14 13:56:34 [deepseek-v4] - When direction = 1, the pin becomes an output, but its level is undefined (depends on
2026-05-14 13:56:34 [deepseek-v4] previous state of `out pins` or default). For tristate DAC you need both:
2026-05-14 13:56:34 [deepseek-v4]   - Direction = 1 → pin acts as output, level determined by `out pins` or `set pins`.
2026-05-14 13:56:34 [deepseek-v4]   - Direction = 0 → pin is high‑Z (and `out pins` is ignored).
2026-05-14 13:56:34 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:56:34 [deepseek-v4] You should add `out pins, 1` (or `set pins, 1`) before (or after) the `out pindirs, 1` instructions
2026-05-14 13:56:34 [deepseek-v4] to set the data high. For blanking, you would output direction 0. For sync, you output direction 1
2026-05-14 13:56:34 [deepseek-v4] with data 0.
2026-05-14 13:56:34 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:56:34 [deepseek-v4] 2. Missing `.fifo txget` or `.fifo` directive
2026-05-14 13:56:34 [deepseek-v4] The program uses `mov osr, rxfifo[y]` (indexed FIFO read). This requires the PIO to be configured
2026-05-14 13:56:34 [deepseek-v4] as a TX FIFO input (`.fifo txget`). If you don't add that directive, the PIO will treat the
2026-05-14 13:56:35 [deepseek-v4] FIFO normally and the `rxfifo[y]` syntax won’t be recognised. Check your original `colorfifo.pio` –
2026-05-14 13:56:35 [deepseek-v4] it had `.fifo txget`. Add the same line.
2026-05-14 13:56:35 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:56:35 [deepseek-v4] 3. Indexed read timing
2026-05-14 13:56:35 [deepseek-v4] `rxfifo[y]` reads the entry at index `y` in the RX FIFO (which must be pre‑loaded with four 32‑bit
2026-05-14 13:56:35 [deepseek-v4] words). This is fine, but ensure the FIFO is filled before the state machine starts.
2026-05-14 13:56:35 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:56:35 [deepseek-v4] 4. The program does not handle sync or blanking
2026-05-14 13:56:35 [deepseek-v4] If you use `grey.pio` alone, it will only output waveforms during active pixels (when triggered by
2026-05-14 13:56:35 [deepseek-v4] a "stall" pin). During blanking and sync, you need the grey pin to be either high‑Z or driven low.
2026-05-14 13:56:35 [deepseek-v4] Your calling state machine (e.g., `colorpump`) should set the index pins appropriately: for
2026-05-14 13:56:35 [deepseek-v4] blanking, set both index pins to a value that causes `rxfifo[y]` to yield a word with all zeros
2026-05-14 13:56:35 [deepseek-v4] (direction = 0). That word must be pre‑loaded.
2026-05-14 13:56:35 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:56:35 [deepseek-v4] Suggested correction (combine with luma control)
2026-05-14 13:56:35 [deepseek-v4] If the grey pin is part of a two‑pin luma DAC, you likely want a single PIO that drives both luma
2026-05-14 13:56:35 [deepseek-v4] and grey pins together. Currently you have separate PIO machines for colour channels; for luma it
2026-05-14 13:56:35 [deepseek-v4] might be simpler to have one machine that outputs 2‑bit pindirs and 2‑bit pins per pixel. For
2026-05-14 13:56:35 [deepseek-v4] example, a word could contain:
2026-05-14 13:56:35 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:56:35 [deepseek-v4] [bit 0: luma_dir, bit 1: luma_data, bit 2: grey_dir, bit 3: grey_data]
2026-05-14 13:56:35 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:56:35 [deepseek-v4] Then `out pindirs, 2` sets both directions, `out pins, 2` sets both data levels. That would be more
2026-05-14 13:56:35 [deepseek-v4] efficient and synchronised.
2026-05-14 13:56:35 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:56:35 [deepseek-v4] If you prefer separate machines, ensure each machine outputs both direction and data for its pin.
2026-05-14 13:56:35 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:56:35 [deepseek-v4] • Conclusion*: `grey.pio` is a start but needs `out pins` and the `.fifo txget` directive.
2026-05-14 13:56:35 [deepseek-v4] For a tristate system, always control both direction and data to avoid undefined output levels.
2026-05-14 13:56:35 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4] Your updated `grey.pio` now uses `.fifo txget` and depends on initial C++ setting the pin output
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4] high. This approach is entirely valid for a tristate DAC: the pin drives high (3.3 V) whenever its
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4] direction is 1, and is high‑Z when direction is 0. Since the output data register stays high, you
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4] get two logic states: high or high‑Z. Combined with the LUMA pin (also controlled similarly), you
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4] achieve four distinct luma levels as described earlier.
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4] • However, I see a critical timing issue with the pixel trigger handling.*
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4] The `stall` loop problem
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4] stall:
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4]     jmp    pin stall      ; wait while pin is high
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4]     mov    y, pins
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4]     mov    osr, rxfifo[y]
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4]     out    pindirs, 1     ; 6 cycles of pattern
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4]     out    pindirs, 1
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4]     out    pindirs, 1
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4]     out    pindirs, 1
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4]     out    pindirs, 1
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4]     out    pindirs, 1
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4]     set    pindirs, 0
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4] .wrap
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4] - The first instruction spins as long as the trigger pin is high.
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4] - When the trigger pin goes low, execution falls through to the pixel output.
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4] - After 7 cycles (6 `out` + 1 `set`), the program wraps back to `stall:`.
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4] - If the trigger pin is still low at that moment, the `jmp pin stall` will immediately
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4] fall through again, causing a second pixel output without waiting for the next line.
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4] For a 10‑cycle pixel, the pin likely stays low for the entire pixel period (≈60 ns). After your
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4] 7‑cycle output, there are still 3 cycles left in the pixel. The pin is still low, so the program
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4] will instantly start a new pixel – breaking the horizontal timing.
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4] Fix: Wait for pin to go high again
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4] Insert a loop that waits for the trigger pin to return high before accepting the next trigger. For
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4] example:
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4] stall:
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4]     jmp    pin stall       ; wait while pin high
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4]     mov    y, pins
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4]     mov    osr, rxfifo[y]
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4]     out    pindirs, 1
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4]     out    pindirs, 1
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4]     out    pindirs, 1
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4]     out    pindirs, 1
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4]     out    pindirs, 1
2026-05-14 13:59:25 [deepseek-v4]     out    pindirs, 1
2026-05-14 13:59:26 [deepseek-v4]     set    pindirs, 0
2026-05-14 13:59:26 [deepseek-v4] wait_high:
2026-05-14 13:59:26 [deepseek-v4]     jmp    pin wait_high   ; wait while pin low
2026-05-14 13:59:26 [deepseek-v4] .wrap
2026-05-14 13:59:26 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:59:26 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:59:26 [deepseek-v4] Now the state machine will not start the next pixel until the trigger pin has gone high and
2026-05-14 13:59:26 [deepseek-v4] low again. This matches the expected behaviour of a pulse‑triggered pixel.
2026-05-14 13:59:26 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:59:26 [deepseek-v4] Additional notes
2026-05-14 13:59:26 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:59:26 [deepseek-v4] - Your C++ initialisation sets the pin to output high. That remains unchanged – the PIO only
2026-05-14 13:59:26 [deepseek-v4] toggles direction. This is correct.
2026-05-14 13:59:26 [deepseek-v4] - The `rxfifo[y]` is pre‑loaded with four 32‑bit words. Each word’s lower 6 bits control the 6 `out
2026-05-14 13:59:26 [deepseek-v4] pindirs` instructions. The 7th cycle forces direction 0 (high‑Z). The remaining 3 cycles are now
2026-05-14 13:59:26 [deepseek-v4] consumed by the `waithigh` loop? Actually after `set` you have 1 cycle, then the `waithigh`
2026-05-14 13:59:26 [deepseek-v4] loop will spin until pin goes high. That could take variable cycles – not constant. But that’s fine
2026-05-14 13:59:26 [deepseek-v4] because the next pixel must not start until the trigger pin rises again, which should happen at the
2026-05-14 13:59:26 [deepseek-v4] end of the pixel (after 10 cycles total). The waiting loop adds a variable delay but it is bounded
2026-05-14 13:59:26 [deepseek-v4] by the time the pin stays low. If the pin is driven low for exactly 10 cycles by the caller, then
2026-05-14 13:59:26 [deepseek-v4] the `wait_high` loop will wait for the remaining 3 cycles (since the 7 `out`+`set` cycles already
2026-05-14 13:59:26 [deepseek-v4] consumed 7). That will give the exact 10‑cycle timing.
2026-05-14 13:59:26 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:59:26 [deepseek-v4]    Actually, you could also replace the `set pindirs,0` with a `nop` and let the `wait_high` loop
2026-05-14 13:59:26 [deepseek-v4] consume the remaining cycles. But as long as the trigger pin timing is precise, this works.
2026-05-14 13:59:26 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:59:26 [deepseek-v4] Summary
2026-05-14 13:59:26 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 13:59:26 [deepseek-v4] - Using initial high output + direction‑only control is a valid, efficient way to drive a tristate
2026-05-14 13:59:26 [deepseek-v4] DAC.
2026-05-14 13:59:26 [deepseek-v4] - Add the `wait_high` loop to prevent premature re‑triggering.
2026-05-14 13:59:26 [deepseek-v4] - Otherwise the PIO code is sound for producing a 6‑cycle pulse (or any pattern) followed by high‑Z
2026-05-14 13:59:26 [deepseek-v4] for the rest of the pixel.
2026-05-14 13:59:26 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] Your code has several compilation and logic errors, mostly in `vidbit.cpp`. Here are the critical
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] ones:
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] 1. `grey_init` uses wrong PIO block
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] pio_sm_put_blocking(pio2, sm, bitTrain);   // should be pio, not pio2
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] `pio2` is never defined or used elsewhere; change to `pio`. This will cause a crash or undefined
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] behaviour.
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] 2. Missing `smconfigsetjmppin` in `grey_init`
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] The `grey.pio` program uses `jmp pin` (for trigger). You must set the jmp pin:
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] sm_config_set_jmp_pin(&config8, PUMP0_ENABLE);   // or whatever trigger pin
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] Without this, the state machine will never exit the `stall` loop (default jmp pin 0 may be high).
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] 3. `greyinit` does not initialize GREYPIN as PIO output
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] You call `piogpioinit` for index pins, but not for `GREY_PIN`. Add:
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] pio_gpio_init(pio, GREY_PIN);
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] In `main()`, you then call `gpioinit(GREYPIN)` and `gpiosetdir(GREYPIN,
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] GPIOIN)` – this overrides PIO control. Remove those lines; let PIO handle direction.
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] 4. `grey_init` sets consecutive pindirs for wrong pins
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] pio_sm_set_consecutive_pindirs(pio, sm, COLOR_I0, 3, false);
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] This sets pins 0,1,2 as inputs (index pins). That’s correct, but `COLORI0` is 0, `COLORI1`
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] is 1, `PUMP0ENABLE` is 2 – fine. However, you must also set `GREYPIN` as output-capable
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] (direction controlled by PIO). That’s done by the `piosmsetconsecutivepindirs`
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] inside the config? No – you need to explicitly set the pin direction to output via
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] `smconfigsetoutpins` and then `piosmsetconsecutivepindirs(...,
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] true)` for the output pin after init. Currently you set it as input; change to:
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] pio_sm_set_consecutive_pindirs(pio, sm, GREY_PIN, 1, true);
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] But only after the state machine is initialized and before enabling it? Actually, set it to true
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] before enabling. Or let the PIO control it via the `out pindirs` instructions; but the initial
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] direction must be output for the pin to work. The PIO will override it each cycle. So set it as
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] output initially.
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] 5. `greyinit` missing `smconfigsetout_shift`
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] You commented out the shift config – uncomment it:
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 15:34:20 [deepseek-v4] sm_config_set_out_shift(&config8, true, true, 32);
2026-05-14 15:34:21 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 15:34:21 [deepseek-v4] Otherwise the `out pindirs` instructions may not work correctly.
2026-05-14 15:34:21 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 15:34:21 [deepseek-v4] 6. Potential mismatch in frame buffer initializer sizes
2026-05-14 15:34:21 [deepseek-v4] In `pal576i.cpp` and `monobit.cpp`, you initialize arrays with fixed initializers, but the actual
2026-05-14 15:34:21 [deepseek-v4] size `evenWords`, `oddWords`, `progressiveWordCount` depend on constants. If you later change
2026-05-14 15:34:21 [deepseek-v4] `lineWords`, `evenLines`, etc., the initializer will have wrong element count. Use `sizeof` or rely
2026-05-14 15:34:21 [deepseek-v4] on the compiler to warn – but better to use `std::vector` or ensure the initializer length matches
2026-05-14 15:34:21 [deepseek-v4] exactly.
2026-05-14 15:34:21 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 15:34:21 [deepseek-v4] 7. `std::optional` and `std::vector` may cause heap fragmentation on Pico
2026-05-14 15:34:21 [deepseek-v4] Not a compilation error, but `cdcReadLine()` with `std::vector` can fragment memory. For embedded
2026-05-14 15:34:21 [deepseek-v4] use, a fixed ring buffer is safer.
2026-05-14 15:34:21 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 15:34:21 [deepseek-v4] 8. Unused function `uartComms` (dead code, harmless)
2026-05-14 15:34:21 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 15:34:21 [deepseek-v4] 9. The custom `sin` function may shadow `std::sin` in some contexts
2026-05-14 15:34:21 [deepseek-v4] In `nitro.h` you declare `float sin(float rad);`. In `main()` you call `sinf` and use the custom
2026-05-14 15:34:21 [deepseek-v4] `sin` later. That’s fine, but if any header includes `<cmath>` after `nitro.h`, the compiler may
2026-05-14 15:34:21 [deepseek-v4] see both. Consider wrapping in a namespace.
