2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] Your updated code fixes the `pio2` issue in `grey_init`, but several critical problems remain that
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] will prevent the grey channel from working.
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] 1. Missing PIO configuration for grey state machine
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] `grey_init` currently has these lines commented out:
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] //	sm_config_set_out_shift(&config8, true, true, 32);
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] //	sm_config_set_fifo_join(&config8, PIO_FIFO_JOIN_TX);
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] The `grey.pio` program uses `out pindirs` and `.fifo txget` with `rxfifo[y]`, which require:
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] - Output shift register configured (uncomment `smconfigsetoutshift`)
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] - FIFO joined as TX‑only to enable indexed RX reads (uncomment
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] `smconfigsetfifojoin`)
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] Without these, the state machine will not decode `out pindirs` correctly and `rxfifo[y]` will read
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] undefined data.
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] 2. Missing jmp pin configuration
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] You removed `GREYENABLE` and the `smconfigsetjmppin` line. The `grey.pio`
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] program starts with `jmp pin stall` – which GPIO does it wait on? By default, the jmp pin is 0,
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] which may be held high by other circuitry, causing the state machine to stall forever. You must
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] define a pin (e.g., GP2) and set it via `smconfigsetjmp_pin`. Also ensure that this pin
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] is driven low by your video timing state machine during active video (modify `vidbit.pio` or assign
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] a separate pin).
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] 3. Missing `piogpioinit` for `GREY_PIN`
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] You call `smconfigsetsetpins` and `smconfigsetoutpins` for
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] GREY_PIN, but you never initialise that GPIO for PIO control. Add:
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] pio_gpio_init(pio, GREY_PIN);
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] inside `grey_init`.
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] 4. Manual GPIO initialisation overrides PIO in `main()`
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] Lines:
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] gpio_init(GREY_PIN);
2026-05-14 16:17:19 [deepseek-v4] gpio_set_dir(GREY_PIN, GPIO_IN);
2026-05-14 16:17:20 [deepseek-v4] ...
2026-05-14 16:17:20 [deepseek-v4] gpio_put(GREY_PIN,false);
2026-05-14 16:17:20 [deepseek-v4] gpio_set_dir(GREY_PIN, GPIO_IN);
2026-05-14 16:17:20 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 16:17:20 [deepseek-v4] These set the pin to SIO input, which conflicts with PIO. The PIO will override direction each
2026-05-14 16:17:20 [deepseek-v4] cycle via `out pindirs`, but the initial state and potential glitches are undefined. Remove all
2026-05-14 16:17:20 [deepseek-v4] manual GPIO configuration for `GREY_PIN` – let PIO control it fully.
2026-05-14 16:17:20 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 16:17:20 [deepseek-v4] 5. `grey_init` pre‑loads FIFO via push/pull sequence – works only with TX‑join
2026-05-14 16:17:20 [deepseek-v4] The current sequence:
2026-05-14 16:17:20 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 16:17:20 [deepseek-v4] pio_sm_put_blocking(pio, sm, bitTrain);
2026-05-14 16:17:20 [deepseek-v4] pio_sm_exec(pio, sm, pio_encode_pull(false, false));
2026-05-14 16:17:20 [deepseek-v4] pio_sm_exec(pio, sm, pio_encode_mov(pio_isr, pio_osr));
2026-05-14 16:17:20 [deepseek-v4] pio_sm_exec(pio, sm, pio_encode_push(false, false));
2026-05-14 16:17:20 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 16:17:20 [deepseek-v4] Is correct only if the FIFO is joined as TX‑only (the `push` will then go to the RX side). Without
2026-05-14 16:17:20 [deepseek-v4] `PIOFIFOJOIN_TX`, `push` will write to a separate RX FIFO that may be full or improperly
2026-05-14 16:17:20 [deepseek-v4] addressed. So point 1 is essential.
2026-05-14 16:17:20 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 16:17:20 [deepseek-v4] 6. The trigger pin (GP2) must be driven by the video timing state machine
2026-05-14 16:17:20 [deepseek-v4] Currently `vidbit.pio` only drives `VID_PIN` (GP22). It does not toggle any other pin for pixel
2026-05-14 16:17:20 [deepseek-v4] synchronization. For `grey.pio` to be triggered, you need a dedicated line (e.g., GP2) that goes
2026-05-14 16:17:20 [deepseek-v4] low for the duration of each active pixel. You could:
2026-05-14 16:17:20 [deepseek-v4] - Modify `vidbit.pio` to output a short low pulse on a second pin every 10 cycles during active
2026-05-14 16:17:20 [deepseek-v4] video.
2026-05-14 16:17:20 [deepseek-v4] - Or use an IRQ handshake (more complex but avoids extra pins).
2026-05-14 16:17:20 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 16:17:20 [deepseek-v4] Given the current design, the grey state machine will never exit its `stall` loop because the jmp
2026-05-14 16:17:20 [deepseek-v4] pin stays high.
2026-05-14 16:17:20 [deepseek-v4] 
