2026-05-15 11:30:59 [deepseek-v4] bytes) and found a small but meaningful change:
2026-05-15 11:30:59 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-15 11:30:59 [deepseek-v4] Change detected
2026-05-15 11:30:59 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-15 11:30:59 [deepseek-v4] In the `greyinit()` function, you now check the return value of `piosm_init()` for failure:
2026-05-15 11:30:59 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-15 11:30:59 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-15 11:30:59 [deepseek-v4] int pioOffsetStatus = pio_sm_init(pio, sm, offset8, &config8);
2026-05-15 11:30:59 [deepseek-v4] if(pioOffsetStatus==-1){
2026-05-15 11:30:59 [deepseek-v4]     return false;
2026-05-15 11:30:59 [deepseek-v4] }
2026-05-15 11:30:59 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-15 11:30:59 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-15 11:30:59 [deepseek-v4] Previously, the return value was ignored. This is a good defensive improvement—it prevents the
2026-05-15 11:30:59 [deepseek-v4] state machine from being enabled if the program could not be loaded into PIO instruction memory
2026-05-15 11:30:59 [deepseek-v4] (e.g., insufficient space). The program now returns `false` in that case, and `main()` will fall
2026-05-15 11:30:59 [deepseek-v4] through to `romresetusb_boot` correctly.
