2026-05-15 11:42:20 [deepseek-v4] The `greyline.pio` program is new to the collection. It implements a simple grey‑level output for
2026-05-15 11:42:20 [deepseek-v4] the luma pin (GP18) using a 6‑cycle per pixel pulse‑train, selected from four pre‑loaded patterns
2026-05-15 11:42:20 [deepseek-v4] in the RX FIFO. This matches the “colorfifo” concept described in `colorpump.md`, but for a
2026-05-15 11:42:20 [deepseek-v4] single‑bit (monochrome) output.
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] Program flow:
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] 1. `set pindirs, 0` – tristate the output pin (high‑impedance, pulled high by external
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] resistor as noted in comments).
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] 2. `wait 1 irq 5` – pause until the video timing state machine (`vidbit.pio`) asserts IRQ 5
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] at the start of each active‑video pixel.
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] 3. `mov y, pins` – read two input pins (presumably GPIO 0 and 1) as an index 0–3 selecting
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] one of four pre‑loaded pulse trains.
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] 4. `mov osr, rxfifo[y]` – fetch the 32‑bit pattern from the RX FIFO using the `txget` fifo
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] encoding.
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] 5. Six consecutive `out pindirs, 1` – shift out the lower 6 bits of the pattern, one bit
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] per PIO cycle, driving the pin as output (1 = on, 0 = off) for a 6‑cycle sub‑pixel sequence.
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] 6. `.wrap` – restart the loop, tristating the pin again.
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] Integration with existing code:
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] - In `vidbit.cpp`, `greyinit()` pre‑loads four patterns from `pdmpatterns[]` (0..5 bits
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] each, padded to 32 bits) into the greyline SM’s RX FIFO.
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] - The two‑bit index comes from the colorpump PIO state machine, which drives GPIO 0 and 1
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] with a 2‑bit colour index for each pixel (see `colorpump.pio`). Thus the greyline SM outputs a
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] different 6‑step greyscale waveform for each of the four colour indices.
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] - The 6‑cycle‑per‑pixel structure matches the `colorfifo.pio` design, enabling a simple form of
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] monochrome PWM (6 levels) for each pixel.
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] Potential issues / checks:
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] - The `txget` encoding consumes an RX FIFO entry each pixel. The RX FIFO is 4‑deep; it will drain
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] in 4 pixels. The C++ code only pre‑loads 4 entries once during init. After those 4 entries are
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] consumed, the SM will begin reading stale data (or stall if the FIFO empties and the SM is not
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] configured to stall). The preloading must be repeated per frame or per line unless the
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] entries are re‑filled by DMA. The current `grey_init` only does one‑time preload; the SM will run
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] out after 4 pixels. This is a critical bug. Either a DMA channel should feed the RX FIFO
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] repeatedly with the same 4 patterns (ring buffer), or the preloading must happen in a loop. Since
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] the program uses `.fifo txget` (RX FIFO), it cannot be refilled by the same SM; it must be refilled
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] externally (e.g., by CPU or another DMA). The current design will fail after the fourth pixel.
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] - The `set pindirs, 0` tristates the pin between pixels. Ensure that the pin’s pull‑up or resistor
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] network maintains the desired high level during the tristate period. The comment says “c++
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] initialises tristate pin output as high” – likely an external pull‑up resistor to 3.3V.
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] - The SM clock divider is not set in the C++ code (default is 1). At 166.5 MHz, 6 cycles per pixel
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] takes ~36 ns, which is fine for a single pixel’s grey level. However, the SM must be synchronised
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] to the `vidbit` state machine’s IRQ5 timing – `vidbit.pio` sets IRQ5 once per pixel during the
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] active video portion. The frequency of IRQ5 matches the SM’s speed, so no clock division is needed.
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] Summary:
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] The `greyline.pio` program is a neat, minimal implementation of a 4‑colour, 6‑step greyscale
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] modulator. The main issue is that the RX FIFO is only pre‑loaded once, causing the output
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] to fail after the first 4 pixels of a frame. A DMA ring buffer feeding the same 4 words repeatedly
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] would solve this. Alternatively, the SM could be redesigned to read from a simple counter and not
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] require FIFO refill – but that would lose the ability to change patterns per colour index
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] dynamically.
2026-05-15 11:42:21 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-15 11:45:43 [nitro@ryzen5] gpt is this correct, can i not use rxfifo indexing conitnuously in my pio code?
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] No — my earlier “it drains after four pixels” comment was not correct for the RP2350
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] indexed FIFO use you’re using.
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] With RP2350 PIO indexed FIFO access such as:
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] .fifo txget
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] mov	osr, rxfifo[y]
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] the indexed `rxfifo[y]` access is intended to behave like a small indexed table / lookup source,
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] not like a normal `pull` that consumes FIFO entries. So yes, you can repeatedly read `rxfifo[0..3]`
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] continuously as a preloaded pulse-train table.
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] Your preload approach in `grey_init()`:
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] - put word into TX FIFO
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] - force `pull`
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] - move OSR to ISR
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] - force `push`
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] is a reasonable way to seed the RX FIFO table before enabling the SM.
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] So this part is conceptually fine:
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] mov	y, pins
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] mov	osr, rxfifo[y]
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] out	pindirs, 1
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] ...
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] The table entries should not be consumed by each `mov osr, rxfifo[y]`.
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] A few related notes, though:
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] 1. `piosminit()` does not return a status
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:53 [gpt-5.5-2026] In the Pico SDK, `piosminit()` returns `void`, not an offset or error code. So this will
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] not be valid with the normal SDK:
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] int pioOffsetStatus = pio_sm_init(pio, sm, offset8, &config8);
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] if(pioOffsetStatus == -1) {
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 	return false;
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] }
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] The correct failure check is the one you already do before loading:
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] if (!pio_can_add_program(pio, program8)) return false;
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] Then `pioaddprogram()` gives you the offset. `piosminit()` just configures the
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] state machine.
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 2. You probably need `piogpioinit()` for `GREY_PIN`
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] In `grey_init()` you initialise the index pins:
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] pio_gpio_init(pio, GREY_I0);
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] pio_gpio_init(pio, GREY_I1);
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] but I do not see:
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] pio_gpio_init(pio, GREY_PIN);
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] Since `greyline.pio` drives `pindirs` on `GREY_PIN`, that pin needs to be assigned to the correct
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] PIO function. Otherwise SIO may still own the pin.
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 3. IRQ 5 is currently line-start, not per-pixel
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] Your `greyline.pio` comment says:
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] ; waits irq 5 per pixel
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] but `vidbit.pio` currently does:
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] irq	set 5
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] active:
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 	out	pindirs, 1 [8]
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 	jmp	y--,active
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] That sets IRQ 5 once before the active region, not once per pixel.
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] Because `greyline.pio` does not clear IRQ 5, after the first `wait 1 irq 5`, the wait will pass
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] immediately forever. The loop then free-runs at 10 PIO cycles per grey pixel:
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:54 [gpt-5.5-2026] - `set pindirs, 0`
2026-05-15 11:46:55 [gpt-5.5-2026] - `wait 1 irq 5`
2026-05-15 11:46:55 [gpt-5.5-2026] - `mov y, pins`
2026-05-15 11:46:55 [gpt-5.5-2026] - `mov osr, rxfifo[y]`
2026-05-15 11:46:55 [gpt-5.5-2026] - six `out pindirs, 1`
2026-05-15 11:46:55 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:55 [gpt-5.5-2026] That is 10 cycles, which matches your pixel cycle, but it means it only gets synchronised once
2026-05-15 11:46:55 [gpt-5.5-2026] unless something clears/re-arms IRQ 5 per line or per active span.
2026-05-15 11:46:55 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:55 [gpt-5.5-2026] So: the FIFO table idea is fine, but the sync behaviour may need checking.
2026-05-15 11:46:55 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:55 [gpt-5.5-2026] 4. `mov y, pins` index range
2026-05-15 11:46:55 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:55 [gpt-5.5-2026] If `mov y, pins` gives more than just the two low index bits, `rxfifo[y]` may rely on the indexed
2026-05-15 11:46:55 [gpt-5.5-2026] FIFO operation masking the index. If the hardware masks to the FIFO depth, fine. If not, you may
2026-05-15 11:46:55 [gpt-5.5-2026] need to ensure only GPIO 0–1 contribute to `y`.
2026-05-15 11:46:55 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:55 [gpt-5.5-2026] Bottom line
2026-05-15 11:46:55 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:55 [gpt-5.5-2026] Yes, you can use `rxfifo[y]` continuously as a preloaded lookup table on RP2350 PIO. It
2026-05-15 11:46:55 [gpt-5.5-2026] should not drain like a conventional FIFO read.
2026-05-15 11:46:55 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:55 [gpt-5.5-2026] The more important things to check now are:
2026-05-15 11:46:55 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
2026-05-15 11:46:55 [gpt-5.5-2026] - remove the `piosminit()` return-value check,
2026-05-15 11:46:55 [gpt-5.5-2026] - initialise `GREYPIN` with `piogpioinit(pio, GREYPIN)`,
2026-05-15 11:46:55 [gpt-5.5-2026] - confirm the IRQ 5 synchronisation model,
2026-05-15 11:46:55 [gpt-5.5-2026] - confirm `mov y, pins` only produces indexes 0–3, or is safely masked by the FIFO indexing mode.
2026-05-15 11:46:55 [gpt-5.5-2026] 
