2026-05-15 12:17:04 [kimi-k2.6] Deepseek did not claim `jmp` is always single‑cycle. In both RP2040 and RP2350 datasheets, `jmp`
2026-05-15 12:17:04 [kimi-k2.6] costs 1 cycle when the condition is false (fall‑through) and 2 cycles when the
2026-05-15 12:17:04 [kimi-k2.6] condition is true (branch taken). Deepseek applied the 2‑cycle figure to `jmp y--, active` for
2026-05-15 12:17:04 [kimi-k2.6] every iteration where `y` was non‑zero — which is the documented behaviour.
2026-05-15 12:17:04 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-15 12:17:04 [kimi-k2.6] If you are assuming `jmp` costs 1 cycle even when taken, then your loop timing becomes:
2026-05-15 12:17:04 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-15 12:17:04 [kimi-k2.6] `out` (1) + `irq set 6 [6]` (7) + `irq clear 6` (1) + `jmp` (1) = 10 cycles
2026-05-15 12:17:04 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-15 12:17:04 [kimi-k2.6] That would make `[6]` correct. But on actual PIO hardware the taken branch incurs an extra cycle to
2026-05-15 12:17:04 [kimi-k2.6] reload the program counter, so the conservative reading gives 11 cycles with `[6]`, and
2026-05-15 12:17:04 [kimi-k2.6] 10 cycles only if the delay is reduced to `[5]`.
2026-05-15 12:17:04 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-15 12:17:04 [kimi-k2.6] If you have scope measurements or RP2350 errata showing a taken `jmp` executes in 1 cycle, that
2026-05-15 12:17:04 [kimi-k2.6] would override the datasheet — otherwise the hardware will likely run one cycle slower per pixel
2026-05-15 12:17:04 [kimi-k2.6] than your intended budget.
