2026-05-15 13:36:19 [claude-haiku-4-5] 3. colorfifo.pio vs. colorpump.pio Coordination
2026-05-15 13:36:19 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-05-15 13:36:19 [claude-haiku-4-5] From your markdown:
2026-05-15 13:36:19 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-05-15 13:36:19 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-05-15 13:36:19 [claude-haiku-4-5] ; colorfifo waits on jmp pin 2, then reads indexed RX FIFO
2026-05-15 13:36:19 [claude-haiku-4-5] ; colorpump outputs 2-bit color index on pins, pulses side-set to trigger colorfifo
2026-05-15 13:36:19 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-05-15 13:36:19 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-05-15 13:36:19 [claude-haiku-4-5] • Architecture looks sound*, but:
2026-05-15 13:36:19 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-05-15 13:36:19 [claude-haiku-4-5] - colorfifo stalls on `jmp pin stall` (waiting for pixel enable from vidbit).
2026-05-15 13:36:19 [claude-haiku-4-5] - colorpump decodes a 2-bit index into 4 side-set pulses (pins 7, 11, 13, 14).
2026-05-15 13:36:19 [claude-haiku-4-5] - Each colorfifo.rxfifo[0-3] pre-loaded with a 6-cycle pulse train.
2026-05-15 13:36:19 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-05-15 13:36:19 [claude-haiku-4-5] • Question:* How does colorpump's ISR index sync to colorfifo's `mov y, pins`? Are GP46-47
2026-05-15 13:36:19 [claude-haiku-4-5] being driven by colorpump continuously, or only on pixel boundaries?
