2026-05-17 08:51:13 [claude-haiku-4-5] Here are the main approaches to enumerate Windows COM ports in C++:
2026-05-17 08:51:13 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-05-17 08:51:13 [claude-haiku-4-5] • 1. Registry Query (Most Common)*
2026-05-17 08:51:13 [claude-haiku-4-5] Query `HKEYLOCALMACHINE\HARDWARE\DEVICEMAP\SERIALCOMM` for serial port mappings.
2026-05-17 08:51:13 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-05-17 08:51:13 [claude-haiku-4-5] • 2. WMI (Windows Management Instrumentation)*
2026-05-17 08:51:13 [claude-haiku-4-5] Query Win32_SerialPort class for detailed port information.
2026-05-17 08:51:13 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-05-17 08:51:13 [claude-haiku-4-5] • 3. SetupAPI*
2026-05-17 08:51:13 [claude-haiku-4-5] Use device setup functions to enumerate serial devices with full control over filtering and
2026-05-17 08:51:13 [claude-haiku-4-5] properties.
2026-05-17 08:51:13 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-05-17 08:51:13 [claude-haiku-4-5] • 4. Direct Registry Enumeration*
2026-05-17 08:51:13 [claude-haiku-4-5] Walk `HKEYLOCALMACHINE\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Enum\PCI` or USB paths for connected
2026-05-17 08:51:13 [claude-haiku-4-5] devices.
2026-05-17 08:51:13 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-05-17 08:51:13 [claude-haiku-4-5] • 5. COM Port Brute Force*
2026-05-17 08:51:13 [claude-haiku-4-5] Simply attempt to open `COM1` through `COM256` and catch failures (crude but functional).
2026-05-17 08:51:14 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-05-17 08:51:14 [claude-haiku-4-5] • Recommendation for your use case:*
2026-05-17 08:51:14 [claude-haiku-4-5] Given the UF2/microcontroller context, SetupAPI or Registry query would be most
2026-05-17 08:51:14 [claude-haiku-4-5] reliable. SetupAPI is more robust for detecting newly connected devices.
2026-05-17 08:51:14 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-05-17 08:51:14 [claude-haiku-4-5] Which approach interests you? I can provide working code if you'd like to implement one.
2026-05-17 08:51:14 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
