2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] Based on the files you shared, the term "hub" most likely refers to the HUB75
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] interface used in the `hub75` example. That example demonstrates driving a RGB LED matrix panel
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] (1/32‑scan) using PIO.
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] What is HUB75?
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] HUB75 is a standard connector interface for large RGB LED panels (commonly found in advertising
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] displays, scoreboards, and decorative lighting). It gets its name from the 75‑pin connector (often
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] two rows of pins) used to connect the panel to a controller.
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] Key characteristics of the HUB75 interface (from the readme and code):
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] - 6 colour‑data lines (R0, G0, B0 for the upper half of the screen; R1, G1, B1 for the
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] lower half)
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] - 5 row‑select lines (A, B, C, D, E) to select one of 32 rows
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] - Clock (CLK) – shifts data into the panel’s shift registers
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] - Strobe (STB) / Latch – latches the shifted row data into the output
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] - Output Enable (OEn) – turns the LEDs on/off, used for PWM dimming
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] - Ground pins
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] The Pio example (`hub75.c` and `hub75.pio`) implements the complete timing to drive such a panel:
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] - Two PIO state machines are used:
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4]   - One to shift pixel data for RGB888 (or RGB565) into the panel’s shift registers.
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4]   - Another to control row selection, latch, and output‑enable timing.
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] - The algorithm uses bit‑plane PWM: it sends the same frame 8 times, each time outputting a
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] different bit of the colour data, while the other state machine controls the PWM pulse width (1, 2,
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] 4, … 128 units) to build up 8‑bit colour depth.
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] Practical use
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] If you have a HUB75‑compatible 64×32 or 128×64 RGB panel (often 1/16 or 1/32 scan), you can use
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] this example with minimal wiring changes. The readme gives the exact pin mapping, and the image
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] file `mountains128x64rgb565.h` is a pre‑converted test image.
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] In summary, “hub” in this context means the standard HUB75 interface for driving large RGB
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] LED matrices using PIO‑based state machines. Let me know if you want a deeper dive into the code or
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] help adapting it to a different panel size.
2026-05-17 12:31:57 [deepseek-v4] 
