2026-05-18 15:47:41 [nitro@ryzen5] lets focus on the colorpump, can i use sidepin?
2026-05-18 15:49:14 [kimi-k2.6]  ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.6 ⛲  kimi-k2.6 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.130 318.5KB 92.32s
2026-05-18 15:49:14 [kimi-k2.6] You can, but it will not shrink the program the way you might hope.
2026-05-18 15:49:14 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 15:49:14 [kimi-k2.6] Side-set values are opcode immediates — they are baked into each instruction word at
2026-05-18 15:49:14 [kimi-k2.6] assembly time, not loaded from a register at run time. Because your selector is dynamic (the 2-bit
2026-05-18 15:49:14 [kimi-k2.6] value in `X`), you still need the branch tree to pick which instruction runs. Each branch
2026-05-18 15:49:14 [kimi-k2.6] target can carry its own hard-coded side-set pattern, so yes, the four trigger pins can be driven
2026-05-18 15:49:14 [kimi-k2.6] by side-set rather than by a `set pins` group. The trade-off is that you burn the entire 5-bit
2026-05-18 15:49:14 [kimi-k2.6] delay/side field to do it.
2026-05-18 15:49:14 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 15:49:14 [kimi-k2.6] Here is what the rewrite looks like with `.side_set 4 opt` (four data bits plus one enable bit,
2026-05-18 15:49:14 [kimi-k2.6] leaving zero delay bits):
2026-05-18 15:49:14 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 15:49:14 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 15:49:14 [kimi-k2.6] .program colorpump
2026-05-18 15:49:14 [kimi-k2.6] .side_set 4 opt
2026-05-18 15:49:14 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 15:49:14 [kimi-k2.6] 	wait	1 irq 6		side 0b1111
2026-05-18 15:49:14 [kimi-k2.6] 	out	pins, 2			side 0b1111	; colour index to OUT pins
2026-05-18 15:49:14 [kimi-k2.6] 	out	x, 2			side 0b1111	; selector into X
2026-05-18 15:49:14 [kimi-k2.6] 	jmp	x--, t3			side 0b1111
2026-05-18 15:49:14 [kimi-k2.6] 	nop					side 0b1110	; trigger SM0 (active low)
2026-05-18 15:49:14 [kimi-k2.6] 	jmp	done			side 0b1111
2026-05-18 15:49:14 [kimi-k2.6] t3:	jmp	x--, t2			side 0b1111
2026-05-18 15:49:14 [kimi-k2.6] 	nop					side 0b1101	; trigger SM1
2026-05-18 15:49:14 [kimi-k2.6] 	jmp	done			side 0b1111
2026-05-18 15:49:14 [kimi-k2.6] t2:	jmp	x--, t1			side 0b1111
2026-05-18 15:49:14 [kimi-k2.6] 	nop					side 0b1011	; trigger SM2
2026-05-18 15:49:14 [kimi-k2.6] 	jmp	done			side 0b1111
2026-05-18 15:49:14 [kimi-k2.6] t1:	nop					side 0b0111	; trigger SM3
2026-05-18 15:49:14 [kimi-k2.6] done:
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] 	nop					side 0b1111	; all triggers high / inactive
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] .wrap
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] What this costs you
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] • No delay cycles. With four side-set data bits plus the optional-enable bit, the entire
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] 5-bit side/delay field is consumed. You cannot write `[3]` or `[1]` on any instruction. If you need
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] a multi-cycle pulse you must spend extra instruction slots (`nop side 0b1110`, etc.).
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] • The branch tree is still there. Side-set does not give you a data-dependent output path;
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] it only gives you a second, parallel output that is fixed per instruction.
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] • Side-set has its own pin base. You must configure `PINCTRLSIDESETBASE` and
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] `SIDESET_COUNT` separately in the C init. The four triggers move off the `SET` pin group and onto
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] the side-set pin group.
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] Is it worth it?
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] Probably only if you are running out of pin-mapping groups. PIO gives you one `OUT` group,
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] one `SET` group, one `IN` group and one side-set group. If your `OUT` pins are already used for the
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] colour index and the `SET` pins are needed for something else (global enables, DAC levels, etc.),
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] then moving the four FIFO triggers to side-set makes routing sense. Otherwise it just costs you
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] delay bits for no gain.
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] A cleaner alternative
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] Your FIFO word already carries the selector in its lower bits. If you format the word so it also
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] carries a one-hot trigger mask, you can delete the branch tree entirely:
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] .program colorpump
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] 	out	pins, 2			; bits 1:0 → colour index
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] 	out	pins, 4			; bits 5:2 → one-hot trigger mask
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] .wrap
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] This is constant-time (2 cycles), has no decode latency, and frees up both `SET` and side-set. The
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] only requirement is that your DMA buffer packs the mask into the word before the colour index. If
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] the trigger pins must live on a different GPIO range from the colour index, you can follow the `out
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] pins, 2` with an `out pindirs, 4` or remap the FIFO word so the mask goes to a second `OUT` group,
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] but on RP2350 a single `OUT` instruction can only target one contiguous range.
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] One other thing to fix
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] Your original `colorpump.pio` has variable latency — the trigger pulse happens 4, 5, 6 or 7
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] cycles after the `out pins, 2`, depending on which state machine is selected. If the `colorfifo`
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] machines are all watching the same `pins[0..1]` bus, that jitter does not matter because the data
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] is stable early. But if you ever use the trigger pulse to gate something that is sensitive to its
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] position in the line, you will want the constant-time version, whether you use side-set or the
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] FIFO-mask approach.
2026-05-18 15:49:15 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:11:28 [kimi-k2.6]  ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.6 ⛲  kimi-k2.6 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.092 313.1KB 50.42s
2026-05-18 16:11:28 [kimi-k2.6] This is a substantial embedded graphics project. The standout novel aspects are the aggressive use
2026-05-18 16:11:28 [kimi-k2.6] of RP2350 PIO v1 features and the distributed state-machine architecture for analog video
2026-05-18 16:11:28 [kimi-k2.6] generation.
2026-05-18 16:11:28 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:11:28 [kimi-k2.6] • RP2350-specific PIO features*
2026-05-18 16:11:28 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:11:28 [kimi-k2.6] The most significant difference from a typical RP2040 video project is the reliance on RP2350’s
2026-05-18 16:11:28 [kimi-k2.6] indexed RX-FIFO access. In `colorfifo.pio`:
2026-05-18 16:11:28 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:11:28 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:11:28 [kimi-k2.6] .fifo txget
2026-05-18 16:11:28 [kimi-k2.6] stall:
2026-05-18 16:11:28 [kimi-k2.6] 	jmp	pin stall
2026-05-18 16:11:28 [kimi-k2.6] 	mov	y, pins
2026-05-18 16:11:28 [kimi-k2.6] 	mov	osr, rxfifo[y]
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] 	out	pindirs, 3
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] This uses the new `txget` FIFO join mode and the `mov osr, rxfifo[y]` encoding (Section 11.4.9 in
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] the datasheet excerpt you included). On RP2040 this was impossible; the RX FIFO was strictly a
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] queue. Here you are treating the four FIFO entries as a per-state-machine lookup table for
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] chroma/luma pulse trains. That is a genuinely new way to do palette output on this hardware.
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] `colorpump.pio` exploits the same idea from the other direction: one state machine decodes 4 input
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] bits into a color index, then triggers one of four neighbour state machines via side-set pins. The
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] triggered machine does the palette lookup via its local `rxfifo`. This turns the four SMs into a
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] parallel colour renderer.
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] • Analog output via pin-direction toggling*
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] Rather than driving voltage levels directly, `threepin.pio` and `colorfifo.pio` repeatedly issue
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] `out pindirs, 3`. The external resistor networks (100 Ω / 330 Ω / 150 Ω into a 75 Ω load) mean that
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] toggling pin direction changes the divider impedance and therefore the output voltage. Using PIO to
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] do this at pixel-clock rates (166.5 MHz ÷ 10 ≈ 16.65 MHz pixel cadence, or ~17.73 MHz for chroma)
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] is a very tight loop; the `threepin` train is 7 or 10 PIO cycles per pixel, which fits the timing
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] budget exactly.
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] • Clocking discipline*
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] The 166.5 MHz system clock is unusual but mathematically precise for PAL:
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] - 166.5 MHz ÷ 9.3885 ≈ 17.734475 MHz = 4 × PAL f_sc (4.43361875 MHz)
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] - 166.5 MHz ÷ 10656 = 15.625 kHz line rate
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] - 64 µs line period matches PAL spec exactly
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] Most hobby projects approximate this; you have hit the exact ratios.
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] • Interlaced DMA management*
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] `vidbit.cpp` manages even/odd fields separately via `evenFrame[]` and `oddFrame[]` arrays with
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] distinct preambles (equalising pulses and broad sync). The DMA channel is re-pointed per-field in
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] `vid_fieldi()`, and the C++ renderers (`drawFramei`) know which field they are drawing. This is
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] correct 576i, not just progressive scan pretending to be interlaced.
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] • Architecture summary*
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] The overall pipeline is:
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] - CPU renders 1-bit glyphs/vectors into field buffers
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] - DMA feeds `vidbit.pio` for sync/active video
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] - DMA (or direct FIFO writes) feeds `chroma.pio` for the colour subcarrier burst
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] - `colorpump.pio` + `colorfifo.pio` (PIO1 SMs) inject colour information during active video via
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] the resistor DAC
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] - `i2s.pio` handles audio independently on PIO1
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] - `ps2.pio` handles keyboard input
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] That is a lot of responsibility placed in PIO; the CPU is essentially just a framebuffer composer.
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] • Notable gaps*
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] You include references to `colorbit.h` and `colorpumpdmainit()` in `vidbit.cpp`, but the
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] corresponding `colorbit.cpp` or `colorpump.cpp` implementation was not in the shared files. The
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] `colorpump.md` is also truncated. If you want feedback on the DMA setup or the palette encoding,
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] those pieces would be needed.
2026-05-18 16:11:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:11:30 [kimi-k2.6] Likewise, `spdif.pio` is currently a placeholder (`nop [1]`), and `interleave.pio` looks
2026-05-18 16:11:30 [kimi-k2.6] experimental.
2026-05-18 16:11:30 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:11:30 [kimi-k2.6] • Bottom line*
2026-05-18 16:11:30 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:11:30 [kimi-k2.6] The novelty here is not just “video from a Pico,” but specifically a component-capable,
2026-05-18 16:11:30 [kimi-k2.6] palette-indexed, interlaced PAL renderer built around RP2350’s indexed-FIFO instructions. That
2026-05-18 16:11:30 [kimi-k2.6] is a step beyond the usual bit-banged or resistor-DAC approaches on RP2040.
2026-05-18 16:11:30 [kimi-k2.6] 
