2026-05-18 16:55:13 [kimi-k2.6]  ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.6 ⛲  kimi-k2.6 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.135 343.3KB 76.04s
2026-05-18 16:55:13 [kimi-k2.6] That three-instruction sequence is a conditional cross-PIO trigger based on a control field
2026-05-18 16:55:13 [kimi-k2.6] in the DMA bitstream.
2026-05-18 16:55:13 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:55:13 [kimi-k2.6] ╭────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-05-18 16:55:13 [kimi-k2.6] │  Instruction       │  PIO encoding                              │  What it does                                                                                                                                                                                    │
2026-05-18 16:55:13 [kimi-k2.6] ├────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-05-18 16:55:13 [kimi-k2.6] │  `out y, 2`        │  `OUT` dest=Y, count=2                     │  Shifts 2 bits from the **OSR** (Output Shift Register) into scratch register **Y**. If autopull is enabled and the shift threshold is reached, the OSR automatically refills from the TX FIFO.  │
2026-05-18 16:55:13 [kimi-k2.6] │  `jmp !y, noirq`   │  `JMP` condition=!Y                        │  Tests whether **Y == 0**. If true (the 2 bits were `00`), the program counter jumps to `noirq:`, **skipping** the IRQ. If false, execution falls through.                                       │
2026-05-18 16:55:13 [kimi-k2.6] │  `irq next set 6`  │  `IRQ` clear=0, wait=0, idx=NEXT, index=6  │  Asserts **IRQ flag 6** in the **next higher-numbered PIO block** (e.g. from PIO0 to PIO1). It does **not** wait for the flag to be cleared; it fires and immediately continues.                 │
2026-05-18 16:55:14 [kimi-k2.6] ╰────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-05-18 16:55:14 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:55:14 [kimi-k2.6] • Composite effect*
2026-05-18 16:55:14 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:55:14 [kimi-k2.6] It reads a 2-bit token from the video DMA stream. If that token is non-zero, it pings the
2026-05-18 16:55:14 [kimi-k2.6] neighbouring PIO (where your chroma/colour state machines live) by setting flag 6. A state machine
2026-05-18 16:55:14 [kimi-k2.6] in the other block can be waiting with:
2026-05-18 16:55:14 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:55:14 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:55:14 [kimi-k2.6] wait 1 irq 6
2026-05-18 16:55:14 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:55:14 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:55:14 [kimi-k2.6] …to synchronize the start of its line processing with the main video timing.
2026-05-18 16:55:14 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:55:14 [kimi-k2.6] • In your `vidbit.pio` context*
2026-05-18 16:55:14 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:55:14 [kimi-k2.6] Your frame buffer is a packed FIFO stream: vsync preamble → line counter → pixel counter → bitmap.
2026-05-18 16:55:14 [kimi-k2.6] That `out y, 2` (or `out y, 1` in the actual file) is plucking a flag from the first word(s) of
2026-05-18 16:55:14 [kimi-k2.6] each line’s data to tell PIO1 whether this scanline carries active colour/pump data or should be
2026-05-18 16:55:14 [kimi-k2.6] skipped (blanking lines). The `next` modifier is what lets the sync generator on PIO0 wake
2026-05-18 16:55:14 [kimi-k2.6] the colour hardware on PIO1 with cycle-accurate timing.
2026-05-18 16:55:14 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6]  ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.6 ⛲  kimi-k2.6 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.125 348.8KB 51.09s
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] • monobit.h*
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] - `bitsPerPixel` moved from `1` to `2`
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] - `lineWords` therefore doubled from `27` to `54`
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] That doubles the CPU’s framebuffer allocation per line and changes the DMA transfer size.
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] • vidbit.pio*
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] Three meaningful edits in the active-line region:
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] ╭─────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────╮
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] │  Location           │  Before                          │  Now                      │
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] ├─────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┤
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] │  IRQ flag test      │  `out y, 1`                      │  `out y, 2`               │
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] │  Cross-PIO trigger  │  `; irq next set 6` (commented)  │  `irq next set 6` (live)  │
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] │  Pixel data         │  `set pins, 4`                   │  `out pins, 1`            │
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] ╰─────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────╯
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] In PIO terms this means:
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] 1. 2-bit control field — The line preamble now shifts a 2-bit value from the FIFO into `Y`
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] to decide whether to fire the IRQ. On non-zero, it asserts flag 6 in the neighbouring PIO block.
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] 2. Active IRQ — The handshake to PIO1 (chroma/colour/whatever is sitting on the other
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] block) is no longer dead code; it fires on lines where the control field is non-zero.
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] 3. Serial pixel output — Most importantly, `set pins, 4` (which held the output at a
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] constant DC level) has been replaced by `out pins, 1`, so the state machine is now shifting actual
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] pixel data from the FIFO through the OSR to the pin during active video. Vidbit has gone from being
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] a sync-only generator to a sync-plus-luma bit-pump.
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] • Stale data warning*
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] The PIO file’s header comment still claims the bitmap is `[n+3..] = [27 x 309/310]` (i.e. 27
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] words/line), which matched the old `bitsPerPixel=1` layout. With `bitsPerPixel=2` and
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] `lineWords=54`, either:
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] - The PIO program needs to consume 54 words per line (e.g. `out pins, 2` or two `out pins, 1` in
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] the loop), or
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] - The extra bits are intended for a second state machine consuming the same DMA stream.
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] As written, `out pins, 1` in a 864-iteration loop will only drain 27 words, so the FIFO pointer
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] will walk out of phase with the C++ frame buffer if the CPU is really emitting 54 words per line.
2026-05-18 16:58:57 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6]  ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.6 ⛲  kimi-k2.6 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.161 351.8KB 138.12s
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6] `colortrain.pio` is new, and it significantly simplifies the colour path compared with the earlier
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6] `colorpump`+`colorfifo` pair.
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6] • What it does*
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6] ╭────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6] │  Feature               │  PIO mechanism                 │  Purpose                                                                                                        │
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6] ├────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6] │  `.fifo txget`         │  `FJOIN_RX_GET`                │  Turns the RX FIFO into a 4-entry random-access lookup table readable by the state machine.                     │
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6] │  `mov osr, rxfifo[y]`  │  Indexed GET (RP2350 v1 only)  │  Fetches one of four pre-loaded pulse trains in a single cycle.                                                 │
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6] │  `wait 1 irq 6`        │  Cross-PIO flag                │  Line-start synchronisation with `vidbit.pio` (which fires `irq next set 6` at the beginning of active video).  │
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6] │  `out pindirs, 3` × 6  │  3-pin tristate DAC            │  Outputs six phases of chroma/luma waveform per pixel, exactly like the older `colorfifo.pio`.                  │
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6] ╰────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6] In short, this replaces the previous five-state-machine colour subsystem (one `colorpump` selector
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6] + four `colorfifo` renderers) with one state machine that does its own palette lookup.
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6] • Cycle matching*
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6] The inner loop (`mov y, pins` → `mov osr, rxfifo[y]` → six `out pindirs, 3`) is 8 instructions plus
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6] the wrap, which appears intended to lock to vidbit’s active-video cadence. Both machines run from
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6] the same 166.5 MHz clock, so if they start together they stay phase-locked for the entire line.
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6] • Two things to watch*
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6] 1. Missing `.wrap_target`
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6]    As written, `.wrap` jumps back to instruction 0 (`set pindirs, 0`), which means the `wait 1 irq
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6] 6` is re-executed after every pixel. Because `wait 1 irq` clears the flag when it succeeds,
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6] the SM will stall immediately after its first pixel and sleep until the next line.
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6]    If you want the IRQ to act as a line-start gate only, add `.wrap_target` before the
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6] `mov` pair:
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6]    .program colortrain
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6]    .fifo txget
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6]        set     pindirs, 0
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6]        wait    1 irq 6
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6]    .wrap_target
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6]        mov     y, pins
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6]        mov     osr, rxfifo[y]
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6]        out     pindirs, 3
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6]        out     pindirs, 3
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6]        out     pindirs, 3
2026-05-18 17:02:42 [kimi-k2.6]        out     pindirs, 3
2026-05-18 17:02:43 [kimi-k2.6]        out     pindirs, 3
2026-05-18 17:02:43 [kimi-k2.6]        out     pindirs, 3
2026-05-18 17:02:43 [kimi-k2.6]    .wrap
2026-05-18 17:02:43 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 17:02:43 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 17:02:43 [kimi-k2.6]    With that change the SM idles in blanking, then free-runs at 10 cycles/pixel for the whole
2026-05-18 17:02:43 [kimi-k2.6] active period.
2026-05-18 17:02:43 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 17:02:43 [kimi-k2.6] 2. Palette size
2026-05-18 17:02:43 [kimi-k2.6]    `.fifo txget` exposes only four RX FIFO entries per state machine, so this SM gives you a
2026-05-18 17:02:43 [kimi-k2.6] 4-colour palette. The 16-entry `colortrains[]` array in `vidbit.cpp` implies you may
2026-05-18 17:02:43 [kimi-k2.6] still want 16 colours. To get there you would need either four `colortrain` instances (each with a
2026-05-18 17:02:43 [kimi-k2.6] different quadrant of the palette) or runtime reloading of the four `RXFxPUTGETy` registers
2026-05-18 17:02:43 [kimi-k2.6] before each line.
2026-05-18 17:02:43 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 17:02:43 [kimi-k2.6] • Connection to the rest of the pipeline*
2026-05-18 17:02:43 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-18 17:02:43 [kimi-k2.6] - `vidbit.pio` (PIO0) still generates sync, burst gate and the luma bitstream.
2026-05-18 17:02:43 [kimi-k2.6] - `colortrain.pio` (PIO1, SM1) waits on the cross-PIO flag, samples an index from its input pins,
2026-05-18 17:02:43 [kimi-k2.6] and outputs the 3-pin chroma pulse train.
2026-05-18 17:02:43 [kimi-k2.6] - The CPU renders a 2-bit-per-pixel framebuffer (`bitsPerPixel = 2` in `monobit.h`), but
2026-05-18 17:02:43 [kimi-k2.6] `vidbit.pio` currently consumes both bits for luma (direction + level). If the colour index for
2026-05-18 17:02:43 [kimi-k2.6] `colortrain` is meant to come from those same two bits, you will need to route one of them to the
2026-05-18 17:02:43 [kimi-k2.6] SM’s input pin mapping or change the bit packing so that one bit drives luma and the other drives
2026-05-18 17:02:43 [kimi-k2.6] chroma selection.
2026-05-18 17:02:43 [kimi-k2.6] 
