2026-05-18 17:30:44 [gemini-3.1-lite] The code provided offers a comprehensive look at an RP2350-based video signal generator (VIDBIT)
2026-05-18 17:30:44 [gemini-3.1-lite] using PIO state machines. The implementation covers PAL 576i video generation, World System
2026-05-18 17:30:44 [gemini-3.1-lite] Teletext (WST), I2S audio, PS/2 keyboard/mouse input, and a custom flash filesystem using LittleFS.
2026-05-18 17:30:44 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-18 17:30:44 [gemini-3.1-lite] Observations and Considerations
2026-05-18 17:30:44 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-18 17:30:44 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Chroma Generation: Your `chroma.pio` and the associated `initChroma` function in
2026-05-18 17:30:44 [gemini-3.1-lite] `vidbit.cpp` appear to be setting up a 4x fsc subcarrier. At 166.5 MHz, 4 × fsc (approx
2026-05-18 17:30:44 [gemini-3.1-lite] 17.734 MHz) requires a clock divider of \approx 9.39. The calculation in your code
2026-05-18 17:30:44 [gemini-3.1-lite] `piosmset_clkdiv(pio,sm,2.3471f);` seems to assume a different base frequency or a specific
2026-05-18 17:30:44 [gemini-3.1-lite] oversampling ratio. Ensure your clock divider aligns with the 166.5 MHz system clock if you need
2026-05-18 17:30:44 [gemini-3.1-lite] exactly 17.734 MHz.
2026-05-18 17:30:44 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-18 17:30:44 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Video Pipeline: The use of `pixelpump` → `onebit` → `threepin` creates a modular,
2026-05-18 17:30:44 [gemini-3.1-lite] FIFO-chained architecture. This is a robust way to handle the high-speed requirements of PAL video
2026-05-18 17:30:44 [gemini-3.1-lite] while allowing the CPU to manage frame buffers in memory.
2026-05-18 17:30:44 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-18 17:30:44 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  RP2350 Features: The use of `mov rxfifo[y], isr` and `mov osr, rxfifo[y]` demonstrates
2026-05-18 17:30:44 [gemini-3.1-lite] effective use of the new indexed FIFO access on the RP2350, which is significantly more efficient
2026-05-18 17:30:44 [gemini-3.1-lite] than the RP2040 approach for lookup-table-based signal generation or state tracking.
2026-05-18 17:30:44 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-18 17:30:44 [gemini-3.1-lite] 4.  Hardware Inversion: Your use of `gpiosetoeover(pin,
2026-05-18 17:30:44 [gemini-3.1-lite] GPIOOVERRIDEINVERT)` in `i2cprograminit` is a clever way to handle open-drain bus
2026-05-18 17:30:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] requirements without consuming extra PIO instructions.
2026-05-18 17:30:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-18 17:30:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] Suggested Refinement for `i2s.cpp`
2026-05-18 17:30:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-18 17:30:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] To keep the code clean and reusable, you might consider wrapping the state machine configuration
2026-05-18 17:30:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] into a structure rather than using globals. Here is a cleaner approach for `i2s_init`:
2026-05-18 17:30:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-18 17:30:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-18 17:30:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] struct I2SConfig {
2026-05-18 17:30:45 [gemini-3.1-lite]     uint din_pin;
2026-05-18 17:30:45 [gemini-3.1-lite]     uint bck_pin;
2026-05-18 17:30:45 [gemini-3.1-lite]     uint wsel_pin;
2026-05-18 17:30:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] };
