2026-05-19 17:05:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] In the context of the Raspberry Pi RP2040’s PIO (Programmable I/O) peripheral, DREQ
2026-05-19 17:05:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] stands for Data Request.
2026-05-19 17:05:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:05:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] What is it?
2026-05-19 17:05:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] DREQ is a hardware signaling mechanism used to coordinate data transfers between the PIO state
2026-05-19 17:05:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] machines and the RP2040’s DMA (Direct Memory Access) controller.
2026-05-19 17:05:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:05:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] Because PIO state machines operate independently of the CPU, they often need to move large amounts
2026-05-19 17:05:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] of data (e.g., sending a frame buffer to an LCD or reading samples from an ADC) without CPU
2026-05-19 17:05:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] intervention. DREQ signals tell the DMA controller that the PIO peripheral is ready to perform a
2026-05-19 17:05:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] transfer.
2026-05-19 17:05:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:05:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] How it works
2026-05-19 17:05:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] Each PIO state machine has two FIFO (First-In, First-Out) buffers:
2026-05-19 17:05:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  TX FIFO: Holds data waiting to be sent out by the state machine.
2026-05-19 17:05:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  RX FIFO: Holds data received by the state machine waiting to be read.
2026-05-19 17:05:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:05:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] The DREQ signal acts as a "handshake" based on the status of these FIFOs:
2026-05-19 17:05:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:05:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] • For TX (Outbound): The PIO state machine asserts a DREQ when its TX FIFO is not full
2026-05-19 17:05:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] (i.e., it has space to accept more data). This signals the DMA controller: "I have room, please
2026-05-19 17:05:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] send me more data."
2026-05-19 17:05:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] • For RX (Inbound): The PIO state machine asserts a DREQ when its RX FIFO is not empty
2026-05-19 17:05:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] (i.e., it has data waiting to be read). This signals the DMA controller: "I have data ready,
2026-05-19 17:05:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] please come and collect it."
2026-05-19 17:05:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:05:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] Why is it important?
2026-05-19 17:05:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] Without DREQ, the DMA controller would have to "guess" when to transfer data, which would likely
2026-05-19 17:05:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] result in buffer overflows or underflows, leading to corrupted data or missed samples. By using
2026-05-19 17:05:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] DREQ, the transfer is hardware-flow-controlled. The DMA controller remains idle until the
2026-05-19 17:05:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] PIO peripheral explicitly signals that it is ready, ensuring perfectly synchronized data movement.
2026-05-19 17:05:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:05:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] Mapping DREQ
2026-05-19 17:05:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] The RP2040 has a specific DREQ mapping table. Each state machine’s TX and RX FIFOs are mapped to
2026-05-19 17:05:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] specific DREQ signals (labeled `DREQPIOxTXy` or `DREQPIOxRXy`). When configuring a
2026-05-19 17:05:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] DMA channel, you set the `dreq` parameter to the specific signal corresponding to the state machine
2026-05-19 17:05:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] and FIFO you are targeting.
2026-05-19 17:05:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:05:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Example (C/Pico SDK):*
2026-05-19 17:05:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:05:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] // Configure DMA to transfer data to PIO TX FIFO
2026-05-19 17:05:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] dma_channel_config c = dma_channel_get_default_config(dma_chan);
2026-05-19 17:05:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] channel_config_set_dreq(&c, pio_get_dreq(pio, sm, true)); // 'true' for TX
2026-05-19 17:05:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:05:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:05:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] In summary, DREQ is the essential bridge that allows the DMA controller to feed data to (or drain
2026-05-19 17:05:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] data from) the PIO state machines efficiently and automatically.
2026-05-19 17:05:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:06:55 [nitro@ryzen5] how do i set jmp pins from c++
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite]  ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.6 ⛲  gemini-3.1-lite 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.000 3.230KB 4.53s
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] To set JMP pins in PIO using C++, you must configure the `jmp_pin` field within the state
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] machine configuration. The JMP pin determines which physical GPIO pin the `jmp pin, ...`
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] instruction checks to decide whether to branch.
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] Here is the standard way to configure this using the Pico SDK in C++.
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] The Configuration Process
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] You should use the `piosmconfig` structure and the `smconfigsetjmppin`
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] function.
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] #include "hardware/pio.h"
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] void configure_pio_jmp_pin(PIO pio, uint sm, uint pin_number) {
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	// 1. Get the default configuration for the state machine
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	pio_sm_config c = pio_get_default_sm_config();
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	// 2. Set the JMP pin
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	// This tells the state machine which physical pin to check
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	// when executing the "jmp pin" instruction.
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	sm_config_set_jmp_pin(&c, pin_number);
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	// 3. Initialize the state machine with this configuration
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	// (Assuming you have a loaded program offset)
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	pio_sm_init(pio, sm, offset, &c);
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] Important Considerations
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Pin Mapping: The `pinnumber` passed to `smconfigsetjmp_pin` is the GPIO
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] pin number relative to the chip (e.g., GPIO 0, GPIO 1, etc.).
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Pin Direction: Even though the JMP instruction is reading the state of a pin, you must
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] ensure that the pin is configured correctly in your PIO program's `side-set` or `out` settings if
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] necessary, though for a pure input JMP, you simply need to ensure the pin is assigned to the PIO
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] peripheral.
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  Instruction Behavior: When your PIO assembly code executes `jmp pin, label`, the state
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] machine checks the logic level of the pin you configured. If the pin is high, the jump is
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] taken; if low, the program continues to the next instruction.
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 4.  Multiple State Machines: Each state machine has its own `jmp_pin` setting. Configuring
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] it for one state machine does not affect others.
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] Example PIO Assembly Context
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] For the C++ code above to be useful, your `.pio` file would contain an instruction that utilizes
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] that pin:
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] .program my_program
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite]     ; This will jump to 'target' if the configured JMP pin is HIGH
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite]     jmp pin, target
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite]     ; Otherwise, do something else
2026-05-19 17:07:00 [gemini-3.1-lite]     set pins, 0
2026-05-19 17:07:01 [gemini-3.1-lite]     jmp start
2026-05-19 17:07:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:07:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] target:
2026-05-19 17:07:01 [gemini-3.1-lite]     set pins, 1
2026-05-19 17:07:01 [gemini-3.1-lite]     jmp start
2026-05-19 17:07:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:07:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:07:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] When to use `smconfigsetjmppin`
2026-05-19 17:07:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Dynamic Conditionals: If you are building a protocol where you need to poll an external
2026-05-19 17:07:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] signal (like a clock or a data-ready line) without involving the CPU.
2026-05-19 17:07:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Mode Selection: If you have a physical switch connected to a GPIO, you can use `jmp pin`
2026-05-19 17:07:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] to have the PIO state machine change its behavior based on the hardware state.
2026-05-19 17:07:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:07:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you are using the `pioaddprogram` helper functions generated by `pioasm`, remember that
2026-05-19 17:07:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] the `smconfig` is usually applied during the initialization phase before
2026-05-19 17:07:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] `piosmsetenabled(pio, sm, true)` is called.
2026-05-19 17:07:01 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:10:32 [nitro@ryzen5] do i need to call pio_gpio_init for that pin
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite]  ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.6 ⛲  gemini-3.1-lite 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.000 6.343KB 3.20s
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] Yes, you do.
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] Even though `smconfigsetjmppin` tells the state machine which pin to look
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] at, it does not automatically configure the hardware multiplexer to connect that GPIO to the PIO
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] block.
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] Why `piogpioinit` is required
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] The RP2040 GPIO pins are highly multiplexed. By default, they are often set to SIO (Software
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] Input/Output, controlled by the CPU). To use a pin with a PIO state machine, you must:
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Claim the pin for PIO: `piogpioinit` sets the pin's function select to the PIO
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] peripheral.
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Set the direction (optional but recommended): While `jmp` is an input-only operation,
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] it is good practice to ensure the pin is in the correct state.
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] Implementation Example
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] Here is how you should structure your initialization code:
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] void setup_pio_with_jmp(PIO pio, uint sm, uint pin, uint offset) {
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // 1. Claim the pin for PIO usage
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // This switches the pin function from SIO to PIO
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite]     pio_gpio_init(pio, pin);
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // 2. Configure the state machine
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite]     pio_sm_config c = pio_get_default_sm_config();
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite]     sm_config_set_jmp_pin(&c, pin);
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // 3. Initialize and start
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite]     pio_sm_init(pio, sm, offset, &c);
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite]     pio_sm_set_enabled(pio, sm, true);
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] What happens if you skip it?
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you forget to call `piogpioinit`:
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The PIO peripheral will not be connected to that physical pin.
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The state machine will likely read a constant value (usually 0 or the last state of the internal
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] signal) regardless of what is happening on the actual wire.
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Your `jmp pin` instruction will behave unpredictably or fail to branch when you expect it to.
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] A Note on Input Pins
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] If your `jmp` pin is strictly an input, you do not need to call
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] `piosmsetconsecutivepindirs` for that specific pin, as that function is intended
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] for pins that the PIO will drive as outputs. Calling `piogpioinit` is sufficient for
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] input-only pins.
2026-05-19 17:10:36 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
