2026-05-19 17:14:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] This is a very sophisticated and dense set of PIO implementations. You are clearly pushing the
2026-05-19 17:14:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] RP2350 PIO to its limits for high-speed video generation.
2026-05-19 17:14:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:14:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] Since you asked for comments on the content, here are some observations and potential areas of
2026-05-19 17:14:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] optimization or concern based on the provided assembly:
2026-05-19 17:14:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:14:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. The `pixelpump` Pipeline
2026-05-19 17:14:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] Your `pixelpump` -> `onebit` -> `threepin` architecture is an interesting way to manage a 3-lane
2026-05-19 17:14:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] video pipeline.
2026-05-19 17:14:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Pipeline Stall Risk: Because you are using `pull block` and `push block` heavily, any
2026-05-19 17:14:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] jitter in the system DMA or FIFO access will cause the state machines to stall. Since video timing
2026-05-19 17:14:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] (PAL 576i) is strictly synchronous, ensure your DMA channels are configured with high priority and
2026-05-19 17:14:05 [gemini-3.1-lite] that your `pixelpump` program provides a sufficient "look-ahead" buffer to prevent underflow.
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Instruction Density: You are using `out pindirs, 3` repeatedly in `threepin`. On RP2350,
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] you now have the `mov pindirs, x` instruction. If you can pre-calculate the pindir states in
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] memory, `mov pindirs, x` might be significantly more efficient than unrolling the `out`
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] instructions.
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. `threepin.pio` - Alpha Bits
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] You mentioned `out null, 2` for the 2-bit alpha skip.
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Optimization: If you ever need more headroom, remember that on RP2350, the `MOV`
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] instruction can now manipulate `PINDIRS`. If your alpha/blanking logic is static, you might be able
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] to use `MOV` to switch entire banks of pins between high-impedance and output mode in a single
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] cycle, rather than shifting through a bit-train.
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3. `onebit.pio` - Loopback Pin
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] You noted the need for a "single loopback pin for pixel state GPIO 46".
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Safety: Ensure that GPIO 46 is not configured for any other peripheral. Since you are
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] using it as a state-branching pin (`jmp pin`), ensure `piogpioinit` is called, and consider
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] if you need to set the input synchronizer bypass (`INPUTSYNCBYPASS`) for that pin to save
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] the 2-cycle synchronization latency if your timing budget is tight.
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] 4. `colortrain.pio` (The new file)
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] This seems to be a variation of your `greyline.pio`.
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] • `rxfifo[y]` usage: You are using `mov osr, rxfifo[y]`. This is a powerful RP2350 feature.
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] Ensure `SHIFTCTRLFJOINRX_GET` is correctly configured in your C++ init code, or this
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] instruction will trigger undefined behavior.
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] • IRQ 6: You are waiting on `irq 6`. In your `vidbit.pio`, you have `irq next set 6`. This
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] is excellent usage of the new RP2350 cross-PIO IRQ capability. It keeps the timing of the
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] `colortrain` strictly locked to the `vidbit` line timing.
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] 5. C++ Integration (General)
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Atomic Updates: Since you are doing video, you are likely double-buffering. Ensure that
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] when you update the `evenFrame` or `oddFrame` arrays, you use
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] `dmachanneltransferfrombuffer_now` or similar to avoid tearing.
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] • JSON Parser: You included a JSON parser. If this is used for configuring video parameters
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] at runtime, ensure the parser is not running on the same core that is servicing the PIO DMA
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] interrupts, as the parser's memory allocation/deallocation could introduce latency spikes that
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] cause the video to jitter.
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] A Quick Suggestion on `vidbit.pio`
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] In `vidbit.pio`, you have:
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] pedestal:
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite]     set pindirs, 1 [2]
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] nextpixel:
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite]     jmp y--, active [6]
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you find you are running out of cycles for high-resolution modes, check if you can use the `MOV`
2026-05-19 17:14:06 [gemini-3.1-lite] instruction to update `PINDIRS` in bulk, as noted above. It is significantly faster than `SET` or
