2026-05-21 12:17:27 [nitro@ryzen5] in c++ where does setw come from?
2026-05-21 12:17:29 [gemini-3.1-lite]  ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.6 ⛲  gemini-3.1-lite 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.000 501B 1.58s
2026-05-21 12:17:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] In C++, `setw` is provided by the `<iomanip>` header file.
2026-05-21 12:17:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:17:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] It is a manipulator used to set the width of the field in which the next output will be displayed.
2026-05-21 12:17:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] Because it takes an argument (the width), it requires the inclusion of `<iomanip>` rather than just
2026-05-21 12:17:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] the standard `<iostream>`.
2026-05-21 12:17:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:17:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] Here is a simple example of how to use it with a named function:
2026-05-21 12:17:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:17:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:17:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] #include <iostream>
2026-05-21 12:17:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] #include <iomanip>
2026-05-21 12:17:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:17:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] void printFormattedValue(int value, int width) {
2026-05-21 12:17:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	std::cout << std::setw(width) << value << std::endl;
2026-05-21 12:17:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-05-21 12:17:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:17:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] int main() {
2026-05-21 12:17:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	printFormattedValue(42, 10);
2026-05-21 12:17:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	return 0;
2026-05-21 12:17:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-05-21 12:17:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:17:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:17:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Key points:*
2026-05-21 12:17:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Namespace: It resides in the `std` namespace.
2026-05-21 12:17:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Persistence: Unlike manipulators like `std::setprecision` or `std::setbase`, `std::setw`
2026-05-21 12:17:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] is non-persistent. It only affects the single next output operation. After that, the width
2026-05-21 12:17:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] resets to the default (which is usually just enough space to fit the content).
2026-05-21 12:17:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:50:53 [nitro@ryzen5] how would an spdif receiver work in terms of logic no code
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite]  ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.6 ⛲  gemini-3.1-lite 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.000 1.704KB 3.79s
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] An S/PDIF (Sony/Philips Digital Interface) receiver operates by converting a serial stream of
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] electrical or optical pulses back into parallel digital data (usually I2S or PCM) that a processor
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] or DAC can understand.
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] Because S/PDIF is a "self-clocking" protocol, the receiver must perform several distinct logical
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] steps to extract the data correctly.
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. Signal Conditioning and Threshold Detection
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] The incoming signal (often a Biphase Mark Code) is an analog signal that must first be converted
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] into a clean digital square wave. The receiver uses a comparator to determine if the voltage is
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] above or below a specific threshold. This converts the physical pulses into a logical stream of 1s
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] and 0s.
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. Clock Recovery (The Phase-Locked Loop)
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] S/PDIF does not send a separate clock signal. Instead, the clock information is embedded within the
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] transitions of the data itself. The receiver uses a Phase-Locked Loop (PLL) to track these
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] transitions. The PLL "locks" onto the frequency of the incoming transitions, allowing the receiver
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] to generate a local clock that is perfectly synchronized with the sender. This is critical for
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] preventing jitter.
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3. Decoding Biphase Mark Code (BMC)
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] S/PDIF uses BMC, where a transition always occurs at the start of every bit period, and an
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] additional transition occurs in the middle of the bit period only if the data bit is a `1`.
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Logic: The receiver looks at the timing between transitions. If a transition occurs
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] exactly at the bit boundary, it identifies the start of a new bit. If a transition occurs in the
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] middle, it registers a logical `1`; if no transition occurs in the middle, it registers a logical
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] `0`.
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] 4. Preamble Detection (Frame Synchronization)
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] The data stream is organized into "frames." To know where one frame ends and the next begins, the
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] receiver looks for specific "preambles." These are special patterns that violate the standard
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] Biphase Mark rules (they contain transitions that occur too close together). By detecting these
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] unique patterns (labeled B, M, and W), the receiver identifies the start of a new frame and the
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] start of a new sub-frame.
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] 5. Sub-frame and Block Extraction
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] Once the receiver is synchronized to the frames, it parses the bits into the standard S/PDIF
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] structure:
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Audio Data: Extracts the actual PCM audio samples (usually 16 to 24 bits).
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Validity Bit: Checks if the audio data is suitable for conversion.
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] • User Data & Channel Status: Extracts metadata (such as sample rate, copyright
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] information, or category codes).
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Parity Bit: Performs a parity check to ensure the data was not corrupted during
2026-05-21 12:50:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] transmission.
2026-05-21 12:50:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:50:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] 6. Data Formatting (The Output Stage)
2026-05-21 12:50:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] Finally, the receiver buffers the extracted audio data and reformats it into a standard digital
2026-05-21 12:50:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] audio interface format, such as I2S (Inter-IC Sound). This output is then sent to a
2026-05-21 12:50:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] Digital-to-Analog Converter (DAC) or a Digital Signal Processor (DSP) for final processing.
2026-05-21 12:50:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:50:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] In summary, the logic moves from raw electrical pulses → clock extraction →
2026-05-21 12:50:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] pattern-based decoding → frame synchronization → error checking →
2026-05-21 12:50:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] parallel data output.
2026-05-21 12:50:59 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:51:52 [nitro@ryzen5] is there a simple middle man prootocol that rp2350 pio could use to send audio received
2026-05-21 12:51:56 [gemini-3.1-lite]  ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.6 ⛲  gemini-3.1-lite 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.000 5.203KB 3.02s
2026-05-21 12:51:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] For the RP2350, the PIO (Programmable I/O) is exceptionally well-suited for acting as a "bridge"
2026-05-21 12:51:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] between an S/PDIF receiver and the rest of the system.
2026-05-21 12:51:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:51:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] To act as a "middle man" protocol, you need something that is hardware-friendly, low-overhead, and
2026-05-21 12:51:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] supported by most audio DACs and DSPs. The standard choice for this is I2S (Inter-IC
2026-05-21 12:51:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] Sound).
2026-05-21 12:51:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:51:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] Why I2S is the ideal "Middle Man"
2026-05-21 12:51:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] I2S is the industry-standard interface for moving digital audio between chips. It is simple enough
2026-05-21 12:51:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] that the RP2350 PIO can generate it with very little logic.
2026-05-21 12:51:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:51:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Synchronous: It uses a Clock (BCLK), a Word Select (WS/LRCK), and Data (SDATA).
2026-05-21 12:51:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] • PIO-Friendly: The PIO can easily toggle these three pins in a loop.
2026-05-21 12:51:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Hardware Compatibility: Almost every external DAC or codec chip on the market accepts
2026-05-21 12:51:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] I2S.
2026-05-21 12:51:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] • DMA Integration: The RP2350 can use its DMA (Direct Memory Access) controller to push
2026-05-21 12:51:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] audio samples from the S/PDIF receiver's buffer into the PIO's FIFO, meaning the CPU doesn't have
2026-05-21 12:51:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] to touch the audio data at all.
2026-05-21 12:51:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:51:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] How the PIO Logic would work
2026-05-21 12:51:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you are receiving S/PDIF and want to output it as I2S, your PIO state machine would perform
2026-05-21 12:51:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] these logical steps:
2026-05-21 12:51:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:51:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Wait for FIFO: The PIO waits for a 32-bit audio sample to be pushed into its TX FIFO by
2026-05-21 12:51:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] the DMA or the CPU.
2026-05-21 12:51:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Word Select (WS) Toggle: The PIO toggles the WS pin to indicate whether the current
2026-05-21 12:51:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] data is for the Left or Right channel.
2026-05-21 12:51:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  Bit-Banging Data: The PIO shifts the 32-bit word out of the FIFO one bit at a time,
2026-05-21 12:51:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] synchronized with the Bit Clock (BCLK).
2026-05-21 12:51:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] 4.  Looping: The state machine repeats this process indefinitely.
2026-05-21 12:51:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:51:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] An Alternative: TDM (Time Division Multiplexing)
2026-05-21 12:51:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] If your application requires more than two channels (e.g., 4, 8, or 16 channels), you would use
2026-05-21 12:51:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] TDM.
2026-05-21 12:51:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:51:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] TDM is essentially an extension of I2S. Instead of having just one Left and one Right slot per
2026-05-21 12:51:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] frame, the frame is extended to contain multiple slots. The PIO logic remains very similar to I2S,
2026-05-21 12:51:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] but the "Word Select" signal becomes a narrow pulse that marks the start of a much longer frame of
2026-05-21 12:51:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] multiple audio channels.
2026-05-21 12:51:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:51:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] Recommendation for RP2350
2026-05-21 12:51:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Use I2S for simplicity: If you are just moving stereo audio, implement I2S. It
2026-05-21 12:51:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] is the most robust "middle man" protocol.
2026-05-21 12:51:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Use PIO for the "Glue": Let the PIO handle the timing-critical task of shifting bits
2026-05-21 12:51:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] out to the DAC.
2026-05-21 12:51:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  Use DMA for the "Transport": Configure the DMA to move data from your S/PDIF processing
2026-05-21 12:51:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] buffer (where you parsed the S/PDIF stream) directly into the PIO TX FIFO.
2026-05-21 12:51:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:51:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] By using I2S as your middle-man protocol, you decouple the complex S/PDIF decoding logic from the
2026-05-21 12:51:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] physical audio output, making your system much easier to debug and more modular.
2026-05-21 12:51:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
