2026-05-21 12:53:22 [nitro@ryzen5] ; spdif.pio (c)2026 Simon Armstrong; fclk: 148.5mhz; in pins - DATA (GP16); biphase mark code decoder for iec 60958 spdif; 48.4 cyc/bit @ 48khz, 24.2 cyc/bit @ 96khz, 12.1 cyc/bit @ 192khz.program spdif	nop	[1].wrap
2026-05-21 12:53:27 [gemini-3.1-lite]  ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.6 ⛲  gemini-3.1-lite 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.000 8.454KB 4.14s
2026-05-21 12:53:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] To decode S/PDIF using PIO, we need to detect transitions (Biphase Mark Code). In BMC, a transition
2026-05-21 12:53:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] occurs at every bit boundary, and an additional transition occurs in the middle of the bit period
2026-05-21 12:53:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] if the data is a `1`.
2026-05-21 12:53:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:53:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] Since you are running at 148.5 MHz, we can use the `in` instruction to sample the pin and
2026-05-21 12:53:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] the `jmp` instruction to detect transitions.
2026-05-21 12:53:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:53:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] Here is a PIO program structure to handle the sampling. This uses a "wait for transition" approach.
2026-05-21 12:53:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:53:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:53:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] .program spdif
2026-05-21 12:53:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] .side_set 0
2026-05-21 12:53:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] ; We sample at a high frequency to detect the BMC transitions.
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] ; The goal is to detect the edge, then count cycles until the next edge.
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] public entry:
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]     wait 1 pin 0        ; Wait for line to go high (start of bit/transition)
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]     wait 0 pin 0        ; Wait for line to go low (transition)
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]     set x, 0            ; Reset counter
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] count_loop:
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]     jmp pin, count_loop ; If pin is still high, keep counting
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]     jmp bit_detected    ; If pin goes low, we found the mid-bit transition
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] count_loop_inc:
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]     jmp x--, count_loop ; Increment our cycle counter to measure duration
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] bit_detected:
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]     in x, 8             ; Push the duration count to the RX FIFO
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]     jmp entry
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] % c-sdk {
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] static inline void spdif_program_init(PIO pio, uint sm, uint offset, uint pin) {
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]     pio_sm_config c = spdif_program_get_default_config(offset);
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // Set the pin as input
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]     pio_gpio_init(pio, pin);
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]     sm_config_set_in_pins(&c, pin);
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // Set pin as JMP pin for the state machine
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]     sm_config_set_jmp_pin(&c, pin);
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]     pio_sm_init(pio, sm, offset, &c);
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]     pio_sm_set_enabled(pio, sm, true);
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] %}
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] How this logic works:
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Transition Detection: Instead of trying to sample at exactly the bit center, this code
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] measures the time between transitions.
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  BMC Logic:
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   A transition at the boundary is guaranteed.
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   A transition in the middle indicates a `1`.
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   By measuring the time between the boundary transition and the next transition, the PIO
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] captures the "delta" time.
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  The "Middle Man" (FIFO): The `in x, 8` instruction sends the measured cycle count to
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] the RX FIFO.
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 4.  CPU Processing: Your C code on the RP2350 reads these values from the FIFO. If the
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] value is "small" (approx. 0.5 bit period), you know a `1` occurred. If the value is "large"
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] (approx. 1 bit period), you know a `0` occurred.
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] Considerations for your 148.5MHz Clock:
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Resolution: At 148.5 MHz, one cycle is ~6.7ns. At 192kHz audio, a bit period is ~12
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] cycles. This gives you enough headroom to distinguish between a mid-bit transition and a boundary
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] transition.
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] • FIFO Depth: The PIO RX FIFO is 8 words deep. You will need to use a DMA channel to drain
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] these "timing values" into a buffer in RAM so the CPU can reconstruct the S/PDIF frame without
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] missing edges.
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Synchronization: The `wait` instructions effectively re-sync the state machine to the
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] incoming signal on every bit, which handles minor clock drift between the transmitter and the
2026-05-21 12:53:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] RP2350.
