2026-05-21 13:34:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] To configure the DMA as a reader (pulling data from the PIO state machine into memory), the
2026-05-21 13:34:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] direction of the data transfer must be reversed compared to your current snippet.
2026-05-21 13:34:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 13:34:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] In the RP2040 DMA architecture, "reading" from the perspective of the CPU/memory means the DMA
2026-05-21 13:34:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] controller is acting as the bus master to move data out of the PIO's RX FIFO and into your system
2026-05-21 13:34:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] RAM. Here are the specific areas that require your attention:
2026-05-21 13:34:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 13:34:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. FIFO Selection (Source vs. Destination)
2026-05-21 13:34:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] Your current code points the DMA to `&pio0->txf[sm]`. The `txf` (Transmit FIFO) is used to send
2026-05-21 13:34:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] data from the CPU/DMA to the PIO. To read data from the PIO, you must point the DMA
2026-05-21 13:34:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] source address to the `rxf` (Receive FIFO) register: `&pio->rxf[sm]`.
2026-05-21 13:34:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 13:34:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. Increment Logic
2026-05-21 13:34:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] The DMA controller needs to know which side of the transfer is a static register (the PIO FIFO) and
2026-05-21 13:34:28 [gemini-3.1-lite] which side is a memory buffer.
2026-05-21 13:34:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Source (`read_increment`): Set this to `false`. You are reading from a single, fixed
2026-05-21 13:34:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] memory-mapped register (the FIFO).
2026-05-21 13:34:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Destination (`write_increment`): Set this to `true`. You are writing to an array or
2026-05-21 13:34:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] buffer in RAM, so the address must increment after every 32-bit transfer.
2026-05-21 13:34:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 13:34:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3. DREQ (Data Request)
2026-05-21 13:34:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] The DREQ signal acts as the hardware flow control. When reading from the PIO, you must use the RX
2026-05-21 13:34:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] DREQ. If you use the TX DREQ while trying to read, the DMA will wait for the PIO's TX FIFO to be
2026-05-21 13:34:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] empty (which it likely won't be, if you are only reading), causing the transfer to hang. You should
2026-05-21 13:34:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] use `piogetdreq(pio, sm, false)` for RX operations.
2026-05-21 13:34:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 13:34:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] 4. Memory Buffering
2026-05-21 13:34:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] Because you are reading into memory, ensure your target buffer is correctly aligned for the DMA.
2026-05-21 13:34:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] While the RP2040 DMA is quite flexible, it is best practice to ensure your buffer is word-aligned
2026-05-21 13:34:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] (32-bit) in memory. Additionally, if you intend to process this data while the DMA is still
2026-05-21 13:34:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] running, you may want to consider using a ping-pong buffer strategy or a DMA chain to prevent the
2026-05-21 13:34:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] CPU from accessing the same memory index that the DMA is currently writing to.
2026-05-21 13:34:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 13:34:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] By swapping the FIFO target to `rxf`, setting the `read_increment` to `false`, and updating the
2026-05-21 13:34:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] `dreq` to the RX variant, the DMA will correctly pull the data captured by your PIO state machine
2026-05-21 13:34:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] into your designated memory buffer.
