2026-05-21 13:53:15 [gemini-3.1-lite] Yes, Deno can send a string to a serial port on Windows. Since Deno does not have a built-in serial
2026-05-21 13:53:15 [gemini-3.1-lite] API, you must use the Web Serial API polyfill or a native Deno FFI (Foreign Function
2026-05-21 13:53:15 [gemini-3.1-lite] Interface) library.
2026-05-21 13:53:15 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 13:53:15 [gemini-3.1-lite] The most common and modern approach is using the `serial` package from `jsr:@serial/serial` (which
2026-05-21 13:53:15 [gemini-3.1-lite] wraps the necessary system calls).
2026-05-21 13:53:15 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 13:53:15 [gemini-3.1-lite] Prerequisites
2026-05-21 13:53:15 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. Ensure you have Deno installed.
2026-05-21 13:53:15 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. You will need to grant Deno permission to access hardware devices using the `--allow-ffi` flag.
2026-05-21 13:53:15 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 13:53:15 [gemini-3.1-lite] Example Implementation
2026-05-21 13:53:15 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 13:53:15 [gemini-3.1-lite] Here is an example using a reusable function to open a port and send a string.
2026-05-21 13:53:15 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 13:53:15 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 13:53:15 [gemini-3.1-lite] import { SerialPort } from "jsr:@serial/serial";
2026-05-21 13:53:15 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 13:53:15 [gemini-3.1-lite] /**
2026-05-21 13:53:15 [gemini-3.1-lite]  * Sends a string to a specified serial port.
2026-05-21 13:53:15 [gemini-3.1-lite]  * @param portPath The COM port (e.g., "COM3")
2026-05-21 13:53:15 [gemini-3.1-lite]  * @param data The string to send
2026-05-21 13:53:15 [gemini-3.1-lite]  */
2026-05-21 13:53:15 [gemini-3.1-lite] async function sendToSerial(portPath: string, data: string) {
2026-05-21 13:53:15 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	const port = new SerialPort({
2026-05-21 13:53:15 [gemini-3.1-lite] 		path: portPath,
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 		baudRate: 9600,
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	});
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	try {
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 		await port.open();
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 		const encoder = new TextEncoder();
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 		const writer = port.writable.getWriter();
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 		await writer.write(encoder.encode(data));
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 		writer.releaseLock();
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 		await port.close();
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 		console.log(`Successfully sent: "${data}" to ${portPath}`);
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	} catch (error) {
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 		console.error("Failed to communicate with serial port:", error);
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	}
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] // Usage
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] await sendToSerial("COM3", "Hello Serial Port!");
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] Important Execution Notes
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Permissions: Because this interacts with system hardware via FFI, you must run the
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] script with specific flags:
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite]     deno run --allow-ffi --allow-read --allow-write your_script.ts
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Baud Rate: Ensure the `baudRate` in the script matches the configuration of the device
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] you are communicating with, otherwise, you will receive garbled data or no data at all.
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  Port Availability: Ensure no other application (like Arduino Serial Monitor or PuTTY)
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] is currently holding the COM port open, as Windows serial ports typically allow only one connection
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] at a time.
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] 4.  Dependencies: If you prefer not to use a third-party library, you would need to use
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] `Deno.dlopen` to call `kernel32.dll` functions (`CreateFileW`, `WriteFile`, etc.) directly, which
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] is significantly more complex and requires manual memory management. Using a library is recommended
2026-05-21 13:53:16 [gemini-3.1-lite] for maintainability.
