2026-05-21 13:58:55 [nitro@ryzen5] kimi, does deno support serial ports?
2026-05-21 13:59:35 [kimi-k2.6]  ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.7 ⛲  kimi-k2.6 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.007 588B 40.28s
2026-05-21 13:59:35 [kimi-k2.6] Deno does not currently implement the Web Serial API or provide any other built-in API for
2026-05-21 13:59:35 [kimi-k2.6] accessing serial ports. You cannot use `navigator.serial` or a Deno-native equivalent out of the
2026-05-21 13:59:35 [kimi-k2.6] box.
2026-05-21 13:59:35 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-21 13:59:36 [kimi-k2.6] If you need to communicate with serial devices, there are three main approaches:
2026-05-21 13:59:36 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-21 13:59:36 [kimi-k2.6] 1. npm compatibility
2026-05-21 13:59:36 [kimi-k2.6]    You can attempt to import the Node.js [`serialport`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/serialport)
2026-05-21 13:59:36 [kimi-k2.6] package via Deno’s Node.js/npm compatibility layer. Modern versions of `serialport` use N-API for
2026-05-21 13:59:36 [kimi-k2.6] their native bindings, and Deno has been expanding its N-API support, but success depends on your
2026-05-21 13:59:36 [kimi-k2.6] platform and Deno version. It is not guaranteed to work without issues.
2026-05-21 13:59:36 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-21 13:59:36 [kimi-k2.6] 2. FFI (Foreign Function Interface)
2026-05-21 13:59:36 [kimi-k2.6]    The most robust path is to use Deno’s FFI to call the OS serial APIs directly—for example,
2026-05-21 13:59:36 [kimi-k2.6] `termios` on Linux and macOS, or the Windows COM APIs. This bypasses Node native-addon
2026-05-21 13:59:36 [kimi-k2.6] compatibility entirely, though it requires writing low-level bindings.
2026-05-21 13:59:36 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-21 13:59:36 [kimi-k2.6] 3. Third-party modules
2026-05-21 13:59:36 [kimi-k2.6]    Community modules on `deno.land/x` or JSR may wrap FFI calls into a more convenient interface,
2026-05-21 13:59:36 [kimi-k2.6] but this part of the ecosystem is less mature than Node’s.
2026-05-21 13:59:36 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-21 13:59:36 [kimi-k2.6] In short, serial port access is not a first-class feature in Deno, but it is achievable through
2026-05-21 13:59:36 [kimi-k2.6] interoperability or direct system calls.
2026-05-21 13:59:36 [kimi-k2.6] 
