2026-05-22 15:38:08 [kimi-k2.6]  ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.7 ⛲  kimi-k2.6 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.089 298.5KB 97.62s
2026-05-22 15:38:08 [kimi-k2.6] A few things stand out as genuinely novel, and a handful look like works-in-progress. Treating this
2026-05-22 15:38:08 [kimi-k2.6] as an RP2350-native project (not a port from RP2040) makes sense — you are using PIO version 1
2026-05-22 15:38:08 [kimi-k2.6] features that simply do not exist on the earlier silicon.
2026-05-22 15:38:08 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-22 15:38:08 [kimi-k2.6] • What is new or different compared to typical RP2040 PIO usage*
2026-05-22 15:38:08 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-22 15:38:08 [kimi-k2.6] • Indexed RX FIFO lookups (`txget` / `putget` modes).
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6]     `chroma.pio`, `greyline.pio` and `colortrain.pio` all declare `.fifo txget` and use `mov osr,
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] rxfifo[y]`. On RP2350 this repurposes the RX FIFO storage as a random-read lookup table for the
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] state machine while leaving the TX path intact. That is a very different pattern from the old
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] RP2040 approach of streaming everything through the FIFOs in strict order. It is a good fit for
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] colour-burst or per-line palette tables that the CPU loads once and the SM indexes on every line.
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] • Cross-PIO IRQ synchronisation.
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6]     `vidbit.pio` fires `irq next set 6 / clear 6` and `chroma.pio` waits on `irq 6`. Because RP2350
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] IRQs propagate across PIO blocks with no cycle penalty, you can lock the chroma generator to the
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] luma/sync generator even when they are on different PIO instances. Doing this cycle-accurately on
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] RP2040 was either impossible or required burning a GPIO as a sync wire.
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] • Pre-baked vertical sync in the FIFO.
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6]     The `vidbit.pio` preamble is elegant: the C++ side queues the vsync pulse train as raw FIFO
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] words (`E0,E1`, `B0,B1`, etc.) followed by the line count and active width. The SM consumes it as a
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] pure dataflow program. This is a clean way to handle the equalising/broad-sync preamble without
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] consuming instruction memory on branches.
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] • Architecture observations*
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] The `pixelpump → onebit → threepin` pipeline is a nice separation of concerns:
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] • pixelpump acts as a scanline double-buffer scheduler (IRQ wait + 27 FIFO swaps).
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] • onebit resolves a single serial pixel bit into one of two 32-bit “wave trains”.
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] • threepin expands that train into actual pin-direction changes for a resistor DAC.
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] That lets you keep the CPU-side framebuffer as 1-bit or 2-bit packed data while the PIOs do the
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] cycle-exact pin wiggling.
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] • Items that look unfinished or worth a second look*
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] • `interleave.pio` does not seem coherent yet — `c0` and `c1` are defined but never branched to,
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] `y` is consumed by `mov x, x|y` before it is initialised, and there is no `.wrap`. It reads like a
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] draft.
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] • `spdif.pio` is understandably a stub (just `nop [1]`), but the comment claims 48.4 cycles/bit @
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] 48 kHz which would need a real BMC decoder. The `rc5.pio` pulse counter is more complete.
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] • `json.h` pulls in `<codecvt>` and `std::wstring_convert`. Those were deprecated in C++17 and are
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] removed in C++20. If you move to a newer toolchain it will break. The parser also leaks in
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] `parseRHS` (allocates a `JSValue` into the local `result` but never hands ownership back to the
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] caller).
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] • `threepin.pio` comment says “10 cycles per count” but the instruction path is 9 cycles (seven
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] `out pindirs, 3`, one `set pindirs, 0`, one `out null, 11`). You may already know and have
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] accounted for it elsewhere, but the mismatch jumped out.
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] • Overall impression*
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] This is one of the more aggressive uses of RP2350-specific PIO features I have seen in the wild —
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] particularly the `rxfifo[y]` lookups and cross-PIO IRQs. The video side is far enough along that
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] the pipeline is generating real timing, while the audio (`i2s.pio`) and serial (`ps2.pio`,
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] `rc5.pio`) modules look like they are being brought up alongside it. If you want another pair of
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] eyes on the cycle budget for the active region (the `out pindirs, 2 [8]` loop in `vidbit.pio`
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] versus the `threepin` train length), that would be an easy place to dig in.
2026-05-22 15:38:09 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-22 18:16:27 [nitro@ryzen5] what is clocks if ; 44.1K with 166.5mhz Pico2 PIO
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6]  ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.7 ⛲  kimi-k2.6 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.096 302.5KB 172.67s
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] With the existing `i2s.pio` frame format (3125 state-machine cycles per stereo sample) you have two
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] choices:
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] • 1. Keep the program as-is and use the fractional clock divider*
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] Target PIO clock = 44,100 × 3125 = 137.8125 MHz
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] Clock divisor = 166.5 / 137.8125 = 1.208163…
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] In the RP2350 16.8 fixed-point `CLKDIV` register:
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] ╭───────┬────────┬──────────────────┬─────────────────────────────┬──────────────┬───────────╮
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] │  INT  │  FRAC  │  Register value  │  Actual rate                │  Error       │ undefined │
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] ├───────┼────────┼──────────────────┼─────────────────────────────┼──────────────┼───────────┤
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] │  1    │  53    │  `(1 << 16) \    │  (53 << 8)` = `0x00013500`  │  ~44,141 Hz  │  +0.09 %  │
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] │  1    │  54    │  `(1 << 16) \    │  (54 << 8)` = `0x00013600`  │  ~43,999 Hz  │  −0.23 %  │
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] ╰───────┴────────┴──────────────────┴─────────────────────────────┴──────────────┴───────────╯
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] So `0x00013500` (1 + 53/256) is the closer match.
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] • 2. Run the SM at full system speed (divisor 1.0) and retime the program*
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] 166.5 MHz / 44.1 kHz = 3775.51 cycles per sample
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] If you stretch the program to exactly 3775 cycles per left+right frame you get:
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] 166,500,000 / 3775 = 44,099.34 Hz
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] That is within 0.7 Hz (0.0016 %) of true 44.1 kHz and avoids any fractional-divider jitter. You
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] would need to add 650 cycles of delay to the existing 3125-cycle program (for example, by
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] padding the `[n]` delay fields in the `left16` / `right16` loops).
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] If you want the exact register write for option 1 in the SDK style:
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] pio->sm[sm].clkdiv = (1u << 16) | (53u << 8);   // 44.141 kHz
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-22 18:19:27 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:21:55 [nitro@ryzen5] a circuit for pico ADC pin to phono connector for electric guitar
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6]  ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.7 ⛲  kimi-k2.6 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.077 527B 246.85s
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] Here is a practical, robust interface circuit. It handles the three main problems:
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] • AC coupling & bias – a guitar signal swings positive and negative, but the RP2040 ADC
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] only accepts 0 V to 3.3 V.
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] • Over-voltage protection – plugging in, switching pedals, or hot pickups can create spikes
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] well outside the safe range.
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] • Impedance matching – guitar pickups like to see a high impedance, while the ADC samples
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] best from a low-impedance source.
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] Two options are shown: an active buffered circuit (recommended, best performance) and a
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] passive circuit (fewer parts, acceptable for simple projects).
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] Recommended: Single-Supply Op-Amp Buffer/Attenuator
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] An inverting stage gives a predictable gain below unity, a very low output impedance for the ADC,
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] and a bias point that sits cleanly at mid-rail.
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6]                     Pico 3V3 (Pin 36)
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6]                          |
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6]                         Rb1
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6]                        100k
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6]                          |
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6]                          +--- U1 Pin 3 (V+)
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6]                         Cb   MCP6002
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6]                        100nF
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6]                          |
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6]                         GND
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] Guitar ----+--- C1 ---- Rin ----+--- U1 Pin 2 (V-)
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] (RCA tip)  |   100nF   1M      |
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6]           Rgnd               Rf
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6]           1M                 470k
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6]            |                  |
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6]           GND                |
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6]                                |
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6]                          U1 Pin 1 (Output)
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6]                                |
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6]                               Rout
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6]                               1k
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6]                                |
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6]                                +-----> Pico ADC (GP26 / 27 / 28)
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6]                                |
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6]                    +-----------+
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6]                    |           |
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6]                   D1          D2
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6]                   |^|         |^|
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6]                   | |         | |
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6]                 3.3V        GND
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] • How it works*
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] • Rgnd (1 MΩ) provides the DC path to ground that the guitar needs and sets the AC input
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] impedance to about 500 kΩ (acceptable for most pickups).
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] • C1 blocks DC. With Rin it forms a high-pass filter at ~1.6 Hz, preserving low
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] E-string fundamentals.
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] • Rin (1 MΩ) and Rf (470 kΩ) set an AC gain of –0.47. A hot ±3 V guitar
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] signal is reduced to about ±1.4 V, keeping the output inside the 0 V – 3.3 V rails with the 1.65 V
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] bias.
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] • Rb1/Rb2 (100 kΩ each) bias the op-amp’s non-inverting input at 1.65 V. Cb
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] keeps that reference quiet.
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] • Because the input is AC-coupled, the op-amp’s DC gain is 1, so the output rests at 1.65 V with no
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] signal.
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] • Rout (1 kΩ) and the Schottky clamp diodes D1/D2 protect the ADC pin from
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] transients that exceed 0 V – 3.3 V.
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] • Cfilter (100 pF) at the ADC pin tames sampling glitches.
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] BOM – Buffered Version
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] ╭────────────┬────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] │  Ref       │  Value / Part          │  Notes                                           │
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] ├────────────┼────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-05-25 15:26:04 [kimi-k2.6] │  U1        │  MCP6002 (or MCP6001)  │  Rail-to-rail I/O, 3.3 V capable.                │
2026-05-25 15:26:05 [kimi-k2.6] │  J1        │  RCA phono jack        │  Panel mount; sleeve to GND.                     │
2026-05-25 15:26:05 [kimi-k2.6] │  Rgnd      │  1 MΩ                  │  Metal film, 1 %. Input pulldown.                │
2026-05-25 15:26:05 [kimi-k2.6] │  Rin       │  1 MΩ                  │  Sets input impedance along with Rgnd.           │
2026-05-25 15:26:05 [kimi-k2.6] │  Rf        │  470 kΩ                │  Feedback resistor; gain ≈ 0.47.                 │
2026-05-25 15:26:05 [kimi-k2.6] │  Rb1, Rb2  │  100 kΩ                │  Bias divider.                                   │
2026-05-25 15:26:05 [kimi-k2.6] │  Rout      │  1 kΩ                  │  Output isolation / current limit.               │
2026-05-25 15:26:05 [kimi-k2.6] │  C1        │  100 nF                │  Film or C0G; input coupling.                    │
2026-05-25 15:26:05 [kimi-k2.6] │  Cb        │  100 nF                │  Bias decoupling.                                │
2026-05-25 15:26:05 [kimi-k2.6] │  Cdec      │  100 nF                │  Op-amp VDD decoupling, placed close to the IC.  │
2026-05-25 15:26:05 [kimi-k2.6] │  Cfilter   │  100 pF                │  ADC pin filter.                                 │
2026-05-25 15:26:05 [kimi-k2.6] │  D1, D2    │  BAT54 (or 1N5819)     │  Schottky clamp diodes.                          │
