2026-05-25 15:26:06 [kimi-k2.6] Usage Tips
2026-05-25 15:26:06 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:26:06 [kimi-k2.6] • ADC reference: The Pico defaults to a 3.3 V reference. With no guitar plugged in, the ADC
2026-05-25 15:26:06 [kimi-k2.6] should read roughly half-scale (~2048 for 12-bit raw).
2026-05-25 15:26:06 [kimi-k2.6] • Sampling rate: In the passive version, the ADC is driven through a high resistance. If
2026-05-25 15:26:06 [kimi-k2.6] you sample too fast the reading may bleed between channels or settle poorly; either add a small
2026-05-25 15:26:06 [kimi-k2.6] delay between readings or reduce the ADC clock.
2026-05-25 15:26:06 [kimi-k2.6] • Op-amp stability: Keep leads to the MCP6002 short and place the 100 nF decoupling cap
2026-05-25 15:26:06 [kimi-k2.6] directly across its supply pins.
2026-05-25 15:26:06 [kimi-k2.6] • Shielding: Use shielded cable between the RCA jack and the board, and keep the analog
2026-05-25 15:26:06 [kimi-k2.6] section away from the Pico’s switching regulator if possible.
2026-05-25 15:26:06 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:34:13 [nitro@ryzen5] circuit to measure paddle pinsatari 9 pin from pico?
2026-05-25 15:37:52 [kimi-k2.6]  ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.7 ⛲  kimi-k2.6 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.049 8.588KB 219.10s
2026-05-25 15:37:52 [kimi-k2.6] Atari paddles are passive: each is just a 1 MΩ (approx.) potentiometer wired as a two-terminal
2026-05-25 15:37:52 [kimi-k2.6] rheostat between the +V pin and the sense pin, plus a momentary button that shorts to ground.
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] The original Atari console measured them by discharging a capacitor and timing how long it took to
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] charge back through the pot. You can replicate that exactly with the Pico’s GPIOs—no ADC
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] required—which avoids the high source-impedance headaches you get trying to read a 1 MΩ divider
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] directly.
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] Below is the recommended GPIO RC-timing circuit, plus a simpler ADC fallback if you
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] prefer a voltage-based approach.
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] Atari DE-9 Pinout (Paddle Functions)
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] ╭───────────┬───────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────╮
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] │  DB9 Pin  │  Paddle Function              │  Direction                 │
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] ├───────────┼───────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────┤
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] │  1        │  Button A (Left)              │  Active-low → GND          │
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] │  2        │  Button B (Right)             │  Active-low → GND          │
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] │  5        │  Potentiometer B (wiper)      │  Input to Pico             │
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] │  7        │  +5 V (excitation)            │  Output **from** Pico 3V3  │
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] │  8        │  GND                          │  Common ground             │
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] │  9        │  Potentiometer A (wiper)      │  Input to Pico             │
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] │  3, 4, 6  │  Unused for standard paddles  │  —                         │
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] ╰───────────┴───────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────╯
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] • Important: Because the Pico is a 3.3 V device, do not* connect this adapter to a
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] powered Atari console while the Pico is plugged in. Your circuit should supply 3.3 V to DB9 pin 7
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] itself.
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] Recommended: RC Timing with GPIOs
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] This gives the best linearity and keeps the ADC channels free.
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] Schematic (one paddle channel; duplicate for second)
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] Pico 3V3 (Pin 36) ----+---------------------------------------------------> DB9 Pin 7
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6]                       |
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6]                      Cdec
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6]                     100 nF
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6]                       |
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6]                      GND
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] DB9 Pin 9 (Paddle A) ---- Rseries ----+---- Ctiming ---- GND
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6]                                       |
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6]                                       +----------------> Pico GPx (digital)
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6]                                       |
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6]                                 (optional)
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6]                                 D1  D2
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6]                                 |^| |^|
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6]                                GND  3V3
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] DB9 Pin 1 (Button A) ---- Rlimit ----+----> Pico GPy (digital, pull-up)
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6]                                      |
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6]                                     Rpull
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6]                                     10 k
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6]                                      |
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6]                                     3V3
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] How It Works
2026-05-25 15:37:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] 1. The Pico drives GPx as an output-low. This discharges Ctiming through
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] Rseries.
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] 2. After a few microseconds, the Pico switches GPx to a high-impedance input.
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] 3. Ctiming now charges toward 3.3 V through the paddle potentiometer.
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] 4. The Pico counts how long until GPx reads high.
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6]    Time ∝ Resistance, so the count is proportional to paddle position.
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] Because both channels can be discharged and timed in parallel on two GPIOs, a full two-paddle scan
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] takes only one charge interval.
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] BOM – RC Timing Version
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] ╭────────────┬─────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] │ Ref        │ Value / Part    │ Notes                                              │
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] ├────────────┼─────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] │ Ctiming A,B│ 4.7 nF (C0G/NP0)│ Timing capacitor. 2.2 nF–10 nF works; see below.   │
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] │ Cdec       │ 100 nF          │ Decouple the 3.3 V excitation near the connector.  │
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] │ Rseries    │ 1 kΩ            │ Limits current when GPIO discharges cap (min pot   │
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] │            │                 │ resistance can be near 0 Ω).                       │
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] │ Rpull A,B  │ 10 kΩ           │ External pull-ups for buttons (optional; Pico      │
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] │            │                 │ internal pull-ups may also be used).               │
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] │ Rlimit A,B │ 330 Ω           │ Series protection for button GPIOs.                │
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] │ D1,D2      │ BAT54S (dual)   │ Optional clamp; keeps pin within –0.3 V … +3.6 V. │
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] ╰────────────┴─────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] • Choosing Ctiming*
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] ╭──────────┬────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] │  Value   │  Max time (≈1 MΩ)  │  Best for                                                     │
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] ├──────────┼────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] │  10 nF   │  ~8 ms             │  Very low noise; fine if you sample both paddles in parallel  │
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] │  4.7 nF  │  ~4 ms             │  Good compromise (recommended starting point)                 │
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] │  2.2 nF  │  ~2 ms             │  Faster reads; small cable capacitance matters more           │
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] │  1 nF    │  ~0.9 ms           │  Use only with short, low-capacitance cables                  │
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] ╰──────────┴────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] A 1 m controller cable contributes roughly 50–150 pF of parasitic capacitance; 4.7 nF swamps that
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] nicely without making frame deadlines tight.
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] Alternative: ADC Voltage Divider
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] If you strongly prefer an analog voltage, use a divider and buffer it. A 1 MΩ source
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] directly into the Pico ADC is not ideal for 12-bit accuracy because of leakage and sample-hold
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] charge injection.
2026-05-25 15:37:54 [kimi-k2.6] 
