2026-05-26 16:27:59 [kimi-k2.6] For a standard DB9 joystick VCC pin (pin 7 on Atari/Commodore/Sega-style sticks), you do not
2026-05-26 16:27:59 [kimi-k2.6] want a series resistor. Wire it directly to your regulated supply rail.
2026-05-26 16:27:59 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-26 16:27:59 [kimi-k2.6] Why no resistor?
2026-05-26 16:27:59 [kimi-k2.6] The VCC pin is a power rail, not a logic signal. A series resistor causes a voltage drop
2026-05-26 16:27:59 [kimi-k2.6] under load (`V = I·R`), which can sag the supply and make active electronics inside the pad
2026-05-26 16:27:59 [kimi-k2.6] (autofire, shift registers, etc.) behave erratically or reset.
2026-05-26 16:27:59 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-26 16:27:59 [kimi-k2.6] What to do instead
2026-05-26 16:27:59 [kimi-k2.6] ╭────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-05-26 16:27:59 [kimi-k2.6] │  Situation                                                         │  Recommendation                                                                                                                                                                                               │
2026-05-26 16:27:59 [kimi-k2.6] ├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-05-26 16:27:59 [kimi-k2.6] │  **Passive stick** (switches only—most Atari/Commodore joysticks)  │  You often do **not** need to connect DB9 pin 7 at all. Just tie DB9 pin 8 (GND) to your MCU ground, connect the direction/fire pins to GPIOs, and enable the MCU’s internal pull‑ups.                        │
2026-05-26 16:27:59 [kimi-k2.6] │  **Active stick** (Sega, autofire, paddles, etc.)                  │  Connect the DB9 VCC pin directly to the same voltage the stick expects—almost always **+5 V**. Do not power a 5 V device from a 3.3 V MCU rail; use a 5 V regulator if your MCU board does not provide one.  │
2026-05-26 16:27:59 [kimi-k2.6] │  **Short‑circuit / overcurrent protection**                        │  If you are worried about a short in the cable or stick, use a **PTC resettable fuse** (e.g., 100–250 mA) rather than a resistor.                                                                             │
2026-05-26 16:27:59 [kimi-k2.6] │  **Decoupling**                                                    │  Place a **100 nF ceramic capacitor** between DB9 VCC and GND right at the connector to suppress switching noise.                                                                                             │
2026-05-26 16:27:59 [kimi-k2.6] ╰────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-05-26 16:27:59 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-26 16:27:59 [kimi-k2.6] If you meant signal pull‑ups
2026-05-26 16:27:59 [kimi-k2.6] In case you are actually asking about the direction/fire lines: enable your microcontroller’s
2026-05-26 16:27:59 [kimi-k2.6] internal pull‑ups (typically 20–50 kΩ), or add an external 10 kΩ resistor between your MCU
2026-05-26 16:27:59 [kimi-k2.6] VCC and each DB9 signal pin. Those go on the signal pins, not the VCC pin.
2026-05-26 16:27:59 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-26 16:27:59 [kimi-k2.6] • Bottom line:* Power pin = direct copper (0 Ω). Use a fuse for safety, and a decoupling
2026-05-26 16:27:59 [kimi-k2.6] cap for stability.
2026-05-26 16:27:59 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-26 16:28:35 [nitro@ryzen5] for reading the paddle x and y
2026-05-26 16:30:21 [kimi-k2.6]  ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.7 ⛲  kimi-k2.6 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.027 3.867KB 105.96s
2026-05-26 16:30:21 [kimi-k2.6] For paddles you still wire DB9 pin 7 straight to your VCC rail—no series resistor there.
2026-05-26 16:30:21 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-26 16:30:21 [kimi-k2.6] The resistor you are thinking of belongs on the paddle signal lines (DB9 pins 5 and 9), not
2026-05-26 16:30:21 [kimi-k2.6] on the power pin.
2026-05-26 16:30:21 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-26 16:30:21 [kimi-k2.6] Simple RC reading circuit (the usual way)
2026-05-26 16:30:22 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-26 16:30:22 [kimi-k2.6] ╭───────────┬───────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-05-26 16:30:22 [kimi-k2.6] │  DB9 pin  │  Function         │  Connection                            │
2026-05-26 16:30:22 [kimi-k2.6] ├───────────┼───────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-05-26 16:30:22 [kimi-k2.6] │  7        │  +5 V (or 3.3 V)  │  Direct to your regulated supply rail  │
2026-05-26 16:30:22 [kimi-k2.6] │  8        │  GND              │  Direct to MCU ground                  │
2026-05-26 16:30:22 [kimi-k2.6] │  5        │  Paddle A / X     │  Series resistor → MCU GPIO            │
2026-05-26 16:30:22 [kimi-k2.6] │  9        │  Paddle B / Y     │  Series resistor → MCU GPIO            │
2026-05-26 16:30:22 [kimi-k2.6] ╰───────────┴───────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-05-26 16:30:22 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-26 16:30:22 [kimi-k2.6] Add a capacitor (10–100 nF, e.g. 47 nF) from each of those GPIOs to ground.
2026-05-26 16:30:22 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-26 16:30:22 [kimi-k2.6] - Series resistor: 220 Ω to 1 kΩ (470 Ω is a good default).
2026-05-26 16:30:22 [kimi-k2.6]   This protects the GPIO when you drive it low to discharge the capacitor; if the paddle knob is at
2026-05-26 16:30:22 [kimi-k2.6] minimum resistance, the resistor limits current from VCC into the pin.
2026-05-26 16:30:22 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-26 16:30:22 [kimi-k2.6] - Capacitor: Needed for the RC timing method (replicate how the original hardware worked).
2026-05-26 16:30:22 [kimi-k2.6] You discharge it through the GPIO, release the pin, then measure how long the pot resistance takes
2026-05-26 16:30:22 [kimi-k2.6] to charge the cap to your logic-high threshold.
2026-05-26 16:30:22 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-26 16:30:22 [kimi-k2.6] Voltage level note
2026-05-26 16:30:22 [kimi-k2.6] If your microcontroller is a 3.3 V part, power the paddles from 3.3 V on pin 7 so the
2026-05-26 16:30:22 [kimi-k2.6] capacitor cannot charge above your pin rating. If you feed pin 7 with 5 V while using 3.3 V GPIOs,
2026-05-26 16:30:22 [kimi-k2.6] you will over-voltage the pins when the pot is at minimum resistance.
2026-05-26 16:30:22 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-26 16:30:22 [kimi-k2.6] Why not just use the ADC?
2026-05-26 16:30:22 [kimi-k2.6] Original Atari/Commodore paddles use the pot as a one-megohm rheostat (two terminals), not
2026-05-26 16:30:22 [kimi-k2.6] as a voltage divider. You can rewire or add an external pull-down to force a divider for an ADC,
2026-05-26 16:30:22 [kimi-k2.6] but resolution ends up poor and non-linear. The RC timing method above is the standard approach and
2026-05-26 16:30:22 [kimi-k2.6] gives the full 0–1 MΩ range cleanly.
2026-05-26 16:30:22 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-26 16:35:18 [nitro@ryzen5] can i just use internal pullup or down for the paddles
2026-05-26 16:36:37 [kimi-k2.6]  ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.7 ⛲  kimi-k2.6 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.021 6.193KB 77.73s
2026-05-26 16:36:37 [kimi-k2.6] No—internal pull-ups or pull-downs will not give you usable readings from standard Atari-style
2026-05-26 16:36:37 [kimi-k2.6] paddles.
2026-05-26 16:36:37 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-26 16:36:37 [kimi-k2.6] A paddle is a 1 MΩ rheostat between VCC and the signal pin. A microcontroller’s internal
2026-05-26 16:36:37 [kimi-k2.6] pull resistor is typically 20 kΩ–50 kΩ, and the datasheet usually specifies that value only
2026-05-26 16:36:37 [kimi-k2.6] as a broad range (it varies by lot, temperature, and voltage). That mismatch ruins both
2026-05-26 16:36:37 [kimi-k2.6] voltage-divider and timing approaches.
2026-05-26 16:36:37 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-26 16:36:37 [kimi-k2.6] Why it fails
2026-05-26 16:36:37 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-26 16:36:37 [kimi-k2.6] ╭────────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-05-26 16:36:37 [kimi-k2.6] │  Method                        │  What happens                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                │
2026-05-26 16:36:37 [kimi-k2.6] ├────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-05-26 16:36:37 [kimi-k2.6] │  **Internal pull-down + ADC**  │  You create a divider: V_pin = VCC × R_internal / (R_paddle + R_internal). With 50 kΩ against 1 MΩ, the pin only rises to about **0.24 V** at full resistance. Nearly the entire knob travel is compressed into the bottom few percent of your ADC range, giving coarse, non-linear steps. And because R_internal is poorly specified, the same paddle position yields different values on different chips.  │
2026-05-26 16:36:37 [kimi-k2.6] │  **Internal pull-up**          │  The paddle does not have a ground lead inside the cable; the potentiometer connects between VCC (pin 7) and the signal pin. To use a pull-up you would have to rewire pin 7 to GND, which is non-standard. Even then, the divider is hyperbolic and most of the travel is squashed near the top of the ADC range, again with lot-to-lot variation.                                                          │
2026-05-26 16:36:37 [kimi-k2.6] │  **Digital timing**            │  The classic RC method needs a capacitor to ground and a known charge curve. Internal pull resistors are not precision timing elements; their value is too high and too loose to charge an external capacitor in a predictable, linear way.                                                                                                                                                                  │
2026-05-26 16:36:37 [kimi-k2.6] ╰────────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-05-26 16:36:37 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-26 16:36:37 [kimi-k2.6] What you actually need
2026-05-26 16:36:37 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-26 16:36:37 [kimi-k2.6] For the standard RC timing method (one GPIO per paddle):
2026-05-26 16:36:37 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-26 16:36:38 [kimi-k2.6] • Capacitor: 47–100 nF from the signal pin to ground.
2026-05-26 16:36:38 [kimi-k2.6] • Series resistor: 470 Ω–1 kΩ between the DB9 signal pin and your MCU GPIO. This limits
2026-05-26 16:36:38 [kimi-k2.6] current when the GPIO drives low to discharge the capacitor and the paddle happens to be at 0 Ω.
2026-05-26 16:36:38 [kimi-k2.6] • VCC on DB9 pin 7: Wired direct to your supply rail (3.3 V or 5 V).
2026-05-26 16:36:38 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-26 16:36:38 [kimi-k2.6] Drive the GPIO low to discharge the cap, release it to input, then count the time until the pin
2026-05-26 16:36:38 [kimi-k2.6] reads high. The charge time is directly proportional to paddle resistance (t ≈ 0.7·R·C), so the
2026-05-26 16:36:38 [kimi-k2.6] result is linear and repeatable across the whole knob travel.
2026-05-26 16:36:38 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-26 16:36:38 [kimi-k2.6] If you absolutely must avoid a capacitor and use the ADC, you would need an external
2026-05-26 16:36:38 [kimi-k2.6] precision resistor to ground (on the order of 100 kΩ–1 MΩ) and some buffering to keep the response
2026-05-26 16:36:38 [kimi-k2.6] reasonably linear—but the RC method is far simpler and matches how the original hardware worked.
2026-05-26 16:36:38 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-05-26 17:51:48 [nitro@ryzen5] i want a pratical simple way of measuring with adc pin valud of db9 paddles
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] [gpt-5.3-codex] Yes — simplest practical ADC method is:
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] -	Power paddle on DB9 pin 7
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] -	GND on pin 8
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] -	Read pin 5 (X) and pin 9 (Y) as voltage dividers by adding one resistor per channel to
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] GND.
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] Because Atari paddles are usually ~1M rheostat to VCC, you must add a pull-down to create a
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] divider.
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] Minimal wiring (per paddle channel)
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] -	DB9 pin 7 → MCU VREF/VCC (3.3V preferred if MCU is 3.3V)
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] -	DB9 pin 8 → GND
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] -	DB9 pin 5 (or 9) → ADC input
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] -	ADC input → 100k resistor to GND
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] -	Optional: ADC input → 100nF capacitor to GND (smoothing/noise)
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] So for X and Y you use two 100k resistors (and optionally two caps).
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] Why 100k?
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] It gives a usable ADC swing with a 1M paddle:
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] \[
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] V_{adc}=Vcc \cdot \frac{R_{pd}}{R_{paddle}+R_{pd}}
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] \]
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] With \(R_{pd}=100k\), \(R_{paddle}=0..1M\):
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] -	at 0Ω: ~Vcc
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] -	at 1M: ~0.09·Vcc
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] So you get most of the range, and can map in software.
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] Super simple software approach
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] -	Take 8–16 ADC samples and average.
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] -	Calibrate min/max at startup (user turn full left/right).
2026-05-26 17:52:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] -	Map calibrated range to 0–100% (or game coordinate).
2026-05-26 17:52:07 [gpt-5.3-codex] -	If direction is reversed, invert in software.
2026-05-26 17:52:07 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 17:52:07 [gpt-5.3-codex] Important
2026-05-26 17:52:07 [gpt-5.3-codex] -	Do not put a series resistor in DB9 VCC pin.
2026-05-26 17:52:07 [gpt-5.3-codex] -	If MCU ADC pin is 3.3V max, power DB9 pin 7 from 3.3V (not 5V), unless you add proper
2026-05-26 17:52:07 [gpt-5.3-codex] protection/divider.
2026-05-26 17:52:07 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 17:52:07 [gpt-5.3-codex] If you want, I can give a tiny calibration/mapping routine for your specific MCU (AVR, RP2040,
2026-05-26 17:52:07 [gpt-5.3-codex] STM32, ESP32, etc.).
2026-05-26 17:52:07 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
