2026-05-26 20:02:13 [nitro@ryzen5] this should run it and send a magic value through the fifo?
2026-05-26 20:02:24 [gpt-5.3-codex] You’re very close, but there are two important ordering/config issues:
2026-05-26 20:02:24 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:02:24 [gpt-5.3-codex] 1. You write to the FIFO before the SM is initialized
2026-05-26 20:02:24 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	`pio_sm_put_blocking(pio, sm, ...)` should generally happen after `pio_sm_init(...)` (and
2026-05-26 20:02:24 [gpt-5.3-codex] usually before enabling, or after enabling depending on program behavior).
2026-05-26 20:02:24 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:02:24 [gpt-5.3-codex] 2. Pin directions for PIO SET/OUT should be configured in SM config
2026-05-26 20:02:24 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	You should set pin directions via:
2026-05-26 20:02:24 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- `sm_config_set_set_pins(...)` / `sm_config_set_out_pins(...)` (you did),
2026-05-26 20:02:24 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- and `sm_config_set_out_shift(...)` / pindirs setup as needed by your `.pio`,
2026-05-26 20:02:24 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- often `pio_sm_set_consecutive_pindirs(...)` after init to ensure output direction from SM side.
2026-05-26 20:02:24 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:02:24 [gpt-5.3-codex] Also, your “magic value” expression `165e6/440` is a floating literal; it’ll convert, but it’s
2026-05-26 20:02:24 [gpt-5.3-codex] cleaner to make it explicit integer math (e.g. `125000000u / 440u` depending on your real clock).
2026-05-26 20:02:24 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:02:24 [gpt-5.3-codex] So yes: conceptually this is “send period/divider value through TX FIFO,” if your PIO
2026-05-26 20:02:24 [gpt-5.3-codex] program is written to `pull` and use that value. But adjust init order and ensure SM pin dir/config
2026-05-26 20:02:24 [gpt-5.3-codex] matches the program.
2026-05-26 20:02:24 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:02:54 [nitro@ryzen5] this is pio code that runs the loop, will the tone be audible?
2026-05-26 20:02:56 [nitro@ryzen5] ; spkr - toggle gpio in audio space; 166.5mhz Pico2 PIO .program spkr		pull	block.wrap_target	set     pins, 1	mov	y, osrloop1:	jmp     y--, loop1	set     pins, 0	mov	y, osrloop2:	jmp     y--, loop2.wrap
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] Short answer: yes, it should produce an audible tone (square wave), assuming the pin is
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] connected to a piezo/speaker circuit correctly.
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] Your PIO does:
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] - `set pins, 1`
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] - delay loop for `OSR` counts
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] - `set pins, 0`
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] - delay loop for `OSR` counts
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] - repeat
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] So one full period is two equal delays → ~50% duty square wave.
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] Frequency estimate
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] Each `jmp y--, loopX` takes 1 cycle, and each loop runs about `N+1` cycles if `Y=N`.
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] So total period is roughly:
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] - high part: `1 (set) + (N+1) + 1 (mov)`
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] - low part: `1 (set) + (N+1) + 1 (mov)`
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] - plus wrap/jump overhead (small, ~1–2 cycles)
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] Approximate:
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] \[
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] f \approx \frac{f_{PIO}}{2N + 6 \text{ (ish)}}
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] \]
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] If you load `N = 165e6 / 440 ≈ 375000`, output will be around 220 Hz, not 440 Hz, because
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] you have two delay loops per full period.
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] So for 440 Hz, use roughly:
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] \[
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] N \approx \frac{f_{PIO}}{2 \cdot 440}
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] \]
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] (then fine-tune for instruction overhead).
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] Practical notes
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] - A raw GPIO pin can drive a piezo buzzer directly (usually).
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] - For an 8Ω speaker, use a transistor/amplifier (don’t drive directly from GPIO).
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] - You only `pull block` once, so pitch is fixed until SM restart or program changed. If you want
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] dynamic notes, add another `pull` in the wrap loop.
2026-05-26 20:03:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:04:22 [nitro@ryzen5] i added a capacitor between signal and ground at the connector, maybe i should snip it
2026-05-26 20:04:27 [gpt-5.3-codex] [gpt-5.3-codex] Yes — for a tone output, a signal-to-ground capacitor at the connector is
2026-05-26 20:04:27 [gpt-5.3-codex] usually a bad idea.
2026-05-26 20:04:27 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:04:27 [gpt-5.3-codex] It forms a low-pass load on the GPIO, which:
2026-05-26 20:04:27 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:04:27 [gpt-5.3-codex] - rounds off the square wave,
2026-05-26 20:04:27 [gpt-5.3-codex] - reduces amplitude at audio harmonics (and sometimes fundamental if large enough),
2026-05-26 20:04:27 [gpt-5.3-codex] - can make it quieter or seem “dull,”
2026-05-26 20:04:27 [gpt-5.3-codex] - increases pin current spikes.
2026-05-26 20:04:27 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:04:27 [gpt-5.3-codex] So if that cap is directly from GPIO signal to GND, I’d remove/sn ip it (or make it very small only
2026-05-26 20:04:27 [gpt-5.3-codex] for EMI, e.g. tens to low hundreds of pF, not nF/µF).
2026-05-26 20:04:28 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:04:28 [gpt-5.3-codex] Better approaches:
2026-05-26 20:04:28 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:04:28 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Piezo: drive directly or through a small series resistor (e.g. 100–330 Ω).
2026-05-26 20:04:28 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Speaker: GPIO → resistor/transistor/amplifier stage → speaker.
2026-05-26 20:04:28 [gpt-5.3-codex] - If you want AC coupling to an amp input, use a series capacitor in the signal path, not
2026-05-26 20:04:28 [gpt-5.3-codex] shunt-to-ground at the pin.
2026-05-26 20:04:28 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:04:28 [gpt-5.3-codex] If you want, I can suggest a minimal “safe GPIO to buzzer/speaker” wiring for Pico 2.
2026-05-26 20:04:28 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:19:32 [nitro@ryzen5] this is rca to tv speaker
2026-05-26 20:19:40 [gpt-5.3-codex] Got it — RCA line-in on a TV is different from driving a speaker directly.
2026-05-26 20:19:40 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:19:40 [gpt-5.3-codex] For RCA audio, a capacitor to ground on the signal is usually not what you want.
2026-05-26 20:19:40 [gpt-5.3-codex] You typically want:
2026-05-26 20:19:40 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:19:40 [gpt-5.3-codex] - GPIO/PWM/PIO output → series resistor (e.g. 1k–4.7k)
2026-05-26 20:19:40 [gpt-5.3-codex] - then series coupling capacitor (e.g. 1–10 µF, electrolytic with correct polarity)
2026-05-26 20:19:40 [gpt-5.3-codex] - then RCA center pin
2026-05-26 20:19:40 [gpt-5.3-codex] - grounds tied together
2026-05-26 20:19:40 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:19:40 [gpt-5.3-codex] So yes, if you added a shunt cap from signal to ground at the connector, remove it.
2026-05-26 20:19:40 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:19:40 [gpt-5.3-codex] Also important: GPIO square wave is “hot” and has DC component/noise. TV line-in expects ~line
2026-05-26 20:19:40 [gpt-5.3-codex] level analog. At minimum, use a resistor divider or larger series resistance to tame level. Even
2026-05-26 20:19:40 [gpt-5.3-codex] better: simple RC smoothing or PWM audio filter before RCA.
2026-05-26 20:19:40 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:19:40 [gpt-5.3-codex] So, concise answer: snip the signal-to-ground cap; use series coupling instead.
2026-05-26 20:19:40 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:21:00 [nitro@ryzen5] i have 330ohm in series
2026-05-26 20:21:07 [gpt-5.3-codex] 330 Ω series is a good start for protection, but for TV RCA line-in it’s usually still too
2026-05-26 20:21:07 [gpt-5.3-codex] “stiff/loud” from a 3.3 V GPIO square wave.
2026-05-26 20:21:07 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:21:07 [gpt-5.3-codex] What I’d do:
2026-05-26 20:21:07 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:21:07 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Keep the 330 Ω if you want, but add attenuation (target around 0.2–1.0 Vpp at RCA).
2026-05-26 20:21:07 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Add a series DC-block capacitor to RCA center (e.g. 1–10 µF).
2026-05-26 20:21:07 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Remove any signal-to-ground cap at the connector (as discussed), unless it’s a tiny EMI
2026-05-26 20:21:07 [gpt-5.3-codex] cap.
2026-05-26 20:21:07 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:21:07 [gpt-5.3-codex] A simple practical chain:
2026-05-26 20:21:07 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:21:07 [gpt-5.3-codex] • GPIO/PIO pin → 330 Ω → 2.2 k–10 k → coupling cap (1–10 µF) → RCA tip*
2026-05-26 20:21:07 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:21:07 [gpt-5.3-codex] And optionally a resistor from RCA tip to GND (e.g. 10 k) to define input bias.
2026-05-26 20:21:07 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:21:07 [gpt-5.3-codex] If the tone is distorted/too harsh, reduce level further (larger series resistor) and/or add a
2026-05-26 20:21:07 [gpt-5.3-codex] small RC low-pass before the coupling cap.
2026-05-26 20:21:07 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-26 20:21:07 [gpt-5.3-codex] So: 330 Ω alone works electrically, but for line input you generally want more attenuation + AC
2026-05-26 20:21:07 [gpt-5.3-codex] coupling.
2026-05-26 20:21:07 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
