2026-05-28 11:47:04 [nitro@ryzen5] any reason why writecomport would hang?
2026-05-28 11:47:15 [gpt-5.3-codex] Yes — a few common reasons on Windows.
2026-05-28 11:47:15 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-28 11:47:15 [gpt-5.3-codex] Most likely in your current code: the COM port isn’t configured and no write timeouts are
2026-05-28 11:47:15 [gpt-5.3-codex] set.
2026-05-28 11:47:15 [gpt-5.3-codex] With default driver behavior, `WriteFile` can block unexpectedly.
2026-05-28 11:47:15 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-28 11:47:15 [gpt-5.3-codex] Likely causes
2026-05-28 11:47:15 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-28 11:47:15 [gpt-5.3-codex] - No `SetCommState` call (baud, byte size, parity, stop bits not explicitly set).
2026-05-28 11:47:16 [gpt-5.3-codex] - No `SetCommTimeouts` call (write can wait a long time or appear hung).
2026-05-28 11:47:16 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Flow control mismatch (RTS/CTS or XON/XOFF enabled on one side, not the other).
2026-05-28 11:47:16 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Device not actually consuming data (driver/USB-serial stack can stall writes).
2026-05-28 11:47:16 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Port opened by another process previously in odd state.
2026-05-28 11:47:16 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Large write buffer + blocked downstream endpoint (less likely for `"\nBOOT\n"` but
2026-05-28 11:47:16 [gpt-5.3-codex] possible with bad state).
2026-05-28 11:47:16 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-28 11:47:16 [gpt-5.3-codex] What to do
2026-05-28 11:47:16 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-28 11:47:16 [gpt-5.3-codex] 1. After `CreateFileA`, call:
2026-05-28 11:47:16 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- `SetupComm`
2026-05-28 11:47:16 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- `GetCommState` + `SetCommState` (8N1, desired baud)
2026-05-28 11:47:16 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- `GetCommTimeouts` + `SetCommTimeouts` (set sane write timeout)
2026-05-28 11:47:16 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- optionally `PurgeComm`
2026-05-28 11:47:16 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-28 11:47:16 [gpt-5.3-codex] 2. In `writeComPort`, log `GetLastError()` when `WriteFile` fails.
2026-05-28 11:47:16 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-28 11:47:16 [gpt-5.3-codex] 3. Optionally use overlapped I/O for non-blocking behavior and explicit timeout control.
2026-05-28 11:47:16 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-28 11:47:16 [gpt-5.3-codex] Quick practical check
2026-05-28 11:47:16 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-28 11:47:16 [gpt-5.3-codex] If you add only one thing first, add `SetCommTimeouts` with a nonzero `WriteTotalTimeoutConstant`
2026-05-28 11:47:16 [gpt-5.3-codex] (e.g. 500–2000 ms).
2026-05-28 11:47:16 [gpt-5.3-codex] That alone often turns a “hang” into a proper timeout/failure you can diagnose.
2026-05-28 11:47:16 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
