2026-05-28 12:01:25 [gpt-5.3-codex] Good question — you’re very close.
2026-05-28 12:01:25 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-28 12:01:25 [gpt-5.3-codex] In your function, you compute `ok` but then always `return true;`, so failure is ignored.
2026-05-28 12:01:25 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-28 12:01:25 [gpt-5.3-codex] Also, Windows uses:
2026-05-28 12:01:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] - `BOOL` (WinAPI type, `TRUE`/`FALSE`)
2026-05-28 12:01:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] - C++ `bool` (`true`/`false`)
2026-05-28 12:01:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-28 12:01:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] They interoperate fine; just convert explicitly in return paths.
2026-05-28 12:01:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-28 12:01:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] Use:
2026-05-28 12:01:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-28 12:01:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] - `return ok != FALSE;`
2026-05-28 12:01:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] or
2026-05-28 12:01:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] - `return ok == TRUE;`
2026-05-28 12:01:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-28 12:01:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] Also worth checking `hSerial` for `INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE`.
2026-05-28 12:01:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-05-28 12:01:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] So logically your function should:
2026-05-28 12:01:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] 1. validate handle
2026-05-28 12:01:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] 2. `GetCommState`
2026-05-28 12:01:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] 3. change fields
2026-05-28 12:01:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] 4. `SetCommState`
2026-05-28 12:01:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] 5. return success/failure based on that call
